window.GRAPH_EN_LAYER = {"scenes": {"В1-01": {"title": "The Lamp Room", "date": "September 2009", "photo_caption": "The Glubokaya lamp room. The issue window", "text": [{"md": "Two years of mining college I sat through for one piece of paper, and in September of '09 they hand it to me. With it I go to the Glubokaya mine, to Dad's crew, as a trainee coalface miner. A coalface miner is the man who cuts coal at the longwall face. His trainee hands him the wrench, hauls props and tries to stay out from under his feet. That's exactly what it says in my employment book now, and Mom holds that book in her hands all evening like a diploma."}, {"md": "Vitka's here too, on the next crew over. Welder can't reach him underground, and we pay off our debt to him out of every paycheck, five thousand a head; Vitka calls it \"a mortgage with no apartment.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "Vitka came back from the army last summer and got a job here right away, on the next crew over. He says after Chita it's actually cozy underground.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "In the smoking room Vitka suddenly says, without looking at me: \"Listen, Andryukh. I had this dream that you and me got buried in some pit in a birch wood, and you're dragging me to a ladder by the scruff like a kitten. I've never been in a pit like that in my life.\" He laughs and lights a second one before he's finished the first.", "if": {"op": "lived", "id": "В4-04"}}, {"md": "A morning at the mine starts in the pithead building: first the bathhouse, where you leave your clean clothes and put on overalls stiff with yesterday's dust, then the assignment room, where the foreman reads out who goes where, then the lamp room. The lamp room looks like a ticket window at a train station. Behind the window are racks where hundreds of lamps sit charging in their slots, each with its own number. You hand a tin tag through the window and get a cap lamp and a self-rescuer, an iron can on a belt that gives you half an hour of air when there's nothing around you to breathe. After the shift you hand in the lamp and take back your tag. By the tags on their hooks you can see who's come up top and who hasn't."}, {"md": "The lamp-room girl is called Tanya. She's twenty-two, her bangs are dyed eggplant purple, and to everyone who hands in a lamp she says the same thing: \"Brought it back. Good boy.\" On my second day I ask, \"And if I don't?\" and get smacked on the helmet with the issue log. My tag is number two hundred forty-six; Tanya punched it for me herself and said it's a lucky number, because its last owner retired alive."}, {"md": "\"Don't joke with her like that,\" Dad says later in the smoking room. \"Her old man, Grisha Laptev, was down there with us in '05. His lamp sat on the rack for four days.\""}, {"md": "He finishes his cigarette and doesn't say anything else. Of those twelve, he was one of the last to come out alive."}, {"md": "They pair me up with Seryoga Pakhomov from Beryozovsky, four years older than me. Seryoga eats pork fat with sugar, laughs like a horse, and knows everything about the mine except why he works in it. The boss at the face is old Mikhalych. He's been underground forty years, since '69, his hands look like roots, and he tells his jokes in rotation, the same one every third shift."}, {"md": "The first time, the cage, the iron box of the mine lift, drops so fast my ears pop and my stomach stays up top at the level of the bathhouse. Thirty men stand in it shoulder to shoulder in silence. Down below it smells of wet rock and machine oil. The light from my lamp jumps around the walls, and I can't make it hold still."}, {"md": "Underground you can't be alone; everybody who can be bothered explains that to me in the first week. You have to stick with somebody. Up top too, as it turns out."}, {"md": "Some nights I still wake up at exactly four-twelve. Dad doesn't sleep either, on the other side of the wall; I hear him open the balcony door. We never talked about it, not then and not later.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I follow Dad around like a tail, and he doesn't chase me off. Underground he talks more than he does at home in a week, and all of it to the point. \"Listen to the roof. Before it comes down, it always talks.\" I listen to the roof, and it says nothing."}, {"md": "At the end of the month we come up together, and in the bathhouse he scrubs my back with a washcloth like when I was little, silently and hard. The men laugh. Dad doesn't even look at them."}], "B": [{"md": "Mikhalych takes me under his wing on the third day, after I drop a prop on my own foot. \"Listen here, Samokhin. Every safety rule is written in blood, but not always the right blood.\" What that means he doesn't explain, and I don't ask."}, {"md": "He does teach me the signs that the seam is getting ready to blow: the coal crackles like dry firewood in a stove, fine grit trickles out of the face, and you feel a cold draft. \"When you hear it shooting, don't stand there, get out. Don't argue with it.\""}], "C": [{"md": "I hand in my lamp last, slowly, on purpose. Tanya takes it, wipes the glass with a rag and sets it in its slot carefully, the way Mom puts away the good china."}, {"md": "\"What are you standing there for?\" she asks."}, {"md": "\"Waiting for my tag.\""}, {"md": "The tag is hanging from her hand. She doesn't give it back right away, and in the second week she pours me tea from her thermos on top of it, strong and sweet like on a train. We drink it in silence, on opposite sides of the window."}], "D": [{"md": "Nika picks up on the second ring, as if she'd been sitting by the phone."}, {"md": "\"Well?\" she says. \"What's it like?\""}, {"md": "I tell her about the cage, about my ears, about Mikhalych and his joke. She laughs in the right places, and at the end she says, \"Samokhin, you nearly drowned when you were seven. Why would you go somewhere dark?\" I don't know what to say, so I tell her another joke. Every call costs me fifty rubles, and by the end of the month my phone is three hundred in the red."}], "E": [{"md": "I ask to transfer to the next crew, to Vitka's, and the foreman looks at me like I'm an idiot: the father's at the face, and the son runs off to his buddy. Dad doesn't say anything. In the assignment room he stands at the other end and doesn't turn around."}, {"md": "Vitka works fast and sloppy, and I follow him around listening to the roof, though nobody ever taught me to. Once something above us clicks, quietly, like a twig underfoot. I grab him by the collar before I understand why. The roof is silent. Vitka looks at my hand on his collar and asks, \"What, again?\""}, {"md": "What \"again\" means, neither of us knows, and we don't talk about it for the rest of the shift."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Stick with Dad. He's been at this face twenty years"}, "B": {"label": "Stick with Mikhalych. You don't last forty years underground by accident"}, "C": {"label": "Hang around Tanya's window after shifts"}, "D": {"label": "Call Nika in Novosibirsk every evening and tell her what it's like down there"}, "E": {"label": "Stick with Vitka. We've crawled out from under the same roof once already… Not in this life"}}}, "В1-02": {"title": "Black Water", "date": "14 November 2009, 04:12", "photo_caption": "(frame not preserved)", "text": [{"md": "Night shift, the third longwall, past four in the morning. Dad's on first shift today, up top, and Mikhalych, Seryoga and I are finishing the end stall by the face, where the shearer can't reach and the coal is cut the old way, with a jackhammer. The jackhammer weighs as much as a good-sized pig and shakes so hard my teeth chatter by themselves. Mikhalych shovels coal onto the conveyor and for the third time this shift tells the joke about the mother-in-law and the parachute. For the third time I laugh, out of respect for his years. Seryoga is fiddling around twenty meters off, by the conveyor drive, singing \"Vladimirsky Tsentral,\" a prison ballad, off-key."}, {"md": "The seam starts to crackle. Quietly at first, like a stove when you've thrown in dry wood, then louder. Fine grit trickles out of the face onto my helmet, and a cold draft brushes my face, though there's nowhere for a draft to come from."}, {"md": "\"She's shooting,\" says Mikhalych, and stops shoveling. \"Hold on a minute.\""}, {"md": "Coal shoots at the Glubokaya every week, and every week nothing happens. The shift ends at six, we haven't made quota, and the foreman has promised to cut the whole crew's bonus. Mikhalych stands with his shovel, listening to the seam with his head cocked to one side like a hen."}, {"md": "My lamp picks out a crack above my jackhammer. Thin, like a hair. I look at it, and it looks back at me, I think."}], "after": {"D": [{"md": "I lay the jackhammer down on the floor, carefully, like something asleep, and step back from the face. Then another step. My knees won't bend, and I walk like I'm on stilts."}, {"md": "\"Mikhalych,\" I say. \"Let's go have a smoke.\""}, {"md": "You can't smoke underground, and we both know it. Mikhalych looks at me, at the crack, at me again, throws down his shovel and says, \"All right, let's go.\" We go behind the drive, to Seryoga, and I drag the two of them farther, toward the junction with the drift, though Seryoga swears and digs in his heels; he hasn't finished his verse."}, {"md": "The seam breathes out when we're forty meters away. There's almost no sound, just a shove of air in our backs and a rustling, steady and powerful, like inside a giant hourglass. The coal flows down the face like water, black and gleaming, fills the stall where I was standing, the conveyor, the drive, and stops ten meters from our boots. Then the gas comes, and the three of us run with our self-rescuers on, and I run first, because I know where."}, {"md": "Up top, Mikhalych sits on the bench outside the bathhouse and can't get his laces untied for a long time. \"Forty years,\" he says, to nobody in particular. \"Forty years I've been listening to her. How did you hear her, Samokhin?\" I shrug. I can't explain it to him; I've got nothing to say myself.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "МИХАЛЫЧ"}}}, {"md": "Up top, Mikhalych sits on the bench outside the bathhouse and can't get his laces untied for a long time. Then he asks quietly, \"How did you know?\" I don't answer. I've got nothing to answer with.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "МИХАЛЫЧ"}}}, {"md": "Tanya takes my lamp and holds it in her hands longer than she needs to."}, {"md": "\"Brought it back,\" she says. \"Good boy.\""}, {"md": "At home Bublik meets me. He's thirteen and hardly gets up off the mat by the door anymore, but now he stands up, pushes his nose into my knees and whines, thin, like the puppy he was at the backwater. I sit down on the floor in the front hall and hug him without taking off my jacket. Mom comes out at the noise, looks at us and doesn't ask anything.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БУБЛИК"}}}], "E": [{"md": "I yank the self-rescuer out of its case on my belt, rip off the seal and shove the mouthpiece in my mouth before I have time to think how it looks. The rubber is sour, breathing through it is tight. Mikhalych looks at me like I'm crazy."}, {"md": "\"What's got into you, Samokhin? She's only shooting.\""}, {"md": "Without a word I point at the crack, then at his self-rescuer, and tug at his sleeve. Mikhalych has forty years underground. He spits, but he puts it on, and we go to the drive, and from there, grabbing Seryoga, to the junction with the drift. Seryoga swears through his mouthpiece, and it all comes out as mooing."}, {"md": "The seam breathes out when we're forty meters away. First my ears pop, like in the cage, the air turns empty and thick, and the gas detector on Mikhalych's belt starts squealing, thin as a mosquito. Then behind us the coal rustles, steady and powerful, like inside a giant hourglass. We breathe through rubber and run, and I run first."}, {"md": "Up top, Mikhalych sits on the bench outside the bathhouse and can't get his laces untied for a long time. \"Gas before coal,\" he says, to nobody in particular. \"I read about that in a book, back in '79. Where'd you read it, Samokhin?\" I say I read it in a book too."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Keep cutting. It always shoots"}, "B": {"label": "Call Mikhalych over to look at the crack"}, "C": {"label": "Drop the jackhammer and run back along the face"}, "D": {"label": "\"I've seen this before.\" Back away from the face while it's only shooting, and take Mikhalych with me"}, "E": {"label": "\"The gas comes first.\" Pull on my self-rescuer right now, shove a second one at Mikhalych and lead him to the drift"}, "F": {"label": "\"I remember they dug Seryoga out alive.\" Lie down behind the drive, next to Seryoga, and wait it out"}}}, "В1-03": {"title": "Methane", "date": "3 March 2010, 04:40", "photo_caption": "The drift. Taken on a phone you're not allowed to use in the mine", "clock_label": "before the gas sinks to my face", "text": [{"md": "I don't tell anyone about November. Mikhalych keeps quiet too; only at New Year's he brought a bottle of Belochka vodka and a jar of pickled mushrooms to the locker room, set them on the bench and didn't leave for a long time. Seryoga tells everybody that Samokhin has a nose like a rat, and rats are the first to leave a sinking ship. That's his way of praising me."}, {"md": "Dying, it turns out, isn't something I can manage. What I can manage is remembering how I died, and going to work with that. Sometimes at lunch I suddenly feel coal between my teeth and wash it down with stewed-fruit juice for a long time."}, {"md": "A little after four the gas detector on my belt, the small box everybody has to carry, starts beeping. It's beeped before, at the Glubokaya it beeps all the time, but now the lights go out at the drive and the conveyor stops. The ventilation stops. The face goes quiet, so quiet you can hear water dripping from the roof. Forty meters behind me Seryoga is swearing, his lamp darting around the walls; he's yanking at something by the drive and can't let go of it."}, {"md": "Methane has no smell and you can't see it. That's the first thing they tell you in training and the only thing I'm sure I remember right now. It's light, and it collects under the roof. It's eight hundred meters to the shaft along the main drift, and the air there has stopped just like here. To the right is the old crosscut, a short passage to the next drift, half filled with fallen rock."}, {"md": "I know the section's ventilation plan by heart; it hangs in the assignment room, and I copied it into a notebook in college. The crosscut comes out on the next drift, and there's a different air current there, from a different fan. Only in the middle of the crosscut there's a stopping, a concrete wall with a door in it, and it's shut tight.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "The back of my neck goes cold, as if somebody were standing behind me breathing on it. Somebody who already stood on this spot and didn't go any farther. The chill pulls me left and down, past the crosscut, into the old incline.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 25}}}, {"md": "The back of my neck goes cold, briefly, and lets go right away.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 25}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}]}}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I run. Eight hundred meters along the drift, over the sleepers, with the self-rescuer in my mouth, and at five hundred my knees start arguing with me for real. I don't argue back, I keep running. My body is strong; it has enough for this."}, {"md": "At the shaft a mine rescuer in a mask catches me by the shoulders and sits me down on a rail. Half an hour later guys in masks bring Seryoga out, alive, angry and without his helmet. He sits down next to me and says, \"You rat, you could've at least called me.\" I give him my water."}], "B": [{"md": "In the crosscut I find the door in the stopping and remember which way it opens before I have time to get scared. The door is jammed; I hit it with my shoulder, and on the third try it gives. Cold air pulls through the gap, a real fresh current from the next drift, and I sit by that gap until the rescuers come."}, {"md": "They bring Seryoga out the same way, through our crosscut. On the way he trips over my leg, sees me and says, \"Oh. You're alive.\" As if we'd run into each other in the canteen."}], "C": [{"md": "I go into the old incline, where nobody goes. My legs carry me by themselves. After about a hundred meters damp air brushes my face, and I come out at the pump station, where there's a separate pump and its own ventilation pipe, old, Soviet, that nobody ever switched off. You can breathe here."}, {"md": "I sit by the pipe as if by a stove and think that somebody stood here before. Somebody I don't know, who headed for the shaft and didn't make it. This is his chill. The rescuers find Seryoga by the drive; he put on his self-rescuer and lay down like they teach you, and nothing will happen to him except a reprimand for abandoning his helmet."}], "D": [{"md": "I head back toward his lamp. Seryoga is sitting by the drive, ripping the self-rescuer off his face: he thinks there's nothing in it to breathe, and he's yelling that he's suffocating. I sit down next to him and talk, slow and boring, the way Mikhalych tells jokes: \"Seryoga. Breathe. There's air in it. Breathe in. Come on.\" And I hold the mouthpiece to his mouth until he starts breathing on his own."}, {"md": "Then I drag him under the arms to the shaft. He's heavy, but he walks on his own feet; he just keeps sitting down. At the shaft he tells the rescuers, \"Samokhin got me out,\" and then to me, quietly: \"I owe you, got it?\" I say I've got it."}], "E": [{"md": "Methane is light, it's up under the roof, there's less of it down low. I lie face down on the wet floor, pull my jacket over my head and breathe through it, slowly, as if asleep. After an hour my head starts to buzz; after two I forget how much time has passed. I remember a puddle under my cheek and wanting very badly to sleep."}, {"md": "They bring us up after six hours. I throw up twice right in the cage, my fingers won't work, and Seryoga unties my laces for me in the bathhouse. The doctor at the first-aid post shines a flashlight in my eyes: \"Hypoxia. You'll live.\" For a month afterward my head aches in a particular way, in one spot over my left eyebrow, like there's a nail in it."}], "H": [{"md": "I remember an elevator full of nitrogen, though I've never been stuck in any elevator full of nitrogen. I remember sitting down on the floor because it was more comfortable, and not being scared. There, the cold lay low. Here, at the Glubokaya, methane collects up top, under the roof, and sinks slowly, a centimeter at a time, and the gas detector on my belt beeps faster and faster."}, {"md": "So I go bent double, almost on all fours, face to the floor, holding the gas detector in my hand down by my boots. Down low it beeps less often. I crawl to the crosscut, feel my way to the stopping and the door in it, and hit it with my shoulder, lying down, from below. On the third try it gives, and cold air pulls through the gap, a real fresh current."}, {"md": "The rescuers find me by the gap an hour later. They bring Seryoga out the same way, through our crosscut, and he asks why I'm lying there like a dog under a fence. I don't know how to explain to him that in another life I died sitting on a floor in Tomsk."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Run for the shaft along the main drift"}, "B": {"label": "Put on the self-rescuer, go into the crosscut and wait there"}, "C": {"label": "Go where the chill is pulling me"}, "D": {"label": "Go back for Seryoga"}, "E": {"label": "Lie down on the floor, pull my wet jacket over my face and wait for the rescuers"}, "F": {"label": "Self-rescuer in my teeth and straight into the crosscut, to the stopping, while the gas is still up under the roof"}, "G": {"label": "First hold the gas detector up to the roof, then down to the floor, and work out where the gas is going"}, "H": {"label": "\"I've fallen asleep in air you can't see. Not in this life.\" That gas was heavy and lay low; this one's light and collects under the roof. Get down and go along the floor to the crosscut"}}}, "В1-03б": {"title": "The Stopping", "date": "3 March 2010, 04:44", "photo_caption": "The crosscut. The door in the stopping", "clock_label": "before the gas sinks to my face", "text": [{"md": "Up at the roof the gas detector chokes; down at the floor it beeps every other second. The gas is lying in the face in a layer, from the roof down, and it's sinking. It's being drawn toward the shaft, the same way everyone will run. The crosscut comes out on a different current, from a different fan. I've seen the ventilation plan on the assignment room wall a hundred times, between the shift schedule and the poster about helmets, and I only understand it now, by the beeping.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 45}}}, {"md": "The crosscut is dark and smells of old rock, like a root cellar. My lamp beam hits a concrete stopping, and in it is an iron door, small, chest-high, with a white arrow painted on it by hand, crooked. The arrow says \"pull.\" In front of the door is a rock fall from the roof, knee-deep, and the door is wedged against it."}, {"md": "I try the handle. The door sits as if it had grown there. From under it, through the gap at the floor, comes the cold, damp air of the next drift, and I hold my palm under the gap like under a tap. The self-rescuer is sour in my mouth, the mouthpiece rubs my gums, and every breath from it is hot and tastes of rubber."}, {"md": "A door in a stopping opens toward you, so the air current presses it shut against the frame. I read that later too. Back then all I knew was the arrow.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I hit the edge with my shoulder, by the hinges, where the rock holds it least. The door shudders; on the second blow the gap opens a hand's width, on the third two, and I squeeze through sideways, scraping my jacket on the frame. Rock spills into my boots. On the other side there's a draft like from an open window in winter, and the gas detector on my belt goes quiet in mid-beep.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ГИПОКСИЯ"}}}, {"md": "I sit on the other side, on a rail, breathing without the mouthpiece. The rescuers come an hour later. They bring Seryoga out the same way, through our crosscut, and as he passes he slaps my helmet:", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ГИПОКСИЯ"}}}, {"md": "\"You rat, Samokhin.\"", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ГИПОКСИЯ"}}}, {"md": "That's his way of praising me.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ГИПОКСИЯ"}}}, {"md": "I hit the door with my shoulder, once, twice, five times. The door booms like a barrel and doesn't move. The rock is holding it from below, and I'm pushing it away from me, against the arrow, and I don't even notice. My shoulder goes numb. Then my arms go numb, and the self-rescuer gets heavy and pointless, and I sit down by the door, face to the gap under it where the cold comes from, and breathe that gap for as long as I can.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ГИПОКСИЯ"}}, {"md": "They bring us up after six hours. I throw up twice in the cage. The doctor at the first-aid post shines a flashlight in my eyes: \"Hypoxia. You'll live.\" For a month afterward my head aches in a particular way, over my left eyebrow, like there's a nail in it.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ГИПОКСИЯ"}}], "B": [{"md": "I get down on my knees and dig the rock away with my hands, stone by stone, throwing them back into the dark. My gloves tear in the third minute. More rock trickles down from the roof, a little at a time, and I dig again. I count stones so I won't count breaths. At the hundred and twelfth stone the bottom of the door comes free. I pull the handle toward me, the way the arrow says; the door creaks, sticks, and then suddenly swings all at once, and I sit down hard on my backside."}, {"md": "A fresh current blows from the other side. I climb through, close the door behind me the way you're supposed to, and only then sit down on a rail. The gas detector in my hand is silent; I shake it, and it's still silent."}, {"md": "The rescuers find me an hour later by the knocking: I'm sitting there hitting a pipe with a wrench, once a minute. The squad leader looks at the cleared door, at my bare hands, and says, \"By the book, then.\" They bring Seryoga out the same way. He doesn't say anything, just gives me his gloves."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Shoulder the door open, right now, while I still have the strength"}, "B": {"label": "Clear the rock away from the door by hand and open it the way the arrow says, toward me"}}}, "В1-04": {"title": "The Second Explosion", "date": "15 May 2010, 01:10", "photo_caption": "The pithead building. The night of the fifteenth", "text": [{"md": "My shift is brought up at ten at night. Dad stays on for a second shift, covering for Kolya Yeryomenko, whose wife is giving birth. In the bathhouse I've just soaped my head when the lights go out. Then they come back on, and the floor under my feet jolts, short, like a train car going over a rail joint. Every man in the bathhouse goes quiet at once, washcloths in hand."}, {"md": "At 23:20 methane explodes at the Glubokaya. There are over three hundred men below, and Dad is one of them. I find this out ten minutes later, in wet shorts and a jacket over my bare skin, outside the dispatcher's office, where every phone is ringing at once and nobody picks up."}, {"md": "By midnight the mine rescue buses are standing by the shaft, the paramilitary rescue unit, guys in orange with breathing sets in boxes on their backs. The squad leader, gray-haired Palych, with a voice like it's coming out of a barrel, is lining his men up by the cage."}, {"md": "It's the same Palych I hauled tanks for, three nights in '05. He recognizes me, nods and says, \"Grown up, Lucky Junior.\" And turns back to his men.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т08"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПАЛЫЧ"}}}, {"md": "The first squad goes down at half past midnight. Men come out of the mine on their own, one at a time and in groups, black, with white eyes, and at the lamp room Tanya checks off tags in the log and doesn't raise her head. Dad isn't among them. Down below, they say, longwall five is on fire, and the gas keeps coming."}, {"md": "A crowd gathers at the cage. Men from my shift, from the neighboring sections, some in overalls, some in house clothes; they need to go down, everyone has somebody down there. Vitka is here too, in slippers on bare feet, holding the shift foreman by the sleeve and saying something to him, close, right in his face."}, {"md": "I remember what Palych said in '05, on the third night, when I was falling asleep on the bench. \"Where there's fire and the gas keeps coming, it'll blow a second time. We wait four hours. Then we go.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т08"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПАЛЫЧ"}}}, {"md": "In our safety course they told us about secondary explosions. If there's a fire below and gas is still coming out, the next explosion is only a matter of time. The lecturer repeated it three times, and half the group was asleep.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т08"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПАЛЫЧ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}]}}, {"md": "My head is buzzing over my left eyebrow, like in March, when I lay face down in a puddle. I rub my eyebrow with a finger and can't stop.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ГИПОКСИЯ"}}, {"md": "The cage comes up empty and stands with its door open. Smoke and warm iron drift out of it."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I jump into the cage with the second squad. Palych looks at me but doesn't throw me out; he has no time for me. Down at the shaft bottom it smells of burning, and from the drift that leads to longwall five comes a warm wind, like from a stairwell where the garbage chute is on fire."}, {"md": "That's when I remember his \"four hours,\" and my legs stop by themselves. I stay at the shaft bottom and haul the men who come out of the drifts on their own to the cage. Palych goes into the smoke with his men.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т08"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПАЛЫЧ"}}}, {"md": "That's when it hits me that the gas is still coming down there and the fire is still burning, and I stop dead. I don't go any farther. I stay at the shaft bottom and haul the men who come out of the drifts on their own to the cage.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т08"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПАЛЫЧ"}}}, {"md": "At 3:50 the drift breathes out fire. A wave of hot air knocks me off my feet and rolls me along the rails toward the shaft, and I lie with my arms over my head until the roaring stops. Later three men from the second squad come out of the smoke, and Palych last, with his mask torn down around his neck. The others don't come out."}, {"md": "Dad is brought up after seven in the morning, through the ventilation shaft at the far end of the field. At home he doesn't lie down. He goes to the china cabinet, takes the envelope with the wax seal marked \"For Kolya\" out from behind the crystal, where Mom put it ten years ago, and breaks the seal over the kitchen table. Inside is a single sheet, blue ink, Granddad's handwriting. He reads it to himself for a long time, then hands it to me."}, {"md": "\"Kolya. If you're reading this, I didn't come back. You're lucky, son, and it isn't luck. I passed it on to you without meaning to, and forgive me for that. Don't go below two hundred. And if you have a son, keep him away from the water.\" At the bottom, a date: January '86."}, {"md": "\"Three times,\" says Dad. \"In '05 I only had a feeling, Andryukha. But this time I remember. Like watching a movie about myself.\""}, {"md": "\"Me too,\" I say. \"Since November.\""}, {"md": "He nods, as if that's what he thought. Then he says he knows. His father didn't work at the mine, he worked under it, below every level. Grandma used to say Tisha went too deep, and Dad thought all his life she meant his work. In '86 there was gas down there, six men died, and Granddad was the seventh. They buried him in a closed coffin, poured concrete into the level and told everyone to forget. \"I never went below two hundred,\" says Dad. \"Twenty-four years at minus one-eighty. They kept trying to transfer me, and I kept refusing. Didn't know why myself.\""}, {"md": "\"Remember, in the garage, I told you about a door? That's where he went.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДВЕРЬ"}}}, {"md": "\"I burned his notebooks back then, in the stove,\" he says. \"But I couldn't bring myself to burn the letter.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СОЖЖЕНО"}}}], "B": [{"md": "I sit down on a rail by the shaft and wait. It's the hardest thing I've ever done. The second squad walks past me into the cage. I grab Palych by the sleeve and tell him about the second explosion, and he looks at me like a kid teaching him to tie his shoelaces. \"I know,\" he says. \"I've got men down there.\" And he goes down."}, {"md": "At 3:50 the ground shakes a second time, harder than the first. Dust blows up out of the shaft, and the cage isn't brought up for a long time. When it is, they carry out orange suits, and I count them, the way I once counted names on the board outside the pithead building. Palych isn't among them; he walks out on his own, last, and sits down on the ground by the cage without taking off his mask."}, {"md": "Dad is brought up after seven in the morning, through the ventilation shaft at the far end of the field. At home he doesn't lie down. He goes to the china cabinet, takes the envelope with the wax seal marked \"For Kolya\" out from behind the crystal, where Mom put it ten years ago, and breaks the seal over the kitchen table. Inside is a single sheet, blue ink, Granddad's handwriting. He reads it to himself for a long time, then hands it to me."}, {"md": "\"Kolya. If you're reading this, I didn't come back. You're lucky, son, and it isn't luck. I passed it on to you without meaning to, and forgive me for that. Don't go below two hundred. And if you have a son, keep him away from the water.\" At the bottom, a date: January '86."}, {"md": "\"Three times,\" says Dad. \"In '05 I only had a feeling, Andryukha. But this time I remember. Like watching a movie about myself.\""}, {"md": "\"Me too,\" I say. \"Since November.\""}, {"md": "He nods, as if that's what he thought. Then he says he knows. His father didn't work at the mine, he worked under it, below every level. Grandma used to say Tisha went too deep, and Dad thought all his life she meant his work. In '86 there was gas down there, six men died, and Granddad was the seventh. They buried him in a closed coffin, poured concrete into the level and told everyone to forget. \"I never went below two hundred,\" says Dad. \"Twenty-four years at minus one-eighty. They kept trying to transfer me, and I kept refusing. Didn't know why myself.\""}, {"md": "\"Remember, in the garage, I told you about a door? That's where he went.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДВЕРЬ"}}}, {"md": "\"I burned his notebooks back then, in the stove,\" he says. \"But I couldn't bring myself to burn the letter.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СОЖЖЕНО"}}}], "C": [{"md": "I stand in the cage door with my arms spread, like a goalkeeper. Vitka comes at me first, in his slippers, and I hold him until he stops pushing. Then Yeryomenko, whose wife is giving birth and whom Dad covered for. He punches me in the cheekbone, clumsily, and sits down on the ground himself. \"Tikhonych is down there for me,\" he says. \"For me.\" I hold him too."}, {"md": "At 3:50 the ground shakes a second time, harder than the first. Dust blows up out of the shaft, and everyone I held back is standing by the cage alive, looking down the shaft. Nobody tries to go anywhere anymore. Vitka puts his arm around my neck and says nothing; my cheekbone swells and stays yellow for a week."}, {"md": "Dad is brought up after seven in the morning, through the ventilation shaft at the far end of the field. At home he doesn't lie down. He goes to the china cabinet, takes the envelope with the wax seal marked \"For Kolya\" out from behind the crystal, where Mom put it ten years ago, and breaks the seal over the kitchen table. Inside is a single sheet, blue ink, Granddad's handwriting. He reads it to himself for a long time, then hands it to me."}, {"md": "\"Kolya. If you're reading this, I didn't come back. You're lucky, son, and it isn't luck. I passed it on to you without meaning to, and forgive me for that. Don't go below two hundred. And if you have a son, keep him away from the water.\" At the bottom, a date: January '86."}, {"md": "\"Three times,\" says Dad. \"In '05 I only had a feeling, Andryukha. But this time I remember. Like watching a movie about myself.\""}, {"md": "\"Me too,\" I say. \"Since November.\""}, {"md": "He nods, as if that's what he thought. Then he says he knows. His father didn't work at the mine, he worked under it, below every level. Grandma used to say Tisha went too deep, and Dad thought all his life she meant his work. In '86 there was gas down there, six men died, and Granddad was the seventh. They buried him in a closed coffin, poured concrete into the level and told everyone to forget. \"I never went below two hundred,\" says Dad. \"Twenty-four years at minus one-eighty. They kept trying to transfer me, and I kept refusing. Didn't know why myself.\""}, {"md": "\"Remember, in the garage, I told you about a door? That's where he went.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДВЕРЬ"}}}, {"md": "\"I burned his notebooks back then, in the stove,\" he says. \"But I couldn't bring myself to burn the letter.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СОЖЖЕНО"}}}], "D": [{"md": "I catch Palych at the cage by the strap of his breathing set."}, {"md": "\"At 3:50 it'll blow a second time,\" I say. \"In the drift to longwall five. I've been there.\""}, {"md": "He looks at me for a long time, and I can see him searching my eyes for vodka. He doesn't find any. \"You've been there,\" he repeats, not as a question. Then he turns to his men and holds the second squad at the shaft bottom: \"We wait. I said, we wait.\" The first squad is already down; there's no shouting to them."}, {"md": "At 3:50 the drift breathes out fire. The second squad is standing by the shaft, and it only knocks them off their feet. The first squad doesn't come out. Palych comes up top, sits down next to me on a rail and says, looking at the ground, \"You left me five today. But not six.\" I know that myself. I counted."}, {"md": "Dad is brought up after seven in the morning, through the ventilation shaft at the far end of the field. At home he doesn't lie down. He goes to the china cabinet, takes the envelope with the wax seal marked \"For Kolya\" out from behind the crystal, where Mom put it ten years ago, and breaks the seal over the kitchen table. Inside is a single sheet, blue ink, Granddad's handwriting. He reads it to himself for a long time, then hands it to me."}, {"md": "\"Kolya. If you're reading this, I didn't come back. You're lucky, son, and it isn't luck. I passed it on to you without meaning to, and forgive me for that. Don't go below two hundred. And if you have a son, keep him away from the water.\" At the bottom, a date: January '86."}, {"md": "\"Three times,\" says Dad. \"In '05 I only had a feeling, Andryukha. But this time I remember. Like watching a movie about myself.\""}, {"md": "\"Me too,\" I say. \"Since November.\""}, {"md": "He nods, as if that's what he thought. Then he says he knows. His father didn't work at the mine, he worked under it, below every level. Grandma used to say Tisha went too deep, and Dad thought all his life she meant his work. In '86 there was gas down there, six men died, and Granddad was the seventh. They buried him in a closed coffin, poured concrete into the level and told everyone to forget. \"I never went below two hundred,\" says Dad. \"Twenty-four years at minus one-eighty. They kept trying to transfer me, and I kept refusing. Didn't know why myself.\""}, {"md": "\"Remember, in the garage, I told you about a door? That's where he went.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДВЕРЬ"}}}, {"md": "\"I burned his notebooks back then, in the stove,\" he says. \"But I couldn't bring myself to burn the letter.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СОЖЖЕНО"}}}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Jump into the cage with the second squad, down, after the men"}, "B": {"label": "Wait at the shaft. Four hours, until it burns out down there"}, "C": {"label": "Stand in the cage door and hold back the men who are trying to get down"}, "D": {"label": "\"I've seen it blow a second time.\" Stop Palych"}}}, "В1-05": {"title": "Mine Rescue", "date": "June 2011", "photo_caption": "Vitka's birthday. The garages in Rudnichny", "text": [{"md": "The six men from the first squad are buried on the same day, at Kedrovka cemetery, to a band that plays so slowly it seems the trumpeters are falling asleep. The governor gives a speech. Palych stands in the front row in dress uniform and never once lifts his eyes from the ground."}, {"md": "After the funerals the Glubokaya is shut for six months. Then it opens again, with new ventilation, a new director and the same old men. Dad goes back to the face on the very first day. Now, sometimes, in the middle of a shift, he switches off his hammer, listens, and says to the whole crew, \"Let's get out of here.\" And the crew goes, nobody argues. Twice out of those times the roof came down at the face half an hour later."}, {"md": "In June Palych catches me outside the bathhouse."}, {"md": "\"I need men,\" he says. \"Six of them. You'll do, Samokhin. You know when not to go in. That's rare. The rest only know when to go in.\""}, {"md": "The rescue unit pays less than the face, but you only go down to the places everybody else is running up from. Training takes six months, and they teach you everything, down to how to pull a man out from under rock without finishing him off. Then it's twenty-four-hour duty every fourth day, and every alarm in the region is yours."}, {"md": "In the evening it's Vitka's birthday, twenty-three, at the garages, with shashlik on a grill made from an old fridge shelf. Nika comes. She's finishing medical school in Novosibirsk and in two years will do her internship at the regional hospital, here, in Kemerovo. Her hair is long, below her shoulder blades, Vitka's jacket is over her shoulders, and she still counts everything: the skewers, the bottles, how many times Vitka has said \"basically.\""}, {"md": "Seryoga is spinning her a yarn about the mine, how in March he walked out of a gassed-up face \"all by himself.\" Nika laughs with her head thrown back, sitting on the hood of Vitka's Lada Nine and swinging her legs."}, {"md": "For two years I've called her every evening, fifty rubles a call. Seryoga makes her laugh in one evening, for free.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "Dad finishes his cigarette by the garage and says to me without looking, \"Decide for yourself. Only I need you at the face.\" It's the first time he's ever said anything like that to me.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОТЕЦ"}}}, {"md": "Dad finishes his cigarette by the garage and says, \"Decide for yourself.\" Nothing more; he's not much of a talker.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОТЕЦ"}}}, {"md": "Palych stands off to one side, by the last garage, smoking and waiting for an answer."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "For six months I train at the unit's base in the Kirovsky district. I carry a stretcher with a sack of sand instead of a man up seven flights of stairs, run in a breathing set through a smoke-filled training tunnel, learn to listen to the roof through a prop with my ear pressed against it. The straps of the set rub my shoulders raw, and by December the raw turns to skin."}, {"md": "At the swearing-in Palych shakes my hand and holds it in his. \"Seventh,\" he says quietly. He took six men to replace those six, and I'm the extra one, the seventh. At first I don't understand what he means by it. Then I understand that he doesn't know himself."}, {"md": "Dad doesn't come to the swearing-in. In the evening he knocks on my door, puts a bottle and two glasses on the table and says, \"Well, rescuer.\" He pours and drinks his without clinking."}], "B": [{"md": "\"I'm staying,\" I tell Palych."}, {"md": "He shrugs, puts out his cigarette against the garage and leaves without saying goodbye. There's no offense in it. He has six vacancies and not a minute for people who aren't coming."}, {"md": "At the face Dad puts me next to him, in the same team. We work in silence, like two pumps, and I learn from him how to listen to the quiet. That's when the face goes quiet in a way it isn't usually. He can't explain how exactly. He just says, \"When you feel it, run.\" I'll feel it, I say. I already do."}], "C": [{"md": "\"I'm going to study,\" I tell Palych. \"By correspondence. For foreman.\""}, {"md": "Palych looks at me for a long time, the way you look at the roof when it hasn't yet decided whether to come down. \"Management, then,\" he says without malice, and puts out his cigarette against the garage. Then he slaps me on the shoulder, heavily, with his palm. \"Well, at least one manager will know when not to go in.\""}, {"md": "In September I enroll at the polytechnic, mining, by correspondence. For four years I go into Kemerovo for exams, sleep on commuter trains, write term papers in the assignment room between shifts on the backs of work orders. Dad looks at my textbooks like somebody else's boots in the front hall. \"Paperwork,\" he says. But when I bring home the diploma, he takes it in his hands and reads the name for a long time, moving his lips."}, {"md": "Tanya hands me my lamp for the shift and asks if it's true I'll be wearing \"a white helmet\" now. I say not for a while. \"Bring it back, good boy,\" she says. My helmet is orange for now, like everyone's.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Join Palych's unit"}, "B": {"label": "Stay on the crew with Dad"}, "C": {"label": "Neither one. Night school at the polytechnic for mine foreman, and then a section of my own"}}}, "В1-06": {"title": "Nika's Wedding", "date": "16 August 2014", "photo_caption": "The Beryozka café, Rudnichny. The front steps", "text": [{"md": "The wedding is at the Beryozka in Rudnichny, in a hall with a mirror ball and an MC from Leninsk who in every pause yells \"Gorko!\" as if he's being murdered. Seryoga is in a black suit he bought to grow into about five years ago, and he keeps fixing his tie. Nika is in white. Vitka is best man, with a WITNESS sash across his chest, and he's already lost the rings twice, both times in his own pocket."}, {"md": "They met three years ago at Vitka's garages. I was sitting right there and watched it happen. Nika was counting skewers then, and then she stopped counting."}, {"md": "For two years I called her every evening. Then every other evening. Then on holidays. In January she called me herself and said, \"Samokhin, Seryoga proposed. Are you happy for me?\" I said I was, and then sat for half an hour holding the phone with the dial tone humming in it.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "Seryoga is still at the face, a team leader on Dad's crew. When I come to the Glubokaya with the unit for an inspection, he yells across the whole drift, \"Rescuer! Rescue me from the foreman!\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВГСЧ"}}}, {"md": "Seryoga is on our team, team leader; Dad picked him himself. We've been shoulder to shoulder underground for five years now, and I know how he breathes when he's tired and how he sings when he's scared.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БРИГАДА"}}}, {"md": "After ten Nika comes out on the steps, takes off her shoes and sits down on a step. I'm standing there too, smoking, though I quit. Vitka is by the door on the phone with the MC, who left early, and he's swearing."}, {"md": "\"What's wrong with you, Samokhin?\" asks Nika. \"You sat there all evening like it was a wake.\""}, {"md": "\"Tired,\" I say. \"Had a duty shift.\""}, {"md": "She looks up at me for a long time, the way she looked in '96 when only the ears were left by the snag. Then she says:"}, {"md": "\"Twenty-six steps in your stairwell. I still remember. Seryoga doesn't know, but I remember.\""}, {"md": "Vitka hangs up and turns to us."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "\"To you two,\" I say, and raise a glass I'm not holding. Nika laughs, and Vitka shoves his into my hand."}, {"md": "In the hall I dance with her aunt from Novosibirsk, the one with the dacha. The aunt leads and tells me in my ear that when Nika was little she talked everyone's ears off about me, \"Andryusha this, Andryusha that.\" I step on her foot. She says never mind, both her feet are used to it."}, {"md": "At three in the morning Seryoga, drunk and happy, hugs me by the taxi and says, \"You'll be godfather to our first, got it? You pulled me out of that face.\" I promise and put him in the taxi head first, so he won't bump it."}], "B": [{"md": "\"I love you,\" I say. \"Since the backwater. Twenty-six steps, Nika. I remember too.\""}, {"md": "It goes so quiet you can hear somebody in the hall putting on Laskovy Mai, the old boy-band hit. Nika doesn't get up off the step. She puts on her shoes, one, then the other, slowly, and only then says:"}, {"md": "\"You kept quiet for eighteen years, Samokhin. You could have managed one more evening.\""}, {"md": "She goes back into the hall, to Seryoga. Vitka comes right up to me, his sash twisted. He doesn't hit me. He says quietly, \"That's my sister. And that's my friend. And who are you?\" Then he leaves too. For the rest of the evening he doesn't speak to me, and for seven years after that he hardly does."}], "C": [{"md": "I drink vodka straight from the bottle behind the garages past the Beryozka and catch a taxi to the Glubokaya. The driver takes me to the gates and won't take my money: \"Go on, miner.\""}, {"md": "I don't have a night shift, I'm a rescuer; my next duty is the day after tomorrow. But my legs carry me to the lamp room, the way they always have.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВГСЧ"}}}, {"md": "The night shift is on in the lamp room, and Tanya is behind the window. I hand her my tag. She looks at me, at the tag, and puts it away in a drawer."}, {"md": "\"I'm not giving a lamp to a drunk,\" she says. \"Kill me if you like.\""}, {"md": "Then she opens the side door and lets me in, behind the racks, where it smells of electrolyte and hot plastic. She pours me tea from her thermos. I sit on a crate and tell her everything: the backwater, the steps, November, the way coal flows. She listens and changes lamps in their slots. Toward morning I fall asleep right there on the crate, my head on her quilted jacket, and Tanya hands out lamps until the end of the shift, trying not to make any noise."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Say nothing. Raise a glass to them and go dance with her aunt"}, "B": {"label": "Tell her. Now, in front of Vitka, since she's brought up the twenty-six steps"}, "C": {"label": "Get drunk and go to the mine, for the night shift"}}}, "В1-07": {"title": "The Seal", "date": "February 2015", "photo_caption": "Longwall seven. The sensor at the junction", "text": [{"md": "In '15 the ruble falls by half, coal gets cheaper abroad, and the holding company announces that bonuses will only be paid to sections that make quota. Longwall seven doesn't make quota. It's gassy. Methane comes out of the seam steadily and stubbornly; the fixed sensor at the junction, where the face meets the ventilation drift, reads one and a half percent and cuts the power to the shearer. Twenty minutes later the gas thins out and the shearer goes back on. Forty minutes after that it all happens again. Over a shift that's three hours of downtime."}, {"md": "On Saturday Sonka comes to see me, without calling, with a backpack, in Mom's jacket. She's seventeen, graduating in the spring. She sits on the windowsill, finishing my dumplings straight from the frying pan, cold, and says she's going to medical school, in Novosibirsk, and hasn't told Mom yet. \"Just don't be like Dad,\" she says, already at the door. \"He still smokes on the balcony at night. I hear him.\""}, {"md": "The section foreman, Bondarenko, fat and honest in his fatness, catches me at the junction."}, {"md": "I'm here with the unit for an inspection, measuring gas along the whole face with my own instrument and writing it up. There's a plastic bag over the sensor at the junction, tied on with blue electrical tape. I take it off to check the sensor and see that it works fine. Bondarenko comes over while I'm holding the bag.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВГСЧ"}}}, {"md": "I'm here on Dad's team; Dad's off today. Bondarenko takes a plastic bag and a roll of blue electrical tape out of his pocket and holds them out to me, because I'm standing closest to the sensor.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БРИГАДА"}}}, {"md": "\"Samokhin,\" he says. \"Just till the end of the shift. Put it on and tape it up. The gas won't go over two, I promise you, I'll stand here with the instrument myself. The guys have mortgages. Pakhomov's got a kid on the way. Come on, Samokhin. You're one of us.\""}, {"md": "I've heard this conversation before, in '05, in the smoking room by the pithead building. That time a foreman said under his breath that the sensors in the section had been taped over to make quota, and out of twelve men they brought nine up alive. I told it all to the reporter back then. They still remember the name Samokhin at this mine.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т08"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДАТЧИКИ"}}}, {"md": "Seryoga, at the shearer, pretends he isn't listening. The bag rustles in my fingers. The face is silent, waiting to be allowed to work again."}, {"md": "The bag was from Maria-Ra, with handles. For about ten years after, I saw bags like that in every dream I had about the mine.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I put the bag over the sensor and wind the tape around it, three turns, like a bandage. My fingers feel like cotton, and the tape sticks to my glove. Bondarenko slaps me on the shoulder: \"Good man.\" The shearer comes on and doesn't stop once for the rest of the shift."}, {"md": "In the inspection report I write \"no comments\" and sign. The pen writes badly, and I go over my signature twice.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВГСЧ"}}}, {"md": "That night I don't sleep; I listen to Dad going out on the balcony to smoke. At four-twelve I go out too. He holds out the pack to me without asking, and we smoke together in silence. I can't bring myself to tell him about the bag."}], "B": [{"md": "\"No,\" I say, and put the bag in Bondarenko's breast pocket. \"Let it stand.\""}, {"md": "In the report I write the sensor, the bag, the tape and the time, to the minute. The face is stopped for three days for inspection. Bondarenko gets a reprimand, the section loses its bonus, and for two months the men from seven don't say hello to me in the bathhouse.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВГСЧ"}}}, {"md": "Bondarenko puts me on nights for a week, on cleanup, where they put the guys being punished. For two months the men from seven don't say hello to me in the bathhouse. Seryoga does, but he looks away.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БРИГАДА"}}}, {"md": "In March the director calls me in, a new one, young, in rimless glasses. He looks at his papers for a long time and then says, \"Samokhin. Was it you who talked to the TV people in '05?\" I say it was. He nods and lets me go. Nothing happens, and that makes it worse.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т08"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДАТЧИКИ"}}}], "C": [{"md": "\"All right,\" I say, and wind the tape, three turns."}, {"md": "That night I come to the lamp room without a work order. Tanya is behind the window. She looks at me, then at the clock, then hands me a lamp and a self-rescuer without asking anything, and writes something illegible in the log. \"Bring it back,\" she says. She doesn't say \"good boy\" anymore."}, {"md": "I go down with the night shift, get to seven, rip the bag off the sensor and carry it out in my pocket. In the morning Bondarenko yells all over the section about \"that bastard from the night shift.\" The face gets gassy again and stops. Nobody in the section ever finds out who it was. Tanya knows. Later I throw the bag in the trash can outside her building."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Tape it over, like the foreman asks"}, "B": {"label": "Refuse. Let the face stand idle"}, "C": {"label": "Agree, and then go down at night and take it off"}}}, "В1-08": {"title": "A Year Later", "date": "9 March 2016, 11:40", "photo_caption": "Longwall seven. Seryoga's phone", "text": [{"md": "A year later I'm back at longwall seven."}, {"md": "In the cage I count my breaths: one, two, three. I keep a hand on my self-rescuer, though it's buckled on. The guys think it's the cold.", "if": {"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "seen", "id": "✝В1-02.B", "life": true}, {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В1-03.A", "life": true}, {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В1-03.B", "life": true}, {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В1-03.C", "life": true}]}, "body": true}, {"md": "Inspection is once a year, on schedule, and the schedule brings me here again, with an instrument on my belt and a folder of reports under my arm. Bondarenko meets me at the junction, shakes my hand and looks past me.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВГСЧ"}}}, {"md": "Our team has been moved back to seven because the seam ran out at five. Dad's on vacation at the dacha near Yagunovsky, planting seedlings, and he calls me in the evenings to ask about the roof.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БРИГАДА"}}}, {"md": "The Maria-Ra bag is still on the sensor, on the same blue tape, only gray with dust. Nobody took it off. Seven has made quota all year, Bondarenko got a certificate of merit, the guys bought cars, fridges. I don't look at the bag anymore. My eye has gotten used to it, like a stain on the bathroom ceiling.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ЗАКЛЕИЛ"}}, {"md": "The sensor at the junction hangs there clean. Over the year it's stopped seven about two hundred times, the section never saw its bonus, and Bondarenko doesn't say hello to me. Nobody puts bags on the sensors here anymore.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ЗАКЛЕИЛ"}}}, {"md": "Seryoga, at the shearer, takes off a glove and shows me his phone. On the screen is a fat baby in a knitted bobble hat: Mishka, ten months old."}, {"md": "\"Six teeth,\" says Seryoga. \"At night he screams like the siren at the pithead. Nika carries him around and counts her steps. Says she got to a thousand and he's still screaming.\""}, {"md": "Vitka became Mishka's godfather. Nika didn't invite me to the christening; Seryoga told me himself, looking away: \"You understand.\" I understand.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В106"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СКАЗАЛ"}}}, {"md": "I'm Mishka's godfather. At the christening in Znamensky Cathedral I held him, wet and angry, and he peed on my shirt. Seryoga still tells everybody about it like a joke.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В106"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СКАЗАЛ"}}}, {"md": "The shearer hums, cutting the seam, and coal pours onto the conveyor evenly, like grain from a sack. I need to go up to the top corner of the face, to the junction. Seryoga asks me to stay with him by the shearer while he moves a cable; it's awkward alone."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "In the top corner I measure the gas. Point eight, normal; the sensor shows the same. I write the number in my notebook next to the date and stand there by the sensor a long time, I don't know why. Then I go back to the shearer, and the shift ends the way hundreds of shifts end, with the bathhouse and the canteen."}, {"md": "Three weeks later, at night, the unit is called out. At the Severnaya, the neighboring mine twenty kilometers away, methane has exploded at the face. We go down as the fourth squad. There's nobody alive at the face. We carry out nine men on stretchers, one at a time. At the junction I shine my lamp on the fixed sensor and see a bag. White, with handles, wrapped in electrical tape. I take it off and put it in the evidence sack, and at that moment my hands aren't shaking at all.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВГСЧ"}}}, {"md": "Three weeks later, in the morning, somebody in the bathhouse turns on the radio. At the Severnaya, the neighboring mine twenty kilometers away, methane exploded at the face during the night. Nine men. Later, in the smoking room, people say the sensor at the junction had been wrapped in a bag. Seryoga finishes his cigarette, stubs it out on the bench and says, \"That's like what we had back then.\" We don't talk about it after that.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БРИГАДА"}}}], "B": [{"md": "Seryoga and I move the cable, and he tells me Nika wants a second kid and he wants a car, and that Nika will win, like always. The shearer roars, and Seryoga shouts over it. The shift ends the way hundreds of shifts end, with the bathhouse and the canteen."}, {"md": "Three weeks later, at night, the unit is called out. At the Severnaya, the neighboring mine twenty kilometers away, methane has exploded at the face. We go down as the fourth squad. There's nobody alive at the face. We carry out nine men on stretchers, one at a time. At the junction I shine my lamp on the fixed sensor and see a bag. White, with handles, wrapped in electrical tape. I take it off and put it in the evidence sack, and at that moment my hands aren't shaking at all.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВГСЧ"}}}, {"md": "Three weeks later, in the morning, somebody in the bathhouse turns on the radio. At the Severnaya, the neighboring mine twenty kilometers away, methane exploded at the face during the night. Nine men. Later, in the smoking room, people say the sensor at the junction had been wrapped in a bag. Seryoga finishes his cigarette, stubs it out on the bench and says, \"That's like what we had back then.\" We don't talk about it after that.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БРИГАДА"}}}], "C": [{"md": "I go to the shearer's control panel and hit the emergency stop. The shearer goes quiet, and Seryoga cusses me out. I go up to the top corner and rip the bag off the sensor, tape and all, and the sensor starts howling right away: three point eight, four, four point two. There's enough gas at the face for a good explosion, and it's been building up here for a year under the bag I put on myself."}, {"md": "I drive everyone off the face, some with swearing, some by the collar. Bondarenko comes running down the drift toward me, red-faced, and stops when he sees the bag in my hand. He doesn't say anything. The degassing units run at the face for three days, and the shearer is only switched on again on the fourth."}, {"md": "The men I drove out don't talk to me for a week; their shift got cut. Seryoga does. That evening he catches up with me at the bus stop and without a word stuffs an Alyonka chocolate bar, the one with the girl in the headscarf, into my jacket pocket; he'd bought it for Mishka. I never did eat it. It's still lying around somewhere."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Go up to the top corner, to the junction with the sensor"}, "B": {"label": "Stay by the shearer and help Seryoga with the cable"}, "C": {"label": "\"I remember taping it over.\" Kill the shearer and get everyone off the face"}}}, "В1-09": {"title": "Going Back for Vitka", "date": "18 March 2021, 06:20", "photo_caption": "The conveyor drift. Smoke", "text": [{"md": "At half past four in the morning the conveyor belt in the main drift of the Glubokaya catches fire. The rubber burns slow and greasy, the smoke from it is yellow, and there's so much carbon monoxide in it that two breaths are enough. There are two hundred ninety men underground."}, {"md": "The unit is called out at 4:40. Palych is sixty-two; they've been trying to push him into retirement for three years, and he won't go. He leads us down himself, as the first squad. In breathing sets, along the rails, into the draft, the way he taught everyone he ever taught.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВГСЧ"}}}, {"md": "I'm down below, at a face at minus one-eighty, on Dad's old team, only Dad hasn't been in the mine for two years now; he's retired. We go out by the emergency exit, into the draft, with our self-rescuers on, and at the fork we meet the mine rescuers. Palych is in front.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БРИГАДА"}}}, {"md": "A hundred meters past the fork Palych stops and sits down on a rail. He says into his radio, \"Guys, I'm going to sit for a bit.\" And he lies down on his side, still in his breathing set, slowly, the way old men lie down on a couch after a shift. His heart. Two of us drag him back, and I go on, because farther on, in the smoke, is Vitka's crew."}, {"md": "I find Vitka by the crosscut. The heat has warped a support section above him, and a beam has pinned both his legs below the knees. He's lying on his back, his lamp shining up at the roof, a self-rescuer mouthpiece in his mouth. Next to him lies a hydraulic prop, a jack as tall as a man; somebody dragged it here and dropped it."}, {"md": "Something comes down in the drift, and a rock hits me in the back. The air hose of my breathing set is cut, and I have to switch to the spare self-rescuer, the one we carry for casualties. Thirty minutes or so, if I don't run. The smoke gets thicker; my lamp lights it up and nothing else."}, {"md": "Vitka spits out his mouthpiece for a second."}, {"md": "\"Samokha,\" he says. \"That you?\""}, {"md": "\"I put you up to it back then,\" he says. \"The snag. I've remembered it my whole life.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СПОР"}}}, {"md": "\"Seven years I didn't talk to you,\" he says. \"Because of Nika. I'm an idiot. You were right to tell her that time; I'd have done the same. She told me later she remembers too. Those steps of yours.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В106"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СКАЗАЛ"}}}, {"md": "He shoves the mouthpiece back in. The beam over his legs creaks. It's two hundred meters to the fork, eight hundred to the shaft."}, {"md": "They buried Palych four days later, at Kedrovka, next to his six.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I take hold of the beam, brace my boots against the floor and pull up. At first the beam won't move, then it gives a finger's width, two, and Vitka yanks one leg out from under it, then the other, and yells into his mouthpiece so loud you can hear it through the rubber. I grab him under the arms and drag. Two hundred meters to the fork I drag him on my back, walking backward over the sleepers, and I count the sleepers like Nika would."}, {"md": "At the fork the guys from the second squad take him. In the cage they give Vitka a shot of painkiller, and all the way up he tells me something about beer. His legs are broken in six places. Six months later he's walking, with a cane, and a year later without one, only limping on the right leg and when the weather changes."}], "B": [{"md": "\"I'm going for the guys,\" I tell Vitka. \"I'll come back. Hear me?\""}, {"md": "He nods. I go to the fork, two hundred meters, and back, with two men from the second squad and a stretcher. It takes forty minutes. In forty minutes the beam settles another hand's width."}, {"md": "We put the hydraulic prop under it and get Vitka out alive. Up top the doctors look at his legs and say nothing. At the regional hospital they take off both, below the knee. A year later he comes out of rehab on prostheses, gets behind the wheel of a converted Niva and says that now, like a pilot, his feet are one thing and his hands another. He talks to me. Only we never talk about the mine again."}], "C": [{"md": "I take hold of the beam, and Vitka hits my hand."}, {"md": "\"Yours is already dead,\" he says, pointing at my self-rescuer. I can hear it myself; it's wheezing, breathing with effort. Vitka pulls the mouthpiece out of his own mouth, rips his self-rescuer can off himself and shoves it into my hands. \"Take it. I'll sit here a while. My legs are resting.\""}, {"md": "\"Vitya.\""}, {"md": "\"You pushed me toward that snag back then,\" he says. \"And went yourself. I remember, Samokha. Twenty-five years I've remembered. Now we're even. Go on.\""}, {"md": "He shoves me in the chest with both hands, hard, like in the yard in '96 when he was grabbing back his half of the bike. I go. I walk over the sleepers to the fork breathing his self-rescuer, and every breath I take is stolen. The guys from the second squad reach the crosscut forty minutes later. Vitka is lying under the beam on his back, and his lamp is still shining up at the roof. In his fist he's holding my empty self-rescuer."}], "D": [{"md": "I remember how this beam comes down when you pull it without support. So first I set the hydraulic prop under it, the one lying nearby, kick its base into place, and pump the handle until the beam rises a hand's width. Only then do I pull Vitka out."}, {"md": "He doesn't understand anything and keeps asking through his mouthpiece how I knew. I drag him under the arms to the fork, and the second squad takes him. His legs are broken, but they're still his. A year later he walks without a cane and tells everybody at the beer bar on Shakhtyorov Street he got out by himself."}, {"md": "In this world Palych is still lying a hundred meters past the fork, on the rail. I got here in time, but I could never have gotten to him in time. There's no rewind for me from his heart."}], "E": [{"md": "The hydraulic prop is heavy as a rail, and one of its feet is battered. I drag it over, set it under the beam next to Vitka's knee, and knock the base into place with my helmet, because I've got nothing else. Vitka swears into his mouthpiece, at length and very clearly. He thinks I'm wasting time."}, {"md": "The handle is stiff. At every stroke the beam answers with a creak, and rock trickles down from above, fine as salt. At the tenth stroke there's a gap the width of a finger between the beam and his shin. At the twentieth a hand fits in, and I pull Vitka by his belt, smoothly, without a jerk."}, {"md": "His legs are broken, but they're still his. At the fork the second squad takes him, and all the way up Vitka asks where I learned that. I say from Dad. Dad would have done the same, probably."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Pull him out. Myself, now"}, "B": {"label": "Go for help, to the fork, and come back with the guys"}, "C": {"label": "Vitka pushes me toward the exit himself"}, "D": {"label": "\"I remember how this beam comes down.\" Set the hydraulic prop first"}, "E": {"label": "Shove the hydraulic prop lying next to him under the beam and pump it"}}}, "В1-10": {"title": "Dad", "date": "October 2027", "photo_caption": "Regional Hospital, neurology. The bed by the window", "text": [{"md": "A week before his stroke Dad brings an old canvas storm jacket to my garage, with a patch on the elbow. He says he found it in Grandma's trunk when he was clearing out the top shelves."}, {"md": "\"Must be Granddad's,\" he says, and lays it on the hood. \"Grandma kept it all her life. Let it be yours.\""}, {"md": "Why, he doesn't explain. A week later, on Saturday morning, Mom calls me and is silent on the phone, and I understand everything before she starts talking."}, {"md": "The stroke is on the left side, a massive one. At the regional hospital, in neurology, they give Dad a bed by the window, and from the window you can see the spoil heap of the Glubokaya, which was shut down last year. His right arm doesn't move, the right half of his face hangs like it belongs to someone else. He can hardly speak. The words come out of him one at a time and with effort, as if he were sending them up top from the face, lump by lump."}, {"md": "Sonka flies in from Moscow on the third day, in an expensive coat, and immediately starts fighting with the doctors, because there's nothing else she can do. Mom sits by the bed holding Dad's good hand."}, {"md": "Vitka drives us to the hospital in his converted Niva every day, twice. He doesn't come into the room; he sits in the car smoking out the window.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В109"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПОМОЩЬ"}}}, {"md": "Vitka comes on the second day, limping on his right leg, brings Dad oranges he can't eat, and sits by the bed for an hour talking about soccer.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В109"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВЫТАЩИЛ"}}}, {"md": "Vitka has been gone six years. On the second day Dad looks at me for a long time and suddenly says, \"Vitka.\" Nothing else, just the name. I nod.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В109"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "РАСЧЁТ"}}}, {"md": "On the fifth night he wakes up at four-twelve, and so do I, in the chair by the bed. He turns his head toward me and beckons me closer with his good hand, two fingers."}, {"md": "\"Under Krasnaya Gorka,\" he says. Each word on its own breath. \"Granddad's there.\" He breathes for a long time. \"Don't open it.\""}, {"md": "Krasnaya Gorka is where the Kuzbass began, on the Tom, the first mine, an open-air museum where they took us in fifth grade to look at the coal carts. I sit and wait for him to say more. He closes his eyes and holds on to my sleeve."}, {"md": "The doctors said later that with a stroke like that he couldn't have said that many words in a row. Mom didn't argue with them.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I tell him in a whisper so as not to wake the man in the next bed. About how everything ends, and then I'm standing in the same place again, in the same minute, with the taste of what killed me still in my mouth."}, {"md": "About coal pouring out of a crack like water from a burst pipe.", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В1-02.A", "life": true}}, {"md": "About the sleepers I counted so I wouldn't count my breaths.", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В1-03.A", "life": true}}, {"md": "About how underground it suddenly gets warm and sleepy, like in a banya, and you want to lie down.", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В1-03.B", "life": true}}, {"md": "About the white light with no sound that's everywhere at once.", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В1-04.A", "life": true}}, {"md": "About gas that tastes of caramel.", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В1-09.A", "life": true}}, {"md": "I count on my fingers and lose track."}, {"md": "Dad listens with his eyes closed, and then lifts his good hand and shows me on his fingers how many he has. Four. Then he makes a fist and shows four more. Eight. He smiles with half his face, and it comes out like a boy who's beaten his big brother at something."}, {"md": "He dies the next night, at four-twelve. They call me from the nurses' station, but I'm not asleep anyway."}], "B": [{"md": "\"Dad, what's there? Under the Gorka?\""}, {"md": "He opens his eyes and looks at me, long and angry, the way he did when I was little and went for an outlet."}, {"md": "\"Don't,\" he says. \"O-pen. It.\""}, {"md": "I ask again, and again. He turns away to the window, to the spoil heap, and doesn't say another word, not even to Mom. He dies the next night, at four-twelve, and right to the end he lies turned away."}], "C": [{"md": "The letter has been in my wallet for seventeen years, folded in four, the wax all crumbled off. I put it in Dad's good hand, and his fingers close on it by themselves."}, {"md": "He lifts the sheet to his eyes, though he can't read anymore. His lips move. I know which line. \"You're lucky, son, and it isn't luck.\" Dad knocks twice on the bedside table with his knuckles, very quietly, knock on wood."}, {"md": "He dies the next night, at four-twelve, with the letter in his fist. In the morning the nurse gives it back to me, crumpled and warm. I smooth it out on my knee and put it back in my wallet."}], "D": [{"md": "Tanya comes a little after seven in the morning, straight from her shift in the lamp room, which has been moved to the Severnaya now. She smells of electrolyte. She sits down on the edge of the bed, and Dad looks at her for a long time."}, {"md": "\"Grisha,\" he says. And then, with effort: \"Sorry. I. Got out.\""}, {"md": "Tanya takes his hand, the good one, and holds it. \"You got out, Uncle Kolya, and that's good,\" she says. \"Dad would've been glad.\" Then she sits with him until lunch, and I stand in the hallway by the window looking at the spoil heap. Dad dies the next night, at four-twelve."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Tell him how many times I've died myself. He'll understand"}, "B": {"label": "Ask what's down there, under Krasnaya Gorka"}, "C": {"label": "Put Granddad's letter \"For Kolya\" into his good hand"}, "D": {"label": "Call Tanya to see him. He's long wanted to tell her something"}}}, "В1-11": {"title": "The Door", "date": "September 2044", "photo_caption": "The old incline under Rudnichny. The door", "text": [{"md": "The coal decline really got going in the thirties, the way snow comes, a little at a time and then knee-deep all at once. Nobody needed coal anymore. The Glubokaya was the first to close, back in '26, then the others one after another. The Severnaya is still holding on; they left it for last. The mining villages around Kemerovo are emptying out like villages after a war: windows boarded up, a warehouse in the House of Culture, nobody at the bus stops. I'm a decommissioner now. For twelve years I've been going down into mines my father opened and closing them for good, sealing the shafts with concrete and letting water into the workings. It pays well, because nobody wants to do it."}, {"md": "In August of '44 the ground subsides in Rudnichny, behind the school. At night, without a sound, a pit three meters deep, and two garages and a poplar go down into it. The city calls us in, because under Rudnichny there are old workings from the thirties that nobody remembers anything about. I dig out the plans in the archive. On a 1939 plan an adit runs from Krasnaya Gorka under the bed of the Tom, long as a gut, to the old incline under Rudnichny. From the incline a passage goes down, and on the plan it's been cut off with scissors. Cut neatly, along a ruler."}, {"md": "\"Don't open it,\" Dad said. I asked him three times what was there, and he turned away to the window. Now I'm looking at the cut plan, and I know what I was asking about.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В110"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДВЕРЬ"}}}, {"md": "\"Under Krasnaya Gorka. Granddad's there. Don't open it,\" Dad said. For seventeen years I did what he said. Now I'm looking at the cut plan, and I don't think I have a choice anymore.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В110"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДВЕРЬ"}}}, {"md": "We go down the incline, three of us, me and two young guys, Gosha and Rustam. Past the cut, the passage goes down a long way, with turns, and ends in a brick wall. In the wall is an iron door, an airtight one, with white stenciled letters: \"LEVEL −640. AUTHORIZED ENTRY ONLY.\" Somebody bricked the wall up in a hurry once; the bricks are crooked. Rustam spends half a day breaking through them with a hammer drill."}, {"md": "Behind the door is a corridor, and it's flooded. The water is black, like the tea Dad drank after a shift, and it stands level with the top of the door frame. By the plan it's forty meters to the next door. Forty meters underwater, in the dark, along a passage nobody has seen since '86."}, {"md": "At thirteen I swam across Black Backwater with Nika, there and back. My body still remembers it, more than forty years later.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т06"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПЕРЕПЛЫЛ"}}}, {"md": "At seven I swore never to talk about water again. I don't talk about it. Only my throat closes up when I look at this water, so tight I can't swallow.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т03"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВОДОБОЯЗНЬ"}}}, {"md": "The voices are humming at the back of my head. I've heard them for forty years, and I've never heard them this loud. They're there, past the water. Waiting.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "There's a chill at the back of my head, steady and strong, like a draft under a door.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "Gosha says we could put in a pump and drain it, a week's work. Rustam says in a week the whole thing will cave in to hell. They both look at me. I'm the senior man."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "My throat is clenched the whole way, and I swim more by the voices than by my arms. They lead me along the corridor the way you lead a blind man by the elbow, and at every turn one of them knows the way.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т03"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВОДОБОЯЗНЬ"}}}, {"md": "The water is ice-cold. I swim along the wall, holding a cable on hooks with one hand; the flashlight reaches half a meter and stops in the murk. At twenty meters there's an air pocket under the ceiling; I come up into it, hit my helmet on the concrete, and breathe. At forty I feel steps. I climb out of the water into a chamber, and the light on my helmet lights it all at once."}, {"md": "The room is about six meters by eight, dry, the walls covered in frost. A cot upholstered in leatherette, above it a ring of copper coils. A control panel with toggle switches and dials, all Soviet, all gray. On the wall, in big chalk numbers: \"41.\" On the table, on an oilcloth, lies a thick school notebook in cellophane, held with a rubber band. On the cover, in ink: \"Research topic TUNING FORK. Observation log. Facility Level 640.\""}, {"md": "I sit down on the cot and read. The handwriting is the same as on the letter \"For Kolya.\" A table, ruled with a ruler: number, date, time, place, how, what I heard. \"No. 1. 12.04.61. 4:12. Ob Sea, ice hole. Water, 4 min. They sang.\" About forty rows. Almost all of them 4:12."}, {"md": "I've seen this table before, at eleven, in a box from a pair of Yugoslav boots. Back then I copied it onto the last page of my school notebook and understood nothing.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}}, {"md": "Between the tables Granddad writes in words, and I hear his voice, though I never heard it. \"Everyone has a tuning fork in their head. In most people it stays silent until they die. Mine was struck in the ice hole, and since then I hear the deaths of the men I might have been. I call the voices the Choir; that's what a choirmaster is supposed to do. Kolya, I'm afraid, got it from me. On duty on the line up top: V. P. Lunyov, grad student, sharp, only smokes too much.\""}, {"md": "\"T. S. and V. P., the Tom, 1981.\" The second man in Granddad's photo, skinny, with a briefcase.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ФОТО"}}}, {"md": "The last entry: \"28.01.86. 4:12. The fortieth. Six men in the drift, can't get a single one out. They can't hear, and I hear them all. Vadim, synchronize watches. Forty-first. Going.\""}, {"md": "By the far wall, where water from the old adit once came into the chamber and left silt, lies a child's sandal. Brown, with a one-hole buckle. I pick it up. On the insole, in ballpoint, in a mother's handwriting, the way you label shoes for summer camp: \"Lunyov D.\" Next to it in the silt lies something light and white, and I don't look there. The old woman on the ground floor said that back in the eighties a boy from Shakhtyorskaya Street drowned near the backwater and they never found him. I found him, fifty-six years later, under the Tom, in Granddad's chamber. Dmitry. Mitka, probably. The son of that grad student who smokes too much."}, {"md": "The Choir stands shoulder to shoulder in this room, packed tight, like in the cage. The boy isn't among them. He's somewhere apart, still looking for which way is up.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "I wrap the sandal in the cellophane from the notebook and carry it up top, through forty meters of water, pressed to my chest."}], "B": [{"md": "We put the pump in two days later. It pumps for five days and nights, humming day and night, and the level in the corridor drops a centimeter at a time. Gosha sleeps by the pump on a camp bed. On the third day the filter clogs; Rustam cleans silt, rags and some small stuff out of it and dumps it all in the settling pit without looking. I'm up top at that moment, at a meeting at city hall."}, {"md": "On the seventh day we walk down the corridor knee-deep in muck and come out into the chamber. The room is about six meters by eight, the walls covered in frost. A cot, a ring of copper coils, a control panel. On the wall, in big chalk numbers: \"41.\" On the table, on an oilcloth, lies a thick school notebook in cellophane, held with a rubber band. On the cover, in ink: \"Research topic TUNING FORK. Observation log. Facility Level 640.\""}, {"md": "I read it sitting on the cot. The handwriting is the same as on the letter \"For Kolya.\" A table, ruled with a ruler: number, date, time, place, how, what I heard. \"No. 1. 12.04.61. 4:12. Ob Sea, ice hole. Water, 4 min. They sang.\" About forty rows. Almost all of them 4:12."}, {"md": "I've seen this table before, at eleven, in a box from a pair of Yugoslav boots.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}}, {"md": "\"Everyone has a tuning fork in their head,\" Granddad writes. \"In most people it stays silent until they die. Mine was struck in the ice hole, and since then I hear the deaths of the men I might have been. I call the voices the Choir; that's what a choirmaster is supposed to do. Kolya, I'm afraid, got it from me. On duty on the line up top: V. P. Lunyov, grad student, sharp, only smokes too much.\""}, {"md": "\"T. S. and V. P., the Tom, 1981.\" The second man in Granddad's photo, skinny, with a briefcase.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ФОТО"}}}, {"md": "The last entry: \"28.01.86. 4:12. The fortieth. Six men in the drift, can't get a single one out. They can't hear, and I hear them all. Vadim, synchronize watches. Forty-first. Going.\""}, {"md": "By the far wall, where water from the old adit once came in, the pump has scraped the silt down to the concrete. There's nothing there. Later, up top, by the settling pit, I stand for a long time for some reason, looking into the brown slurry."}], "C": [{"md": "I dive and swim along the cable on its hooks, unhurried, counting meters. At twenty I come up under the ceiling, to the pocket. It's where I remember. I breathe with my cheek pressed against the concrete, a minute, two, until my heart stops pounding in my ears. Then another twenty meters, the steps, and I climb out of the water into the chamber."}, {"md": "The room is about six meters by eight, dry, the walls covered in frost. A cot, a ring of copper coils, a control panel. On the wall, in big chalk numbers: \"41.\" On the table, on an oilcloth, lies a thick school notebook in cellophane, held with a rubber band. On the cover, in ink: \"Research topic TUNING FORK. Observation log. Facility Level 640.\""}, {"md": "The handwriting is the same as on the letter \"For Kolya.\" A table, ruled with a ruler: number, date, time, place, how, what I heard. \"No. 1. 12.04.61. 4:12. Ob Sea, ice hole. Water, 4 min. They sang.\" About forty rows. Almost all of them 4:12. I look at the forty rows and think that my table in this life is already no shorter."}, {"md": "I've seen this table before, at eleven, in a box from a pair of Yugoslav boots.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}}, {"md": "\"Everyone has a tuning fork in their head,\" Granddad writes. \"In most people it stays silent until they die. Mine was struck in the ice hole, and since then I hear the deaths of the men I might have been. I call the voices the Choir; that's what a choirmaster is supposed to do. Kolya, I'm afraid, got it from me. On duty on the line up top: V. P. Lunyov, grad student, sharp, only smokes too much.\""}, {"md": "\"T. S. and V. P., the Tom, 1981.\" The second man in Granddad's photo, skinny, with a briefcase.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ФОТО"}}}, {"md": "The last entry: \"28.01.86. 4:12. The fortieth. Six men in the drift, can't get a single one out. They can't hear, and I hear them all. Vadim, synchronize watches. Forty-first. Going.\""}, {"md": "By the far wall, in the silt, lies a child's sandal, brown, with a one-hole buckle. On the insole, in ballpoint: \"Lunyov D.\" The old woman on the ground floor said that back in the eighties a boy from Shakhtyorskaya Street drowned near the backwater and they never found him. I found him, under the Tom, in Granddad's chamber. Mitka, probably. That grad student's son. Next to it in the silt lies something light and white, and I don't look there."}, {"md": "I wrap the sandal in the cellophane from the notebook and carry it up top, pressed to my chest."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Dive. Forty meters, with a flashlight and a rope around my waist"}, "B": {"label": "Put in the pump, drain the water and wait a week"}, "C": {"label": "\"I remember where there's air in the corridor.\" Dive to the pocket, catch my breath, and go on"}}}, "В1-12": {"title": "Black Lungs", "date": "February 2045", "photo_caption": "Pulmonology. An X-ray on the light box", "text": [{"md": "My cough started in November and still hasn't gone by February. In the mornings I cough for about ten minutes, sitting on the edge of the bed, and spit gray phlegm into the sink. All the decommissioners cough like that, Gosha says; it's the mine coming out of us. I'm fifty-six, and thirty-six of those years I've spent underground."}, {"md": "My head aches over my left eyebrow every morning now, in that same spot that remembers the puddle at the face. I'm used to it and walk around with it like a watch on my wrist.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ГИПОКСИЯ"}}, {"md": "At the regional hospital's pulmonology department Nika sees me. She's head of the department now, gray-haired, cropped short, with glasses on a chain. Twenty-five years ago, they say, she didn't leave this place for three days at a stretch when covid came to the Kuzbass. Seryoga is retired; Mishka is with the mine rescue unit."}, {"md": "He serves where I served.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В105"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВГСЧ"}}}, {"md": "\"Well, hello, Samokhin,\" she says, looking at me over her glasses. \"Thirty years you haven't come to see me. You said what you said at the wedding and disappeared.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В106"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СКАЗАЛ"}}}, {"md": "\"Well, hello, Samokhin,\" she says. \"Get undressed. Don't be shy, I saw you in your underpants back at the backwater.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В106"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СКАЗАЛ"}}}, {"md": "She hangs my X-ray on the light box and studies it for a long time, tapping her pen against her teeth."}, {"md": "On the X-ray my lungs are gray, finely speckled, like a spoil heap under the first snow. Nika is silent longer than she should be. Then she takes off her glasses, and they hang on their chain.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ТЕЛ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "\"In Novosibirsk they do lung lavage,\" she says. \"One lung at a time, under anesthesia. There's a free quota next year, and if you pay, tomorrow. Only there's no point if you go back down in April.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ТЕЛ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "On the X-ray my lungs are spotted, finely speckled around the edges, but clear in the middle. \"You'll live,\" says Nika. \"If you stop going down. Dust is slow, Samokhin. You can't make a deal with it at the last minute, the way you like to.\" She mentions Novosibirsk, where they wash out lungs for money, in passing, like a spa for the rich.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ТЕЛ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "On her wall hangs a glossy promotional calendar from Moscow: \"Second Chance. The Siberian Resonance Foundation.\" Nika catches my look."}, {"md": "\"A procedure for people with more money than conscience,\" she says. \"They put something in your blood, and then you don't die when you're supposed to. They offered me a department here. I said no. I don't understand what they make it from, and when I don't understand something, I don't take it on.\""}, {"md": "Tanya is waiting for me in the hallway on a bench. We've been on and off for thirty-six years now: she has her apartment on Shakhtyorov Street, I have mine, and we have each other's keys. She looks at me the way she used to look at a lamp I'd brought back.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}, {"md": "Tanya is sitting on a bench in the hallway. She came on her own; nobody called her; she heard from Gosha. She still lives alone on Shakhtyorov Street, and sometimes I drop by for tea, the way I used to drop by the lamp-room window.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "Nika puts me in the ward for three weeks. Bronchoscopy, IV drips, breathing exercises with old women who blow up balloons and curse the government. I blow up balloons with them."}, {"md": "At discharge Nika is silent for a long time, holding my chart. \"It's anthracosis, Andrei,\" she says at last. \"Dust. I'm not promising you anything.\" It's the first time in her life she's called me by my first name.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ТЕЛ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "At discharge Nika hands me my chart and says, \"Not one foot underground, understand? Not even on a tour.\" I promise. The way she looks at me makes it clear how much she believes me.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ТЕЛ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}], "B": [{"md": "\"I'm closing the Severnaya in April,\" I tell Nika. \"The last one. Then I'll check in.\""}, {"md": "She shrugs and prescribes pills I don't take. In April I go down into the Severnaya and seal her shaft with concrete, twelve truckloads. I stand up top by the shaft until the concrete comes up level with the ground, coughing."}, {"md": "By summer I can't climb to the third floor without stopping. Nika puts me in the ward by force and doesn't joke anymore.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ТЕЛ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "In summer Gosha says there's nothing left in the Kuzbass to decommission. I retire on my own, without a cake, with one certificate of merit, and hang it in the toilet.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ТЕЛ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}], "C": [{"md": "I write my retirement application the same day, in the hallway of the regional hospital, on my knee. Tanya reads it over my shoulder and corrects one comma."}, {"md": "That evening she comes to my place with a bag, sets her slippers in the front hall and hangs her quilted jacket on the hook, the same one from the lamp room. In the mornings she makes me drink a brew of some herb, foul as water from the mine sump. A month later I cough for five minutes in the morning instead of ten. Six months later, two."}, {"md": "\"Thirty-six years,\" says Tanya. \"For thirty-six years I've been waiting for you to stop going down there, Samokhin.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}, {"md": "\"I've been waiting since '09,\" says Tanya. \"For you to finally bring the lamp back for good.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}], "D": [{"md": "I dictate the application to Gosha over the phone, right from the hospital hallway. Gosha breathes heavily into the phone for a long time. \"And the Severnaya?\" he says. \"Who's going to seal her, me?\" It turns out, him. I don't call the guys, not one of them, because I don't know what to say to men I've been riding the cage with for thirty years."}, {"md": "I sell the garage within a week, along with Dad's keys on their nail, the jars of bolts, the workbench where Dad gave me Granddad's storm jacket. Everything else goes to the neighbor at half price. Tanya is the last to find out, from that same neighbor."}, {"md": "\"You didn't even ask me,\" she says at the station. \"Thirty-six years, Samokhin. I'd have come with you.\""}, {"md": "In Novosibirsk they wash out my left lung first, then three weeks later the right. Cloudy gray water comes out of me by the liter; the nurse holds the jar up to the light and says, \"Well, there's a miner for you.\" By summer I'm climbing to the third floor without stopping. The cough stays, but I don't sit on the edge of the bed for ten minutes in the mornings anymore.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ТЕЛ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "In Novosibirsk they wash out one lung; the doctor advises leaving the other alone, it'll do. Gray water comes out of me into a jar; the nurse holds it up to the light and says, \"Well, there's a miner for you.\" I almost stop coughing.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ТЕЛ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "I come home to Rudnichny in September. I call Tanya from the front hall; she answers on the fifth ring and talks to me politely, like a neighbor."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Check in for tests, like Nika says"}, "B": {"label": "Work while I can. In April we close the Severnaya"}, "C": {"label": "Retire. Go to Tanya"}, "D": {"label": "Come up from underground today. Sell the garage and go to Novosibirsk for the lavage, alone"}}}, "В1-13": {"title": "The Adit", "date": "27 July 2061, 23:10", "photo_caption": "The mouth of the adit. Krasnaya Gorka", "text": [{"md": "They call me after eleven at night. It's Mishka Pakhomov, Seryoga's son, platoon commander in the mine rescue unit, forty-six years old, and on the phone his voice sounds like Seryoga's in 2010, when he lost his helmet."}, {"md": "\"Andrei Nikolaich,\" he says. \"Some kids from Rudnichny crawled into the adit under the Gorka. Three of them. Two came out, Pashka didn't. Eight years old. You know that adit; you've seen it on the plan.\""}, {"md": "I know the adit. 1939, from Krasnaya Gorka under the riverbed to the old incline under Rudnichny. In '44 I followed its plan to Granddad's door. Back then its mouth was underwater, but for three summers now, since the Tom went, it's been sticking out of the bluff as a brick arch, and kids run in there to scare each other."}, {"md": "Tanya is up before I put the phone down. She takes a lamp out of the closet, mine, number two hundred forty-six, which she carried out of the lamp room thirty-five years ago when they closed the Glubokaya. She charges it, checks it and hands it to me. \"Bring it back,\" she says. She doesn't say \"good boy.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}, {"md": "I have my own lamp, number two hundred forty-six; Tanya gave it to me when they closed the Glubokaya. I charge it, check it and call Tanya to tell her where I'm going. Tanya is silent, and then she says, \"Bring it back.\" She doesn't say \"good boy.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}, {"md": "I put on a striped sailor's undershirt and Granddad's canvas storm jacket, the one with the patch on the elbow. For thirty-four years it hung in my front hall, and I never once wore it underground. At the mouth of the adit Mishka's guys look at my storm jacket and are politely silent. They're in new suits with built-in lights, with breathing sets the size of a thermos. Half of them went to the Wager in '55; that's what they called the war where everybody was sure he wouldn't die, because somebody had bought him a Second Chance. Not all of them came back. Mishka did."}, {"md": "Vitka's here too, by the truck, seventy-three, limping on his right leg. He stands at the edge of the bluff shining a flashlight into the arch and says, \"Samokha, you're old.\" I say he's older.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В109"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВЫТАЩИЛ"}}}, {"md": "Vitka is sitting in his Niva at the edge of the bluff with the door open, his prostheses on the floor mat. He shines his headlights into the arch so the guys can see. He doesn't look down; he looks at me.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В109"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПОМОЩЬ"}}}, {"md": "Vitka's name was carved forty years ago on the stone by the old pithead building, in the common list. I drive past that stone on my way here and, as always, slow down.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В109"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "РАСЧЁТ"}}}, {"md": "Nika calls me when I'm already standing at the arch. \"Samokhin,\" she says. \"Mishka's in there. Bring him back to me. And come back yourself, you hear? For once in your life, show up when you said you would.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В106"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СКАЗАЛ"}}}, {"md": "Nika calls me when I'm already standing at the arch. \"Samokhin,\" she says. \"Mishka's in there. Are you his godfather or what? Bring him back.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В106"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СКАЗАЛ"}}}, {"md": "The Choir at the back of my head hums low and steady and pulls me into the arch, under the riverbed, in the direction where, under the left bank, Black Backwater is waiting. It pulls the way home pulls after a long trip away."}, {"md": "Can you get everyone out? I asked myself that all my life, since '09. That night they gave me a short answer.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I go first, the guys behind me, Mishka at the rear. The adit is low, a brick vault, a thin stream runs along the floor, and the farther we go, the deeper it gets. At the fork, where an old working branches off the adit, I say, \"Right,\" though there's nothing there on the plan. The back of my neck tells me."}, {"md": "We find Pashka at half past two, in a dead end a hundred meters past the fork. He's sitting on a rock hugging his knees, and his flashlight has died. He isn't crying. When the light falls on him, he says, \"I was counting. I got to a thousand, and then I lost count.\" Mishka wraps him in a foil blanket and carries him up top himself, in his arms."}, {"md": "\"Andrei Nikolaich, come on,\" says Mishka."}, {"md": "\"Go ahead,\" I say. \"I'll take a look farther on, in case there's a second one.\""}, {"md": "There isn't a second one, and Mishka knows it. But he doesn't know how to argue with me; Seryoga raised him that way. I go on down the stream, and the adit dips under the riverbed, and the water rises to my knees, my waist, my chest. The vault goes down into the water. I take a breath, like at seven by the snag, and dive. The gas detector says 04:08. Up above, through the black, the water starts to grow light, though at four in the morning there's nothing to light it."}], "B": [{"md": "I leave the guys at the arch, tie a rope around my waist and go alone. Mishka curses at my back, quietly, in Seryoga's words. The adit is low, a brick vault, a stream runs along the floor. At the fork I turn right, though there's nothing there on the plan. The back of my neck tells me."}, {"md": "I find Pashka at half past two, in a dead end. His flashlight has died, and he's sitting on a rock hugging his knees. \"I was counting,\" he says when the light falls on him. \"To a thousand, and then I lost count.\" I give him my lamp, number two hundred forty-six, and put him on the rope. \"Follow it up, hand over hand. There are men up there. Give the lamp to Auntie Tanya, got it?\" He nods and goes, shining it at his feet. The rope jerks twice, then twice more. He made it."}, {"md": "I untie the rope from my waist. Without a lamp the adit is as dark as it was in '86 behind Granddad's door. I go on down the stream by feel, and the water rises to my knees, my waist, my chest. The vault goes down into the water. I take a breath, like at seven by the snag, and dive. Up above, through the black, the water starts to grow light, though at four in the morning there's nothing to light it."}], "C": [{"md": "\"Mishka,\" I say. \"Right at the fork, about a hundred meters. There's a dead end. He's there.\""}, {"md": "Mishka looks at me, at my storm jacket, nods and leads the guys into the arch. I sit down on a rock at the edge of the bluff. Tanya sits next to me and pours me tea from her thermos, strong and sweet, like on a train, like in the lamp room."}, {"md": "At half past two the radio at Tanya's ear crackles in Mishka's voice: \"Found him. Alive. Says he counted to a thousand.\" Tanya switches off the radio and says, \"There.\" She doesn't say anything else."}, {"md": "It still pulls at me. Tanya sees it; she's seen everything since '09. She takes my arm, and we climb down the bluff onto the dry riverbed and walk over the stones to the backwater, slowly, the way old people walk. The comet hangs over us. Tanya carries the lamp herself, shining it at my feet."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Lead the guys down myself. I'm the only one who knows this adit"}, "B": {"label": "Go alone, on a rope. Leave the guys at the entrance"}, "C": {"label": "Give the adit to Mishka. Take Tanya and walk to the backwater over the top"}}}, "В1.2-00": {"title": "The Work Order", "date": "February 2015", "photo_caption": "The assignment room of section seven", "text": [{"md": "Since the fall I've been mine foreman of section seven, still without a diploma, in my last year of correspondence school. They haven't given me a white helmet yet; they gave me an orange one with a white stripe and a desk in the assignment room, by the window, where you can see the headframe. At this desk every morning I write the work order: who goes where, on what job, how many meters. Twenty-three names in a column. Seryoga Pakhomov on the fifth line, Vitka on the eleventh."}, {"md": "In '15 the ruble falls by half, and the holding company announces that bonuses will only be paid to sections that make quota. Longwall seven doesn't make quota, because it's gassy. The fixed sensor at the junction reads one and a half percent methane and cuts the power to the shearer; twenty minutes later the gas thins out and the shearer goes back on; forty minutes after that it all happens again."}, {"md": "The section chief, Bondarenko, sits down on the edge of my desk, and the desk creaks. Bondarenko is fat and honest in his fatness, twenty years underground, two kids in paid colleges in Kemerovo."}, {"md": "\"Andryukha,\" he says. \"You're the foreman. It's your shift and your work order. I'm not telling you anything, I'm just showing you.\" He lays a Maria-Ra bag with handles and a roll of blue electrical tape on the work order. \"Pakhomov's got a kid on the way. Your Vitka's paying off a loan on his Lada. You're one of us. Sort it out.\""}, {"md": "He goes out to smoke. The bag lies on my work order, across twenty-three names."}, {"md": "In '05 I told a reporter how the sensors in the section were taped over to make quota and they brought up nine men out of twelve. They remember the name Samokhin here. Bondarenko remembers too, which is why he doesn't hand me the bag; he puts it on the desk.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т08"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДАТЧИКИ"}}}, {"md": "The chill behind my ear touches me when I look at the bag and goes away when I look out the window. It can only show me, too.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "Vitka comes into the assignment room for his lamp, sees the bag and whistles."}, {"md": "\"Oh,\" he says. \"Management's growing up. Samokha, whatever you do, sign the work order, or they won't let us down without one.\" He laughs and leaves."}, {"md": "For thirty years Dad signed the work order for his team. Later I understood why he took so long writing out his name.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "Seryoga takes the bag from my hands and doesn't look me in the face. At the face he pulls it over the sensor and winds the tape, three turns, like a bandage. I stand next to him with a portable instrument on my belt and don't take my eyes off the screen all shift. The gas doesn't go above two. The shearer doesn't stop once that shift, and seven makes quota for the first time in six months."}, {"md": "In March a commission from the region checks the sensor without warning. By then there's no bag on it; I took it off myself on the last day of the month. Bondarenko gets his bonus and shakes my hand in the bathhouse with a wet palm. \"Good man,\" he says. From that bag on, everything went for me, as they say in the mine, on a level floor."}, {"md": "At night I dream I'm writing the work order and the names are sliding down the page like water into a drain. I wake up in the dark and count them from memory, down to Vitka on the eleventh line."}], "B": [{"md": "\"What are you doing, Samokhin,\" says Bondarenko when he reads the work order. He stands there a long time, turning red, then tears my page out of the book and throws it in the trash. I write it again, the same words, and sign again. He doesn't tear out the second page."}, {"md": "The face stands idle for three days while they measure and remeasure the gas. The section loses its bonus. The men from seven don't say hello to me for a month, and Vitka does, but loudly, so everyone hears: \"Hi there, boss.\" They haven't made me section chief yet that year, and that's worse than swearing."}, {"md": "In April the chief engineer calls me in and says that a foreman who stops a face doesn't last long in a section. I say I'll last. He looks at me over his glasses and writes something in a notebook."}], "C": [{"md": "The chief engineer, Grinberg, tall and stooped, listens without interrupting and looks at the ventilation diagram I drew on the back of a work sheet. \"You did this yourself?\" he says. \"Without the institute?\" I say with the institute, by correspondence. He snorts and signs the request for an auxiliary fan, and tells me to put the downtime on my section."}, {"md": "For a week seven stands idle while the fitters run the duct and install the fan. Bondarenko doesn't talk to me, and the men count my money out loud. Then the fan is switched on, the gas at the junction drops to half a percent and doesn't go up again. The face runs."}, {"md": "In the bathhouse Bondarenko hands me a birch whisk without a word. He takes the Maria-Ra bag off my desk himself and carries his pork fat to work in it."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Give the bag to Seryoga. Let him put it on till the end of the shift, and I'll stand there with the instrument"}, "B": {"label": "Write in the work order \"longwall seven: stop pending gas check\" and sign it"}, "C": {"label": "Go to the chief engineer and fight for a fan at the junction. Take a week of downtime on myself"}}}, "В1.2-01": {"title": "The Belt", "date": "14 November 2023, 10:05", "photo_caption": "The conveyor drift. The belt runs toward the crosscut", "text": [{"md": "They gave me the white helmet in 2020. Chief of section seven, forty-two men, two shearers, a face a kilometer and a half from the shaft. On my wrist now there's a tablet in a rubber case that shows gas, air, and the men as dots, who's where. They put this system in after the fire in '21, when the belt in the main drift burned and Palych lay down on the rail at meter one hundred. That night I sat at the dispatcher's on the intercom, counting tags."}, {"md": "On the morning of the fourteenth I'm at the face, by the shearer, with the crew. The tablet on my wrist suddenly starts beeping, short and fast, like an alarm clock somebody forgot to turn off. Carbon monoxide at crosscut number three, forty, a minute later sixty. The crosscut is a kilometer and a half from here, toward the shaft. Beyond it is the old longwall five, worked out, closed off behind a stopping, and coal in its goaf must have started to smolder. It happens: coal in a goaf heats up for years and one day catches fire by itself."}, {"md": "The air current runs from the shaft toward us. Smoke from five will come along it, through the crosscut, into our face, and the crew has only one way out, through that smoke."}, {"md": "The door at the crosscut has been open for six days, propped with a prop, so they can haul timber faster. I allowed it, because of quota. I signed the ventilation log and forgot about it. Close that door, and the smoke goes into the return, past us.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В120"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПЛАН"}}}, {"md": "The door at the crosscut, I see on the tablet, is open: somebody's propped it with a prop to haul timber faster. Close that door, and the smoke goes into the return, past us.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В120"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПЛАН"}}}, {"md": "The intercom at the shearer is dead; the cable's been cut since the night shift, and the dispatcher can't hear us. Twenty-three men at the face pull on their self-rescuers and look at me. Seryoga, Vitka, young Rustam. Next to us the belt hums, carrying coal to the shaft, past the crosscut, at a good trot. Riding it is forbidden. I've docked men's bonuses for it myself."}, {"md": "The cold behind my ear stands still, like water in a barrel. It doesn't say anything; it's waiting for me to choose.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "\"Boss,\" says Vitka through his mouthpiece. \"Well?\""}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I lie flat on the coal, face forward, and the belt carries me into the dark. The support arches flash by so low over my head that I press my helmet into the coal. Before the crosscut the belt rises to the transfer point, to the drum, and twenty meters out I get up on all fours, push off with both feet and jump sideways into the walkway. My knee hits a rail, my helmet a prop, but I'm on the floor, and the belt goes under the drum without me."}, {"md": "The door is propped with a prop. I kick the prop out, throw my shoulder into the door, and it shuts with a bang that makes my ears pop. The numbers on the tablet freeze at eighty, then slowly creep down. The smoke goes into the return."}, {"md": "Forty minutes later the crew walks out to the crosscut on their own, all twenty-three, in self-rescuers. Vitka sees my smashed knee and says, \"Belt rider,\" and never tells anybody else about it."}], "B": [{"md": "I run along the drift, over the sleepers, in my self-rescuer, counting sleepers so I won't count minutes. At the six hundredth sleeper smoke starts coming toward me, clear at first, then yellow. My lamp hits it and shines no farther. I walk along the belt by feel, holding on to the frame."}, {"md": "I reach the door in the twenty-seventh minute. I kick out the prop and shoulder the door shut. The smoke is still standing in the drift, but no new smoke is coming. The crew walks out through it in single file, holding on to each other, the way I taught them in drills. Rustam and Vitka bring Seryoga out under the arms. He swallowed carbon monoxide while helping a young guy with his self-rescuer."}, {"md": "Seryoga spends two weeks in the hyperbaric chamber at the regional hospital. Nika, who works there in pulmonology, comes out to me in the hallway and doesn't say anything, just looks. Then she says about Seryoga, \"Alive.\" And, already leaving: \"You made it, Samokhin. On foot, but you made it.\" At the wake for longwall five, when it's finally flooded, Vitka drinks to me first."}], "C": [{"md": "I lie down on the coal and ride. The support arches flash overhead, the belt shudders under me on its rollers. I count the stretches the way I remember them: the third stall, the switch, the distribution point. Past the distribution point a red emergency-stop cord runs along the frame. I sit up on the coal, grab it in my fist and yank."}, {"md": "The belt stops with a screech, fifty meters short of the transfer point, and I climb down along the frame to the walkway like off a stove. Coal trickles down my collar. I walk to the door, kick out the prop and shoulder it shut. The numbers on the tablet freeze and creep down."}, {"md": "Past the transfer point there's a spot where the belt goes under the drum, and now my knees know that spot. A minute ago, for me, it wasn't there."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Onto the belt. Lie down on the coal and ride to the crosscut in five minutes"}, "B": {"label": "Run along the drift. A kilometer and a half, however long it takes"}, "C": {"label": "\"I remember where the belt goes under the drum.\" Ride it, and fifty meters before the transfer point yank the emergency cord"}}}, "В1.2-02": {"title": "The Crust", "date": "9 September 2033, 17:30", "photo_caption": "The Glubokaya spoil heap. Smoke on the slope", "text": [{"md": "The Glubokaya was closed in '26. Its spoil heap is still there, a gray cone above the Tom by Krasnaya Gorka, a hundred and twenty meters of waste rock that was hauled up top for seventy years and dumped in one pile. Since '29 it's been burning. From the inside, without flame: coal in the rock heats up and smolders, smoke comes off the slopes, white and yellow, and all of Rudnichny smells of rotten eggs. I'm chief engineer at Kuzbass Decommissioning, a subsidiary of the holding company, and this spoil heap is mine, by order, with a stamp."}, {"md": "Dad has been gone six years. Before he died, in the regional hospital, after the stroke, he said three sentences to me, one per breath: \"Under Krasnaya Gorka. Granddad's there. Don't open it.\" And I don't. Under Krasnaya Gorka I've got other worries right now."}, {"md": "Vitka calls at half past five. He drives a BelAZ, one of those giant dump trucks, at the open-pit mine near Belovo, and doesn't come home on weekdays, but today is Friday."}, {"md": "\"Samokha,\" he says, and his voice is torn, like in '96. \"My boy's on your spoil heap. Yegorka. Three of them went up on a dare, two came down.\""}, {"md": "For ten years Vitka has been calling me \"belt rider,\" and he's never once asked how I knew where to jump.", "if": {"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В121"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЛЕНТА"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В121"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТРОС"}}]}}, {"md": "Since '23, since the crosscut, wherever Vitka drinks, he drinks to me first.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В121"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПЕШКОМ"}}}, {"md": "I get there in twelve minutes. Vitka is standing at the bottom by the fence with the sign \"DANGER! BURNING WASTE DUMP,\" in his work coveralls, white under his ginger stubble. Yegorka is eleven. He's standing in the middle of the slope in a red T-shirt, about forty meters above us, and not moving. Steam is coming out of the cracks around him. Under his feet is crust, baked rock, gray on top, and under it nothing, and in that nothing it's six hundred degrees. I know from the measurements; there's a pyrometer in my car."}, {"md": "\"Yegor!\" Vitka yells. \"Get down, I said!\""}, {"md": "Yegorka shakes his head and cries. His sneakers are smoking."}, {"md": "The wind is blowing off the river, along the slope, carrying the smoke onto the road where we're standing. In my trunk I've got a crowbar, the pyrometer and canvas mittens. The firefighters from the Rudnichny station will be here in about twenty minutes, and a bulldozer from the open pit in an hour."}, {"md": "Dare you, don't dare you. In our yard that word drowned more kids than the current ever did.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "I go around the spoil heap from the river side, into the wind, where the smoke is carried away from the slope. I hold the crowbar out in front of me and strike the crust before every step. Where it rings, I step. Where it booms hollow, I go around. The pyrometer on my belt beeps every three meters. Twice the crowbar goes through the crust up to the handle, and heat blasts out like from a stove when you open the damper."}, {"md": "I reach Yegorka in nine minutes. He grabs me with both hands, and I carry him down in my own tracks, over the ringing crust, step for step. At the bottom Vitka snatches him from me and stands holding him close, saying nothing. Yegorka's sneakers have melted, the soles stuck to his socks, but his feet are fine."}, {"md": "That night Vitka comes to my place with a bottle and sets it on the table. \"You're the boss of that pile,\" he says. \"Make it stop burning. Hear me? You're an engineer.\""}], "C": [{"md": "\"Yegor,\" I say, not loud, evenly, so it carries and he doesn't flinch. \"It's Uncle Andrei. Look at your feet. See your footprints? Walk back on them. Backwards. Like down a ladder. Take your time; I'm right here.\""}, {"md": "He looks at me, then at his footprints, gray on gray. He takes a step back, then another. Beside me Vitka jerks to run to him, and I hold him by his coveralls with both hands. \"Stay,\" I say. \"He can do it himself. He knows how.\" Yegorka comes down in his own footprints in five minutes, backwards, one step at a time, and the whole way down I talk to him about soccer."}, {"md": "At the bottom he cries so hard he hiccups, and Vitka cries with him. Then Vitka tells me, without raising his eyes, \"I'd have gone up. I'd have stayed up there.\" That night he comes over with a bottle and says, \"You're the boss of that pile. Make it stop burning.\""}], "D": [{"md": "I grab Vitka by the shoulders, and he elbows me in the stomach, and I hold on. \"You won't help him, you'll go through,\" I yell in his ear. \"Wait.\" He pulls away once more and goes limp. We stand at the fence and watch Yegorka standing in the smoke."}, {"md": "The firefighters come in eighteen minutes, with ladders, and they're scared to go up too. An hour later the bulldozer arrives from the open pit and lays a path of cold rock up the slope. That's how they get Yegorka down. He stood on the crust for almost an hour and a half, and it heated up under him. He has second-degree burns on the soles of his feet, spends a month in the burn unit, and wears flip-flops all autumn."}, {"md": "Vitka doesn't talk to me for a year. In the garages, people say, he said about me, \"Samokha's an engineer. He calculates.\" That night, when I'm already in bed, he calls anyway and says just one thing into the phone: \"Make it stop burning.\""}], "E": [{"md": "I go around the slope from the river side, into the wind, watching my feet. Nothing grows on the dead crust. But where the rock has cooled and holds, wormwood stands, gray and dusty, knee-high, and a crooked strip of it runs up toward Yegorka. I walk on the wormwood. Where it ends, I stop and wait until I see the next clump."}, {"md": "I reach Yegorka in six minutes and carry him down the same way, over the wormwood, step for step. The soles of his sneakers have melted; his feet are fine. Vitka looks from me to the slope and back, at the strip of grass you can see now, once you know where to look."}, {"md": "\"It's like you knew,\" he says."}, {"md": "I say I'm an engineer. That night he comes over with a bottle and asks me to make the pile stop burning."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Run up, straight at him, while the crust holds"}, "B": {"label": "Go around the slope and climb from the other side, tapping every step with the crowbar"}, "C": {"label": "Don't go up. Call him by name, calmly, so he comes down himself, in his own footprints"}, "D": {"label": "Hold Vitka back and wait for the firefighters with ladders. Let the bulldozer build a path up the slope"}, "E": {"label": "\"I remember where the crust holds.\" Into the wind, over the live grass, where the rock is cold"}}}, "В1.2-03": {"title": "The Certificate", "date": "28 November 2033", "photo_caption": "An office. A certificate awaiting signature", "text": [{"md": "Our office is on the nineteenth floor, on Kuznetsky Prospekt, with windows facing the Tom. On a clear day you can see my spoil heap from here: a gray cone by Krasnaya Gorka and the smoke over it, white against the gray sky. In November you can see the smoke especially well, because there's snow on the ground all around, and on the slopes it melts in circles."}, {"md": "On Sunday Sonka video-calls from Moscow, like always at eight our time. It's raining outside her window and snowing outside mine. She tells me about her hospital and the waiting list for MRIs, and then she looks at me through the screen for a long time and asks, \"What's wrong with you? Are they bullying you on that mountain of yours?\" I say I'm the boss myself now, and there's nobody to bully me. \"That's exactly what I'm afraid of,\" she says."}, {"md": "The holding company has new owners now, from Moscow. I've seen them once, on a screen, in a video-call window. Their man in Kemerovo, Arseny Olegovich, is ten years younger than me and wears a sweater that costs as much as my Patriot SUV. He puts a certificate in front of me: \"Fire at the waste rock dump of the Glubokaya mine extinguished. Dump reclaimed.\" At the bottom, a line for the chief engineer's signature. Mine."}, {"md": "\"We'll cap it with clay before New Year's,\" he says. \"Lawn on top, birches in spring. The land under the dump has already been bought; there'll be a development, Gorki, a hundred and twenty houses with a river view. This is what you wanted, Andrei Nikolayevich. For it to stop burning.\""}, {"md": "Under the clay the spoil heap will go on burning for another thirty years. I know it, he knows it, and so does the pyrometer in my desk drawer. Putting it out honestly means taking it apart with excavators layer by layer, flooding it, cooling it: seven years and money the new owners won't give for it."}, {"md": "\"The Kuzbass directorship opens up in January,\" says Arseny Olegovich, looking out the window. \"We need a man who signs. And a house in Gorki, if you like. Front row.\""}, {"md": "A Maria-Ra bag lay on my work order eighteen years ago. Since then I've signed a lot of papers, and they were all smooth.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В120"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПЛАН"}}}, {"md": "Yegorka still wears flip-flops, though there's snow outside, because his feet hurt in boots. Vitka doesn't talk to me.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В122"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЖОГ"}}}, {"md": "Last Friday Vitka came over with a bottle again. \"So, put it out?\" he asked from the doorway, the way people ask \"So, had the baby?\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В122"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЖОГ"}}}, {"md": "Tanya is a caretaker at the museum on Krasnaya Gorka now; she hands out helmets to schoolkids for the adit tour and counts them back in by heads. We've been on and off for over twenty years, and every time it's off, it's because of me. Yesterday she said on the phone, \"Samokhin, just once you could come over when you don't have to run off somewhere.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В101"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}, {"md": "The pen lies across the signature line. Expensive, heavy, with a gold nib. I've never held one like it."}, {"md": "Can you get everyone out? I'd asked myself that since '09, since my first death at the face. Sitting at a desk, it turned out, you could get nobody out and still sign for everyone.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "I set the pen down on the desk next to the certificate, straight, the way you put down a tool after a shift. Arseny Olegovich looks at it, then at me, and isn't surprised. \"A pity,\" he says. \"They'd have appreciated you in Moscow.\" He takes the certificate with him and in a week finds somebody to sign it."}, {"md": "Only by then it can't be signed. On Monday I hand over the chief engineer's job and stay at the same Kuzbass Decommissioning as an ordinary section chief, taking apart waste dumps, at a third of the pay, and on Tuesday I take a copy of the reclamation plan and my own pyrometer readings, dated and signed, to the regional prosecutor's office. The Gorki development is never built. We take the spoil heap apart for seven years, with excavators, layer by layer, and I see the last smoke over it in the spring of '40."}, {"md": "Vitka comes on the last day, with a bottle, and we drink on the cold rock at the very top. \"Put it out,\" he says. I say I put it out. Below us is the Tom, and the ice on it is cracking."}], "C": [{"md": "I leave the certificate on the desk unsigned, with the pen across it. I write my resignation by hand on a sheet from the printer and give it to the secretary. That evening Arseny Olegovich calls and says I'm crazy. I say maybe so."}, {"md": "Tanya opens the door of her apartment on Krasnaya Gorka in a bathrobe with a towel around her head. She looks at me, at my bag, at the clock on the wall. \"For good, is it?\" she says. I say for good. She takes my bag and sets it down in the front hall, the way you set a lamp on the rack."}, {"md": "A week later my deputy signs the certificate. They cap the spoil heap with clay before New Year's and plant birches in the spring. The development is never built on it: the birches go yellow in circles, and the buyers get scared. Every day I see it from Tanya's window, green and warm, and in winter the snow won't lie on it."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Sign. Let it stop burning, at least on paper"}, "B": {"label": "Don't sign. Step down to a section and put it out honestly, however long it takes"}, "C": {"label": "Don't sign, and leave for good. To Tanya, on Krasnaya Gorka"}}}, "В1.2-04": {"title": "The Dump", "date": "27 July 2061, 23:30", "photo_caption": "Krasnaya Gorka. The dump above the dry Tom", "text": [{"md": "These days the boys from Rudnichny walk to Krasnaya Gorka straight across the Tom, in sandals, with jars, over the white stones where the buoys used to stand. On TV scientists spread their hands over the backwater: all the water has gone, and this stays."}, {"md": "The Glubokaya spoil heap is gone. We took it apart over seven years, until '40, cooled it and leveled it, and in its place there's a gentle slope with birches now, cold, the way earth should be. After that I spent another ten years closing mines, one after another, sealing shafts with concrete and letting water into the workings. I closed the last one in Kemerovo in '48. Dad opened them and I closed them, and there's some kind of order in that, though what kind I never figured out.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В123"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОТКАЗ"}}}, {"md": "The Glubokaya spoil heap has been under clay for twenty-eight years now, green, with birches that go yellow in circles. In winter the snow won't lie on it. I see it from Tanya's window every morning, and every morning I put my palm to the glass, like to a stove. The glass is cold, but my palm still knows it's warm. Somebody after me will have to put it out.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В123"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}, {"md": "Under Krasnaya Gorka, Dad said, was Granddad. I never did open it. The door he was talking about is somewhere down there, under the riverbed, under my feet, and these last three summers, since the Tom went, I haven't been drawn to it. I'm drawn to the backwater."}, {"md": "It's been pulling at me all year, and since the comet, more than ever. The comet hangs over the riverbed in the evenings, its tail lying exactly on the water. \"It'll come back on the twenty-eighth of July, sixty-one,\" Galina Ivanovna said at graduation, and we cracked up: seventy-two was never. The whisper behind my ear, which since '09 I've heard before every disaster, has turned this year into a steady hum, like in the assignment room when forty men are waiting for the shift."}, {"md": "Vitka calls me a little after nine. He's seventy-three; he says his knee is acting up again, and Yegor wants him to come live in Novosibirsk, and he won't go. About the Wager, the war of '55, where the generals with their purchased Second Chance decided dying wasn't frightening, and about the two men who never came back from it to his open pit, we haven't talked in a long time. He asks if I'm going out at first light tomorrow. \"Pulling at you?\" he says. I say it is."}, {"md": "Tanya is asleep in the living room on the couch, with the TV on, the way people sleep when they're retired. My lamp, number two hundred forty-six, sits on her shelf. She carried it out of the lamp room when they closed the Glubokaya, and all these years she's charged it once a month, she doesn't know why herself.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}, {"md": "It's quiet at home. The fridge clicks, and on my shelf sits my miner's lamp, number two hundred forty-six. Tanya gave it to me when they closed the Glubokaya. I charge it once a month, I don't know why myself.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАНЯ"}}}, {"md": "Granddad's storm jacket hangs in the front hall. Dad brought it to my garage a week before his stroke, canvas, with a patch on the elbow, and said, \"Must be Granddad's.\" I've never worn it since."}, {"md": "I made a decent section chief. I still remember those twenty-three names in a column, in order, and where every one of them is now. About myself tomorrow I knew nothing.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "At three I get up, put on a striped sailor's undershirt and Granddad's storm jacket, and take the lamp off the shelf. The lamp shines yellow and steady, the way it did in the lamp room when Tanya put it in my hands. I lock the door with one turn."}, {"md": "The yard smells of dust and dry wormwood. The comet hangs over the roofs. I go down to the riverbed by the old fishermen's stairs; the steps lead nowhere now, onto white stones, and I walk over the stones like over sleepers, counting. I lose count at three hundred and start over."}], "B": [{"md": "I sit down on the edge of the couch and touch Tanya's shoulder. She opens her eyes at once, no sleep in them, like in the lamp room when somebody knocked on the window at four in the morning. She looks at me, at the storm jacket, at the clock."}, {"md": "\"To the river,\" she says, and is already swinging her legs off the couch."}, {"md": "She gets up, takes the lamp off the shelf, checks the charge and hands it to me, and then takes it back. \"I'll carry it myself,\" she says. \"I issue it, I take it back.\" We walk to the riverbed together, slowly, her in front with the lamp, and the stones in its light are white as sugar."}], "C": [{"md": "Sonka answers on the third ring, though in Moscow it's past eleven at night and here it's four in the morning. She always answers on the third ring."}, {"md": "\"Andryush, what is it?\" she says. \"Did something happen?\""}, {"md": "\"Nothing,\" I say. \"I'm going to the river. Tell Mom…\" and I stumble, because Mom has been gone eight years. Sonka is silent on the phone. Then she says, \"I'll tell her.\" And: \"Go on already, you poor thing.\" That's what Mom used to say when I ran off to the Tom at seven. I put the phone in the pocket of the storm jacket and walk to the riverbed, with the lamp, by the old fishermen's stairs."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Go alone. Down the dry riverbed, the way I went at seven"}, "B": {"label": "Wake Tanya. Let her issue me a light"}, "C": {"label": "Call Sonka in Moscow. Tell her I'm going to the river, and go"}}}, "В2-01": {"title": "The Dorm", "date": "September 2007", "photo_caption": "The dorm on Lenin Avenue. Our room 412", "text": [{"md": "Tomsk greets me with rain and cabbage pies at the Tomsk-1 station, twelve rubles apiece. I eat three while I look for the right bus, and decide I like this city."}, {"md": "The dorm on Lenin Avenue is old, with stucco on the front and cockroaches in the kitchen. The woman at the front desk looks at my passport as if I'd drawn it myself. There are three of us in 412. Den from Seversk, the closed nuclear town, says everything in his town glows, and laughs louder than anyone at his own joke. Tagir from Kolpashevo silently lines his shelves with cans of stewed meat his mother gave him and shares with nobody. A week later he and I are eating them together, and Den is offended."}, {"md": "For the first six months nobody in the physics department says a word about stars; it's all calculus. At the first lecture the associate professor writes some integral on the board, turns around and says, \"Half of you will be gone after the first exams. Don't take it personally. It's statistics.\" We laugh, and nobody believes him."}, {"md": "On the department noticeboard, between the timetable and a notice about potato-picking duty, hang photos of the faculty. Professor V. P. Lunyov looks out of his card with tired eyes. I've definitely seen him somewhere, and for half a day I can't remember where."}, {"md": "The Tom here is the same as at home, only farther downstream and wider. In the evenings I walk along the embankment and figure how far it is to swim from here to my backwater. It turns out it's no distance at all, because the water flows the other way."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I sit over Demidovich until two in the morning, and Den snores so loud the glass on the nightstand rattles. At the first colloquium the associate professor looks at my record book, then at me, and says, \"Samokhin? From Kemerovo? Well, well.\" I pass my first exams with straight A's, and from that day on everybody who can be bothered asks to copy off me."}], "B": [{"md": "At night Tagir and I unload freight cars at the Tomsk-2 yard: cement, sugar, once a whole car of bananas. The foreman pays cash in an envelope and calls us \"students\" in the voice people usually swear in. By New Year's I have new sneakers, my shoulders are a size bigger, and I fall asleep in calculus lectures."}], "C": [{"md": "I fit into the dorm fast: guitar, the department comedy team, Baltika on the windowsill and talking till morning about everything in the world. I pass my exams with C's, on the second try. But by spring half the dorm knows me, and the woman at the front desk doesn't look at my passport anymore."}], "D": [{"md": "Almost every night I go down to the embankment. I sit on the parapet over the Tom and listen to the water rustle. Sometimes in that rustling I think I hear somebody counting, quietly, in two voices. I don't tell anybody, and each time I get back to the dorm when the windows have already gone dark."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Study hard: Demidovich's problem book, colloquiums, first in every seminar"}, "B": {"label": "Unload freight cars at night at the Tomsk-2 yard: money and shoulders"}, "C": {"label": "Live the dorm life: guitar, the comedy team, Baltika on the windowsill"}, "D": {"label": "Go down to the embankment at night and listen to what the water whispers"}}}, "В2-02": {"title": "The Freight Elevator", "date": "9 December 2008, 16:40", "photo_caption": "\"No passengers\"", "clock_label": "until I pass out", "text": [{"md": "The associate professor didn't lie: after the first exams half our year was gone. I'm in second year, I've held on, and I even work part-time as a lab assistant in the department. I wash flasks, haul instruments and listen to the grad students swear."}, {"md": "Zoya Mikhailovna, our senior lab technician, asks me to bring a Dewar flask of liquid nitrogen, about forty liters, up from the basement to the third floor. The stairs are being renovated, the railings removed, the steps covered in cement. That leaves the freight elevator, which has NO PASSENGERS stenciled on its doors. Everybody ignores the sign, and so do I. I wheel the flask inside on a cart; it's heavy, pot-bellied, and hisses quietly under its lid."}, {"md": "Between the second and third floors the elevator jerks and stops. The bulb in the ceiling flickers and stays on at half strength. I press \"3,\" then \"2,\" then every button in a row, like when I was a kid in our stairwell. Nothing. My Nokia has no bars; there's no signal in this iron box."}, {"md": "The flask's lid hisses louder. White mist creeps across the floor around my sneakers, like from a fog machine at a Mumiy Troll concert, only cold. The cold gets into my sneakers, through my socks, to my ankles. But up to my waist the cabin is warm, even a little stuffy, and smells of machine oil. I stand there watching the mist cover the floor and think it's actually kind of beautiful."}], "after": {"D": [{"md": "I stand on the handrail, press the top of my head against the cabin ceiling and put my face to the grille. It's warm there and smells of dust. With my free hand I bang the lab key against the wall, evenly, once a second, and don't stop even when my arm goes numb."}, {"md": "Twenty minutes later somebody outside shouts, \"Who's in there?!\" They open the elevator with a crowbar. Zoya Mikhailovna sees the mist on the floor, goes pale, first swears and then bursts into tears."}, {"md": "They pour tea into me in the department. My hands are shaking so hard I spill it. Everybody thinks it's the cold."}], "E": [{"md": "The panel by the door is old, with buttons in two rows, and under them a red mushroom button painted over along with the panel, as if nobody had ever pressed it. I push it with my palm until the paint cracks. The bulb in the ceiling goes out completely, and somewhere up in the shaft a relay clicks. Quiet. Now the elevator is dead as a wardrobe, and for some reason that makes me calmer."}, {"md": "I pry the doors apart with the lab key and my fingers; my nails break off to the quick. The gap is half a meter, and beyond it is the third-floor landing and a pair of brown shoes, those same ones. The shoes belong to Uncle Vitya, the watchman; he's smoking by the window and drops his cigarette when a student comes crawling up at him out of the floor. I climb out to the waist, then all the way, and scrape my stomach on the sill. My sweater catches on the edge and tears. The mist doesn't follow me through the gap; it stays lying on the cabin floor."}, {"md": "\"Where the hell did you come from?\" asks Uncle Vitya."}, {"md": "Ten minutes later Zoya Mikhailovna comes running with a mechanic. The mechanic shines a flashlight down at the white lake on the cabin floor, whistles, and says the elevator probably wouldn't have moved by itself, and with the Stop pressed nothing at all could have happened. I nod. My hands are shaking, and everybody thinks it's the cold."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Press the call button and wait for the dispatcher"}, "B": {"label": "Pry the doors open and climb out through the gap between floors"}, "C": {"label": "The flask is hissing louder and louder. Open the lid to let the pressure out"}, "D": {"label": "\"I know what's about to happen.\" Climb up high, to the grille under the ceiling where the air is warm, and bang on the wall until somebody hears"}, "E": {"label": "\"I remember how it started moving.\" First find the Stop button on the panel and cut the power to the cabin, and only then pry the doors"}, "F": {"label": "\"I remember where the cold comes from.\" Plug the neck of the flask with my jacket so the mist stops coming"}}}, "В2-03": {"title": "Lagerny Garden", "date": "14 January 2009, 02:30", "photo_caption": "Old New Year at Lyokha's in Stepanovka", "text": [{"md": "After the elevator I sleep with the light on for a month. Den thinks I've got a broken heart and slips me Splean CDs. I don't tell anyone anything. I can't exactly say I died in an elevator, only not all the way."}, {"md": "For Old New Year, the one by the old calendar on the night of the thirteenth of January, Lyokha from our year invites everybody to his place in Stepanovka. His parents have gone to Kolpashevo; for eight people there are two bottles of vodka and a pack of dumplings that nobody ever gets around to boiling. His dad's felt boots with rubber galoshes stand in the front hall, and all evening Lyokha kicks them into the corner to get them out of the way. The vodka runs out at two in the morning; the trolleybuses stopped at eleven."}, {"md": "Outside it's forty-two below. The air rings, the streetlights stand in rust-colored fog, and the snow doesn't creak underfoot, it shrieks. It's six kilometers to the dorm on Lenin Avenue if you go through Lagerny Garden, the park along the steep bank above the Tom. For a taxi I have exactly two hundred rubles, and it's nine days till my stipend."}, {"md": "I'm wearing white Adidas with three stripes, bought in the fall with freight-car money. I threw out my old winter boots back then. I like myself in the new sneakers, and so far I haven't been cold in them.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В201"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЖЕЛЕЗО"}}}, {"md": "The cold starts at my ankles, like that time in the elevator, and creeps up. I stamp my feet outside the entrance and catch myself looking down to see if anything white is spreading there."}, {"md": "Forty-two below happens in Tomsk once in ten years. I got it the night the vodka ran out at two.", "kind": "old"}, {"md": "The back of my neck is colder than my cheeks in the frost. And I already don't want to look at the bench by the monument in Lagerny Garden, though you can't even see it from here.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "Lena from third year comes out with me, the last of us. She lives right here, in the next entrance, but she doesn't go home; she stands picking at the frost on the railing with her mitten."}, {"md": "\"Freezing,\" she says to the railing. \"I wouldn't go anywhere in this.\""}, {"md": "And she keeps picking, though the railing there is down to bare iron."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I walk fast and don't stop once. I go past the bench by the monument without looking at it, though my legs are begging to sit. I get to the dorm at half past four, with white cheeks and, it seems to me, no toes. The toes come back later, and that hurts more than the frost."}], "B": [{"md": "The driver takes all two hundred rubles and still grumbles that in frost like this nobody drives for less than three hundred. For nine days till my stipend I eat instant noodles and borrow stewed meat from Tagir. Tagir gives it and says nothing, and that's more shameful."}], "C": [{"md": "\"Can I wait it out at your place till morning?\" I ask Lena. \"I won't make it to Lenin Avenue.\""}, {"md": "Lena laughs and says, \"About time.\" We drink tea in her kitchen until six in the morning, and for the first time since December I fall asleep with the light off."}], "D": [{"md": "I listen to the chill at the back of my neck and don't go through Lagerny Garden; I take the long way along the avenue, where windows are lit and the occasional car goes by. It comes out two kilometers longer. I never do look at the bench by the monument, not that night, not later."}], "E": [{"md": "I walk through Lagerny Garden counting my steps. Over forty below, the streetlights standing in rust-colored fog, the bench by the monument white with frost and empty. After a kilometer my feet stop feeling cold, and I know that's bad. Somewhere in another life I got warm just like this among boxes of chicks in cowboy hats, and I sat down. Here I don't sit down. I rub my ears with my mitten until they start to hurt again, and walk on."}, {"md": "By the bench I want to sit so badly that I stop and look at it like a dog that might bite. Then I walk past. I get to the dorm on Lenin Avenue a little after four, with white cheeks and fingers that won't bend until morning. Auntie Galya at the front desk pours me tea from her own thermos and scolds me. And the streetlights in Lagerny Garden, when I looked back at them from the edge of the bluff, weren't standing where they'd stood: they were one span further to the left."}], "F": [{"md": "Lyokha's dad's felt boots are two sizes too big for me, and the galoshes slap against my heels. Lyokha watches me put them on and says, \"What are you, Samokha, an old man?\" I carry my sneakers in a bag from the Yarche supermarket."}, {"md": "In the park I walk along the very edge, but the felt boots grip the ice like paws. At the turn by the bench I stop and look down for a long time at the white riverbed with steam rising off it. Then I walk on, holding on to the bushes, though I don't need to."}, {"md": "I get to the dorm at half past four. I can't feel my feet, but they're mine. I return the felt boots to Lyokha a week later, washed, with a bottle of Sibirskaya Korona beer in each one."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Walk through Lagerny Garden. Six kilometers is nothing"}, "B": {"label": "Call a taxi with my last two hundred rubles"}, "C": {"label": "Ask Lena if I can stay over"}, "D": {"label": "Listen to the chill at the back of my neck and don't go past that bench"}, "E": {"label": "\"I've fallen asleep in the cold when it suddenly gets warm. Not in this life.\" Walk through the park, never once sit down, and rub my ears the moment they stop hurting"}, "F": {"label": "\"I remember my soles sliding.\" Borrow Lyokha's dad's felt boots and walk through the park"}}}, "В2-04": {"title": "The Tuning Fork", "date": "April 2009", "photo_caption": "The department. Room 218", "text": [{"md": "In April I go to the department's open seminar on quantum measurement. Nobody invites second-years, but nobody throws them out either. Professor Lunyov runs it, the one from the card on the noticeboard. He speaks quietly, holds the chalk like a cigarette, and keeps rubbing his eyes. At the second session I finally remember where I've seen him."}, {"md": "City Hospital No. 2, the children's ward, July of '96. A gray suit, a briefcase, two test tubes of blood. \"What did you hear down there?\""}, {"md": "After the seminar he says, \"Samokhin, stay a moment.\" We go up to 218. He locks the door, switches on the kettle and sits down across from me, so close I can see how red his eyes are."}, {"md": "Under the glass on his desk lies a yellowed clipping from the *Kuzbass*. Half a column about a boy from Rudnichny looking for the grandpa in the striped shirt. My notice. So this man has kept it under his hand for thirteen years.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т03"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЗАМЕТКА"}}}, {"md": "\"On the ninth of December you got stuck in the freight elevator with forty liters of nitrogen,\" he says. \"Zoya Mikhailovna says you climbed right up under the ceiling before the mist reached your knees. How did you know nitrogen collects at the floor?\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В202"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СТОП"}}}, {"md": "\"On the ninth of December you got stuck in the freight elevator with forty liters of nitrogen,\" he says. \"The mechanic says you pressed Stop before you climbed into the gap. The red mushroom button, painted over along with the panel. Nobody had touched it in thirty years, not even Zoya Mikhailovna, and she rides that elevator every day. How did you know the elevator was live?\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В202"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СТОП"}}}, {"md": "\"Read it somewhere.\""}, {"md": "\"Where?\""}, {"md": "I say nothing. The kettle roars and clicks off."}, {"md": "\"First you sat on the floor for a while,\" Lunyov says quietly. \"Didn't you?\"", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "seen", "id": "✝В2-02.A", "life": true}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В202"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СТОП"}}]}}, {"md": "\"You'd already died in there once,\" Lunyov says quietly. \"And then you climbed up under the ceiling. Didn't you?\"", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "not", "a": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В2-02.A", "life": true}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В202"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СТОП"}}]}}, {"md": "\"You'd already climbed into that gap once,\" Lunyov says quietly. \"Without the Stop. Didn't you?\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В202"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СТОП"}}}, {"md": "The cup in my hand starts rattling against the saucer, and I set it down on the table so he won't see."}, {"md": "He pulls out a drawer and lays an old steel tuning fork in front of me, dark with age, with a worn inscription on the stem: \"Miners' House of Culture. Rudnichny Choir.\" Then he strikes it on the edge of the desk and presses the stem to my temple."}, {"md": "The fork hums, steady, on one note, right into the bone. And under the hum, quietly, as if through water, I hear them. The crowded whisper from under the snag. That same one, from '96. They're counting."}, {"md": "I jerk so hard I knock over my cup. Lunyov nods, as if I'd answered a question."}, {"md": "\"A hammer hummed like that,\" I say, I don't know why. \"A geologist's hammer, on a long birch handle. Right by my shoulder, on some bald peak.\"", "if": {"op": "lived", "id": "В3-02"}}, {"md": "Lunyov raises his eyes to me and is silent for a long time.", "if": {"op": "lived", "id": "В3-02"}}, {"md": "\"A yellow handle, polished smooth?\" he asks. \"Tikhon used to say geologists always have a tuning fork in their hand, only they don't know it. How do you know about the hammer, Andrei?\"", "if": {"op": "lived", "id": "В3-02"}}, {"md": "I don't know how. I've never held a hammer like that.", "if": {"op": "lived", "id": "В3-02"}}, {"md": "\"In the hospital you told me, 'They were counting,'\" he says. \"For thirteen years I've been thinking about those three words.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ХОР"}}}, {"md": "He points to a photo above the desk. A miners' choir in a House of Culture, men in identical jackets, and in front of them a man with his hand raised. In his hand is a tuning fork."}, {"md": "\"Your grandfather. Tikhon Savelyevich Samokhin. On Saturdays the choirmaster in a miners' choir, on weekdays a physicist. I was his grad student. He used to say every person has a tuning fork in their head. In most people it stays silent all their life. But if you strike it, it sounds. He struck his own in '61, in an ice hole on the Ob Sea, on Gagarin's day. That's why the project was called Tuning Fork.\""}, {"md": "Granddad's lab was underground, Lunyov says, at level minus six hundred forty, under Krasnaya Gorka. At the same Glubokaya mine where my father has been cutting coal for twenty years without knowing what's under his feet."}, {"md": "\"'The observer does not see his own death. Tested,'\" I say suddenly. \"It was in his notebook, in the margin.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}}, {"md": "Lunyov sits without moving for a long time, and then laughs with his shoulders only, almost silently, and can't stop.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}}, {"md": "\"And he called the voices the Choir,\" says Lunyov. \"A choirmaster, what do you expect. The Choir is you, Andrei. All the yous that died where you chose differently. They don't go anywhere. Their last minute flows into the one who survived. Into you.\""}, {"md": "He takes a Mayak reel-to-reel tape recorder out of the cupboard and threads a Svema reel. His hands aren't shaking at all now, like a man who has done this many times."}, {"md": "\"The twenty-eighth of January, '86,\" he says. \"He called me from below, from the level. I was standing up top by the shaft, recording everything that came over the line.\""}, {"md": "He presses a key. Crackle, hum, the distant ring of dripping water. Then a voice, tired and even, like a man who hasn't slept in a long time:"}, {"md": "\"Vadim. Synchronize watches. Four-twelve. I don't know how many times I've been here now, I've lost count. I can't get them out, Vadim, not one of them. I can hear them. They're singing. It's all me… shut it down…\""}, {"md": "After that, only noise. Lunyov doesn't turn off the recorder until the tape runs out and the end starts slapping against the reel."}, {"md": "\"Six dead, and he was the seventh,\" he says. \"He was trying to get everyone out. He went back, into the same night, again and again, until he lost count himself. It was his forty-first experiment, and he didn't come back from it. The report said 'sudden gas outburst.'\""}, {"md": "He takes off his glasses and rubs the bridge of his nose for a long time."}, {"md": "\"For twenty years I've been thinking about what it was like for him,\" he says. \"For us, he died. But he never once saw himself dead; nobody sees their own death. Somewhere he got out of that chamber, Andrei. Only for him to survive there, everything around him had to turn out differently, in small ways and big ones, over and over. That world is so unlikely and so unlike ours that your grandmother and your father probably aren't in it. He got out somewhere nobody was waiting for him.\""}, {"md": "Then he looks at me over his glasses and says what, it seems, he called me in for:"}, {"md": "\"I've been waiting for you, Andrei. Thirteen years. You've died already, haven't you? Recently?\""}, {"md": "I nod. I can't say it out loud."}, {"md": "He notices the watch on my wrist. I've worn it since '96; it's stopped at four-twelve, and in the dorm they laugh at me for it. Lunyov presses his palm to his chest. Then, without a word, he pulls out another drawer and lays a second one beside it. The same Shturmanskie watch with a little star on the dial, even the same scratch on the glass.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧАСЫ"}}}, {"md": "\"This is his,\" says Lunyov. \"He took it off before he went down and gave it to me; in the lab you had to take off anything metal. I've kept it with me for twenty-three years. I could never bring myself to wear it.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧАСЫ"}}}, {"md": "Only mine is worn as if it had been worn sixty years longer. He turns his over; on the back is an engraving: \"Synchronize watches. 12.04.61.\" The twelfth of April, '61. Gagarin's day. Turn the day and month around and you get 04:12. I turn mine over. The same words.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧАСЫ"}}}, {"md": "\"Synchronize watches,\" Lunyov says in Granddad's words, and his voice goes hoarse.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧАСЫ"}}}, {"md": "He'd waited thirteen years for me. What for, exactly, I found out much later, and I'd have been better off not knowing back then, at twenty.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "\"Teach me,\" I say."}, {"md": "Lunyov nods as if he'd expected no other answer, and puts the tuning fork in my palm. \"Keep it. It's half a century old and still holds its pitch.\" Then he writes a list of books on a sheet of paper. Half of them in English, one handwritten, in Granddad's hand, a photocopy."}, {"md": "At the end of May I call Dad and tell him where I spend my evenings now. Dad listens, and then says, \"Don't go there, Andryukha. Granddad did.\" And hangs up. We don't talk until the fall."}], "B": [{"md": "\"You're sick,\" I say, and go to the door."}, {"md": "He doesn't stop me; he only says to my back, \"The door's locked. The key's in it.\""}, {"md": "For a week I skip the seminar. And on Friday night I wake up at four-twelve because the radiator is humming. Steady, on one note, like the tuning fork at my temple. Under the hum somebody is counting. On Monday I knock on 218 myself."}, {"md": "\"Let's go again,\" I say. \"From the beginning.\""}], "C": [{"md": "I go out into the hallway and call Dad on my cell, with the last money on it. I tell him everything, getting it tangled, jumping around. Dad is silent on the line for a long time; I can hear him smoking."}, {"md": "\"Don't go there, Andryukha,\" he says at last. \"Granddad did.\""}, {"md": "For the first time in his life, he's brought up Granddad himself. I go back into 218, and Lunyov, without asking anything, pours me fresh tea."}], "D": [{"md": "\"Was it you standing by the backwater every July?\""}, {"md": "Lunyov takes off his glasses and wipes them for a long time."}, {"md": "\"The buoy keeper told you?.. Yes. My son, Mitya. '88, nine years old. Went under by the snag; they never found the body. As long as there's no body…\" He doesn't finish. \"All right. Drink your tea.\""}, {"md": "From that day on he talks to me differently, like someone who knows something about him that nobody else knows."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "\"Teach me\""}, "B": {"label": "\"You're sick.\" Get up and leave"}, "C": {"label": "Go out into the hallway and call Dad"}, "D": {"label": "\"Was it you standing by the backwater every July?\""}}}, "В2-05": {"title": "Electrodes", "date": "October 2011", "photo_caption": "Akademgorodok. EEG, 128 channels", "text": [{"md": "Two and a half years have gone by. For the second year now I've been living in two cities. All week I study in Tomsk, and on Wednesday evenings I climb onto an upper side berth in the night train to Novosibirsk, with somebody's socks right in my face, and from there I go on to Akademgorodok. Lunyov has made an arrangement with the Institute of Physiology, and now my head is being studied for real. Every Thursday they sit me in a chair, put a cap with a hundred and twenty-eight electrodes on me and ask me to \"remember something unpleasant.\" I remember the elevator. Something flashes on the monitor, and the lab assistants exchange looks."}, {"md": "The cap is put on me by a grad student with short hair and a face like a strict vice principal. She adjusts the electrode behind my ear, leans in and says quietly:"}, {"md": "\"Two hundred and fifty-two, Samokhin. Hold still.\""}, {"md": "That's when I recognize Nika."}, {"md": "Five years ago there was the dacha and the sunrise, and then the send-off I didn't go to. After that we both pretended for a long time that nothing had happened. She seems to be keeping it up even now; she just takes longer than she needs to with the electrode behind my ear.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "Of the living, two people know about the dark under the water: Lunyov and her. I told her myself, on the bank, when we were thirteen. So she understands what we're measuring here better than anyone in this lab.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т06"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТАЙНА_НИКИ"}}}, {"md": "I remember Vitka and me cracking up about \"the Novosibirsk girl\" at the backwater. Judging by the look she gives me, so does she.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т06"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПАЦАНЫ"}}}, {"md": "In the evening we stand on the steps of the institute. Squirrels are jumping around the birches by the steps, as cheeky as old women at a front desk. Nika smokes a thin cigarette and asks, without looking at me:"}, {"md": "\"What are they doing to you, Andrei? Honestly.\""}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I tell her everything, from the elevator to Granddad's lab under Krasnaya Gorka. Nika hears me out to the end and doesn't interrupt once. Her cigarette burns down to the filter, and she doesn't notice."}, {"md": "\"I knew you didn't just climb out that time for nothing,\" she says at last."}, {"md": "From that evening on we see each other more than just on Thursdays."}], "B": [{"md": "\"Measuring,\" I say. \"You're measuring too.\""}, {"md": "Nika nods, stubs out her cigarette on the trash can and goes back into the building. After that it's all business: Thursday, the cap, a hundred and twenty-eight electrodes, \"thank you, Samokhin, you're free to go.\" Sometimes it seems to me she wants to ask something, but she never does."}], "C": [{"md": "We walk around Akademgorodok until three in the morning, from Morskoy Prospekt all the way to the Ob Sea and back. I tell her about half of it; the rest, it seems, she works out on her own."}, {"md": "At her dorm she says, \"You haven't changed, Samokhin. You've just gotten better at lying.\" And kisses me on the cheek, quickly, as if she were stamping a document."}], "D": [{"md": "In the morning I go to Lunyov and ask him to take Nika off the project. I say it's dangerous to be near me, and I believe it myself."}, {"md": "A week later she's transferred to another lab. She finds out who asked, and after that she nods to me in the hallway like a neighbor on the landing."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Tell her everything: the elevator, Granddad, Tuning Fork"}, "B": {"label": "\"Measuring. You're measuring too.\" Keep my distance: she's a grad student, I'm a test subject"}, "C": {"label": "\"Let's go for a walk, I'll tell you everything on the way.\" And walk until late at night"}, "D": {"label": "Next morning ask Lunyov to take her off the project: it's dangerous to be near me"}}}, "В2-06": {"title": "Gleb", "date": "November 2014", "photo_caption": "Gleb at the seminar. The last photo where he's laughing", "text": [{"md": "Three more years go by on trains between Tomsk and Akademgorodok. I defend my thesis, become Lunyov's grad student, and turn into the strangest grad student in the department. Lunyov now has two \"instruments\": me and Gleb Rostotsky."}, {"md": "In November Sonka comes to Tomsk for two days, for a chemistry olympiad. She's sixteen, with bangs over her eyes and a backpack full of tangerines from Mom. I give her my bed and sleep on the floor on my jacket. In the evening she sits on the windowsill, looking at the Tom, and says she'll never be a physicist. \"I'm going to medical school,\" she says. \"You're always climbing where you shouldn't. Somebody in this family has to know how to resuscitate.\""}, {"md": "Gleb is twenty-four, from Leninsk-Kuznetsky, thin and sharp as a compass, and about three times smarter than me. At nineteen he was in a crash on the highway near Yurga. His mother was beside him, in the passenger seat. They got him breathing again after six minutes; they couldn't save her. Lunyov says Gleb's grandfather was entered in the \"Registry of the Lucky\" back in my grandfather's day. Granddad kept a list like that: people who survived where nobody survives. \"Your father's in there too,\" says Lunyov. \"Number one hundred seventeen. And in the margin, in Granddad's hand: 'Do not recruit. Mine.'\" Gleb doesn't like to hear about it."}, {"md": "He hears the Choir, like me, only in snatches."}, {"md": "He hears it, to be honest, better than I do. I only hear my Choir clearly when Lunyov presses the tuning fork to my temple, like that time in 218.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "He's found his own way to live with it: he's looking for a world where his mother is alive."}, {"md": "In November he shows me an Excel spreadsheet on his laptop. Dates, places, marks: \"rewind 1,\" \"rewind 2,\" forty rows in all. Behind every row is a neighboring world where they buried Gleb."}, {"md": "\"I'm getting closer,\" he says, and smiles in a way that makes me want to take his laptop away. \"Yesterday in Kemerovo… well, in Shcheglovsk… oh, never mind. It doesn't matter. I need to go further, Andrei. You know how to go further. Teach me.\""}, {"md": "My city is called Shcheglovsk only in history textbooks: in 1932 it was renamed Kemerovo."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I teach him what I learned myself: don't chase the voices away, listen, tell one from another. Gleb writes it all down in a notebook in tiny handwriting and nods. A month later he says he can hear \"the one who's driving.\" I don't ask who he means."}], "B": [{"md": "\"You can't bring back the dead, Gleb,\" I say. \"Not in any world.\""}, {"md": "He closes the laptop, thanks me politely and leaves. The next day he says hello to me as if nothing had happened. That's worse than if he'd been offended."}], "C": [{"md": "I go to Lunyov and tell him about the spreadsheet. That same evening Lunyov takes Gleb's lab keys away and forbids him to work until spring."}, {"md": "Gleb finds out who did this to him and stops talking to me."}], "D": [{"md": "I take him to a psychiatrist at the regional hospital, almost by force. Gleb doesn't resist; he just looks out the window of the minibus and says nothing. The doctor prescribes pills. For six months he takes them faithfully, and for six months I think it's all turned out all right."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Teach him to listen to the Choir"}, "B": {"label": "\"You can't bring back the dead, Gleb.\" Say no"}, "C": {"label": "Tell Lunyov about the spreadsheet"}, "D": {"label": "Take him to a doctor, by force if I have to"}}}, "В2-07": {"title": "The Protocol", "date": "March 2016", "photo_caption": "Lunyov. Level 640, after pumping", "text": [{"md": "In March of '15 Gleb didn't come back. One morning he just didn't show up to the seminar, and by lunchtime the police called. I'm not going to say any more about it."}, {"md": "I taught him to listen. Every night when I can't sleep, I remember him nodding and writing it down.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В206"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПОМОГ"}}}, {"md": "Lunyov took his keys away and forbade him to work. It didn't stop Gleb, and Lunyov knows that better than I do.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В206"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СДАЛ"}}}, {"md": "He'd stopped the pills a month before. People told me I'd done everything right; it didn't make it any easier.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В206"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВРАЧ"}}, "fate": true}, {"md": "All that year without Gleb, Lunyov hardly sleeps. His hair has gone white, and he spends nights in the department, on a camp bed behind a cabinet. The kettle in 218 never gets cold anymore."}, {"md": "In March Lunyov takes me to Kemerovo. Under Krasnaya Gorka, at the Glubokaya, they're opening up a mothballed level for him. The money came from some foundation called Siberian Resonance. Its curator, a polite man of nearly seventy in a good coat, shakes my hand and calls me \"a young man with an interesting heredity.\" His hand is dry and warm, and he holds mine a second longer than he needs to, as if taking my pulse. His name is Shadrin."}, {"md": "The cage goes down six hundred and forty meters. My ears pop somewhere around four hundred; a lit landing floats past in the dark, with two men in helmets smoking on it and not looking at us. Down below it's warm as a banya; the rock gives off heat here, and it took six months to pump the water out of the old drift. Granddad's chamber sits on a rise; the water never reached it, only silt dried by the far wall. Lunyov stands over it for half an hour without a word. The chamber is small, lined with lead, and its walls are frosted, because a new refrigeration unit is humming in the corner. On the floor stands a medical cooling bed, still in its plastic, and next to it a heart-lung machine that looks like a Soviet washing machine."}, {"md": "On the wall, in almost-erased chalk: \"41. 28.01.86. 04:12.\" I look at it and hear the crackle of the Svema tape in 218 and that tired voice: \"Synchronize watches. Four-twelve.\" Lunyov runs a finger under the numbers without touching the chalk."}, {"md": "\"Forty-one,\" he says."}, {"md": "I look at my watch. As always it says four-twelve, and here, down below, for the first time in my life it doesn't seem funny.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧАСЫ"}}}, {"md": "\"Your grandfather failed because he started as an adult,\" says Lunyov. His voice is even, like a man who has rehearsed this a hundred times. \"Your tuning fork was struck in childhood. Here's the protocol. Eighteen degrees, twelve minutes of arrest, start at four-twelve. At that hour the body is at its coldest, and the fork is easiest to hear. That was the only time your grandfather went down. If it works, you'll know everything you only hear now.\""}, {"md": "\"And if it doesn't?\""}, {"md": "\"Then you'll come back.\" He looks at me over his glasses. \"You always come back.\""}, {"md": "Hospitals do this too, only they cool you for hours, through a machine, with a team of surgeons standing around. Here it will be Lunyov, the machine and me. According to every textbook, you can't do this. Lunyov says Granddad didn't read textbooks; he wrote them."}, {"md": "Somewhere above us, one level up, a shift is working right now, and Dad is there. He doesn't know I'm here. If he did, he wouldn't let me."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "\"All right,\" I say."}, {"md": "Lunyov nods, takes the plastic off the bed and sets a date: the fourteenth of April, four-twelve. For a month I sleep badly and wake up every night at exactly the right time."}], "B": [{"md": "\"All right,\" I say. \"But Nika will be at the monitor.\""}, {"md": "Lunyov winces, but agrees. Nika comes from Novosibirsk a week early, spends half a day silently reading the protocol, and then says, \"You're both sick.\" And stays."}], "C": [{"md": "\"No,\" I say, and walk to the cage."}, {"md": "Lunyov doesn't argue and doesn't even turn around. I go up alone, and all the way, six hundred and forty meters, I hear the refrigeration unit humming down there with him."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Agree"}, "B": {"label": "Agree, but Nika will be standing at the monitor"}, "C": {"label": "Refuse and go back up"}}}, "В2-08": {"title": "Twelve Minutes", "date": "14 April 2016, 04:12", "photo_caption": "The monitor. Minute nine", "text": [{"md": "They cool me for four hours, through the machine. The cold comes from below, from the bed, and rises up my spine like water in a bath. Thirty-two degrees, thirty, twenty-six. Lunyov reads out the numbers in an even dispatcher's voice. At twenty-eight my teeth start chattering; at twenty-four they stop, and that's worse."}, {"md": "Nika stands at the monitor in a jacket over her lab coat, keeping her fingers on my wrist, though there are sensors for that. \"If anything goes wrong, I'm pulling you out,\" she said up top. \"I don't care about the protocol.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В207"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "\"Eighteen,\" says Lunyov."}, {"md": "The monitor takes one long note, steady, at one pitch, like the tuning fork at my temple. My heart stops, as promised. It's nothing like the elevator. I'm simply where I was at seven, in the warm, crowded dark."}, {"md": "Only now I can tell the voices apart. The one from the elevator. And many I don't know. Somebody coughing like Dad after a shift, somebody wheezing like an old man. The quietest voice is a child's. It says:"}, {"md": "\"You're living on my four minutes. Remember?\""}, {"md": "My palm starts aching along the scar, from the thumb to the wrist, though I don't have a palm here."}, {"md": "In the ninth minute, outside, muffled, as if through water, the monitor starts beeping, and it's beeping wrong. Lunyov is shouting something. The voices around me get louder and come closer."}, {"md": "A sentence from Granddad's notebook suddenly comes back to me: \"The observer does not see his own death. Tested.\" Granddad got one word wrong. The observer doesn't need to see anything. He needs to listen.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}}, {"md": "Somebody takes my hand with cold fingers, and I can't tell whether it's outside or in here."}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "I turn my back on the voices and tear toward where the monitor is beeping outside. It's like surfacing from a great depth when the air ran out halfway up."}, {"md": "Lunyov sees the spike on the EEG and stops the protocol. They warm me through the machine for two hours. My heart restarts on the third shock."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Stay to the end and hear it all"}, "B": {"label": "Tear myself back toward the light. Lunyov will see it on the EEG and stop the session"}, "C": {"label": "Go toward the thinnest voice, the child's"}}}, "В2.1-00": {"title": "The Standard", "date": "14 April 2016, morning", "photo_caption": "The first morning after", "text": [{"md": "The machine warms me for three hours, and my heart restarts in the eleventh minute, on the first shock. I open my eyes and know that Lunyov is about to drop his glasses, and he drops them. I know that in four seconds the bulb over the door will flicker. In four seconds it flickers."}, {"md": "The Choir speaks clearly now, one at a time. I can tell each of them apart, like classmates at roll call in School No. 41. There's the one from the elevator, there's the seven-year-old, there's somebody with Dad's cough.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 60}}}, {"md": "The Choir speaks now, though from far off, like a radio in the next room. I can't make out the words yet, but I can already tell how many there are.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 60}}}, {"md": "Nika is sitting on the floor of the chamber, leaning against the lead wall, crying without wiping her face. Then she says, \"You were dead for eleven minutes, Samokhin. I counted.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В207"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "Lunyov gropes around the floor for a long time, finds his glasses, puts them on and stares at the monitor for a long time. On the monitor a steady note runs on, forty hertz, and it isn't going to fade."}, {"md": "\"It's ringing,\" he says in a whisper. \"Tikhon Savelyevich, it's ringing.\""}, {"md": "He isn't saying it to me."}, {"md": "They bring me up in the cage wrapped in a wool blanket, like a fire victim. My ears pop at four hundred, the same as on the way down. Through the hum of the cable the Choir keeps talking, and one voice I hear separately. This me, right now, this very minute, is behind the wheel somewhere in fog, on a highway, and shouting one word: \"Truck!\" Then he stops shouting."}, {"md": "Shadrin is waiting for me at the cage. He takes my palm in both of his, like a politician meeting voters, and holds it until I stop shaking."}, {"md": "\"What are you hearing right now, Andrei Nikolayevich?\""}, {"md": "\"One of me just crashed his car,\" I say. My tongue moves badly after the cold. \"Just now. There's fog.\""}, {"md": "Shadrin takes a leather-bound notebook and a gold pen out of his inside pocket. He looks at his watch and writes down the time. He finds nothing strange in what I've said."}, {"md": "\"Where?\""}, {"md": "\"I don't know. He didn't have time to look.\""}, {"md": "\"Next time let him have time,\" says Shadrin, and puts the notebook away. \"There's a place outside Moscow where you're expected, Andrei Nikolayevich. A lab, an apartment, a salary your father never saw in his whole life. Vadim Petrovich is coming with you as your supervisor. The plane is at nine tomorrow. You're going to have a very interesting life now.\""}, {"md": "The words \"let him have time\" only reach me later, in the bathhouse by the pithead, under a hot shower, when my teeth stop chattering."}, {"md": "Shadrin was sixty-eight then. That was how he held the hand of everyone he bought. I found that out later.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "In the morning I'm sitting in the back seat of Shadrin's black car, and through the tinted glass the Glubokaya headframe and the garages on Shakhtyorov Street float away. At the airport Shadrin buys me coffee and a copy of the *Kuzbass*, like an uncle seeing his nephew off to the army."}, {"md": "On the plane I fall asleep, for the first time in months without dreams, and wake up over Moscow because one of mine in the Choir is going through the April ice on the Ob Sea. Lunyov sits across the aisle and doesn't sleep at all. Every half hour he touches the inside pocket of his jacket, the way you touch a ticket."}, {"md": "I call Mom from Moscow. She asks when I'll be back. I say soon, and that day I believe it myself."}], "B": [{"md": "\"I'm not going without Nika,\" I say."}, {"md": "Shadrin nods at once, without a second's thought. \"Of course. We need a neuroscientist with clearance anyway.\" I'm standing in a blanket by the cage, and I get colder than I was down below."}, {"md": "Nika steps out of the cage behind me and hears the last words. \"You didn't even ask me, Samokhin,\" she says. Then she takes the empty tea cup out of my hands and throws it in the trash. \"I'm coming. Somebody has to count for you.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В207"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "I call Nika in Novosibirsk from the locker room, on the duty officer's phone. She's silent for a long time, then asks why my voice sounds like I've spent the night lying in a snowdrift. I tell her everything, getting it tangled. \"I'm coming,\" she says. \"Somebody has to count for you.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В207"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}], "C": [{"md": "\"First to my father,\" I say. \"Then wherever you say.\""}, {"md": "\"A week,\" Shadrin answers, and smiles. \"Family is sacred.\""}, {"md": "Dad sits in the kitchen in Rudnichny, smoking out the vent window and listening to me without interrupting. When I mention the man in the good coat who holds your hand longer than he needs to, he stubs out his cigarette on a saucer, though it's only half smoked."}, {"md": "\"Dry sort of fellow?\" he asks. \"He used to come see your granddad, in '80, in '85. Grandma gave him tea. He didn't have gray hair then.\" Dad looks out the vent window at the headframe for a long time. \"Well, if they're paying, go. Only call your mother, Andryukha. Call every week.\""}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Go with Shadrin to the institute"}, "B": {"label": "Go, but only with Nika"}, "C": {"label": "\"First to my father, in Kemerovo. Then wherever you say\""}}}, "В2.1-01": {"title": "The Oracle", "date": "14 March 2017, 04:12", "photo_caption": "Site Nine. The basement of building two", "text": [{"md": "Outside Moscow they keep me at Sosnovka. It's a former departmental sanatorium in a pine forest, forty kilometers past the ring road. A concrete fence with barbed wire on top, buildings of yellow brick, an empty pool with peeling dolphins on the bottom. The sign at the gate says NKTs-9, and everybody here just calls it Site Nine. The money came from Siberian Resonance, and it shows. The canteen serves salmon, and in the basement of building two stands a chamber lined with lead, exactly like Granddad's at six hundred forty, only with air conditioning."}, {"md": "Sessions three times a week, at four-twelve. They don't take me as far as cardiac arrest anymore; thirty-two degrees is enough. Since the Glubokaya the Choir talks anyway; you only have to listen."}, {"md": "Listening with me is an operator, Polina, a girl from Podolsk with a stopwatch around her neck. Her voice is like a dispatcher's at a bus station. Lunyov sits in the corner on a chair, watching the EEG. On the first day he brought me a thick notebook with an oilcloth cover, ruled by hand into columns: number, date, time, place, how, what I heard."}, {"md": "\"Your grandfather kept it like this,\" he said. \"Give them whatever you want, but write everything here.\""}, {"md": "Nika sits at the second monitor and doesn't look my way. It's easier for both of us.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "Today is the first real assignment. Shadrin dictated it over the phone, and Polina reads it off a sheet: \"Follow the voice to the end. Bring back the place and time.\""}, {"md": "I find the voice fast. It's one of mine, older, with a rattle in his chest. In his world I must have stayed in the Kuzbass and gone down the mine. He's lying in a drift face down in wet coal grit, and there's nothing to breathe. There are other men nearby; I hear them through him, like neighbors through a wall. Somebody is calling the foreman by his patronymic, Mikhalych, Mikhalych, and nobody answers."}, {"md": "\"Forty minutes,\" I say. How I know that, I don't understand myself. For him it's happening now, and here it will happen in forty minutes. \"A mine. Gas.\""}, {"md": "\"Which mine?\" Polina clicks her stopwatch. \"Andrei Nikolayevich, which mine?\""}, {"md": "He doesn't remember the name; he has other things on his mind. You can only find out by going with him to his last breath and looking through his eyes at the sign by the crosscut, if he looks there at all. My body on the bed starts twitching. I feel it from far away, like somebody else's. Lunyov gets up from his chair."}, {"md": "Forty minutes. Later they explained to me that in forty minutes you can get a shift out of a mine from any level. If you call.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I follow him. It's dark in the drift, my chest squeezed by the same hoop as his. He crawls toward the crosscut, and I crawl with him. On his last breath he raises his head, and in the light of his lamp a tin sign hangs on a prop: \"TALAYA MINE. SECTION 3.\" Then the light goes out for both of us."}, {"md": "I open my eyes on the bed. Polina has her pen ready."}, {"md": "\"Talaya,\" I say. \"Section three. Eight minutes to five.\""}, {"md": "Polina writes it down, goes out into the hallway and makes a call. Through the door I hear: \"Confirming. Four fifty-two.\" She isn't calling the mine. At six in the morning the news reports a methane outburst at the Talaya mine in Leninsk-Kuznetsky, section three, eleven dead. Polina puts a check mark on her sheet. In the evening I write the first line in Lunyov's notebook. In the column \"what I heard,\" I write: \"Mikhalych.\""}], "B": [{"md": "I tear the electrode cap off my head. The wires whip my face, the sensor on my chest comes off with hair. The monitor wails. Polina shouts, \"Andrei Nikolayevich, you can't!\" and I sit on the bed, soaked, shaking all over."}, {"md": "Nika is beside me before security is. She pulls out the last wire, puts her palm on my chest and holds it there until my heart stops racing. She tells security, \"Out. Equipment failure.\" Security goes out.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "At six in the morning the news reports a methane outburst at the Talaya mine in Leninsk-Kuznetsky, eleven dead. I knew about the mine and the forty minutes. I just didn't know which mine, and there are over a hundred in the Kuzbass."}], "C": [{"md": "\"Whose are they?\" I ask without opening my eyes. \"Whose deaths are these?\""}, {"md": "Polina is silent; that question isn't on her sheet. Lunyov answers from the corner."}, {"md": "\"Yours, Andrei. All of them yours. There's nobody in there but you.\""}, {"md": "I understand it slowly, while the Choir withdraws. I only hear the disasters I could have been caught in myself, in one of my worlds. A mine where I could have worked. A highway I could have been driving on. Everything else is mute to me. At six in the morning the news talks about the Talaya mine in Leninsk-Kuznetsky, eleven dead, and Polina writes in the margin of her sheet: \"Instrument limitation: own branches only.\" In the evening, through the wall, I hear Shadrin tell Lunyov on the phone, \"Then we have to move him around more.\""}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Listen to the end"}, "B": {"label": "Rip the wires off my head"}, "C": {"label": "Ask whose deaths these are"}}}, "В2.1-02": {"title": "Numbers", "date": "18 September 2019, 11:00", "photo_caption": "Remezov's office. The whiteboard", "text": [{"md": "In two and a half years I bring them two hundred and ten dates. Shadrin calls them dates, though all I have are minutes: forty, thirty-five, once fifty-two. The Choir never hears further ahead than a little over forty."}, {"md": "Minutes, it turns out, are a commodity too. In the office of the deputy director, Remezov, there's a whiteboard on the wall with columns in marker: number, minute, place, \"probability,\" client. The clients are marked with letters. \"I\" is insurers; forty minutes is enough for them to do something with their paperwork. \"M\" is some people with the stock market. The letter \"X\" Remezov writes with particular pleasure, with a flourish. Once I saw two men in identical jackets with identical haircuts come for that letter."}, {"md": "Warning anybody is not allowed. Remezov explained this to me in the first week, patiently, like to a freshman. If you warn, the disaster doesn't happen, the forecast isn't confirmed, and the client doesn't pay. \"You're a physicist, Andrei Nikolayevich. Measurement changes the system. We simply don't measure anything we don't need to.\""}, {"md": "Lunyov was retired in '18, with a certificate of merit and a bouquet of carnations. He went back to Tomsk and hasn't called since. I've filled his notebook; I'm keeping a second one now, in the same columns."}, {"md": "Shadrin signed Lunyov's certificate. Lunyov, they say, hung it at home in the toilet, facing the door.", "kind": "old"}, {"md": "The sign at the gate now says NITs-9 instead of NKTs-9. The guard says it's always been that way. I've walked past it every day for three years, and I remember every letter.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "On Sunday Mom mentions on the phone, in passing, that Dad quit smoking in '98, \"when you were nine, don't you remember?\" All my life Dad smoked at night on the balcony, at four-twelve, and I fell asleep to the smell of his Primas. I tell Mom I remember.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "Today the Choir spoke up on its own, in the daytime, while I was standing in line in the canteen for meatballs. One of mine is riding a commuter train into Moscow. In his world I must live in this village and commute to work like everyone else. The train slams into a fuel truck that has stalled on a level crossing. The whole first car burns at once. I stand with my tray listening to him scream, and the meatball on my plate gets cold."}, {"md": "My brother names the crossing himself. Kilometer forty-seven, by the platform, six kilometers from our fence. There it happened at eleven. Here it will be at eleven-forty."}, {"md": "Remezov hears me out, nods and writes on the board: \"211. 11:40. Xing km 47.\" After a moment's thought he puts an \"I\" in the last column. Then he picks up the phone and says to me, covering the receiver with his palm:"}, {"md": "\"Thank you, Andrei Nikolayevich. Get some rest.\""}, {"md": "The clock over the board says 11:02."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "Without a word Nika hands me her old push-button phone, the one she keeps only for her mother. \"I don't think they listen to mine,\" she says, and stands by the door with her back to the hallway.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "I borrow a phone from Auntie Zina, the dishwasher. She has a push-button Nokia, the same kind I had in the dorm on Lenin Avenue.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "\"112, go ahead.\""}, {"md": "\"Kilometer forty-seven, the crossing by the platform. At eleven-forty a fuel truck will stall on the tracks. Stop the train.\""}, {"md": "\"Who's calling?\""}, {"md": "I hang up. At eleven-forty nothing happens. In the evening Remezov comes to my room himself, which he's never once done. The train was stopped a kilometer before the crossing, after a call, he says. The fuel truck stalled on the crossing, the driver was pulled out, nobody was hurt. The client is unhappy. Remezov stands in the doorway and looks at me for a long time, like at a thermometer that's lying."}, {"md": "\"You've been getting things mixed up lately, Andrei Nikolayevich. Get some rest.\""}], "B": [{"md": "I go to my room, lie down on top of the blanket and stare at the ceiling. At 11:42 sirens come through the open vent window. They come from the direction of the station one after another, and I count them until I lose track."}, {"md": "In the evening the news reports six dead at the crossing. A bonus lands in my account; I see the text and don't open it. In the notebook I write line number two hundred eleven. I leave the column \"what I heard\" empty."}], "C": [{"md": "I'm allowed outside the fence until six in the evening; Remezov calls it \"a walk.\" The guard at the checkpoint has a bicycle, an old Ural with no fenders. I say I'll bring it back in an hour, and he waves his hand."}, {"md": "Six kilometers along the forest road I pedal so hard I taste iron in my mouth. I reach the crossing at 11:31. The fuel truck is already crawling up to the barrier, slowly, in jerks, its engine sputtering. I drop the bike and stand in front of the hood with my arms spread. The driver slams on the brakes and leans out the window, red-faced."}, {"md": "\"Are you nuts?!\" And another word, the one Mom used to slap me on the mouth for when I was little."}, {"md": "The fuel truck's engine dies right there, twenty meters from the rails. At eleven-forty the train goes by, horn blaring. The driver watches it go and silently crosses himself. In the evening Remezov tells me, without raising his eyes from his papers, \"You went for a bike ride, Andrei Nikolayevich. Around kilometer forty-seven. Don't take a phone with you anymore. No need.\""}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Call 112 anonymously"}, "B": {"label": "Do the job. Remezov's right; I'm an instrument"}, "C": {"label": "Go to the crossing myself"}}}, "В2.1-03": {"title": "Nika Leaves", "date": "March 2021", "photo_caption": "A parcel box from Kemerovo", "text": [{"md": "Dad died in December of '20, of covid, in the regional hospital in Kemerovo. Site Nine was under quarantine then; the gates were shut, and nobody was allowed out."}, {"md": "A week before the hospital Dad called me himself, the first time in a year. He coughed into the phone and talked about nothing much, the garage, how the honey mushrooms came up late this year. At the end he said, \"I sent you a parcel. A storm jacket. It was Grandma's, from her trunk. Must be Granddad's.\" The parcel arrived in January, after he'd been buried. I never opened it. It sits in the closet in its postal box and smells of somebody else's mail."}, {"md": "Sonka called me every evening. She's in her sixth year of medical school and since November has been working in the red zone, in the same building where Dad was."}, {"md": "\"He's waiting for you, Andryukha,\" she said, her voice hoarse from the mask. \"Well, not waiting, he's on a ventilator, unconscious. But I can see it.\""}, {"md": "The Choir was silent about Dad. Dad isn't me, and none of mine ever lay in that building. And his death had been building for thirty years, breath by breath, in the coal dust of the Glubokaya; covid just finished it. A death like that has no minute you can grab on to."}, {"md": "They buried Dad without me. That day I was giving Polina the minutes for when a warehouse near Podolsk would catch fire.", "kind": "old"}, {"md": "Nika has been packing a suitcase since morning. She folds her sweaters neatly, sleeves tucked in, like in a store, and doesn't look at me.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "\"Last night you were telling me about me again,\" she says to a sweater. \"At night, in your sleep. At first I liked it. Then I stopped liking it.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "\"You were talking about me smoking on the steps in Akademgorodok, thin ones, with squirrels jumping in the birches.\" She pulls the zipper, and it sticks at the corner. \"Andrei, I've never smoked in my life. Not once. That wasn't me.\"", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 10}}]}, "drift": true}, {"md": "\"For five years I've been sleeping with a man who has a train station in his head,\" she says. \"At four-twelve you sit up in bed and announce minutes. In somebody else's voice. And in the daytime you look at me like you're checking me against a photograph. I'm going to Novosibirsk, Andrei. Mom's alone.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "In February a letter comes from Nika, on paper, with a Novosibirsk postmark. Her handwriting is round, a top student's, the letters standing straight as if on a ruler.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "\"Andrei. I'm writing on paper because what they say at our institute about your Sosnovka makes me not want to send email there. If even half of it is true, get out of there. I keep dreaming I'm counting at the backwater again and losing my place at the same spot every time. Come. Or write that you won't come, and then I'll stop waiting. N.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "I reread the letter three times. The Choir is silent meanwhile, as if it's reading too.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I carry the suitcase to the checkpoint. At the taxi Nika takes it out of my hands, heavy as it is, and puts it in the trunk herself.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "\"Don't see me any further,\" she says. \"There are cameras.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "The taxi drives off down the forest road. That night, at four-twelve, I sit up in bed and give Polina the minutes, and nobody beside me wakes up.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "I don't answer the letter. I put it in my notebook, between the pages with numbers two hundred eighty and two hundred eighty-one, and close the notebook.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "Nika doesn't write again. Sometimes I take out the letter and check that it's still there, the way you check for your keys in your pocket.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}], "B": [{"md": "\"Stay,\" I say."}, {"md": "I talk for a long time, until morning, sitting on the floor by the bed. About how without her, inside this fence, I'll stop being able to tell my own voice from the others. How she's the only one here who remembers me the way I was before the Glubokaya. Nika listens, hugging her knees, and toward dawn she nods."}, {"md": "She never unpacks the suitcase. It stays under our bed, packed, with the sweaters' sleeves tucked in. We sleep in separate rooms now but have breakfast together, and at breakfast she sometimes touches my hand, checking whether it's warm."}], "C": [{"md": "\"I remember you differently,\" I say. \"I don't know which of you is the real one. Maybe both. Maybe neither.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "Nika stands with the suitcase in her hand for a long time.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "\"Thank you for telling me,\" she says at last. \"So figure that out first, Samokhin. And I'll wait somewhere further away.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "She carries the suitcase to the checkpoint herself.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "I write the answer at night, on a page from my notebook. I write that I won't come. That I remember her not the way she probably is, and every year I remember worse. That the people around me change and I don't notice until they tell me themselves. I give the letter to Auntie Zina to drop in the mailbox in the village.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}, {"md": "A month later the answer comes, one line: \"You're an idiot, Samokhin. I'll wait anyway.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В210"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "С_НИКОЙ"}}}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Let her go"}, "B": {"label": "Talk her into staying"}, "C": {"label": "Tell her the truth: I remember her differently"}}}, "В2.1-04": {"title": "The Water Heater", "date": "14 November 2024, 01:30", "photo_caption": "A kitchen by the station. A Neva water heater", "text": [{"md": "Since '22 I've been allowed to live outside the fence, in the village. Remezov calls it \"the trust regime.\" I rent a one-room apartment on the third floor of a five-story block by the station. The landlady, Tamara Petrovna, has moved in with her daughter in Moscow and left me a rug on the wall, a china cabinet full of crystal, and a Neva gas water heater in the kitchen. The village has no central heating; the water heater warms both the water and the radiators, and it hums all night behind the wall, steady, on one note."}, {"md": "In '22 half the young people left Site Nine. Polina and her husband live in Yerevan now and send me photos of mountains. I look at them for a long time. On those mountains none of mine ever dies."}, {"md": "After sessions I always lie in a hot bath. After thirty-two degrees the cold sits in my bones until lunch, and only boiling water drives it out."}, {"md": "Yesterday, while I was at Site Nine, two men from the gas service came by for a routine inspection. The downstairs neighbor let them in; she has a key the landlady left her. On the phone Tamara Petrovna was very surprised. She hadn't called anybody."}, {"md": "Today I lay in the bath for an hour and a half. It's half past one in the morning now, and my head aches as if a wedge had been driven into my temple, and I'm a little sick to my stomach. The tiles swim when I look at them. That happens after sessions; I'm used to it. I'm sitting on the edge of the couch in my bathrobe, and the pillow seems like the softest thing in the world."}, {"md": "The Choir is quieter than usual tonight. In the neighboring worlds, in this same apartment, three of mine are dying right now, the ones who went to bed half an hour earlier. They aren't screaming or choking. They're asleep, breathing more and more slowly, in time with the water heater behind the wall.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 60}}}, {"md": "On the shelf in the front hall my phone buzzes; the screen says \"Vitya.\" For nine years Vitka has been on the police force in Podolsk, forty kilometers from here, and he calls me on Fridays, cheerful and a little drunk. Today is Thursday, half past one in the morning.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 50}}}, {"md": "At eighteen Vitka dodged the army through Welder, and now he's a major himself. He's always the first to laugh about it.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 50}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}]}}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "I throw open the kitchen window, and November tumbles into the apartment, damp, smelling of fuel oil from the station. I shut the valve on the pipe by the water heater with both hands; it's stiff. The little flame in the blue window goes out. Then I climb to the fifth floor, holding the railing, and ring the doorbell of Uncle Gena, a retired warrant officer I sometimes smoke with on the stairs."}, {"md": "Uncle Gena opens the door in his undershirt with a hammer in his hand, just in case. He doesn't ask anything, makes up the couch for me and covers me with an army blanket. I sleep until nine."}, {"md": "In the morning Uncle Gena is frying eggs and calls into the other room, \"Valya, we're out of bread!\" He's been a widower for six years; we've talked about it on the stairs maybe twenty times. A woman in a bathrobe comes out of the room, puts bread on the table and says to me, \"Andryusha, eat, eat.\" She calls me by my name, though I've never seen her before.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 5}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "In the morning the gas service, the real one, called by Uncle Gena, finds something in the flue that shouldn't be there and neatly writes up a report. And at Site Nine, in the hallway of building two, Shadrin is walking toward me. He stops, which he's never once done in eight years."}, {"md": "\"Sleep well, Andrei Nikolayevich?\""}], "C": [{"md": "\"Andryukha, you awake?\" Vitka is sober today, and that makes me uneasy right away. \"Listen. There's a guy coming up in our reports, a former gas fitter, two prison terms. Yesterday a camera caught him outside your building, with a tool case. There were two of them. I checked: the gas service has no work order for your address. You've got a water heater there, right?\""}, {"md": "\"Yeah,\" I say. My tongue moves like after a session."}, {"md": "\"What's wrong with you? Andryukha! Open the window. Open the window, shut off the gas and go out on the stairs. I'm not hanging up.\""}, {"md": "I do everything he says and sit on the cold steps in my bathrobe, and Vitka talks to me for an hour about fishing on the Pakhra, just so I won't go quiet. In the morning he comes himself, in a police car, looks at the water heater, at the flue, and smokes outside the building for a long time."}, {"md": "\"It wasn't the neighbors,\" he says. \"That's a clean job. It's your people, Andryukha, from behind the fence. Get out of there.\""}, {"md": "\"Nobody gets out of there, Vitya.\""}], "D": [{"md": "I remember lying down on this couch on top of the bedspread. I remember a hand on the blanket that wouldn't lift, and the blue reflections of the water heater on the ceiling. Just looking at the pillow makes me sick."}, {"md": "I shut off the gas, open every window and sit on the stairs until morning, in a jacket over my bathrobe. At eight I go to Site Nine and walk into Shadrin's office without knocking."}, {"md": "Shadrin hears me out to the end, hands folded on the desk, and smiles."}, {"md": "\"So you can hear yourself too,\" he says. \"I confess I had my doubts. An instrument that can't sense its own failure is no use to us. Sit down, Andrei Nikolayevich. Tea?\""}], "E": [{"md": "I remember a banya I've never had: blue flames over the coals, a crimson old man on the bench smiling at the wall, and warm wet boards under my cheek. My head aches exactly the same way, all at once, my temples pounding.", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-04.A", "life": false}}, {"md": "I remember a cave I've never been in: a squeeze passage, tanks on my back, and the air from them, sweet as condensed milk, that made me want to sleep. My head aches exactly the same way, all at once, my temples pounding.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "seen", "id": "✝В5-08.B", "life": false}, {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-04.A", "life": false}}]}}, {"md": "I shut the valve on the pipe by the water heater with both hands; it's stiff. The little flame in the blue window goes out. I throw open the windows in the kitchen and the room, and November tumbles into the apartment, damp, smelling of fuel oil from the station. Until morning I sit on the cold steps in a jacket over my bathrobe, smoking somebody else's cigarettes from a jar on the landing windowsill."}, {"md": "In the morning the gas service finds something in the flue that shouldn't be there. And at Site Nine, in the hallway of building two, Shadrin is walking toward me. He stops, which he's never once done in eight years, and looks at me as if checking me against a photograph."}, {"md": "\"Sleep well, Andrei Nikolayevich?\""}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Go to bed. My head hurts; it'll pass by morning"}, "B": {"label": "Throw the window wide open and go spend the night with the neighbor upstairs"}, "C": {"label": "Pick up the phone. It's Vitka"}, "D": {"label": "\"I've fallen asleep here before\""}, "E": {"label": "\"I know this sweet, heavy air. Not in this life.\" Shut off the gas, open every window and sit on the stairs till morning"}}}, "В2.1-05": {"title": "Antenna", "date": "March 2028", "photo_caption": "Building three. \"Antenna-1\"", "text": [{"md": "Shadrin is eighty and looks sixty. Medicine, they say at Site Nine, and say nothing more. He goes down the stairs to the basement of building three faster than I do."}, {"md": "In February Sonka broke through to Site Nine. She's thirty, a doctor, and she brought me a jar of Mom's meatballs and a blood-pressure monitor. She had no pass, and for half an hour she fought with the guards at the gate so hard that in the end they called me themselves. In the visiting room she took my blood pressure, silently, twice, and silently wrote down the numbers. \"You've lost eight kilos,\" she said at last. \"Andryush. Tell me, did you come here on your own?\" I said I did."}, {"md": "On the door hangs a new plastic sign: \"Antenna-1.\" Inside is a bed with rails, like in intensive care, and IV stands beside it. Over the pillow, on a long boom, hangs a microphone like the ones in radio studios, with a foam cover. Behind a glass wall, a control desk and three chairs with headphones."}, {"md": "\"Have a look, Andrei Nikolayevich,\" says Shadrin. \"This is for you.\""}, {"md": "He explains it calmly, like a realtor showing an apartment. Continuous sedation, body temperature thirty-three. The Choir will speak through my mouth on its own, day and night, no breaks for lunch or sleep. Behind the glass, three transcribers in shifts. Not a single minute will be lost anymore."}, {"md": "\"You won't have to die for them anymore,\" he says. \"You'll sleep. The Choir will do the talking.\""}, {"md": "After eleven years of sessions two fingers on my left hand can't feel the cold, the little finger and the ring finger. I rub them while he talks, and they stay wooden. We never once talk about the water heater. He knows I know, and that's enough for him."}, {"md": "\"You're tired, aren't you?\" says Shadrin. \"I can see it. You haven't had a proper night's sleep in two years. Here you'll always sleep well.\""}, {"md": "He goes upstairs and leaves me alone. I sit down on the edge of the bed. The mattress slowly sinks and adjusts under my back, warm, heated. The microphone over my head hums quietly, like the tuning fork at my temple in room 218. In the Choir, at this minute, one of mine is drowning in the Ob near Barnaul, and another is burning in a car near Tula, and they're all talking at once."}, {"md": "I lie down, just to try it. It really is very comfortable here."}, {"md": "The word \"antenna\" was thought up by the technician who hung the microphone. Shadrin liked it.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "I go upstairs and find Shadrin in the hallway by the window."}, {"md": "\"No,\" I say."}, {"md": "\"Think about it until the fall,\" he answers, without any offense. \"I'm patient.\""}, {"md": "In the evening two men from security come to my one-room apartment by the station, polite, and help me pack. The \"trust regime\" is over. They take my phone and give me a room in building two, behind the fence, with a grille on the window they call \"bird protection.\" The guard carries the parcel box from Kemerovo himself and puts it in my closet without opening it."}], "C": [{"md": "\"I agree,\" I tell Shadrin upstairs. \"Only not right away. Let me finish things with my mother; she's ill.\""}, {"md": "Shadrin looks at me for a long time, then nods. The Antenna, he says, has been decided to be built in Novosibirsk anyway, closer to our coal business and the new clinic, and that's about five years. \"We'll wait. You're not going anywhere, Andrei Nikolayevich.\""}, {"md": "That same evening, in the one-room apartment by the station, I open Dad's parcel for the first time. I don't take the storm jacket out, I only touch the canvas. Under it, at the bottom of the box, I put the first fifty thousand from my bonus. In the notebook, on the last page, I rule Granddad's columns again: number, date, time, place, how. Only now what I'll be writing there is roads."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Agree. Let them do the talking"}, "B": {"label": "Refuse"}, "C": {"label": "Pretend to agree and prepare an escape"}}}, "В2.1-06": {"title": "The R-255", "date": "17 October 2033, 03:20", "photo_caption": "The Siberia highway, somewhere past Bolotnoye", "text": [{"md": "They built Antenna-2 in Novosibirsk, in Akademgorodok, in a new building behind the Institute of Physiology. On those steps Nika once smoked and asked me what they were doing to me. A week ago they flew me here, under guard. The hookup is scheduled for tonight, at four-twelve."}, {"md": "I've been preparing for five years. The money is in Dad's parcel, under the storm jacket. A used Toyota was bought for me through his brother-in-law by Lyosha, a guard I've been bringing pies from the canteen for five years, and it's waiting for me in the garages behind the market. The spare key is in my sock. At two-thirty Lyosha fell asleep at his post, on his own, no pills needed. I tiptoed around him, the way I used to tiptoe around Dad sleeping after a shift.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В215"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПОБЕГ"}}}, {"md": "For five years I said no, and they brought me anyway. They don't ask for consent anymore. At two-thirty Lyosha the driver, going out for a smoke, left the keys in the service UAZ. I walked out through the boiler room with the parcel box under my arm, and nobody stopped me. I guess nobody believed the instrument could walk.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В215"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОТКАЗ"}}}, {"md": "Nika works here too, in the next building, and she's on duty tonight. She opened the last door on the way out with her own pass. \"They won't let two of us out,\" she said. \"Go. I'll come later.\" And she walked back down the corridor without turning her head, so the camera wouldn't see her face.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "The R-255, the Siberia highway. From Novosibirsk to Yurga, two hundred kilometers, straight as a ruler. At three in the morning there's nobody on it but semis. After Bolotnoye fog rises from the hollows, and within twenty minutes I'm driving through milk. Visibility about thirty meters. Ahead, in the white, float the red lights of a semi, blurred, like a cigarette in somebody else's hand."}, {"md": "At four-twelve they'll find the empty bed. Then they'll pull the camera footage from the checkpoints. I have to make it to Yurga by five; there's the turn-off for Tomsk, and they won't look for me there."}, {"md": "The parcel box from Kemerovo is on the passenger seat. I hold it with my right hand on the curves, the way you hold a child."}, {"md": "I remember the cage at the Glubokaya, seventeen years ago, and the voice in the Choir shouting \"Truck!\" in the fog."}, {"md": "Now that voice is here again. One of mine, who left two minutes earlier and drove faster, is lying at kilometer one hundred forty-two under the trailer of a semi that's jackknifed across both lanes. \"One forty-two,\" he says. \"Across the road.\" I'm at one thirty-eight now.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 70}}}, {"md": "In the fog the Choir talks indistinctly, all at once, like a train station. The back of my neck goes cold the way it has before every disaster for seventeen years.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 70}}}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "I slow to sixty and stay behind the red lights, about fifty meters back. They lead me through the fog like a lamp in a mine. The semi drives steadily; its driver knows this road better than I do."}, {"md": "At one forty-two the lights suddenly flare brighter and spread apart. The semi stops, it skids, but it stops. I brake twenty meters short of its rear doors. Ahead, in the white, a second semi is standing across the road, and nobody gets out of its cab."}, {"md": "The driver of the first one jumps out with a flashlight and runs over there, swearing so loud I can hear it through the glass. While he's setting up a warning triangle, I drive around both semis on the shoulder, over the wet grass, slowly, and on into the fog toward Yurga. From Yurga a commuter train leaves for Tomsk at 6:10. I leave the car by the station with the key in the ignition."}], "C": [{"md": "At the sign \"Yurga 14\" I turn onto a dirt road through the birches. I drive the car into a hollow, into the bushes, so it can't be seen from the road. I take the box under my arm and go on foot."}, {"md": "Fourteen kilometers across a soggy field in the fog. My shoes are soaked in the first kilometer; by the fifth I can't feel my toes. My knees, which spent seventeen years freezing on a bed, ache at every step. At dawn, muddy to the waist, I come out at the station in Yurga and get on the commuter train to Tomsk, in the last car. I sleep standing in the vestibule, leaning against the door."}], "D": [{"md": "I remember a gray tarp across the road and a beam in front of my face. I remember the box on the seat banging against the glove compartment."}, {"md": "At one forty-one I slow to thirty and turn on the hazards. A minute later a gray wall slowly grows out of the fog. The semi is standing across both lanes, its tarp wet, with the letters \"…TRANS\" on it. I stop five meters from it and sit for a long time with my hands on the wheel. Then I drive around it on the shoulder, over the wet grass. I reach Yurga at half past four and catch the first commuter train to Tomsk."}], "E": [{"md": "I remember the red lights disappearing from the mirror and how many minutes I drove alone through the white. So the oncoming lane is empty. I pull out from behind the semi and pass it at a hundred and twenty, blind, counting roadside posts. At one forty I slam the brakes, a kilometer before the gray wall, and the car slides sideways on the wet asphalt. I stop so close that the headlights are pressing into the letters \"…TRANS.\""}, {"md": "I drive around it on the shoulder, over the grass, and floor it again. The semi I passed stays behind in the fog, and I don't know whether its driver will manage to brake. The Choir is silent about that, and I don't look back. I reach Yurga a little after four."}, {"md": "The station in Yurga is yellow, with white columns. For ten years I rode past it on the commuter train, and it was always brick, red, with a clock over the entrance. The clock is there over the entrance, but I don't remember any columns. A woman with bundles by the ticket window watches me touch a column with my palm and moves her bundles further away.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 10}}, "drift": true}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Floor it before they notice"}, "B": {"label": "Follow the semi and keep my distance"}, "C": {"label": "Turn off onto a dirt road toward Yurga and ditch the car"}, "D": {"label": "\"I've seen this semi before\""}, "E": {"label": "\"I remember where it's lying across the road.\" Pass the semi now, while the oncoming lane is empty, and make the first commuter train"}}}, "В2.1-07": {"title": "Lunyov", "date": "September 2041", "photo_caption": "Kashtak. A care home, the bed by the window", "text": [{"md": "For eight years I've lived in Tomsk under another name. It took six months and all the money I had to get a passport made out to Andrei Tikhonovich Savelyev. Granddad's name was Tikhon Savelyevich, and I couldn't think of anything better. I rent a room with a stove in a wooden house on Tatarskaya Street and fix watches and radios in a tin booth by the Central Market. The Choir helps: if something in a radio is about to burn out, I hear it before the owner does."}, {"md": "Since last winter there have been strangers' voices in the Choir. Strangers' voices, but my pitch. It's like somebody in the next stairwell playing the same tape you have, and you hear it through the wall, half a beat behind. There aren't many of them yet, and they're all far off to the west."}, {"md": "For all eight years I deliberately didn't look for Lunyov, so as not to lead Site Nine to him. In August a frail old lady in a beret brings an old Okean radio to my booth to be fixed. She doesn't recognize me, but I recognize her at once. Zoya Mikhailovna, our senior lab technician, the one who cried by the elevator."}, {"md": "\"Do you remember Professor Lunyov?\" she asks while I open up the radio. \"He's in the care home at Kashtak now. Doesn't recognize anyone. I go to see him on Thursdays; there's nobody else.\""}, {"md": "On Thursday I go instead of her."}, {"md": "Lunyov lies by the window, with pines outside. He's become small, like a teenager, his glasses hanging on a cord. When he sees me, he tries to sit up."}, {"md": "\"Tikhon Savelyevich,\" he says. \"You've gotten old.\""}, {"md": "\"I have, Vadim.\""}, {"md": "\"And Mitya's down at the river.\" Lunyov speaks confidentially, in a low voice, so the orderly won't hear. \"Measuring the water. I'll call him in a minute; he'll show you where it flows. It's flowing the wrong way, Tikhon Savelyevich. It's flowing underground.\""}, {"md": "Then he fumbles under his mattress for a long time and pulls out a stack of oilcloth-covered notebooks held with a pharmacy rubber band. The rubber band is old and snaps in his fingers."}, {"md": "\"These are for you. I've counted it all out. Give them to Andrei, your grandson. He'll come.\""}, {"md": "I take the notebooks. They're warm from lying under him."}, {"md": "\"I've been waiting for him,\" says Lunyov, and closes his eyes."}, {"md": "He was eighty-five. At the funeral there were three of us: Zoya Mikhailovna, the orderly, and me, Savelyev.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I sit down on the chair by his bed and open the top notebook. The handwriting is small and even, like in a lab log. On the first page, a year, '88, and one word: \"Mitya.\""}, {"md": "After that, a pencil drawing. The backwater, the snag like a hand, our bank. From the snag a dotted line runs down, under the riverbed, and Lunyov has labeled it neatly, as on a blueprint: \"old adit → level −640 → T. S.'s chamber.\" Beside it, pressed harder: \"The current. It carried him there. He's there, with him.\""}, {"md": "They never found Mitya's body because the river drew him underground, to my grandfather's chamber. I sit over that drawing until the orderly brings Lunyov his supper. I open the last notebook too, but it's nothing but columns of numbers, and I can't make sense of them."}, {"md": "Lunyov dies three weeks later, in his sleep, after lunch."}], "B": [{"md": "I put the notebooks in a bag without opening them. I sit down on the chair by the bed. Lunyov finds my hand on the blanket and holds my finger, the way little kids hold grown-ups on a bus."}, {"md": "Then he starts humming something, quietly, without words. I don't recognize the tune at first. They used to play it from the loudspeakers by the House of Culture in Rudnichny on Miners' Day, and Dad would sing along without knowing the words. Lunyov falls asleep in the middle of a verse, and I sit there until it gets dark."}, {"md": "Lunyov dies three weeks later, in his sleep, after lunch. I put the notebooks in a shoebox and set it on top of the wardrobe."}], "C": [{"md": "\"Vadim Petrovich,\" I say. \"What happened in '96?\""}, {"md": "He opens his eyes. For a minute he looks at me, and I see him recognize me, truly, for the first time that day."}, {"md": "\"Andrei,\" he says. \"City Hospital No. 2. Two test tubes. I didn't take them for tests. In '98, during the default, I sold them. To a foreign foundation; Orpheus, it was called. Along with a copy of your grandfather's archive. With that money I hired divers every summer.\" He rubs his eyes, like he used to, with both hands. \"I sold you, Andrei. You at seven. And I don't even know what they made of it.\""}, {"md": "Then something in his face switches off. He looks at me politely and asks whether Tikhon Savelyevich would like some tea."}, {"md": "Lunyov dies three weeks later, in his sleep, after lunch. I put the notebooks in a shoebox and set it on top of the wardrobe."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Read Lunyov's notebooks right here, beside him"}, "B": {"label": "Just sit with him until he falls asleep"}, "C": {"label": "\"Vadim Petrovich, what happened in '96?\""}}}, "В2.1-08": {"title": "The Records", "date": "March 2052", "photo_caption": "The Registry, page one", "text": [{"md": "At the bus stop across from my booth there's been a screen since '44. A young general with ribbon bars looks out from it, calm and a little tired, and under him it says: \"Orpheus. Second Chance. For those who make the decisions.\" What a \"second chance\" is, they explain vaguely: controlled cardiac arrest under cold, awakening of the neural reserve. I know it better than they do. Thirty-two degrees, four-twelve, one step back. Those voices in my pitch that have been sounding in the Choir for eleven years now are them, the ones who bought themselves a step."}, {"md": "All of it was made from the two test tubes Lunyov took from me at City Hospital No. 2.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ОБРАЗЕЦ"}}, {"md": "Where they got my pitch, I can only guess. Sometimes I remember the doctor from Tomsk with his two test tubes, but I don't want to think it through.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ОБРАЗЕЦ"}}}, {"md": "In the pharmacy on Lenin Avenue two old women in line are arguing about whether Kemerovo had a metro or not."}, {"md": "\"Three stations,\" says one, banging her cane. \"I got off at Rudnichnaya for forty years!\""}, {"md": "\"What metro, Lyusya, what metro…\""}, {"md": "They're both right. They call it the Stratification now. There are hundreds of thousands of people steering their fates through Second Chance, and each of them pulls the shared world a little toward themselves."}, {"md": "I remember Tomsk as having one bridge over the Tom, and this morning I walked across a second one. It's old, with peeling paint, and old women sell sunflower seeds on it as if it had always been there.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "On TV they talk about exercises on the border between two blocs that stopped being afraid long ago. It's called the doctrine of acceptable risk, and people just call it the Wager: you bet that you'll survive. My neighbor's grandson was taken off to these exercises in January. Every evening he writes her the same thing: \"All good, Gran. We don't die.\""}, {"md": "In February some website posts the \"Site Nine Registry.\" It stays up for four days. I read it in my booth, on my phone, for two days straight, until the battery dies."}, {"md": "\"No. 1. 14.03.2017. 04:52. Talaya mine, sec. 3. Client I. Confirmed.\""}, {"md": "\"No. 211. 18.09.2019. 11:40. Xing km 47. Client I. Confirmed.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В212"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "РАБОТА"}}}, {"md": "\"No. 211. 18.09.2019. 11:40. Xing km 47. Not confirmed. Note: check source.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В212"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "РАБОТА"}}}, {"md": "By the thirties the \"client\" column says \"X\" almost everywhere. An expert on TV, bald and polite, says the doctrine of risk grew out of \"the predictive technologies of the twenties.\" That was when the generals first understood you could know forty minutes ahead. That's where it all began. I turn off the TV and sit in the dark for a long time. Those forty minutes were sold out of my head."}, {"md": "In the evening I take the shoebox of Lunyov's notebooks down from the wardrobe."}, {"md": "I read all of them but the last one eleven years ago. The last one is nothing but columns of numbers.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В217"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}}, {"md": "For eleven years I haven't opened them.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В217"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}}, {"md": "I recognize the numbers in the last notebook at once: they're my minutes. Forty, thirty-eight, forty-one, all two hundred and some, copied out of the registry. How Lunyov got them, I don't know. Next to them, his curve, and at the end, on the last page, in an old man's handwriting with the letters sliding downhill:"}, {"md": "\"The horizon grows. At the root it equals 65 years. Root: 28.07.2061, 04:12. Bl. backwater. Halley at perihelion. He will be there. I won't.\""}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I buy an optical magnetometer at the flea market by the station, from the Chinese traders who sell decommissioned equipment from the Second Chance clinics. It's the size of a soap dish and costs as much as my booth makes in a month. The trader swears it's hardly been used. I believe him: the Orpheus logo sticker on the case hasn't even worn off."}, {"md": "At home I pry it open with a butter knife and spend half the night over it in my glasses, with an old soldering iron. I draw the circuit on the back of Lunyov's page, next to his curve. I'm sixty-three, my fingers aren't what they were, and the little finger and ring finger of my left hand can't feel the soldering iron at all. But I'm measuring something again, like at twenty."}], "B": [{"md": "I find Nika in Akademgorodok. She's a professor, gray-haired, cropped short, with the same strict vice principal's face. I wait for her on the steps of the Institute of Physiology, by the birches, where squirrels still jump around."}, {"md": "She comes out, sees me and stops on a step. It isn't my face she recognizes me by. I hold out Lunyov's notebook, and she looks at my palm, at the scar from thumb to wrist."}, {"md": "\"Took your time, Samokhin,\" she says. Then she reads the last page, moving her lips, the way you read a prescription. \"The twenty-eighth of July. All right. Call me, and I'll come.\""}], "C": [{"md": "I burn the notebooks in the stove on Tatarskaya Street, one at a time, page by page. The oilcloth burns badly and stinks; the paper catches at once. The last page, with the curve and the date, I hold over the fire longest of all, until it burns my fingers."}, {"md": "After that the Choir gets quieter, like a radio with the volume turned down. But the date stays in my head whole, minutes and all. Fire can't touch it."}], "D": [{"md": "I buy a magnetometer at the flea market by the station, from the same Chinese traders selling off Orpheus equipment. At home I don't open it up. I sit down on a stool in the dark, put the instrument on my knees and put on the headphones from an old player, so I won't hear the street."}, {"md": "The Choir comes fast, the way it did at Site Nine when I listened to the end. In the neighboring worlds mine are dying right now, one by one: one in an elevator, one on a highway, one in a banya. I listen to each one until his last minute and turn the sensitivity knob until the needle twitches at the instant the voice breaks off. By morning it's twitching on its own, without me, right at the fortieth minute."}, {"md": "I switch off the magnetometer and sit for a long time with the headphones around my neck. The ringing in my ears after that night doesn't go away until lunch."}, {"md": "In the afternoon I take Lunyov's last notebook to copy out the curve. On the cover, in his handwriting: \"V. P. Lunyov, NSU.\" For thirty years I've remembered him as the doctor from Tomsk. I run my finger over the letters. The ink is faded, old, and nobody has corrected it.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 25}}, "drift": true}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Get instruments ready. If Lunyov is right, this can be measured"}, "B": {"label": "Find Nika"}, "C": {"label": "Burn the notes. They've had enough of my minutes"}, "D": {"label": "\"The Choir knows these minutes better than any instrument.\" Tune the instruments to the Oracle's numbers. Listen to mine dying and turn the dial"}}}, "В2.1-09": {"title": "The Equation", "date": "27 July 2061, evening", "photo_caption": "Tatarskaya Street. A backpack by the door", "text": [{"md": "The Tom in Tomsk runs out by June now. Under the Kommunalny Bridge kids ride bikes along the dry bottom, and under Lagerny Garden, where in '09 I almost stayed on a bench at forty-two below, people graze goats. From Kemerovo they write that the backwater across from Rudnichny is still standing, as if the river forgot to take only that one."}, {"md": "For two weeks now I've been drawn downstream, to where there's no stream anymore. It pulls steady and strong, like a cable being reeled in by a winch down at the bottom of a shaft. The Choir hums without a break, low, like the main ventilation fan at the Glubokaya, and through it, in my pitch, all their Second Chances hum at once."}, {"md": "In the evenings a comet hangs over the dry riverbed. At graduation Galina Ivanovna told us when it would come back, and we cracked up: seventy-two was never."}, {"md": "Yesterday, for the first time, I took the storm jacket out of Dad's parcel. For forty years it lay in the postal box. The canvas is heavy and smells of Grandma's trunk and mothballs. There's a hole in the elbow. I hang it on a nail by the door and look at it all evening."}, {"md": "Lunyov's last page lies on the table, held down by a mug. \"He will be there. I won't.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В218"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СЖЁГ"}}}, {"md": "I don't have Lunyov's notebooks anymore. The last page is still before my eyes, all of it, with the curve and his sliding letters.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В218"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СЖЁГ"}}}, {"md": "All his life Lunyov was solving one equation. It had one unknown: the old man in the wet striped shirt who in '96 pulled me out of the water, gave me mouth-to-mouth and walked off into the willows. I wanted to find him all my life too. He will be there, Lunyov wrote. I reread it and only now notice that Lunyov never said who \"he\" was."}, {"md": "The instruments are assembled and packed in a backpack. The magnetometer in a candy box, a thermometer with a remote sensor, an old voice recorder and spare batteries. All of it weighs nine kilos. For seventy-two, that's a lot.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В218"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПРИБОРЫ"}}}, {"md": "Nika came from Novosibirsk in May, with one suitcase. She sleeps in the room; I sleep on a camp bed by the stove. Yesterday she said, without looking up from her crossword, \"If you're thinking of going there without me, Samokhin, I'll count how long it takes you to get down the stairs.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В218"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "The last bus to Kemerovo leaves at eight. Three hours along the highway beside the dry river, and then half an hour down on foot."}, {"md": "For seventeen years I brought strangers their minutes. I learned my own nine years ahead, from an old man's notebook.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I put the backpack on both shoulders and tighten the straps, like before going down in the cage. I take the storm jacket off its nail and carry it over my arm.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В218"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПРИБОРЫ"}}}, {"md": "I don't have instruments, so in the evening I gather what I've got. A pharmacy thermometer, a voice recorder from my repair drawer, a magnetic compass from an old UAZ. Lunyov would have laughed. I take the storm jacket off its nail and carry it over my arm.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В218"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПРИБОРЫ"}}}, {"md": "On the bus I sit by the window with the backpack on my knees. Near Yurga, in the dark beyond the glass, the turn-off for the R-255 floats by. For a second the Choir goes quiet on it, all at once, like a class when the principal walks in."}], "B": [{"md": "\"Let's go,\" I tell Nika."}, {"md": "She puts down her crossword, pours boiling water into a thermos and puts on her jacket, all silently and quickly, as if she'd been waiting since morning. She takes the storm jacket off its nail herself and hands it to me."}, {"md": "On the bus she sleeps on my shoulder. Her hair smells of my laundry soap. I don't sleep; I count the kilometer posts until I lose track."}], "C": [{"md": "I leave the key, the notebook and a note for the landlady on the table: \"Gone fishing.\" I take only the storm jacket."}, {"md": "On the bus I sit by the window. The comet hangs over the dry river and doesn't fall behind, like a dog that's followed you home from the market."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Go with the instruments and close the equation"}, "B": {"label": "Take Nika with me"}, "C": {"label": "Alone, with nothing"}}}, "В2.2-00": {"title": "Forty-Two", "date": "April 2016", "photo_caption": "The department, a key on a nail", "text": [{"md": "After the session Lunyov doesn't speak to me for three days. On the fourth he comes with a bottle of cognac, pours two glasses and doesn't drink his.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В208"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПРЕРВАЛ"}}}, {"md": "Lunyov doesn't call me, and I don't call him. He set the date without me, for the fourteenth of April.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В207"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОТКАЗ"}}}, {"md": "Two weeks later, a little after five in the morning, the duty officer from the Glubokaya calls me. The phone crawls across the nightstand on vibrate, and I watch it until it reaches the edge. Professor Lunyov went down to the level alone, on a foundation pass, and hasn't answered since four-twelve. I know what I'll find down there before I even get into the taxi."}, {"md": "I find him in Granddad's chamber. He's lying on the cot, covered with his coat, hands folded on his chest. He's taken off his glasses and put them in his breast pocket, earpieces up, the way he always did before rubbing his eyes. There's frost on his eyebrows. The cot is cold, the monitor is off. On the wall next to the worn-away \"41,\" someone has written \"42\" in fresh chalk, and on the floor under it lie white crumbs."}, {"md": "In his coat pocket there's an envelope. On it, in his small handwriting: \"For Andrei. Open if I don't come back.\" The envelope is thick, ten pages or so."}, {"md": "The investigator arrives around lunchtime. He's young, in a wet jacket, and keeps saying \"Citizen Samokhin.\" He hasn't seen the envelope yet."}, {"md": "He was fifty-nine. I outlived him by thirteen years, and every twenty-eighth of April I set out two glasses in my kitchen.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I sit down on the floor of the chamber, at his feet, and read. The investigator waits outside the door and doesn't interrupt."}, {"md": "The handwriting is small and even, like in a lab log."}, {"md": "\"Andrei. I'll start with what I should have told you when you were twenty. I had a son, Mitya. He was nine. We lived on Shakhtyorskaya Street then, and he ran down to your backwater every day. In '88 I went there with him myself to measure the water, because through the old adit the river drains into the workings, toward your grandfather's chamber. I looked away for two minutes. They never found the body. All these years I've believed that somewhere, in another world, Mitya is alive. After all, you got out.\""}, {"md": "Then comes '96."}, {"md": "\"The two test tubes of your blood I took at the hospital were not for tests. In '98, during the default, when the department hadn't been paid for six months, I sold them to a foreign foundation, Orpheus, along with a copy of your grandfather's archive. The money went into searching for Mitya. Forgive me, Andrei, if you can. I sold what was yours, and I don't even know what they did with it.\""}, {"md": "The last page is short, and the handwriting on it is already different, large, the letters sliding downhill: \"Synchronize watches. I've gone to check whether your grandfather was wrong.\""}], "B": [{"md": "The investigator takes the envelope, weighs it on his palm and puts it in an evidence bag."}, {"md": "\"We'll let you see it, Citizen Samokhin, if needed.\""}, {"md": "They never do let me read it. A month later the case is closed as an accident. The bag with the envelope, I was told later, was taken \"at the foundation's request,\" and I never saw it again."}], "C": [{"md": "There's no fire down below and there can't be: with a lighter they wouldn't even have let me into the cage. I carry the envelope up to the surface under my jacket. At the mine gates the watchman is smoking on the steps, and I bum some matches off him. The paper doesn't want to burn at first, then catches all at once. I hold it until it burns my fingers and shake the ash into the storm drain grate."}, {"md": "Let all of it stay down there, with him and with Granddad. I'm twenty-seven, and I just want to live."}], "D": [{"md": "I put the envelope in the inside pocket of my jacket. In the evening, back in Tomsk, I stop by the department. The kettle in 218 is cold. I open his desk drawer and take the watch, a man's Shturmanskie with the little star. It's stopped at four-twelve. Engraved on the back: \"Synchronize watches. 12.04.61.\" Those are Granddad's words from the tape, so the watch is Granddad's too. I put it in my inside pocket and carry it the way he did, on me. Nobody sees it."}, {"md": "At home I put the envelope in a book on the top shelf. I can't open it, not after a month, not after a year. The watch stays in my inside pocket, even though it doesn't run."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Open the envelope right here, beside him"}, "B": {"label": "Hand the envelope to the investigator unopened"}, "C": {"label": "Burn the envelope without reading it. I've had enough"}, "D": {"label": "Hide the envelope and take what he kept in his desk. Read it later, when I can"}}}, "В2.2-01": {"title": "School No. 41", "date": "September 2017", "photo_caption": "The physics classroom, the first of September", "text": [{"md": "I leave Tomsk with one suitcase and a box of books. I drop my graduate program without finishing the last chapter. Shadrin from the foundation calls twice to offer me \"interesting work.\" I don't pick up."}, {"md": "On the first of September I'm standing at the blackboard in the physics classroom of School No. 41, the same school I once walked into with gladioli and a scar on my palm. Above the board hangs the same bearded Kurchatov. Seventh Grade B looks at me with open boredom, and Shmelyov Junior asks from the back row whether it's true I'm the Vodyanoy."}, {"md": "Apparently it is. Nicknames in Rudnichny last longer than Santa Barbara did."}, {"md": "Mom is glad I've come back and doesn't ask why. Dad coughs in the mornings so you can hear it through the wall, and doesn't ask either. In the evenings I walk to the backwater. The water in it is just as black, and the snag sticks up in the same place."}, {"md": "Nika calls from Novosibirsk every Sunday. We talk for a long time, about everything except the main thing. Last time she said, \"They're offering me a position at the cardiology center here in Kemerovo. Say something, Samokhin.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_НИКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "In the staff room everyone drinks tea from one shared mug that says \"Best Teacher 2009.\" Olga Sergeyevna, literature, nudges the cookies toward me and asks where I got that look, \"as if you've already seen it all.\""}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "\"Come,\" I say."}, {"md": "Nika is silent on the line for so long that I check whether the call dropped."}, {"md": "\"Two hundred and fifty-two,\" she says at last. \"That's me counting how long it'd take you to figure it out. You made it.\""}, {"md": "In December she moves in with two suitcases and a cat. In the summer we sign the papers at the registry office on Lenin Avenue, no dress, no limo. Vitka would have said we've both been sick in the head for a long time."}], "B": [{"md": "\"I have,\" I say. \"Many times.\""}, {"md": "Olga Sergeyevna looks at me closely, without smiling, and tops up my tea. A month later we take Seventh Grade B to the theater together; six months after that I move my books into her place. What exactly I've seen, she never once asks."}], "C": [{"md": "In Seventh Grade B two kids are on the police register, one has been smoking in the bathroom since fifth grade, and Shmelyov Junior, the nephew of that very Shmelyov, has a dad in prison. A year later half the class comes to my extra physics lessons. They don't love physics; I just don't yell at them."}], "D": [{"md": "I rent a one-room flat on Shakhtyorov Street, top floor, with a view of the headframe. Mom brings cutlets in a jar and looks at me the same way she looked at me in the hospital in '96. In the evenings I grade notebooks and listen to the Choir rustle. I don't need anyone, I tell myself, and almost believe it."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Tell Nika: \"Come\""}, "B": {"label": "Answer Olga Sergeyevna honestly: \"I have. Many times\""}, "C": {"label": "Take on homeroom for Seventh Grade B, and leave the rest for later"}, "D": {"label": "Live alone. People aren't safe around me"}}}, "В2.2-02": {"title": "The Envelope \"For Kolya\"", "date": "February 2023", "photo_caption": "Regional hospital, pulmonology", "text": [{"md": "Dad is dying of what miners die of when they're lucky enough not to die in the mine. The doctors call it anthracosis. Dad calls it \"the dust\" and jokes that in thirty years he's breathed himself a second coal seam."}, {"md": "In the regional pulmonology ward he lies by a window that looks out on a spoil heap, and he stares at it all day long. He's not allowed to smoke. Sometimes he takes a cigarette from the nightstand, doesn't light it, and just holds it between his fingers, the way he did that night in the kitchen, after the explosion."}, {"md": "I know nothing can be done, and I know why. You can rewind away from a collapse, from an outburst too; there death has a minute, a fork. Dust has no such minute. It built up over thirty years, breath by breath, and not one of those breaths can be played again. Granddad would probably have had some scientific name for it. I call it the Wall."}, {"md": "On Wednesday Mom brings the envelope from the china cabinet. That one, \"For Kolya,\" with the wax seal, which lay behind the crystal for twenty-three years. Dad takes it in his hands, looks at it for a long time and holds it out to me."}, {"md": "\"You read it, Andryukha. My eyes aren't what they were. And no voice left.\""}, {"md": "I break the wax. It's old and crumbles; red crumbs fall onto Dad's blanket, and he flicks them off with a finger, one at a time. Inside is a single sheet, Granddad's handwriting, blue ink, dated at the bottom, January '86."}, {"md": "\"Kolya. If you're reading this, it means I didn't come back. You're lucky, son, and it isn't luck.\""}, {"md": "Dad knocks twice on the nightstand with his knuckles, very quietly. I read on."}, {"md": "\"I passed it on to you without wanting to, and forgive me for that. Don't go below two hundred.\""}, {"md": "At the words \"below two hundred\" Dad closes his eyes. There's one line left, and I read it to myself, because my lips won't obey me: \"And if you have a son, keep him away from the water.\""}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I read the letter to the end, read it again, slowly, and then tell him things about school, about Seventh Grade B, about Shmelyov Junior. Dad doesn't open his eyes, but in some places he smiles, and that means he can hear."}, {"md": "He dies four days later, at night, at four-twelve. They call me, but I'm not asleep anyway."}], "B": [{"md": "I tell him everything. He listens with his eyes closed. I'm already thinking he's fallen asleep when he says hoarsely through the mask:"}, {"md": "\"Three times. In '05. I told you.\""}, {"md": "\"You did, Dad.\""}, {"md": "\"Well then.\" He breathes for a long while. \"So I wasn't a fool.\""}, {"md": "He dies four days later, at night, at four-twelve."}], "C": [{"md": "I don't say anything. I hold his hand, dry and light as newspaper. In the evening a nurse comes in, checks the drip and goes out without a word. We sit like that until it gets dark outside and the spoil heap disappears."}, {"md": "He dies four days later, at night, at four-twelve."}], "D": [{"md": "I take out the watch and put it in his palm. He brings it up to his eyes, squints at the little star, then turns it over and reads the engraving with his lips, without a sound."}, {"md": "\"Dad's,\" he says. \"He never wore it to work; in the lab you had to take metal off. Where'd you get it?\""}, {"md": "\"Long story, Dad.\""}, {"md": "\"Then don't tell it.\""}, {"md": "He holds the watch in his fist until evening. He dies four days later, at night, at four-twelve, and afterward the nurse gives the watch back to me."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Read it to the end out loud and sit with him until evening"}, "B": {"label": "Tell him the truth: about the backwater, the elevator, about how \"lucky\" means both of us"}, "C": {"label": "Keep quiet, the way he kept quiet all his life, and just hold his hand"}, "D": {"label": "Take out the watch and put it in his palm"}}}, "В2.2-03": {"title": "March Ice", "date": "24 March 2029", "photo_caption": "The Tom by Rudnichny, late March", "clock_label": "until Timur is pulled under the ice", "text": [{"md": "Spring comes early in '29. By late March the Tom by Rudnichny is already gray, the ice has darkened and cracked, but the fishermen still sit near the bank on their crates, like always, until the very last."}, {"md": "After classes I take Sixth Grade A on a field trip to the Krasnaya Gorka museum. On the way back we walk along the embankment, twenty-six kids in pairs, and every five minutes I count heads. Timur Gainullin, the smallest in the class and the quickest, runs down the slope onto the ice on a dare, to a fishing hole someone left behind."}, {"md": "\"Gainullin, get back here!\" I yell."}, {"md": "He turns around and waves at me, pleased with himself. The edge of the hole breaks off under his sneaker like a wafer, and Timur goes down to the waist, to the chest, over his head, so fast I don't have time to close my mouth. Only his backpack is left lying at the edge of the hole. It's about twenty meters away. The girls are shrieking, and one of the boys is already filming on his phone."}, {"md": "The cold pulls the scar on my palm tight before I can think anything. My lips start counting seconds on their own, like Nika did back then on the bank. On the eighth a big bubble comes up out of the hole, and nothing else."}, {"md": "The back of my head goes cold, and through the children's shrieking I hear the familiar close rustle. \"Go left,\" someone says in my voice. \"By the bank the ice holds, by the hole it doesn't.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I run straight, across the gray, and the ice gives underfoot like a trampoline in Zhukov Park. My legs remember this river from childhood. Three meters from the hole I drop onto my side and slide to the edge on my jacket, like down an ice slide."}, {"md": "I yank Timur out by the collar; his backpack stays lying on the ice. He doesn't cry or cough, just stares at me with white eyes and hiccups. On the bank Sixth Grade A is bawling in chorus, and the boy with the phone never thinks to stop recording. That evening the whole school watches the video, and the vice principal calls me in to ask why twenty-six children were walking along the embankment without a second adult."}], "B": [{"md": "I lie down on the ice, spread my weight the way they taught us in school safety class, and crawl, pushing the scarf ahead of me. Timur grabs it on the third try. I pull him toward me slowly, centimeter by centimeter, and the ice holds us both.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 45}}}, {"md": "I crawl across the ice, pushing the scarf ahead of me. The ice cracks. By the time I reach the hole, there's no one above the water. Only the backpack at the edge.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 45}}}, {"md": "Divers bring Timur up two hours later. I sit on the bank, wet to the waist, and can't get up.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 45}}}], "C": [{"md": "I turn the kids with their backs to the river and dial 112. My voice is even, a teacher's voice, and I don't recognize it myself. The rescuers arrive eleven minutes later."}, {"md": "Divers bring Timur up two hours later. Since then his mother crosses to the other side of the street when she sees me, and she's right to."}], "D": [{"md": "I run to the left, along the bank, over dark but unbroken ice, and come at the hole from the side, where the ice is thicker. I pull Timur out by the hand on the first try. He doesn't even have time to swallow much."}, {"md": "The Choir rustles in my head, pleased."}, {"md": "In the evening Mom calls and suddenly remembers how in 1990 she celebrated my first birthday, on the seventh of February. Mine is on the nineteenth. I don't correct her.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 5}}, "drift": true}], "E": [{"md": "I remember ice overhead from the inside, gray, with bubbles, and how I pounded on it with my fist. I remember a dacha pond and a strange boy with my face whom I never pulled out. I have no pond and no dacha. Whose memory that is, I don't stop to figure out. My body knows what to do before I do."}, {"md": "I drop onto my stomach right by the bank, where dry reeds stick up out of the ice, and crawl along the edge toward the hole from the side, undoing my belt as I go. The ice crunches under me but holds, because all of me is lying on it. Timur catches the belt on the second try, buckle across his fingers. I pull him centimeter by centimeter, and he crawls out onto the ice on his belly, paddling with his arms, and immediately starts bawling, louder than anyone."}, {"md": "On the bank Sixth Grade A is bawling in chorus. Timur shivers in my jacket and asks why I crawled like an idiot when in the movies everybody runs. \"Because in March you don't run on ice,\" I say. I don't tell him about the boy with my face."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Run straight across the ice to the hole"}, "B": {"label": "Lie down on the ice, crawl, and hold out my scarf to him"}, "C": {"label": "Keep the other kids on the bank and call the rescue service"}, "D": {"label": "Go left, where the voice says"}, "E": {"label": "\"I've pounded on ice like this from below. Not in this life.\" Lie flat by the bank and crawl along the edge where reeds stitch the ice, my belt in my hand"}}}, "В2.2-03б": {"title": "The Sea", "date": "July 2038", "photo_caption": "Two tickets on the kitchen table", "text": [{"md": "I'm forty-nine, my twenty-second summer at School No. 41. I know how many steps it is from the staff room to the physics classroom and where on the embankment the ice breaks first in March. In summer I get up at five and walk along the Tom to Pritomsky and back. The boys on the piers know me by now and don't dive while I'm around; they wait until I've passed."}, {"md": "Nobody asked me to. One day I just realized I didn't want divers bringing anyone else up.", "kind": "old"}, {"md": "I visit Timur at Kedrovka more often than I visit Dad. It's easier for me that way; Dad would understand.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ТИМУР"}}, {"md": "On Friday Nika lays two tickets on the oilcloth, Novokuznetsk to Adler, third-class sleeper, the twenty-ninth of July. She lays them out evenly, side by side, like the cards on Lunyov's table. \"The sea, Samokhin,\" she says. \"You've never seen it. Neither have I. We'll be fifty soon, and I'm sick of you being tied to the Tom like a dog on a chain.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "On Friday Olga lays two tickets on the oilcloth, Novokuznetsk to Adler, third-class sleeper, the twenty-ninth of July. She doesn't say anything, just straightens them so they lie evenly. Then she does say something after all, looking into her cup: \"Twenty-two years without a vacation, Andrei. I counted.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "On Friday Sonka calls from Novosibirsk. She turned forty in February, they never got around to celebrating, and now she's gathering everyone in August. \"Come,\" she says. \"Mom's coming, I already bought her a ticket. We'll sit down like normal people. You haven't seen me in person since Dad's funeral, only on the phone.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НЕТ"}}}, {"md": "It's true, I've never seen the sea. The water I know can be black, can be icy, and can be warm and close, with a whisper inside. It's never salty. At night I lie awake and listen to the Choir rustling on one note, steady, like a kettle just before it clicks off. Late July is the worst time for the Tom. Last year at Pritomsky they pulled out two in one week."}, {"md": "In the morning I stand in the kitchen holding a ticket."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "On the train I sleep for two days straight. I haven't slept like that since I was nineteen, no dreams, no four-twelve, as if someone had pulled out a wire. Near Tuapse she wakes me and points out the window. Out there it's blue, all the way to the edge, and the edge doesn't end."}, {"md": "The sea is warm and smells of iodine and chebureki. I walk in up to my knees and stand like that for ten minutes. She doesn't hurry me. Then she takes my hand, and we go in up to the waist, then up to the chest, and it holds me up. Salt water holds you by itself; you don't have to talk it into it."}, {"md": "\"Two hundred and fifty-two,\" Nika says in my ear. \"I'm not counting. Swim, Samokhin.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "\"I'm here,\" says Olga. \"I've got you. Though if you ask me, it's the sea that's got you.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "For two weeks the Choir is silent. On the Tom, as I later learn from the news, nobody drowned in those two weeks. The boys on the piers dived without me."}], "B": [{"md": "I put the ticket back on the oilcloth and sit down across from her."}, {"md": "I tell her about five in the morning, about the embankment, the piers, about how in March I walk the ice with my eyes and count children, the way Nika once counted. About how after every summer I cross off in my head the ones they didn't pull out in time, and how that list is longer than a class roster. For twenty-two years nobody knew about it."}, {"md": "She listens to the end. Then she picks up the tickets, goes to the window and looks at the river for a long time."}, {"md": "\"We'll go in September,\" she says. \"In September it's too cold to swim here. You said so yourself. Until then, walk your embankment. I'll walk it with you.\""}, {"md": "We walk it together, every morning, right up to September. And in September we go to the sea, and there, for the first time since I was seven, I swim on my own."}], "C": [{"md": "Novosibirsk greets me with a traffic jam on the Dimitrovsky Bridge. Sonka goes gray beautifully, like Mom, and at the party she seats me next to her instead of with her husband's relatives. Mom sits across from us, small, in her best dress, and all evening looks at the two of us as if she's counting."}, {"md": "\"Two,\" she tells me in the kitchen, when everyone's already having tea. \"Back then, in the hospital, I kept thinking: let there be two. So there'd be someone to live.\""}, {"md": "I don't ask what she means. I think I understand."}], "D": [{"md": "I return the ticket at the station ticket office myself, while she's at work. The cashier asks if I'm sure, and I say I'm sure."}, {"md": "On the twenty-ninth of July at five in the morning I walk along the embankment. Nobody at Pritomsky. Nobody on the thirtieth either. On the thirty-first, by the pier, a boy of about nine dives off a concrete slab and doesn't come up for a long time, and I'm already taking off my shoes when he shoots up to the surface and yells something filthy about the cold."}, {"md": "At home she doesn't ask where I've been. The second ticket stays on the fridge, under the Krasnaya Gorka magnet, until winter.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НЕТ"}}}, {"md": "I write to Sonka that I can't make it, work. She replies with an emoji. I look at that emoji for a long time and can't work out what it means.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НЕТ"}}}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Go to the sea"}, "B": {"label": "Tell her why I haven't left the city in twenty years"}, "C": {"label": "Go to Sonka's birthday"}, "D": {"label": "Stay. Late July is when the most people drown in the Tom"}}}, "В2.2-03г": {"title": "The Bridge", "date": "14 June 2045, 20:05", "photo_caption": "Kuznetsky Bridge, the third span", "text": [{"md": "In spring there's a new craze in the city: jumping off the Kuznetsky Bridge feet first and posting the video. The boys stand outside the railings one at a time, count to three and drop twelve meters. Some get a thousand views, some a hundred thousand. On TV those same days they talk about rich old men to whom some foundation sells a \"second chance\" after death, and the boys in the smoking spot crack up that they've got no use for that."}, {"md": "I watch them from the middle of the bridge and don't go near the railings. Down below the water roars, and my fists clench on their own, like that time the air ran out.", "if": {"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "seen", "id": "✝В2-08.C", "life": true}, {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В2.2-03.A", "life": true}]}, "body": true}, {"md": "Yegor Shmelyov is fourteen. He's first in my register, four summers running, swims better than anyone in the club and has gotten cocky.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ВОДЯНЫЕ"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СЕКЦИЯ"}}}, {"md": "Yegor Shmelyov is fourteen. He has his own key to the hut now; he opens it before I get there and lines the little ones up.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ВОДЯНЫЕ"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПИРС"}}}, {"md": "Yegor Shmelyov is fourteen. He's our senior now; he teaches the little ones to lie on the water and tells them in my words, only with swearing.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ВОДЯНЫЕ"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВДВОЁМ"}}}, {"md": "Yegor Shmelyov is fourteen. When Sonka comes, she trusts him with Gosha the dummy and calls him \"Comrade Paramedic.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ВОДЯНЫЕ"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СОНЬКА"}}}, {"md": "Yegor Shmelyov is fourteen. He still doesn't say hello to me, and he learned to swim on his own, at the quarry, to spite me.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ВОДЯНЫЕ"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ХОР"}}}, {"md": "In the evening, as always, I walk along the embankment to Pritomsky and back, and then climb up onto the Kuznetsky Bridge. A hundred and twelve steps up from the water; I counted them long ago. On the third span there's a knot of boys with phones, all looking down over the railings."}, {"md": "Yegor is standing outside the railings, on a ledge as wide as a palm, holding the handrail with one hand. Under him, straight down, is the bridge pier with its icebreaker, gray, wet, sharp. Yegor isn't planning to jump down but out, to clear the pier and hit the water beyond it."}, {"md": "He sees me and grins from ear to ear. \"Feet first, Andrei Nikolaich, like you taught us!\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ВОДЯНЫЕ"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ХОР"}}}, {"md": "He sees me and bares his teeth. \"Oh, the Vodyanoy's here. Gonna catch me?\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ВОДЯНЫЕ"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ХОР"}}}, {"md": "The boys are filming. Yegor lets go of the handrail to wave at the camera, and grabs it again."}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "I stop three steps from the railings and tell Yegor that the pier under him sticks out two meters farther than it looks from above. That the water beyond the pier is deep, but the current there pins you against the pillar. That feet first means going in legs down, and the way he's standing, he'll go in sideways. I say it evenly, the way I explain Archimedes' principle on the board."}, {"md": "Yegor listens, then looks down, then at me. The boys stop filming. He climbs back over the railings, slowly, and sits down on the asphalt with his back to them. \"Sideways, huh,\" he says, and spits. We walk home together. He doesn't say a word all the way to Krasnaya Gorka.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ЭМП"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 45}}}, {"md": "Yegor listens to me with a smile, nods and jumps. He goes in clean, beyond the pier, surfaces, and yells up from the cold. In a week the video gets two million views. Over the summer forty more people jump off the bridge. In August divers bring one of them up, by the pillar, and I stand on the embankment until they take him away.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ЭМП"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 45}}}, {"md": "While I talk, I think about Timur. His voice from the neighboring worlds sounds in me more quietly than all the others, and today I hear it especially well.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ЭМП"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 50}}, {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ТИМУР"}]}}], "C": [{"md": "Dimka comes running four minutes later in rubber boots and pulls Yegor off the ledge by the scruff, like a kitten. The boys scatter. Yegor doesn't cry or argue, just looks at me over his father's shoulder."}, {"md": "He doesn't come to me anymore. On the embankment I hear from the boys that the Vodyanoy ratted. A week later Dimka gives me back the key to the hut, without a word."}], "D": [{"md": "I lie down on the bridge, on the warm asphalt, and push my arm between the railing posts. Yegor's ankle is thin and wet with sweat. I grip it hard, the way you grip an oar, and tell him to crouch down now, slowly."}, {"md": "He jerks when he realizes he's being held. My shoulder cracks, and my arm goes numb to the elbow. But the bridge holds me, and I hold him. The boys help drag him back, and he sits down next to me on the asphalt, white, his lips trembling."}, {"md": "They set my shoulder at the emergency clinic on Shakhtyorov. Ever since, it aches before rain, and I don't treat it."}], "E": [{"md": "The Choir answers at once, with a hum. In the neighboring worlds Yegor jumps off this ledge many times, and almost always it goes fine. Only when he lets go of the handrail to wave does he fall not out but down. Under the third span, two meters underwater, lies a concrete slab that was dumped there from Pritomsky in '39 and never pulled out."}, {"md": "I tell him about the slab, loudly, in front of everyone. Yegor doesn't believe me, but he stops smiling. Then he climbs back over, and a week later he dives by the pier with a mask and comes to show me a photo: the slab is there, with weed growing on it."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Come up quietly, from behind, and hold out my hand. \"Come on, Yegor. Climb back\""}, "B": {"label": "Don't go closer. Talk to him from here, evenly, like in class"}, "C": {"label": "Call his father. Dimka's at the boat station, five minutes away"}, "D": {"label": "\"I've already gone over these railings.\" Lie down on the bridge and grab his ankle between the posts"}, "E": {"label": "Listen to the Choir: how many times he's already jumped in the neighboring worlds, and where"}}}, "В2.2-03в": {"title": "Vodyanoys", "date": "June 2041", "photo_caption": "The OSVOD lifeguard hut at Pritomsky", "text": [{"md": "Walking the embankment isn't enough. I figure that out by fifty-two, in June, in my twenty-fifth summer at School No. 41. I make it in time to the places I walk to. But people drown where I don't walk: at the Yagunovsky quarry, at the dachas below Metallploshchadka, in the Yaya, where people go on weekends."}, {"md": "At the end of May Timur comes into my classroom with a cake. He's twenty-four, he drives a minibus, and he still calls me \"Andrei Nikolayevich\" across the desk. The cake is for my birthday, even though my birthday is in February. \"I never did learn to swim,\" he says, and laughs. \"What for? You're always around.\"", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ТИМУР"}}}, {"md": "At the end of May Gennady Ivanovich, the gym teacher, mentions in passing in the smoking room that Timur couldn't swim. The school pool closed in 2010, and his mother wouldn't let him go to the river. I stand there with an unlit cigarette and think that for twelve years I didn't know that.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ТИМУР"}}, {"md": "A week later Dimka Shmelyov catches me by the OSVOD hut at Pritomsky. I taught him in Seventh Grade B; now he watches the boat station and wears a bunch of keys on a cord, the way Mom used to wear her pass. The hut was boarded up while I was already here, back in the thirties, when the last boat was written off."}, {"md": "\"This is for it,\" says Dimka, and puts a key in my palm, rusty, with a tag. \"Teach my Yegor, Andrei Nikolaich. My old man never taught me; he was inside.\""}, {"md": "Yegor is ten. He stands behind his father in shorts and rubber flip-flops and looks at me the way kids look at a teacher in summer, that is, with suspicion."}, {"md": "\"So then what'll we be?\" he asks. \"Vodyanoys?\""}, {"md": "It took me until fifty-two to work it out. Granddad would probably have said that for a Samokhin that's quick.", "kind": "old"}, {"md": "In the evening Nika works it out on the calculator on her phone, for a long time, moving her lips. \"There are forty thousand kids in this city,\" she says. \"Twenty a summer. That's about fifteen years' work for you, Samokhin. No longer than you've been walking that embankment.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "In the evening Olga takes a class register out of the wardrobe, Soviet, blank, in an oilcloth cover. She's kept it since teacher's college, not knowing what for. On the first page she writes in her neat teacher's hand: \"Lists.\" And says nothing more.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "In the evening Sonka calls from her ambulance shift; there's a siren wailing on her end. I tell her about the key. \"You teach them to swim,\" she says, \"and I'll teach them to bring people back. But properly, Andryusha, on a dummy, not the way you like doing it.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НЕТ"}}}, {"md": "On the fourth day, at five in the morning, Yegor is standing alone by the hut, waiting. He has his flip-flops in his hand."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "The permit for the club comes through two months later. The hut, it turns out, is a capital construction site, the Tom water has to be tested for E. coli, and the lifeguard has to be certified. I find a lifeguard at the boat station, through Dimka, in a red cap with a crossword."}, {"md": "The first thing I teach them isn't swimming but lying. First they lie down on the water and lie there until they believe the water holds them. Many of them don't believe it for a week. Yegor believes it on the second day, and after that he lies on his back in the middle of the backwater by the hut, arms spread, looking at the sky until I call him."}, {"md": "A year later there are thirty-one names in my register. Not one has drowned. I check it every summer, the way I used to check the embankment."}], "B": [{"md": "I open the hut the next morning with a crowbar, because Dimka's key only fits the padlock, and the inside lock has rusted through. At six three kids come. A week later, twelve. A month later there are forty kids by the hut, and mothers sit on the parapet with thermoses."}, {"md": "The city Telegram channel writes that at Pritomsky a retired teacher is drowning children without a license. The local police officer comes, young, with a tablet. He watches for a long time as Yegor swims breaststroke to the buoy and back, and then asks what time we're on tomorrow. His daughter can't swim either."}], "C": [{"md": "Nika stands on the bank with a stopwatch around her neck and times how long each of them can stay underwater with their eyes open. \"I don't let anyone past thirty seconds,\" she tells me in the evening. \"One record holder on this river is enough for me.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "Olga sits on the bank in a hat and writes them down in the register, by surname, with dates of birth and notes on who's afraid of what. Yegor is first on her first page. Next to his name she's written: \"Afraid of being laughed at.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "The two of us go there every morning, all summer. Me in the water up to my waist, her on the bank. In the evenings we come back along the embankment, and she holds my arm, and for the first time in many years I don't look at the piers."}], "D": [{"md": "Sonka comes in July for two weeks and brings a dummy from Novosibirsk in a gym bag. The dummy's name is Gosha; he has a blue rubber face and a click in his chest when you press right. The kids fight over who gets to bring him back."}, {"md": "Sonka teaches them to count out loud, thirty compressions and two breaths. I stand next to her and listen to ten-year-olds counting to thirty in chorus, and something in my chest clicks, like in Gosha's."}], "E": [{"md": "I never do open the hut. The tagged key hangs on a nail in my hallway. In summer I sleep three hours a night and get up when someone in the Choir starts drowning ahead of time, minutes ahead, on the neighboring forks. Then I take a taxi."}, {"md": "I make it in time to the Yagunovsky quarry, to the Yaya, to the pond in Lesnaya Polyana. The boys I pull out look at me like I'm crazy. Yegor doesn't say hello to me anymore when we meet. Dimka says he calls me \"the Vodyanoy who drowns them himself.\""}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Do it properly. A school swim club, doctor's certificates, a lifeguard on the bank"}, "B": {"label": "No paperwork. Open the hut tomorrow at six and teach everyone who comes"}, "C": {"label": "The two of us. Me in the water, her on the bank with the lists"}, "D": {"label": "Wait for Sonka to come on vacation. I teach them to swim, she teaches them to resuscitate"}, "E": {"label": "Don't open the hut. Listen to the Choir for where people are going to drown, and get there in time"}}}, "В2.2-04": {"title": "Second Chance", "date": "November 2047", "photo_caption": "An ad at a bus stop, Sovetsky Avenue", "text": [{"md": "In '47 they advertise the \"second chance\" at bus stops. On Sovetsky there's a screen: a gray-haired smiling man in a good coat and the words \"Orpheus. Second Chance. For those who have something to lose.\" The procedure costs as much as a three-room apartment in the center. There are clinics in Moscow, Dubai, and, recently, Novosibirsk."}, {"md": "What they're selling, the TV explains vaguely: \"controlled cardiac arrest under cold,\" \"awakening of the neural reserve.\" What that means, I know better than anyone in this city. A person with a \"second chance\" can go back one minute, once, one step, like I did at nineteen, in the elevator."}, {"md": "That evening I finally take the book with Lunyov's letter down from the shelf. For thirty-one years the envelope has lain between its pages. I read it in the kitchen, until morning. About his son Mitya, who in '88 went under the water by our snag, about the two test tubes of my blood, about '98 and the Orpheus foundation. \"Forgive me, Andrei, if you can.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В220"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПОТОМ"}}}, {"md": "Now I understand what's hanging at the bus stop. Every one of their \"second chances\" is made from my blood, from two test tubes taken from a seven-year-old boy at City Hospital No. 2.", "if": {"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "truthy", "n": "F_МИТЯ"}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В220"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПОТОМ"}}]}}, {"md": "Where they got it, I don't know for sure; I can only guess. The two test tubes the doctor from Tomsk took from me at the hospital in '96 must have gone somewhere. Sometimes at night the Choir rustles as if it recognizes something of its own.", "if": {"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В220"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОТДАЛ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В220"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СЖЁГ"}}]}}, {"md": "That same week, for the first time, strangers' voices sound in the Choir. Their voices are strangers', but the pitch is mine, the way a stranger on a bus whistles a song you thought was yours. There are many of them, all far away, in Moscow, in Dubai, in Novosibirsk."}, {"md": "On Friday a polite young man from Siberian Resonance comes to the school. That's the Russian arm of Orpheus now, he explains, as if I didn't know. Shadrin is a hundred years old, he says; Shadrin remembers me and sends his regards. The foundation needs a consultant, and the foundation is prepared to pay very well."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I write down everything I know and send it to three newsrooms. Two don't answer. The third, in Novosibirsk, runs the piece on its website, and six hours later it disappears along with the website."}, {"md": "A week later I'm called in to the principal's office and politely invited to \"take a rest until the end of the term.\" I go on sick leave, and for the first time in thirty years I have nothing to do in the mornings."}], "B": [{"md": "I sign the contract at the Orpheus office in Novosibirsk, on the forty-second floor. They issue me a pass and look at me like a museum exhibit that has suddenly started talking. The work is simple: they ask me what the \"patients\" hear, and I explain."}, {"md": "The money arrives on my card every month, steady and generous. At night there are more and more strangers' voices in the Choir."}], "C": [{"md": "\"No,\" I tell the polite young man. \"Tell Shadrin I remember him too.\""}, {"md": "He smiles, nods and leaves. A month later the school has a new sponsor, Siberian Resonance. The principal is delighted: the physics classroom gets new interactive whiteboards."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Go to the journalists and tell them what the \"second chance\" is made of"}, "B": {"label": "Agree to consult for the foundation. Money doesn't smell, and at least I'll be on the inside"}, "C": {"label": "Refuse and keep out of it. I'm fifty-eight; I have classes to teach"}}}, "В2.2-05": {"title": "Dasha", "date": "September 2049", "photo_caption": "Dasha. Sixth Grade V", "text": [{"md": "I turned sixty in February, no cake and no guests. My knees creak in the mornings like the steps in the dorm on Lenin Avenue; on the stairs I hold the railing, though as recently as spring I was running up to the third floor two steps at a time. The body ages, however many times I die. The Choir doesn't ask about that."}, {"md": "On the twenty-fourth of March, as every year, I buy two carnations and go to Kedrovka, to Timur. He's stayed twelve. His mother left long ago, and nobody visits him but me. I stand by the headstone and think that the ice could have been played again. Only the ice was Timur's, and Timur had no rewind.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ТИМУР"}}, {"md": "Nika says I've started to look like my father, especially when I cough.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "Olga says I've started to look like the Kurchatov above the blackboard, especially the beard.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "In September a new girl joins Sixth Grade V. Dasha Vesnina, twelve years old, family from Novosibirsk, her father something big at a coal company. She sits alone at the last desk with headphones on and spends the whole lesson drawing circles in her notebook, one inside another, like on water."}, {"md": "After class she comes up to my desk and says, without taking off her headphones:"}, {"md": "\"You sound in my head like a radio, only quieter.\""}, {"md": "I find out everything in one evening, because her mother cries and tells me herself. At nine Dasha drowned in the pool at their dacha, three minutes underwater. They brought her back and, \"just in case,\" bought her a \"second chance.\" The procedure was done in Novosibirsk, at the Orpheus clinic. Since then she hears voices and hardly sleeps. In the spring they're calling her back to the clinic, \"for an examination.\""}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "On Tuesdays after classes we sit in the empty physics classroom. I tell her what I know myself. You don't drown the voices out; you learn to tell them apart. The one who calls you isn't always right. At four-twelve you don't have to get up; you can lie there and listen."}, {"md": "Two months later Dasha sleeps through the night for the first time. Her mother brings me a jar of blackcurrant jam, exactly like the one Mom traded for my gladioli in '96."}, {"md": "Once Dasha looks up from her circles and asks, \"Is it true you used to teach people not to drown? In warm water, where the fish are as big as a person. I dream about you with a yellow hose in your mouth.\" I put the kettle on and wait a long time for it to boil.", "if": {"op": "lived", "id": "В5-05"}}], "B": [{"md": "\"I'm a physics teacher,\" I tell Dasha's mother. \"You need a good doctor.\""}, {"md": "Dasha looks at me from behind her mother's back as if I've switched off the one radio that ever picked anything up for her. At the end of the term she's moved to a private school. Sometimes I still hear her in the Choir, quiet and far away."}], "C": [{"md": "I tell Dasha's parents what I have no right to tell them. I know how this works, and at the clinic they won't help her; they'll study her. Dasha's father gives me a long, heavy look, like a section boss sizing up a new face worker. Then he says, \"We'll see in the spring.\""}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Teach her what Lunyov taught me: to listen and not be afraid"}, "B": {"label": "Tell her parents I'm not a doctor, and step aside"}, "C": {"label": "Talk her parents out of taking her to the clinic again"}}}, "В2.2-06": {"title": "The Wager", "date": "March 2055", "photo_caption": "Novosibirsk, Krasny Avenue. The Orpheus clinic", "text": [{"md": "I go to Novosibirsk on the night train, the way I did as a student. Only now I take a lower berth and hold my heart all night. Not that anyone lets you sleep in the compartment anyway. Two men my age argue about the bridge until one in the morning."}, {"md": "\"One bridge in Kemerovo, one!\" shouts the one by the window. \"I drove across it to work for forty years.\""}, {"md": "\"Three,\" says the other, and shoves a phone with a photo under his nose. \"Look, my grandson took it.\""}, {"md": "They're both right. They call it the Stratification, and scientists shrug. In '55 the world lives as if it has nothing to lose. On TV they call all this \"the doctrine of reasonable risk.\" People put it more simply: the Wager. You bet that you won't die. The news talks about exercises on the border and about generals with a \"second chance\" who \"aren't afraid to make decisions.\""}, {"md": "I'm in my eighth year of consulting for Orpheus, and I know what these arguments mean better than the scientists on TV. Everyone who has steered their fate through a \"second chance\" shifts the shared world a little toward themselves. There are hundreds of thousands of them by now.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В224"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОРФЕЙ"}}}, {"md": "Dasha is eighteen. She got into Tomsk, the physics department, \"like you.\" Three weeks ago she was called to the Orpheus clinic in Novosibirsk for a \"routine examination of carriers.\" She never came back. Her phone is off.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ДАША"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УЧЕНИЦА"}}}, {"md": "Dasha is eighteen. I haven't seen her in a long time and only hear her sometimes in the Choir, far off. Three weeks ago that faraway voice broke off in Novosibirsk, on Krasny Avenue, where the Orpheus clinic is. Her mother calls me herself, for the first time in six years, and asks for help.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ДАША"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УЧЕНИЦА"}}}, {"md": "For the third day a crowd has been standing outside the clinic on Krasny Avenue. These are the relatives of \"carriers\" who have been \"kept for examination.\" Women in puffer coats, surnames written by hand on pieces of cardboard, all just like outside the pithead offices in '05, only here nobody is brought up in a cage."}, {"md": "Guards in white body armor stand in a line at the entrance. Drones hang over the crowd. Someone throws a snowball at the glass, and the line takes a step forward."}, {"md": "In the line, third from the left, stands Yegor Shmelyov. He's twenty-four, with a Siberian Resonance patch on his sleeve, and he hasn't seen me yet.", "if": {"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_МОСТ"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДЕРЖАЛ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_МОСТ"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УГОВОРИЛ"}}]}}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I go through the crowd sideways, the way I went at sixteen in the crush outside the pithead offices. People shove me, step on my feet, someone elbows me in the ribs, but I make it to the staff entrance. A young, frightened orderly is smoking there. I tell him the surname. He nods silently at the door."}, {"md": "Dasha is sitting on the floor of an empty corridor in hospital pajamas, drawing circles on the glass with her finger. When she sees me, she says, \"You're loud. I could hear you all the way from the station.\""}], "B": [{"md": "The head of security turns out to be from Leninsk-Kuznetsky. Twenty minutes later we're reminiscing about people we both know. An hour later he brings Dasha out through the staff entrance on the pretext of \"relative, urgent.\" She's eighteen, in hospital pajamas, and she recognizes me from far away.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ЭМП"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 55}}}, {"md": "The head of security listens to me without a flicker in his face, and then says, \"Step back, Grandpa.\" I stand at the barrier until morning. Dasha never comes out.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ЭМП"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 50}}}, {"md": "A month later she's transferred to the Orpheus clinic in Moscow. I never hear her in the Choir again.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ЭМП"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 50}}}], "C": [{"md": "Shadrin answers himself, on the second ring. His voice is young and brisk; he's a hundred and seven."}, {"md": "\"Andrei Nikolayevich,\" he says with pleasure. \"I knew you'd call.\""}, {"md": "Ten minutes later they bring Dasha out to me through the main entrance, with apologies and in someone else's jacket. In return Shadrin asks for nothing, for now."}], "D": [{"md": "I close my eyes and listen. Through glass and concrete I make out her voice, thin, in my pitch. Third floor, left wing. I go around the building through the courtyards, find a fire escape with an unlocked door and come out right at her, at the ward with the number on its plate."}, {"md": "The guards look straight through me. Maybe in this world I'm really not here."}], "E": [{"md": "Yegor recognizes me three steps away and takes off his helmet. His face is grown-up, broad, his father's."}, {"md": "First he looks at my right shoulder, as if checking it's still there.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_МОСТ"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДЕРЖАЛ"}}}, {"md": "I tell him about Dasha, briefly. He's silent, then goes in through the glass doors, and half an hour later brings her out of the staff entrance, in his uniform jacket over her hospital pajamas. \"On my responsibility,\" he tells the head of security, and the head waves a hand."}, {"md": "\"We're even, Nikolaich,\" says Yegor, and puts his helmet back on. I don't argue, though I count it differently."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Push through the crowd to the staff entrance"}, "B": {"label": "Go up to the head of security and talk, long and calm"}, "C": {"label": "Call Shadrin. I've had his number since '17"}, "D": {"label": "Listen to the Choir: somewhere in this building her voice is sounding, in my pitch"}, "E": {"label": "Walk along the barrier to Yegor Shmelyov"}}}, "В2.2-07": {"title": "The Pull", "date": "July 2061", "photo_caption": "The embankment. Five in the morning", "text": [{"md": "Since '59 nobody in Kemerovo drowns. By July there's no Tom in the city, and nowhere to drown. I still go out onto the embankment at five in the morning and walk to Pritomsky, though there's nobody there to count. The piers the boys used to dive off only after I'd passed now stand over the dry bottom, twice my height, and the \"No Swimming\" sign hangs in its old place. The silt under it has cracked into neat squares, like graph paper."}, {"md": "Water is left in the city in one place, across from Rudnichny, in the Black Backwater, and in three summers its level hasn't moved a finger's width. Sixth-graders from School No. 41 are taken to look at it as a visual aid. The physics teacher, whom I once taught myself, gave them homework: explain where the water in the backwater comes from if there's no river. The neighbors' granddaughter brought me that homework. I wrote \"I don't know\" for her and gave myself an F."}, {"md": "I'm seventy-two. Every night I dream of water, and I wake at four-twelve. Mom used to tell me that Dad woke at that time all his life and went out on the balcony to smoke. In the daytime I'm drawn to the river the way you're drawn home after a long work trip. The Choir hums steady and low now, like the transformer box in the yard."}, {"md": "In the evenings the comet is visible in the sky. Galina Ivanovna didn't lie: on the twenty-eighth of July the comet will pass closest to the Sun."}, {"md": "Nika notices everything. On Saturday morning she sets tea in front of me and says, \"You're planning to go to the backwater. Don't lie, Samokhin, I can see it in your eyes. I'm coming with you.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "Olga silently darns the elbow of Granddad's storm jacket, which I took out of Grandma's trunk yesterday. I didn't ask her to. \"You're going there, aren't you,\" she says without looking up from the needle. \"Take me with you.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "Dasha comes from Tomsk in July, grown up, with short hair and a thick notebook in an oilcloth cover. The first page is ruled into columns: No., date, time, place, how, what I heard. \"This is how I keep records,\" she says. \"I came up with it myself, back when I was nine. Can I come too? I'll stand on the bank.\"", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ДАША"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УЧЕНИЦА"}}, {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ДАША_ЗАБРАЛИ"}}]}}, {"md": "Her columns are the same as in Granddad's notebooks, word for word; even \"what I heard\" comes last. I never told her about Granddad's notebooks.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ДАША"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УЧЕНИЦА"}}, {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ДАША_ЗАБРАЛИ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}]}}, {"md": "On the twenty-sixth Shadrin calls. He's a hundred and thirteen. \"Andrei Nikolayevich,\" he says, \"remember, you owe me? I'd like to see this backwater of yours. Together with you.\" I hang up and don't call back.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ДОЛГ_ШАДРИНУ"}}, {"md": "Before I leave, I take off Granddad's Shturmanskie, which I've carried on me for forty-five years, like Lunyov, and lay it on the kitchen table, glass up. Where I'm going, it's already there.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ЧАСЫ_ЛУНЁВА"}}, {"md": "On the evening of the twenty-seventh I leave the house. I don't have far to go, thirty minutes downhill, to the dry riverbed. I have nothing in my pockets but my keys."}], "choices": {"A": {"label": "Take Dasha with me. Let her stand on the bank and write"}, "B": {"label": "Take the one who's beside me"}, "C": {"label": "Go alone"}}}, "В3-01": {"title": "Field Hand", "date": "9 July 2007", "photo_caption": "Mundybash. The field party's base", "text": [{"md": "From Tashtagol to Mundybash I ride in a \"loaf\" van full of crates, sitting on the crates, because the van has no seats. The crates hold rocks. Every crate has a number in marker, and the whole way I read those numbers the way I used to read railcars at the crossing as a kid: zero-forty-seven slash twelve, zero-forty-seven slash thirteen. The driver smokes out the window and says the rocks go to Novosibirsk for analysis, and from there they write back that there's nothing in them. \"Twenty years we've been hauling them like that,\" he says, and laughs."}, {"md": "The party's base is in an old school at the edge of the settlement. Tarpaulin is drying on the porch, two saddled horses stand by the fence, and the yard smells of horse manure, diesel and damp foot wrappings. Above the roof are the mountains, dark, in shaggy forest, and at the very top of one of them a bare gray crown, like Dad's bald spot."}, {"md": "\"That's a golets, a bald peak,\" says the woman on the porch, without saying hello. \"You'll go up there next year, if you live that long. Zoya Arkadyevna. Senior geologist. Show me your hands.\""}, {"md": "I show her. She looks at my palms, looks for a long time at the scar from the snag, and doesn't ask anything about it. She's about fifty-five, with short gray hair, a Belomor in the corner of her mouth and a canvas jacket bleached almost white at the shoulders. Her surname is Kim, and everyone at the base just calls her Kim, even the boss."}, {"md": "Vitka writes from near Chita that the taiga there is just the same, only larch. He's the only one who said \"good call, Andryukha\" about the field party. Dad said, \"Well, well.\"", "if": {"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "АРМИЯ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДВЕРЬ"}}]}}, {"md": "Vitka stayed in town to work off what we owe the Welder, for both of us. Seeing me off, he shoved a carton of Yava into my backpack and said, \"Find gold, don't forget who hauled bathtubs for you.\" Dad said, \"Well, well.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "Nika texted from Novosibirsk: \"Samokhin, you idiot, there are ticks there.\" I answered: \"I know.\" She answered: \"All right then.\" Dad said, \"Well, well.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "Zoya Arkadyevna takes a hammer out of a crate by the porch. It doesn't look like the one we have at home: a long birch handle, polished yellow by palms, a blunt face on one end and a sharp pick on the other. She gives it to me."}, {"md": "\"Yours. Lose it, and I'll take it out of your pay, and your pay is such that you'll still end up owing.\""}, {"md": "Then she picks up a gray rock from the step and strikes it with her own hammer, not hard, a glancing blow. The rock rings, thin and long, like a glass when you run a wet finger round the rim."}, {"md": "\"Sound,\" she says. \"And this one?\" She strikes another. That one answers dull and short: tuk. \"Crack inside. You can't see it from outside, but it told you itself. A rock doesn't lie, Samokhin. The one who lies is the one who didn't listen to it.\""}, {"md": "The ringing in my head lasts longer than the rock rings. It's already quiet, Zoya Arkadyevna is already lighting up, and something at the back of my head is still trembling, thin and cold, and dies away only when she breathes out smoke.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "I tap the rock myself. It rings, and I stand there waiting until the ringing stops completely. It doesn't stop right away. Zoya Arkadyevna watches me wait and snorts.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "In the corner of the yard, by the horses, an old Shor man squats on his heels mending a bridle with an awl. He hasn't once raised his head while we've been talking. Zoya Arkadyevna nods at him:"}, {"md": "\"Miron Todyshev. The horses are his, the mountains are his too; we're guests here. Our boss is Gusev, but Gusev is in Novosibirsk, and we're here. Choose where you'll go. One job, three places.\""}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "By evening the sample pack weighs as much as Sonka, and by the end of the route as much as Sonka with her school bag. Zoya Arkadyevna walks ahead, traveling light, with just her hammer, and every two hundred steps she stops, chips a piece off the rock face, looks at it through a hand lens, wraps it in paper and labels it. Then she hands it to me. I put it in the pack. Eight hours of that."}, {"md": "On the third day I have bloody stripes on my shoulders from the straps. Zoya Arkadyevna looks at them, takes two pieces of felt out of her jacket pocket and silently shoves them under the straps. The felt smells of her Belomors."}, {"md": "\"Rock is heavy because there's time in it,\" she says that evening by the fire. \"A million years per centimeter. Today you hauled forty million. Don't whine.\""}, {"md": "I don't whine. I lie in my sleeping bag and think that forty million years weigh exactly as much as my back hurts."}], "B": [{"md": "She teaches me for a week, and all week I tap. On cliffs, on boulders in the stream, on the school's threshold, on the stove. Granite rings high, limestone dull, shale rustles and flakes like an old book. Sandstone answers as if it can't be bothered."}, {"md": "\"You're listening, but you need to look,\" Zoya Arkadyevna snaps. \"Ringing is for fools.\""}, {"md": "But she listens too, I can see it. Before she strikes, she always freezes for a second, like Dad before opening the stairwell door after a shift. On the seventh day she gives me a box of twenty rocks without labels and tells me to name them all. I name sixteen. Four I get wrong, but about one I say there's a crack in it, and she splits it on the corner of a crate, and the crack is there. Zoya Arkadyevna looks at the halves for a long time, then at me, and writes something in her field log."}], "C": [{"md": "Miron Todyshev doesn't say a word to me for three days. I groom the horses, take them to water at the Kondoma, haul oats. On the fourth day he points his awl at a gelding, a chestnut with a white star on its forehead."}, {"md": "\"Chalkan,\" says Miron. \"He's afraid of thunderstorms. You're afraid too. You'll get along.\""}, {"md": "I'm not afraid of thunderstorms, I'm afraid of water, but you can't argue with Miron; I understand that right away. In the evening he sits by the fire smoking his pipe and looking at the mountain, at the bare crown where the forest ends. Then he says, to no one in particular, that on the golets live the ones who didn't make it. They aren't evil. They just call, because they're lonely. I ask who didn't make it. He shrugs and knocks his pipe out against his heel."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Haul the sample pack behind Zoya Arkadyevna. I've got a back"}, "B": {"label": "Let her teach me the hammer. I want to know what the rock says"}, "C": {"label": "To Miron, to the horses. He doesn't talk, and I like that"}}}, "В3-02": {"title": "The Golets", "date": "16 July 2008, 15:50", "photo_caption": "Kara-Tag golets. Ten to four", "text": [{"md": "They sent me up the golets alone, because it's already my second season and because Zoya Arkadyevna's ankle is swollen. \"Bedrock outcrop on the saddle,\" she said this morning, and jabbed a pencil at the map. \"Twelve samples, top to bottom, one every meter. Lunch up there. Back by six.\" Miron walked me to the tree line and went no farther. He said the horses had no business up there."}, {"md": "Up top there's not a tree, not a bush, only rock. The boulder field creeps down the slope toward the forest, gray blocks in rust-colored lichen, like a frozen river. In the middle of the saddle stands one single cedar, old, crooked, leaning all the way to the east, with dry needles under it. To the right is a little hollow, and in it, between the rocks, runs a stream, thin as a finger. Beyond the stream is a huge boulder with a flat top; two people could stand on it."}, {"md": "The pack on my back is already heavy: nine samples, a can of stew, a pot, a hatchet. The hammer is strapped to the shoulder strap, handle up, so it's close at hand.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В301"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "РЮКЗАК"}}}, {"md": "On my back is the pack: nine samples in paper, stew, a pot, a hatchet. The hammer is strapped to the shoulder strap, handle up, the way Zoya Arkadyevna carries hers.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В301"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "РЮКЗАК"}}}, {"md": "The cloud comes over the ridge in ten minutes. At first I see it out of the corner of my eye and think it's the shadow of another mountain. Then it rolls over the crest, low, black, with white rags hanging beneath, and the sun goes out as if someone had switched off the light in a stairwell. It turns cold. The wind brings a smell I don't know, fresh and sharp, like a socket when you plug a broken iron into it."}, {"md": "The first lightning hits the crest a kilometer from me. The thunder comes three seconds later, and the boulder field underfoot answers it with a faint tremor."}, {"md": "Then the hammer starts to hum. At first I think it's a bumblebee. The sound comes from behind my shoulder, thin and steady, like the transformer box behind our garage. I turn my head. The pick of the hammer above my shoulder is glowing pale blue, barely, the way a watch dial glows in the dark. The hairs on my arms are standing on end, every one."}, {"md": "Miron said Chalkan was afraid of thunderstorms. Chalkan would already be running downhill by now, and I'm standing here watching the pick glow.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В301"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЛОШАДИ"}}}, {"md": "The back of my head is cold. It gets cold like that when someone on a bus stares at your back for a long time.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "The rain comes down all at once, like a wall. The rocks turn black and slippery. The second lightning strikes closer, on the neighboring crown, and the thunder comes with it."}], "after": {"D": [{"md": "The cedar stands alone on the saddle, but its roots, I remember, spread across the rocks in every direction, under the moss, under the lichen, ten meters out. I walk away from it across the boulder field counting steps, and at twenty I drop my pack, pull out the foam pad and squat on it, hugging my knees, feet together, head down. That's how drunks sit on the commuter train when they feel sick."}, {"md": "The strike comes a minute later. I don't see it, my eyes are shut; I see only white through my eyelids, and then a sound as if the sky right beside me has been torn like a bedsheet. It smells of scorched resin. When I raise my head, the cedar is standing, but down its trunk from top to bottom runs a fresh white stripe, stripped to the living wood, with steam pouring out of it. Around the roots the moss is smoking in a ring. The ring ends three steps from my foam pad."}, {"md": "I sit like that for another half hour, until the cloud moves off behind the ridge. Then I get up, take my hammer and, not knowing why, tap the trunk of the cedar with it, on the white stripe. The cedar answers short and dull. Inside it there's a crack, all the way to the roots, and outside it stands just as it stood."}], "E": [{"md": "The hammer is humming, and I remember how this ended, even though it hasn't happened yet. I yank off the pack, throw it on the rocks along with the hammer, the pot and the hatchet, run fifteen paces away and squat right on the wet lichen, feet together, head down. The foam pad is still in the pack, and to hell with it."}, {"md": "The strike hits the pack. Through my fingers I see a white thread from the sky, and my pack jumps on the rock as if alive. The thunder punches me in the chest. I'm shaking, and I don't know if it's from the cold or from something else."}, {"md": "The cloud moves off forty minutes later. I go over to the pack. The canvas is burned clean through, the stew can has swollen, and the hammer's pick has melted; a drop of metal has frozen on the tip, like on a candle. I pick it up. The handle is warm. I tap the boulder with it, and the boulder rings, long, and the ringing in my head doesn't stop for another half minute after it's gone quiet. I won't give this hammer back to Zoya Arkadyevna later, and she won't ask."}], "F": [{"md": "The hollow underfoot has become a chute, and down it, from the crest, runs brown water, and something knocks inside it. I remember what's knocking in it. I climb out of the chute onto the little ridge, a stony hump about five meters off, throw the foam pad on it and squat, curled up, feet together, head between my knees."}, {"md": "A minute later a mudflow comes down the chute. I hear it, and then I see a slurry of water, gravel and blocks the size of a TV crawling past me downhill toward the forest, the rocks in it knocking together with a sound like someone chopping firewood down below. Lightning hits the crest, then the cedar. I don't raise my head."}, {"md": "When the cloud moves off, the hollow is gone. In its place is a fresh gray furrow, bare rocks without lichen. I go down to the forest along the edge, skirting it, and at the tree line Miron is waiting for me. He's standing under a fir with two horses, and it looks like he's been standing there a long time. He looks at me, then at the mountain, and says, \"They called?\" I don't know what to answer. He nods, as if I had."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Under the cedar. It's the only one on the whole saddle, and it's dry under it"}, "B": {"label": "Run down the boulder field to the forest before it hits"}, "C": {"label": "Lie down in the hollow by the stream, low, so I don't stick up above the rocks"}, "D": {"label": "\"I remember where the strike went.\" Not under the cedar but past its roots, twenty paces or so. Squat there on my foam pad, feet together"}, "E": {"label": "\"I remember how the hammer sang.\" Throw off the pack with the iron, move away from it and squat, feet together"}, "F": {"label": "\"I remember where the water went.\" Not into the hollow but onto the little ridge beside it, on my foam pad, curled up"}, "G": {"label": "\"I remember how the water carried rocks.\" Climb onto the big boulder beyond the stream, up high. It won't reach me there"}}}, "В3-03": {"title": "Uncle Gosha", "date": "21 August 2008", "photo_caption": "A winter hut on the Telbes", "text": [{"md": "After the storm Zoya Arkadyevna doesn't let me go past camp alone for two weeks. Then she gives in and sends me with Miron to drive the horses to the far sector, beyond the Telbes. Two days on foot. For the night Miron turns off the trail toward a winter hut that isn't on the map."}, {"md": "\"Gosha,\" says Miron, as if that explains everything."}, {"md": "The hut stands above a stream, low, up to its windows in fireweed. Smoke rises from the chimney. On the threshold sits an old man in a quilted jacket over his bare skin, his white beard sticking out in every direction, his glasses held together with electrical tape. He's picking a tick off himself; he holds it in his fingernails, studies it against the light and drops it into a jar of kerosene. There are about forty in the jar."}, {"md": "\"My collection,\" he says to me instead of hello. \"Twenty-two years I've lived here, never once had the shot. Not one of them's infected, eh? Miron, who's this?\""}, {"md": "\"Field hand. Came down off the golets alive.\""}, {"md": "The old man looks at me over his glasses. Then at the hammer on my belt. Then at me again, at my face, for a long time, so that I get uncomfortable and start studying the jar of ticks."}, {"md": "\"Surname,\" he says."}, {"md": "\"Samokhin.\""}, {"md": "He doesn't say anything. He gets up, goes into the hut and rattles dishes in there. Miron shrugs and leads the horses to the stream."}, {"md": "Inside it's cramped and hot and smells of smoke and larch resin. On a shelf along the wall stand flasks, lab flasks with graduations, and in each one a candle stub. Under the bunk is an ammunition crate, green, with a star. Gosha pours tea into two mugs, for me and for himself; he doesn't pour for Miron. Miron doesn't drink tea."}, {"md": "\"Tikhon Savelyevich's grandson,\" he says, not asking. \"His face. His ears. I washed test tubes for him, boy. Seven years I washed them, underground, down at minus six hundred forty. He called me Gosha, and so did everyone after him. Georgy Ilyich, that's me. Twenty-two years nobody's called me that.\""}, {"md": "I know a few things about Granddad from the Yugoslav boot box I once found in the shed. But there wasn't a word in it about \"minus six hundred forty.\" I set my mug down on the bunk so I won't spill it.", "if": {"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ФОТО"}}]}}, {"md": "About Granddad I know that he was smart, \"too smart,\" as Dad says, and that he was gone before I was born. Nothing more. The box that might have told me more has been gone a long time. I set my mug down on the bunk so I won't spill it.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СОЖЖЕНО"}}}, {"md": "About Granddad I know that he was gone before I was born. Nothing more. The box that might have told me more has been gone a long time. I set my mug down on the bunk so I won't spill it.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПРОДАЛ"}}}, {"md": "Dad said once that Granddad worked under the mine and that there was a door down there. He never said anything more about it.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПРОДАЛ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СОЖЖЕНО"}}]}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДВЕРЬ"}}]}}, {"md": "\"You came off the golets,\" says Gosha. \"And on the golets that day there was a storm. Miron told me. You stood where it strikes, and you came back. So it passed to you too. He used to say it gets passed on. Through the son, through the grandson. Like ears.\""}, {"md": "I keep quiet. I haven't told a single person what happened on the golets. That I died up there, and then I was standing in the rain again. That I remember how the scorched needles smelled. I don't even tell myself. I think it was a waking dream, from fear; that happens."}, {"md": "Back then I decided Gosha was crazy. I kept deciding that for a long time, thirty years or so. It's convenient when the person who explains everything to you is crazy. You don't have to believe him.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "Gosha talks until three in the morning. The candles in the flasks burn down one after another, and he lights new ones from the old. He says my grandfather was a great man and a fool. That under Krasnaya Gorka, under the old mine, they had a level, cold as a root cellar, and in it coils, screens and a chair that people sat in. That people in that chair died and remembered how they died, and then got up."}, {"md": "\"Tikhon wanted to turn the Tom around,\" says Gosha, and raps his finger on the table. \"Not the water. Time. So it would flow backward, even for a minute. He used to say: where it's cold and quiet, time is weak, you can bend it. On the twenty-eighth of January '86 he bent it, and it sprang back. At four-twelve. I'd gone up to the surface for kefir. I came back, and the level was sealed, and a man in a gray coat says, 'You were never here.' So I left, so I'd never be there. Came here.\""}, {"md": "I don't believe a word. But toward morning, when Gosha falls asleep sitting up, I go out of the hut and tap a rock by the stream with my hammer. The rock rings. The ringing in my head lasts until it starts to get light."}], "B": [{"md": "\"Drowned,\" Gosha repeats, and his face turns very old. \"I said that, did I? Well. Tikhon had a graduate student, Vadim. Young, smart, angry. Vadim had a son, Mitka. In '88 that Mitka went swimming in the Tom by Krasnaya Gorka, in the pit, the backwater. Nine years old. They never found him. Not then, not later.\""}, {"md": "I sit holding my mug and can't put it down. I know the pit by Krasnaya Gorka. I was in it, in '96, for four minutes."}, {"md": "\"Vadim wasn't the same after that,\" says Gosha. \"He wasn't right to begin with, but after that, not at all. He thought Tikhon would have pulled Mitka out if he'd had time to finish his machine. But by then Tikhon had been gone two years. Don't you cross Vadim's path, boy. He's a professor somewhere now. He'll see you and want to find Mitka in you.\""}, {"md": "Gosha falls asleep sitting up, toward morning. I go out of the hut. There's fog over the stream, and in the fog it keeps seeming to me that someone small is standing knee-deep in the water, looking down at his feet."}], "C": [{"md": "\"Miron,\" I say by the stream. \"Is he, you know, off?\""}, {"md": "Miron is cleaning Chalkan's hoof and takes a long time to answer."}, {"md": "\"He's been here twenty years,\" he says at last. \"Alone. In winter a bear comes to see him, and he pours the bear tea. That's normal.\""}, {"md": "I lie down on a horse blanket by the fire, next to the horses, and can't sleep for a long time. From the hut I can hear Gosha talking, quietly, in two voices, as if he's arguing with someone who answers him. I can't make out the words. Toward morning he goes quiet. In the morning, as we leave, he stands on the threshold with his jar of ticks and shouts after us: \"Samokhin! Hold on to the cold! You're going to need it!\" I don't turn around."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Keep listening. About Granddad, about the underground, about all of it"}, "B": {"label": "\"Who else drowned down there?\" Ask about the graduate student he let slip"}, "C": {"label": "Go sleep with the horses. The old man talks to himself, and it gives me the creeps"}}}, "В3-04": {"title": "Pyrite", "date": "31 December 2009, 21:30", "photo_caption": "Rudnichny. New Year's", "text": [{"md": "I get home on the thirty-first, toward evening, in a storm jacket, with a beard and a backpack, and Mom doesn't recognize me in the doorway. For a second, no more. Then she does and hits me on the shoulder with a dish towel, hard, and cries, and hits me again."}, {"md": "\"You could have called,\" she says. \"Just once.\""}, {"md": "There's nowhere to call from in the taiga. I wrote. I wrote every week, on pages torn from my field log, and gave them to Miron, and once a month, when Miron drove to Tashtagol, he took them to the post office. There were thirty-two letters."}, {"md": "Dad is smoking on the balcony in an undershirt, though it's thirty below. He sees me through the glass, nods and finishes his cigarette. Then he comes in and shakes my hand like a man off a shift, firm and short."}, {"md": "\"Alive,\" he says."}, {"md": "\"Alive.\""}, {"md": "\"Well, that's that.\""}, {"md": "He sits down in front of the TV. All evening he doesn't ask me another thing."}, {"md": "Sonka is eleven. She follows me around like a tail from the door to the bathroom and from the bathroom to the kitchen, talking the whole time. About school, about her friend Karina, about how the muffler fell off Dad's Moskvich right on Shakhtyorov Street. Then she drags me into her room and pulls a shoebox from under the bed, the box from Mom's boots. In the box are my letters, all thirty-two, in order, tied with a bow made of ribbon."}, {"md": "\"Mom reads them and cries,\" says Sonka. \"Then I read them. Then I put them in here. And Dad doesn't read them. Mom tells him, 'Kolya, read them, he's your son.' And he says, 'Alive? Well, that's that.' And goes out to smoke.\""}, {"md": "I look at the box. The letters lie neatly, but their corners are creased and worn, like old cards that have been shuffled a lot. Thirty-two letters read once each don't get that worn."}, {"md": "\"Did you find gold?\" asks Sonka. \"You promised.\""}, {"md": "I didn't find gold. I reach into my backpack and take out a rock, gray, the size of a fist, and in it are cubes, even, shiny, yellow, as if they'd been cut with a knife. Sonka gasps and grabs it with both hands."}, {"md": "\"Pyrite,\" I say. \"Fool's gold. That's what geologists call it.\""}, {"md": "\"You're the fool,\" says Sonka, and carries the rock off to the kitchen to show Mom under the lamp."}, {"md": "That evening, before the Kremlin chimes, I tap the rock with my hammer, gently, right on the table, between the Olivier salad and the herring. Mom yells that I'll scratch the oilcloth. The rock rings. Sonka listens with her mouth open. Dad turns from the TV for a second and looks at the hammer."}, {"md": "The hammer's pick is melted; a drop has frozen on the tip. Dad sees it. He doesn't ask anything, just looks longer than he needs to and turns back to the TV.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В302"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "МОЛОТОК"}}}, {"md": "After New Year's I have to decide. Zoya Arkadyevna wrote in a letter that she'll take me up north, to Yakutia, from the spring, as a field hand. She also wrote: \"Or go study, you dope. The polytechnic in Tomsk. They'll take you by correspondence; I'll call.\" Mom wants me to study. Sonka wants to come to the taiga with me."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I take my exams in Tomsk in January, in the old building with columns that smells of floor wax and wet fur coats. The mineralogy professor, bald, in a knitted vest, lays five rocks in front of me and asks me to name them. I name all five, and then for some reason tap one with his own pencil and say there's a crack in it. He looks at me over his glasses, the way Gosha did."}, {"md": "\"Who taught you that?\" he asks."}, {"md": "\"Kim. Zoya Arkadyevna.\""}, {"md": "\"Ah,\" he says. \"Well, that explains it. She taught us that way too. Top marks.\""}, {"md": "I'll study by correspondence for six years, two months a year, and the rest in the field. In Tomsk I walk along the embankment and look at the Tom. Here it's the same as at home, only farther downstream and wider. I stand at the parapet and watch the water until my hands freeze."}], "B": [{"md": "In spring I fly to Yakutsk. The first time in my life on a plane, and the whole way I look out the window. Below: taiga, taiga, taiga, then rivers, white, still iced over, winding like cracks in old enamel. Zoya Arkadyevna sleeps in the next seat with her cap pulled over her eyes."}, {"md": "\"Study later,\" she says when we land. \"Study later means never. I know. I used to say it too.\""}, {"md": "In Yakutsk it's twenty below in April, and the air is so dry your lips crack. They put us on a helicopter to the site. In the helicopter, for the first time in a year, I think about Vitka."}, {"md": "Vitka is learning to fly now. He writes that a helicopter is a stool with an engine and that he likes it.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "Vitka writes that he's almost paid off the Welder and next year he'll go train as a helicopter pilot, even if he'll be the oldest in his class. A helicopter, he writes, is a stool with an engine, and he knows how to sit on a stool.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}], "C": [{"md": "\"Honest?\" asks Sonka. \"Swear.\""}, {"md": "I swear on the pyrite. Sonka says that doesn't count; you have to swear on the earth. I kneel in the middle of the room and swear on the earth. Mom laughs in the doorway, for the first time that evening."}, {"md": "In July I take Sonka to Mundybash for a week. Zoya Arkadyevna grumbles that this isn't a kindergarten, and then teaches Sonka to tap with the hammer herself. Sonka taps everything: rocks, the porch, Miron. Miron lets her. When she leaves, he gives her Chalkan's old bridle with a brass plate, and Sonka carries it home to Kemerovo like a medal."}, {"md": "The bridle hung over her bed when she was thirty and when she was fifty. I saw it when I visited. I didn't visit often.", "kind": "old"}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Study. Enroll by correspondence at the polytechnic in Tomsk, in geology"}, "B": {"label": "Up north with Zoya Arkadyevna, as a field hand. Study later"}, "C": {"label": "Promise Sonka I'll take her into the field this summer, even just for a week. And the north later"}}}, "В3-05": {"title": "Overflow Ice", "date": "7 February 2012, 14:10", "photo_caption": "A nameless creek. Fifty-one below", "text": [{"md": "At the drill site it's fifty-one below. I know that for sure, because a thermometer hangs by the trailer, an alcohol one, down to sixty below, and every morning the foreman, Petrovich, taps it with his fingernail and says, \"Well, you can live with it.\" The diesel hasn't been switched off since November. If you switch it off, you won't start it again until April."}, {"md": "Today I'm carrying core samples from the rig to camp. Four kilometers along the creek, on the ice, because you can't get through along the bank; the dwarf pine is waist-high. A pack of sample boxes on my back, felt boots with rubber overshoes, quilted trousers, a scarf over my face, and in ten minutes the scarf goes stiff as cardboard from my breath. My eyelashes stick together, and I have to pull them apart with my mitten."}, {"md": "This winter Zoya Arkadyevna is in Yakutsk, at the office, with her ankles. Before she left she said, \"On the creek, watch where there's steam. Where there's steam, there's water. Overflow ice, Samokhin, is alive underneath.\""}, {"md": "I watch. The creek lies like a white ribbon between the larches, flat as a road, and nothing is steaming anywhere. I walk and count my steps so as not to think about how my fingers are freezing. On step one thousand two hundred forty-six the ice under me sags, softly, as if I'd stepped on a sofa."}, {"md": "My leg goes down to the knee, into water. The water under the crust is warm, or so it seems for the first second, because it's zero and the air is fifty-one below. Then it stops being warm. I yank my leg out. The felt boot is heavy, soaked through, and steam rises off it, thick, like off a saucepan. Half a minute later the steam stops. The overshoe turns white. The cloth at my knee seizes up in a crust of ice, and the knee creaks inside it when it bends."}, {"md": "I can't feel the toes of my right foot anymore. Two thousand steps to camp. On the bank stands a dead larch, gray, barkless, and under it a snowdrift, loose, dry as flour."}, {"md": "I was fourteen, it was December and the ice by the far bank of the Tom, and I lay on it with my arms spread and crawled away from the sunken Lada. I remember how afterward Dad rubbed my feet with snow and swore: \"Snow drinks the water, you idiot, before it sets.\" Dad was right that time. He was often right; he just explained it in a way that made you not want to listen.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т07"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЛЁД"}}}, {"md": "In my first season Zoya Arkadyevna made me learn a safety manual, a yellow one from 1981, and quizzed me on it by the fire like the times tables. There was a part about overflow ice. Dry snow soaks up water like blotting paper before the water has time to freeze. Back then I answered and thought I'd never need it in my life.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т07"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЛЁД"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 45}}]}}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I run. Running in a wet felt boot at fifty below is like running with a bucket on your foot. The boot knocks against the ice, wooden, ringing. My lungs burn with every breath; I breathe through the scarf, and in one minute the scarf becomes a crust of ice. At step eight hundred I stop feeling my leg up to the knee. At step one thousand I stop counting."}, {"md": "In camp Petrovich sees me from the trailer door, grabs me by the collar and sits me down on the bunk. The boot has to be cut off with a knife; it won't come off. The foot under it is white, waxy, like a candle. Petrovich rubs it with vodka, swears and rubs it again. My toes come back by night, with such pain that I bite the sleeve of my quilted jacket. Later the nails come off two of the toes. Petrovich says, \"You'll live a hundred years. An idiot, but a healthy one.\""}], "B": [{"md": "I drop into the drift under the larch, tear off the overshoe and start rolling the boot in the snow, rubbing it in, the way you rub in soap. The snow is dry and powdery; it sticks to the wet felt and immediately darkens and gets heavier. I shake it off and scoop up more. Rub, shake off, scoop. After three minutes the boot is white and dry on the outside, in a crust of snow, and inside it's damp but no longer wet."}, {"md": "Then I get up and run along the creek right by the bank, through the dwarf pine, where the ice can't be alive. My toes ache and prickle as if with needles, and that's good; it means they're still mine. In camp Petrovich takes off my boot, feels my foot and grunts. \"Who taught you?\""}, {"md": "\"My dad,\" I say. Petrovich nods, as if he knows my dad. Maybe he does. All dads in Siberia are the same.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т07"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЛЁД"}}}, {"md": "\"Somebody did,\" I say. Petrovich nods and says everyone gets taught, but only one remembered.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т07"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЛЁД"}}}], "C": [{"md": "The larch is dry as gunpowder. I chop off the lower branches with the hatchet, strip birch bark from a neighboring birch, and the matches break in my fingers one after another; my fingers won't bend. It catches on the seventh. I take off the boot right there on the snow, prop it on a stick by the fire, hold my foot over the flame and look at it, white, someone else's, like a foot from an anatomy atlas."}, {"md": "The boot takes two hours to dry. For two hours I sit by the fire on my pack, and the sun goes down behind the hill, and the frost drops another three degrees. The ice on the creek sings. I didn't know it sang. It hums low and long, as if under the ice someone were drawing a bow across a thick string, and the sound comes from the whole creek at once, bank to bank. It makes the back of my head go cold, and the frost doesn't chill like that; this cold sits inside. I listen until the boot is dry."}, {"md": "I reach camp in the dark. Two toes on my right foot never come back. In spring, in Yakutsk, they cut them off, the little toe and the next one. I won't limp, the surgeon says. Won't dance either."}, {"md": "Afterward I looked for those toes in every one of my deaths. They were never there. They were cut off in this life, and so this is the life I live in.", "kind": "old"}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Run for camp. Two thousand steps, while the leg is still mine"}, "B": {"label": "Straight into the drift: roll the wet boot in dry snow while the snow drinks the water. Then run"}, "C": {"label": "Build a fire right here, from the dead larch. Take off the boot and dry out, whatever it costs"}}}, "В3-06": {"title": "The Tusk", "date": "18 July 2015, 17:00", "photo_caption": "Yedoma on a northern river. The tusk hunters' camp", "text": [{"md": "Sardaana is measuring a mammoth with a tape measure. All you can see of the mammoth is one leg; it sticks out of the bluff at the height of my head, brown, in reddish wool, with skin, and Sardaana stands under it on a crate and pulls the tape from the knee to the hoof. The tape is a dressmaker's tape, her mother's, in centimeters, with little flowers on it."}, {"md": "\"Forty-three,\" she says. \"Samokhin, write it down. Come on, write it down, what are you staring at?\""}, {"md": "I write it down. I've known her four days. She was brought to our base from Yakutsk on a passing helicopter, with a backpack, a rifle and the tape, because tusk hunters on the next channel had found a mammoth in the bluff, and her institute had neither a boat nor money. We had a boat. Zoya Arkadyevna took one look at her and said to me, \"You'll take her. Just don't fall in love, you idiot.\" I took her."}, {"md": "The bluff here is forty meters high, gray, striped, all frozen silt and ice. Yedoma, Sardaana says; it's thirty thousand years old. The tusk hunters wash it out with motor pumps, with river water, two hoses like fire hoses, and that leaves tunnels in the bluff, black, round, twenty meters deep. Liquid mud flows out of the tunnels, and it smells so bad my eyes water: thirty thousand years ago everything here rotted and froze, and now it's thawing back out."}, {"md": "The crew boss is called Gena; he's from Belgorod, with a gold tooth and camouflage, and he wants us to leave. In the next tunnel they have a tusk, a second one, still in the wall, and they're going to saw it, because there's no getting it out whole. Sardaana wants them not to saw it. She wants to take out the whole skeleton, photograph it, measure it. That's three days. Gena says he doesn't have three days; he has a buyer from China and a boat on Friday."}, {"md": "A shotgun leans against the diesel barrel. Next to the shotgun sits Gena's nephew, Slavik, about twenty, drinking something from a mug that isn't tea. Since morning he's been looking at Sardaana's backpack, at the rifle on her back, at my field log."}, {"md": "\"Inspection,\" says Slavik for the third time today. \"Uncle Gen, I'm telling you, it's an inspection. They're writing stuff down.\""}, {"md": "The tunnel with the tusk is behind me. It's dripping inside. Its roof has slumped over the day, I can see it: in the morning it was round, and now it sags in the middle like an old mattress."}, {"md": "The back of my head is cold. Cold from there, from the tunnel, and cold from over there, from the barrel where Slavik is, and I can't tell which is stronger.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "\"Gena,\" I say. \"What are you selling the tusk for? Per kilo?\""}, {"md": "Gena names a price. I shake my head the way Mom used to shake it over her frying pans when the woman in the next row priced hers ten rubles lower. I say that the Chinese buyer will pay a third more for a whole one with a document saying it's a mammoth, and that only this woman with the tape measure can write him that document, and that it'll take her three hours to write it while we measure. Gena looks at Sardaana. Sardaana looks at me as if I'd sold her into slavery."}, {"md": "For three hours we measure, photograph and write. Then Gena saws the tusk in half; I turn away, Sardaana doesn't. She writes Gena a paper, on institute letterhead, with the stamp she carries in her backpack. The paper is genuine; it's just that it says the tusk was sawn illegally."}, {"md": "\"He won't read it anyway,\" she says in the boat, and laughs so loudly that geese take off from the sandbar. \"Samokhin, where did you learn to lie like that?\""}, {"md": "\"At the market. In '98. I was nine.\""}, {"md": "She stops laughing and looks at me for a long time."}], "B": [{"md": "I sit down next to Gena on a log and ask for a smoke, though I don't smoke. I tell him I'm from Kemerovo, Dad's a miner, Mom works at the factory, that I'm out here digging permafrost for twenty thousand a month and couldn't care less about their tusk. That she's no inspection either, just a crazy woman with a tape measure, and that if they let her measure the skeleton, she'll leave happy, and if they don't, she'll sit here until Friday singing in Yakut. Sardaana hears the \"crazy woman\" part and doesn't object."}, {"md": "Gena roars with laughter. Slavik doesn't. Gena tells him, \"Put the gun in the tent, you moron, he's a miner's kid.\" Slavik carries the gun away."}, {"md": "We excavate the skeleton until morning, by the white night, and neither Gena nor Slavik bothers us. Toward morning Gena brings two mugs of tea and silently sets them on a crate. Sardaana drinks hers and says to me without looking, \"You told them the truth about yourself. All of it.\" I nod. \"Strange method,\" she says."}], "C": [{"md": "\"Let's go,\" I say."}, {"md": "\"Go where? There's a mammoth here!\""}, {"md": "\"The mammoth's waited thirty thousand years. It'll wait a bit longer. Slavik won't.\""}, {"md": "She swears in Yakut, for a long time, and I don't understand a word, but I understand everything. She gets into the boat on her own, the tape clenched in her fist, and the whole way back to base she sits with her back to me. Behind us, by the bluff, the motor pumps start up."}, {"md": "That evening at the base she comes to my tent with a bottle and two mugs. She pours, drinks, pours again."}, {"md": "\"You're a coward, Samokhin,\" she says. \"But you're a live coward. That's rare. Usually with me it's the other way around.\""}], "D": [{"md": "I go up to the tunnel and tap the roof with my hammer, the gray frozen wall, at the entrance, then a step deeper, then another. Near the entrance the roof answers solidly, tuk-tuk, like a good stump. At the fifth step the sound changes, hollow, like a crate, and afterward the ringing doesn't end in my head but trembles, thin and cold, and in it I hear water running above, beyond a meter of ice."}, {"md": "\"Gena,\" I say. \"Get your people out. In twenty minutes or so it'll come down.\""}, {"md": "Gena looks at me, at the hammer. Then, for no reason anyone knows, he believes me and yells into the tunnel. Two men come out, with a saw and a crowbar. Forty minutes later the roof comes down. It comes down softly, without a crash, the whole tunnel at once, the way an underbaked pie sinks in the oven, and the tusk stays in the wall under three meters of mud and ice. On Friday the Chinese buyer leaves without a tusk."}, {"md": "Sardaana stands beside me and looks at me. \"How did you hear it?\" she asks. I don't know. I say Zoya Arkadyevna taught me. Sardaana says nobody teaches that. In the evening she measures my palm with her tape, from the scar to the wrist, and writes it down in her notebook."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Haggle with Gena the way the women haggled at the market in '98. Let them saw it, but first we get three hours"}, "B": {"label": "Talk to them like people. We're not an inspection; we've got a boat and a tape measure with flowers on it"}, "C": {"label": "Get Sardaana out of here before it starts. Let them saw; the mammoth doesn't care anymore"}, "D": {"label": "Tap the tunnel roof with my hammer. It's trying to tell me something"}}}, "В3-07": {"title": "The Chopper", "date": "12 September 2023, 10:40", "photo_caption": "A nameless ridge. The chopper is down on its left side", "clock_label": "until the fire reaches the tanks", "text": [{"md": "Vitka puts the chopper down on the ridge. I understand that later; right now all I understand is that I'm lying on the wall of the cabin, and the wall has become the floor, and through the windows over my head I can see fog. A minute ago there was a roar, then a shriek, then an impact so hard my teeth clacked and I bit my tongue. There's blood in my mouth."}, {"md": "Vitka has been flying up north for seven years, a captain now, and this is the third season in a row he's flown us, from the base to the site and back. He says he flies us as a favor. In Yakutsk he drops in on Sardaana and me for tea every time, kicks off his regulation tarpaulin boots in the hallway, toe against heel, without bending down, sits on a stool and talks about engines the way other men talk about women. They issued him boots two sizes too big, and he wears them anyway; with a foot wrapping, he says, they fit just right."}, {"md": "He started flying late, at twenty-three, after the Welder. \"I hauled bathtubs for you,\" he says every time, \"now you ride a jump seat in my cabin. That's fair.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "He went into flying straight after the army, and in his very first letter he wrote that the food at flight school was worse than near Chita, but the view from up top made up for it.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "It smells of kerosene. Then it starts to smell of something else, burnt rubber and paint. From above, through the cabin ceiling, from under the cowlings where the engines are, smoke is coming, gray, lazy; it creeps along the wiring and gathers in the corner like water in a bucket. Up there something is crackling, quietly, as if someone were crumpling foil in their fist."}, {"md": "Sardaana sits against the opposite wall, holding her left arm with her right and looking at it in surprise. Her arm is bent between the elbow and the wrist, where it doesn't bend. Two of our workers, Timur and Vanya, are stirring among the backpacks and the core boxes. The flight engineer, Lyokha, lies by the cockpit door face down and isn't stirring."}, {"md": "Vitka shouts from the cockpit. He shouts calmly, the way you shout from a balcony that dinner's ready:"}, {"md": "\"Andryukh! My legs are pinned! Kostya got out through the window, he ran for the first-aid kit! Andryukh, you alive back there?\""}, {"md": "On the wall by the cargo door hangs a fire extinguisher, red, in clamps. The cargo door is above us now, like a hatch, and it has to be opened by hand. The cockpit door is next to Lyokha, narrow, warped. The smoke from the ceiling is already coming down to my shoulders."}, {"md": "My hammer is on my belt, the one from the golets, with the melted pick. It's humming, barely audible, on one note. I want to rip it off and throw it out the window.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В302"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "МОЛОТОК"}}}, {"md": "How many seconds I had, I learned later from the investigation report. At the time I just counted, like when I was a kid jumping off the garage.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"D": [{"md": "I push Sardaana up and out, then Timur, then Vanya, then climb out myself and drag Lyokha after me by the collar; he's heavy as a sack of core. We run down the slope, over wet reindeer moss, a hundred meters or so. I sit down on a rock, and I'm shaking. Sardaana holds her arm and looks at the chopper; she doesn't look at me."}, {"md": "Kostya, the copilot, runs around the cockpit with the first-aid kit and doesn't know what to do with it. Then he drops the kit, climbs into the blister window feet first and fumbles in there for a long time. The smoke from under the cowlings turns black. Kostya climbs out, hauls Vitka out by the arms, halfway, and Vitka screams so that my ears ring even a hundred meters away. Then the two of them fall into the moss. A minute later the cowlings flare up, yellow, hot, and the chopper burns until evening."}, {"md": "Vitka survives. In Yakutsk they spend four months putting his legs back together, and he'll walk with a cane. He has burns on his arms and neck, up to his chin. When I come to see him in the hospital, he looks at me and turns to the wall. He doesn't say \"you abandoned me.\" He doesn't say anything at all."}, {"md": "Kostya got a medal afterward. I drank with him once, in '35, in Khatanga. He didn't recognize me, and I didn't remind him.", "kind": "old"}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Straight into the cockpit, to Vitka. Sardaana will get out; she's stronger than all of us"}, "B": {"label": "First open the cargo door and push Sardaana and the guys up and out. Then Vitka"}, "C": {"label": "Tear down the extinguisher and knock down the fire under the cowlings while it's small. Then Vitka"}, "D": {"label": "Open the door, get everyone out and move well away. Kostya will pull Vitka out through the window; he's already outside"}}}, "В3-08": {"title": "The Cockpit", "date": "12 September 2023, 10:41", "photo_caption": "Mi-8 cockpit. The left seat", "clock_label": "until the fire reaches the tanks", "text": [{"md": "I open the cargo door, heavy as a root-cellar lid, and push them up into the fog: first Sardaana, who hisses with pain and doesn't scream, then Timur, then Vanya. Vanya jumps down and immediately climbs back in for Lyokha, and I yell at him to drag Lyokha downhill, far away. Then I crawl toward the cockpit. The smoke is already below my shoulders; I crawl under it like under a blanket.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В307"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "САЛОН"}}}, {"md": "I tear the extinguisher out of its clamps, climb onto the boxes up by the ceiling and jam the nozzle into the gap under the cowling where the smoke is coming from. The extinguisher hisses, a white cloud hits me in the face, and the crackling in the gap goes quieter, then silent. Meanwhile Sardaana opens the cargo door one-handed and pushes Timur and Vanya up and out. By the time I jump down off the boxes, they're no longer in the cabin. I crawl toward the cockpit.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В307"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОГНЕТУШИТЕЛЬ"}}}, {"md": "I climb over Lyokha and squeeze sideways into the cockpit. Behind me Sardaana is shouting at Timur to open the door, and the door bangs, and light falls into the cabin. I don't turn around.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В307"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КАБИНА"}}}, {"md": "Vitka hangs in the left seat in his harness, sideways, and smiles at me. His face is white, with a graze on his forehead; blood has run into his eyebrow. His legs are down below, under the instrument panel, which has slid and crumpled, and under the pedals, which are no longer where they were."}, {"md": "\"Finally,\" he says. \"I thought you were back there having tea. Give me a yank, huh? Only a hard one. They don't hurt; I can't feel them.\""}, {"md": "Above us the right blister window is open, the copilot's window, the one Kostya climbed out through. In it is sky, gray, fog, and cold air comes down from it to us, while the smoke goes the other way, up from the cabin, through the cockpit, out the window, like up a chimney. Behind us, through the bulkhead, the crackling gets louder. On the cockpit floor lies a pry bar from Lyokha's toolbox."}, {"md": "I look down, under the panel. Vitka's right foot is pinned by a pedal right at the floor, in its boot, and the left is under the panel where I can't see it."}, {"md": "It's quiet under the cowlings. The extinguisher bought me time; I can feel it in my back. The only crackling now is behind us, at the tail, a hundred meters off.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В307"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОГНЕТУШИТЕЛЬ"}}}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I grab him under the arms and pull. Vitka doesn't scream, just breathes out through his teeth, \"sh-sh-sh,\" like a punctured tire. Something below holds, then lets go with a crunch, and we both tumble backward, through the door, onto Lyokha's toolbox. I drag him through the cabin backward, over the backpacks, toward the square of light in the ceiling."}, {"md": "My strength gives out halfway down the cabin. I drag him in jerks, half a meter at a time, and with every jerk something clicks in my back. Under the hatch I boost him up with my shoulder, and the shoulder pops out of its socket; I hear it go. Vanya grabs Vitka by the collar from above. He pulls me out second.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В307"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОГНЕТУШИТЕЛЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ТЕЛ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 25}}]}}, {"md": "Up top, on the reindeer moss, Vitka lies on his back looking into the fog. His right foot has no boot; the boot stayed down there under the pedal, and his shin is bent at an angle. The cowlings flare up when we're already down the slope, yellow, hot, and the chopper burns until evening. Vitka watches it burn and says, \"Good machine. Twenty years. Don't feel sorry for it.\""}], "B": [{"md": "I shove the pry bar under the pedal assembly and throw my whole weight on it. The metal creaks and won't give. I reposition, lean on it again, and the pedal moves two fingers' width, then a palm's. Vitka pulls his right leg out himself, holding the handle over his head. The left one I work free from under the panel for another minute, centimeter by centimeter, and sweat pours off me, though it's cold in the cockpit. The smoke already hangs thick under the ceiling, and I breathe through my sleeve."}, {"md": "Then I take him on my back like a sack and crawl through the cabin toward the light. He helps with his arms. Vanya takes him by the collar from above, Timur by the belt, and the four of us tumble out onto the moss in a heap. The cowlings flare up when we're halfway down the slope."}, {"md": "Vitka's legs are intact, both of them, just crushed, and in Yakutsk they pin them back together. He'll walk with a cane. A month later in the ward he tells me, \"What took you so long back there, huh? I figured you were stripping the pedals for scrap.\""}], "C": [{"md": "I remember pulling him by the shoulders while something below wouldn't let go, and how it burned at my back afterward. It wasn't the pedal holding him. His foot is in a boot, a size 44 tarpaulin boot, wide in the shaft, and the pedal has pinned the boot, but the foot sits loose inside it. I dive under the panel with my knife, slit the shaft top to bottom along the seam, the way you slit the skin off a sausage, and Vitka's foot in its wool sock slides out of the boot by itself."}, {"md": "The left one he yanks out himself, with his hands, swearing. I drag him through the cabin toward the light, and we tumble out onto the moss before I can count to a hundred. The cowlings flare up when we're on the slope. The boot stays down there under the pedal and burns with the chopper."}, {"md": "Vitka lies in the moss in one boot, laughing. \"Tarpaulin,\" he says. \"Andryukh. You murdered my boot. Government issue. They'll dock my pay.\""}], "D": [{"md": "I back out of the cockpit. Vitka looks at me, and his smile doesn't fade; it stays right there on his face, only his eyes change."}, {"md": "\"Go on, then,\" he says. \"Go on, Andryukh. Kostya's coming.\""}, {"md": "I climb out through the cabin, through the hatch, and fall onto the moss. Kostya runs up with the first-aid kit, drops it and climbs into the blister window feet first. I sit on the slope next to Sardaana. She holds her arm and doesn't look at me. Black smoke pours from under the cowlings, then Kostya hauls Vitka halfway out of the window, and Vitka screams, and they both fall down onto the rocks. A minute later the chopper goes up in flames."}, {"md": "Vitka survives. Burns on his arms, his neck and his cheek; four months to put his legs back together; he'll walk with a cane. In the hospital he doesn't talk to me. Later, a year later, on the phone, he says just one thing: \"I don't blame you. Maybe I'd have done the same.\" I don't know whether that's true."}], "E": [{"md": "I remember a drift that never existed, low, black, and how I dragged him backward toward a white square of sky, and how I fell six meters short. Not backward, not to the exit, not to the cabin, where the smoke is going. The exit is here, above us, in the window, with the sky right overhead."}, {"md": "I plant my feet on the armrest of the second seat, grab Vitka under the arms and pull up, not back. He comes out from under the panel more easily than I thought, with a terrible sound down below, and I shove him into the window, head first into the fog, into the hands of Kostya, who's just run up with the first-aid kit. Then I climb out myself. The smoke follows me out like a cork from a bottle."}, {"md": "We lie on the moss. Vitka holds his leg and looks at me, at my hands, and says, \"How'd you know I'd fit through?\" I don't know. The fog over the ridge is lifting, and the hill opposite isn't standing where it stood when we came down. That's how it seems to me. I look at it for a long time, until the chopper goes up behind us."}], "F": [{"md": "I lie on my side under the instrument panel, cheek on the floor, wet with oil, and find his right foot by touch. The tarpaulin boot is pinned by the pedal right at the sole, but the shaft stands loose, with a foot wrapping flapping inside it. Up above, Vitka says it tickles."}, {"md": "My knife is a folding one, a field knife. I cut the shaft down from the knee along the seam, and the tarpaulin parts like cardboard. Then I pull Vitka's leg up by the trouser leg, and it comes out of the boot along with the wrapping, bare, white. The boot stays under the pedal."}, {"md": "The left one I get out the same way, by touch. I haul him into the cabin by his harness straps. Smoke is already flowing over the panel, and behind us, at the tail, the crackling is louder."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Yank him by the shoulders, hard, backward, through the door into the cabin"}, "B": {"label": "Pry the pedals apart with the bar, free both legs, and only then carry him out through the cabin"}, "C": {"label": "\"I remember it wasn't the pedal holding him.\" Cut the boot with my knife, slip his foot out of the shaft and then pull"}, "D": {"label": "There's no time. Leave him to Kostya and get out through the cabin myself"}, "F": {"label": "Dive under the panel with my knife and slit the boot shaft. The boot's too big for him; his foot will come out"}, "E": {"label": "\"I've dragged him to an exit once before. Not in this life.\" Not back into the cabin but up, through the window, to the sky"}}}, "В7-01": {"title": "The Operator", "date": "October 2007", "photo_caption": "The ammonia unit. Night shift", "text": [{"md": "In August Azot, the big chemical plant, takes me on as a trainee operator in the ammonia unit. Medical board, safety briefing, overalls two sizes too big, and a gas mask in a shoulder bag that you're supposed to carry everywhere, even to the canteen. At the briefing the foreman says ammonia smells like smelling salts, and if you smell it, you run crosswind. I write that down in a notebook and feel incredibly grown-up."}, {"md": "A shift is twelve hours. At night the control panels hum in the operator room, the pressure-gauge needles stand still, and outside the window the synthesis columns glow in the steam, tall as a nine-story building. The senior operator, Gena Shubin, who has a mustache like Lev Leshchenko, the old crooner, teaches me the main thing. You keep the logbook neat, and you don't touch anything until he tells you to."}, {"md": "\"Here, Andryukha, you only make a mistake once,\" says Gena, pouring tea from his thermos into the cup lid. \"Whoever they put in your place is the one who learns from it.\""}, {"md": "I sleep in the daytime, and on Tuesdays and Thursdays I go to night classes at the technical university, chemical engineering. Half the group is from the plant, like me, and in lectures everybody sleeps with their eyes open. That's the first thing you learn there. I give my trainee's wages to Mom. Mom puts them in the china cabinet behind the crystal and says she's saving for my wedding. Sonka laughs so hard she falls off the couch."}, {"md": "On Saturdays Vitka and I work off what we owe Welder. We load crates at a warehouse past Pionersky, and we don't ask what's in them. I tell Mom I've got a side job at the market.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "Vitka writes from near Chita that he's got eight months left till he's discharged and that he's learned to sleep standing up. I write back that so have I, only here it's called the night shift.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "In the middle of October Mom brings an envelope into my room. A real one, paper, with a stamp and a Novosibirsk postmark, and our address on it in Nika's handwriting, round, with curlicues."}, {"md": "Nika has filled three sheets of graph paper. She's a first-year at medical school, her roommate in the dorm is from Barnaul and snores. The anatomy room, Nika writes, isn't scary, it's funny; Novosibirsk is huge, and nobody there knows anybody. She's writing by hand because on ICQ I answer her with a single \"ok.\" At the very end, after her signature, she's added in small letters: \"Don't go drowning on me, Samokhin. I'm far away now. There's nobody to count.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_НИКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "The letter is short, one page. Nika's at medical school in Novosibirsk, everything's fine, and she asks how Vitka's doing. At the end, a polite \"write if anything comes up.\" I read it twice and put it on the windowsill.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_НИКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "That same evening Olya from the third stairwell catches me outside our building. We've lived in the same yard since kindergarten; she's a year younger and works as a cashier at the Maria-Ra supermarket on the corner. She rings up my Baltika without asking for ID. Right now she's got a puffy coat on over her uniform, and her name tag says \"OLGA. Happy to help.\""}, {"md": "\"I've got two tickets for *Saw IV* at the Moskva,\" she says. \"I'm scared to go alone. And they say you're a drowned man, so nothing scares you anymore.\""}, {"md": "And in the afternoon Gena called to ask if I'd take his Friday night. His mother-in-law has a big birthday."}, {"md": "Gena Shubin worked thirty years in that unit and never made a single mistake. I made mistakes more often, and every time they buried me somewhere other than here.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I write it on Friday, on the night shift, on the backs of old pages from the shift log. About the columns in the steam, about Gena and his mustache, about how ammonia really does smell like smelling salts and how at night the unit hums like a big refrigerator. Not a word about water. It comes to six pages. Gena looks over my shoulder and says, \"That's it, the boy's a goner.\""}, {"md": "Nika answers two weeks later: \"Samokhin, it turns out you can write sentences longer than three words.\" For New Year's she comes to stay with Vitka's mom, and on the thirtieth we ride the ice slide on Soviets Square until my jeans are soaked right through."}], "B": [{"md": "At *Saw* Olya grabs my hand at every bloody scene, and there are a lot of them, so we come out of the movie already holding hands, out of habit. At a kiosk she buys one shawarma for the two of us and asks if it's true I drowned that time."}, {"md": "\"It's true,\" I say."}, {"md": "\"You're lying,\" she says, and laughs, and I don't argue. I walk her to the third stairwell. Nika's letter stays on my windowsill till spring, and then Mom puts it away in the china cabinet."}], "C": [{"md": "I take Gena's Friday, and then another one, and another. They pay overtime in an envelope, and with the first one I buy Mom a phone, a Sony Ericsson with a camera. Mom never takes a single picture with it; she's afraid of breaking it."}, {"md": "In November, on a double shift, a girl walks into the operator room in a hard hat over a headscarf, carrying a metal sampler that looks like a milk can. \"That's Olya from the central lab, my niece,\" says Gena. \"Don't stare like that, she bites.\" Olya takes her sample, signs the log, and on her way out says I smell of ammonia. I say so does she. I never answer Nika's letter."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Write Nika a long letter back"}, "B": {"label": "Go see *Saw* with Olya from the next stairwell"}, "C": {"label": "Take the extra shift. The money matters more"}}}, "В7-02": {"title": "The Wedding", "date": "July 2010", "photo_caption": "The Azot canteen. \"Gorko!\"", "text": [{"md": "By the spring of 2010 I'm a grade-four operator, no more \"trainee,\" and in night school I'm moving up to third year. Buttoning my jacket for me in the front hall, the way he did in first grade, Dad says, \"Get married, Andryukha. Or you'll drink yourself to death on that ammonia of yours, like everybody.\" He drinks on Fridays himself, moderately and on schedule, like down the mine."}, {"md": "Vitka and I had worked off our debt to Welder by the spring of '09. Welder shook our hands with that soft warm palm of his and said, \"You're free, boys.\" What was in those crates I never found out, and I don't plan to.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "I've got a whole winter-boot box full of Nika's letters, about forty of them. Two New Year's she spent at Vitka's mom's, and both times I saw her off at the station and both times didn't know what to say on the platform. In March she transferred from Novosibirsk to our medical academy, fourth year. \"Not because of you, Samokhin, don't flatter yourself. The dorms are better here.\" In April, in my parents' kitchen, she said in front of Mom, \"Are you going to propose, or do I have to do it myself?\" Mom dropped the ladle.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В701"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "Olya and I have been going out for two and a half years, since that *Saw*. She knows my shift schedule better than I do, and after a night shift she meets me at the bus stop with meat pies from Maria-Ra, still warm. Mom loves her for being \"a good homemaker,\" Dad because she laughs at his jokes, even the old ones. And Sonka secretly wears her lipstick.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В701"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "Olya from the lab and I have been seeing each other on a schedule for three years now. Every other time her shifts line up with mine, and then she comes into the operator room with her sampler and stands by my panel a minute longer than a sample takes. Gena pretends not to see, and at New Year's he announces in front of the whole unit, \"Marry her while I'm in a good mood. She's my only niece.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В701"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СМЕНА"}}}, {"md": "We set the wedding for July. The union committee at Azot gives us the plant canteen at half price, Mom spends a week chopping Olivier salad by the basinful and hides the vodka from Dad in the washing machine. We buy a suit at the Central Market, black, with sequins on the lapels. The seller swears you can't see the sequins, and you can see them from the other end of the row."}, {"md": "Vitka is going to be my best man. He's bought himself a pink tie and wears it even to work, to get used to it.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "Over the years Vitka and I drifted apart, I don't know how, without any falling-out. My best man is going to be Gena Shubin, in his one and only suit, the one he got married in himself.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "In the end it was Dad who tied my tie, in the kitchen, without a word, in one motion. I don't remember any other advice he gave me about married life.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "We sign the register at the Wedding Palace. Nika is in a dress her mother carried on her lap from Novosibirsk, all four hours on the bus. The canteen smells of bleach and herring salad, there are balloons on the tile wall. The guests shout \"Gorko!\" (it means \"bitter,\" and you have to kiss to sweeten it) and count out loud together. At thirty Nika pulls away from me and whispers, \"I've counted higher than that, Samokhin.\""}, {"md": "Vitka in his pink tie gives a toast, loses his place, waves his hand and just says, \"You're both nuts. Live a long time.\" He kisses his cousin on the forehead and slaps me on the back so hard I choke on my champagne.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "Toward morning Vitka finds me on the steps, sits down next to me and rolls a cigarette between his fingers for a long time. \"Listen. I keep dreaming about the mine, like a beam's got me pinned, it's dark, and you're sitting there next to me. What's that supposed to mean, huh?\" He lights up and doesn't say any more about it.", "if": {"op": "lived", "id": "В1-09"}}, {"md": "Vitka sits on the bride's side, as her cousin, and hardly talks to me all evening. Only at the end, already in his jacket, he shakes my hand and says, \"Take care of her, Vodyanoy.\" Gena Shubin dances with my mom to \"Akh, eta svadba,\" the song every Russian wedding plays, and steps on her feet.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "Sonka catches the bouquet. She's twelve and so embarrassed she nearly cries. Toward morning Dad sits on the canteen steps, smoking and looking at the Azot stacks, where the lights burn all night."}], "B": [{"md": "We sign the register at the Wedding Palace. Olya rented her dress from a bridal shop on Kirov Street, her veil keeps slipping, and once it falls right into the salad. Olya laughs so hard the whole canteen laughs with her, even the waitresses. The guests shout \"Gorko!\" (it means \"bitter,\" and you have to kiss to sweeten it) and count, and at twenty Olya says, \"That's it, my mascara.\""}, {"md": "Vitka in his pink tie gives a toast, loses his place, waves his hand and just says, \"Andryukha, you're a lucky bastard. Don't forget it.\" Then he spends half the evening teaching Olya's dad to drink with his arm hooked through somebody else's.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "Gena Shubin gives a toast about workplace safety in married life. \"You only make a mistake once,\" he says, and points his fork at me. Everybody laughs except me.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}}, {"md": "Sonka catches the bouquet. She's twelve and so embarrassed she nearly cries. Toward morning Dad sits on the canteen steps, smoking and looking at the Azot stacks, where the lights burn all night."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Marry Nika"}, "B": {"label": "Marry Olya"}}}, "В7-03": {"title": "The Icicle", "date": "4 February 2011, 11:20", "photo_caption": "A yard in Rudnichny. Three hundred meters to the pharmacy", "text": [{"md": "Varya was born on the seventh of January, Orthodox Christmas, seven pounds even. My mother-in-law thinks it's a sign. A sign of what, she doesn't say."}, {"md": "This sign screams every evening from six to midnight, steady as a shift schedule, knees pulled up to her belly. The pediatrician from the clinic says it's colic, that it'll pass by three months, and that she needs a walk every day, frost or no frost. We're renting a one-room apartment in Rudnichny, in a five-story block two yards over from my parents'. I'm just off a night shift; my wife hasn't slept in two days."}, {"md": "\"Espumisan, and a gas-relief tube,\" says Nika, red-eyed, stuffing money into my pocket. \"I got an A in pediatrics, Samokhin, and I can't cope with my own kid. Walk her for a good long while. Let me sleep for at least an hour.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "\"Espumisan,\" says Olya without opening her eyes, stuffing money into my pocket. \"And dill water, if they have it. And walk for a good long while. Let me sleep for at least an hour, I'm starting to mix you up with the fridge.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "We bought the stroller through *Iz Ruk v Ruki*, the classifieds paper, green and heavy as a Zaporozhets. Varya sleeps in it in a snowsuit bag; you can only see her nose, and frost is building up on the edge of the blanket from her breathing. For a week there was a thaw, and yesterday it dropped to eighteen below, and everything that had melted froze, hanging on the roofs and lying on the ground."}, {"md": "The pharmacy, a Gubernskie Apteki, is across the road by the bus stop. There are three ways to get there, and I've known all of them since I was a kid."}, {"md": "Along our building, on the footpath the whole yard has polished to a shine. It's the shortest way, and it runs right under the wall."}, {"md": "Along the road, on the shoulder. The sidewalk there is buried, the grader has pushed the snow into a waist-high ridge, and behind the grader the cars are moving, slowly, but moving."}, {"md": "Through the garages. Steam drifts over them from the heating main, the snow there is always half-melted and gray, and it smells like a wet rag."}, {"md": "I stop at the corner of the building and fix Varya's blanket. She smacks her lips in her sleep. Somewhere behind the garages the grader is grinding along, and at the edge of the roof above the footpath something long and clear is hanging, and the sun breaks inside it."}], "after": {"D": [{"md": "I turn the stroller around and haul it back into the stairwell. It smells of cats and somebody's cabbage soup. I stand by the radiator for fifteen minutes, holding the handle with both hands and watching the door through the cloudy window like it's a TV. Varya sleeps. The grader rumbles past the entrance, and behind it, on the turn, a gray Toyota slowly slides sideways and lies down in an empty snowbank. The driver gets out, kicks a tire and swears loud enough for the whole yard to hear."}, {"md": "I walk along the cleared shoulder, pushing the stroller ahead of me with both hands like a wheelbarrow full of cement. At the pharmacy I buy Espumisan and, for some reason, a bottle of green antiseptic. On the way back I look across the yard at our footpath. An icicle is lying across it, broken into three pieces, each the size of my leg. It's lying exactly where I lay with my cheek on the ice.", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-03.A", "life": true}}, {"md": "I walk along the cleared shoulder, pushing the stroller ahead of me with both hands like a wheelbarrow full of cement. At the pharmacy I buy Espumisan and, for some reason, a bottle of green antiseptic. On the way back I hear a short crack behind me, by our building, like the ice on the Tom in spring. I don't turn around.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-03.A", "life": true}}}, {"md": "At home I leave the stroller in the front hall, sit down on the floor next to it without taking off my jacket, and stay like that until my wife comes out to ask what happened. \"Nothing,\" I say. \"I got the Espumisan.\" I've just died, and then I was standing at the corner of the building alive, a minute before. That doesn't happen, but here's the pharmacy receipt in my fist."}, {"md": "At night, when Varya finally falls asleep, I lie there and think, not about myself. Where I stopped being, she was left alone in the cold."}, {"md": "The stroller was rolling downhill, toward the road. Who stopped it?", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-03.A", "life": true}}, {"md": "She was screaming in the snowbank right by the road. Who got her out of there?", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-03.B", "life": true}}, {"md": "She was crying by the garages, over a round hole in the snow. Who heard her?", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-03.C", "life": true}}, {"md": "The woman from the bus stop, the driver of the little PAZ bus, a neighbor in track pants? I'll never know. Somebody caught her, and I was lying right there and didn't see who."}], "E": [{"md": "I turn the stroller away from the wall and push it straight into the deep snow, across the yard, past the buried slide and the iron mushroom over the sandbox. The snow is knee-deep, the wheels sink, and the stroller goes like a tractor through a plowed field. Varya sleeps. The woman on the fifth floor watches me from her vent window like I'm an idiot: there's a path right there."}, {"md": "Behind me, by the wall, there's a short crack, and then a heavy, crunching blow, like somebody dropped a sack of glass off the roof. I don't turn around until I reach the bus stop. An icicle is lying across the footpath, broken into three pieces, each the size of my leg. The woman in the window crosses herself."}, {"md": "At the pharmacy I buy Espumisan and, for some reason, a bottle of green antiseptic. I go back through the deep snow too, in my own tracks. At home I leave the stroller in the front hall, sit down on the floor next to it without taking off my jacket, and stay like that until my wife comes out to ask what happened. \"Nothing,\" I say. \"I got the Espumisan.\" The pharmacy receipt is in my fist."}, {"md": "At night, when Varya finally falls asleep, I lie there and think about who stopped the stroller that was rolling downhill, toward the road. The woman from the bus stop, the driver of the little PAZ bus, a neighbor in track pants? Somebody caught it, and I was lying on the ice and didn't see who."}], "G": [{"md": "I push the stroller through the garages, along the most trampled path, step for step behind somebody in size-twelve felt boots. Steam hangs over the heating main, the snow is gray and soft. I see the smooth white patch from a long way off: round, even, without a single footprint, though everything around it is trampled. I go around it along the garage wall, and my shoulder comes away covered in rust."}, {"md": "On the way back I carry a stick I picked up by the trash bins. I poke it into the smooth patch, and the stick goes into the snow all the way, along with my arm up to the elbow. Warmth and wet-rag smell come up from below. I set an empty diesel drum from the end garage over the hole and go home."}, {"md": "At home I sit down on the floor in the front hall next to the stroller, without taking off my jacket. My wife asks what happened. \"Nothing,\" I say. \"I got the Espumisan.\" The next day the drum is still standing over the manhole, and people have already trampled a path around it."}, {"md": "At night I think about who heard Varya crying by the garages, over the round hole in the snow. I'll never know. Somebody heard her, and I was lying down below, on the pipes, and couldn't answer her."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Cut along the building, on the icy footpath"}, "B": {"label": "By the road, on the shoulder"}, "C": {"label": "Through the garages"}, "D": {"label": "\"I've seen this already.\" Wait in the stairwell until the grader goes by, and walk only with the stroller out in front of me, both hands on it"}, "E": {"label": "\"I remember what's hanging over the footpath.\" Go around through the middle of the yard, through the deep snow, away from the walls"}, "F": {"label": "\"I remember when it falls.\" Run under the wall while it's still hanging"}, "G": {"label": "\"I remember where the manhole is.\" Through the garages, but in other people's footprints, and around the smooth white patch"}}}, "В7-04": {"title": "The Banya", "date": "9 March 2013, 21:30", "photo_caption": "Yagunovsky. My father-in-law's banya", "clock_label": "until we both fall asleep", "text": [{"md": "Last spring we took out a mortgage on a two-room apartment in Lesnaya Polyana, ninth floor, twenty years. Twenty years seems to me like a sentence you could serve and walk out of. Our windows look out on pine trees, the elevator works every other day, and Varya, who's two, calls it \"the elevator-schmelevator.\""}, {"md": "Last fall Nika's father retired from his institute in Akademgorodok, sold his garage, and bought a dacha in Yagunovsky, \"closer to the granddaughter.\" My mother-in-law travels back and forth to Novosibirsk and calls it \"living for two families.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "Olya's father spent forty years in a crane cab at the coking plant, and he built the dacha in Yagunovsky himself, log by log. The banya too, the steam bathhouse out back, and he's prouder of that than of his granddaughter.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "For the Eighth of March, Women's Day, we're invited to my father-in-law's dacha. The women are in the house, Varya is asleep on top of the big brick stove, and my father-in-law and I are in the steam room of the bathhouse for the third time. Outside it's twelve below, the snow blue in the moonlight. He stokes it his own way, hot and without a break, and he doesn't trust anybody else with the damper."}, {"md": "The coals are smoldering in the stove, and little blue flames run over them, light as on a gas burner. My head is heavy, my temples are pounding, like I've just worked two shifts back to back. My father-in-law sits on the bench all crimson, smiling at the wall."}, {"md": "All through second year at night school they drilled carbon monoxide into us. Blue flame over the coals, a heavy head, cherry-red cheeks. I passed that exam with a B, and now I'm sitting here trying to remember what came next on the exam card.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 35}}}, {"md": "My father-in-law talks slowly, almost syllable by syllable, and laughs at the wrong moments, at the birch whisk. I've never seen him like this, not even at the wedding, where he drank more than anyone.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ЭМП"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "The back of my neck goes cold, exactly like that time at the corner of the apartment block, with the stroller.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "\"Andryukha,\" says my father-in-law, waving at the flue. \"Shut the damper. Or all the heat'll go.\""}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "I kick open the door to the changing room, then the one to the outside. The frost bursts into the steam room in a white cloud. My father-in-law is heavy, wet and slippery as a burbot, and doesn't understand what I want from him. I drag him under the arms, he braces his heels against the threshold, and we both fall in the snow. The snow hisses under him."}, {"md": "He throws up in the snowdrift. Then he sits up, shakes his head and asks, \"What's got into you, Andryukha?\" My wife runs out of the house in rubber galoshes on bare feet, my mother-in-law behind her with a towel. The ambulance from Kemerovo takes forty minutes. The doctor takes his blood pressure and says that ten minutes more and we wouldn't have woken him."}, {"md": "\"How did you know?\" my wife asks that night.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 35}}}, {"md": "\"Blue flames over the coals,\" I say. \"It was on the exam.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 35}}}, {"md": "\"How did you know?\" my wife asks that night.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 35}}}, {"md": "\"His face wasn't his face,\" I say. I can't explain it any better. She looks at me for a long time in the dark, and then she holds me the way she hasn't since the wedding.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "РАЗ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 35}}}, {"md": "Carbon monoxide has no smell. To this day, in every banya I walk into, the first thing I do is sniff the air, and people laugh at me.", "kind": "old"}], "C": [{"md": "I step out into the changing room, then onto the porch, and sit down right in the snow in nothing but a towel. The frost hits my head like a bucket of ice water, I feel sick, and I sit there until my head starts working. It works slowly."}, {"md": "The chill at the back of my neck goes away as soon as I'm out in the air, and that calms me down.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "About three minutes later I realize my father-in-law hasn't come out."}, {"md": "He's lying on the lower bench with his cheek on the wet boards, and he isn't snoring. I drag him out into the snow by the legs and yell toward the house, and my wife, in galoshes on bare feet, does CPR the way they taught her in the course, counting out loud. The ambulance from Kemerovo takes forty minutes. The doctor doesn't even open his bag."}, {"md": "We bury him on Wednesday. At the wake my mother-in-law tells everyone that I pulled him out and that none of it is my fault. People feel sorry for me. Nobody asks why I walked out of the banya alone and sat on the porch for three minutes while he lay in there. I keep waiting to wake up on the bench in the steam room and see him wave at the flue again. That happens in dreams. It doesn't happen here: I wasn't the one who died."}, {"md": "The gift only looks after me. I understood that that night in Yagunovsky, and too late.", "kind": "old"}], "D": [{"md": "I remember wet boards under my cheek and the snoring stopping. So I don't think. I kick the door open, grab my father-in-law under the arms and drag him outside into the frost, and he swears and grabs at the doorframe."}, {"md": "In the snow he throws up. Then he sits in the snowdrift, red, steaming, grumbling that I've let all the heat out and now the banya has to be fired up all over again. My wife runs out in her galoshes. I stand over them and don't say anything. I'll never be able to explain to anybody how I know the way people fall asleep in there."}, {"md": "The chill at the back of my neck lets go as soon as my father-in-law starts cursing."}, {"md": "That night my father-in-law sits in the kitchen wrapped in a blanket, drinking tea from a saucer. \"My old man got gassed the same way,\" he tells my wife, \"in sixty-eight, in Granddad's banya out near Izhmorka.\" My wife nods; she's known that since she was little. But at the wedding my father-in-law told me his father drowned in the Tom, fishing, and showed me the spot.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 10}}, "drift": true}], "E": [{"md": "I remember a rented one-room place by the train station, a gas water heater on the other side of the wall, and a hand on the blanket that wouldn't lift. I've never had a water heater like that. The blue flames over the coals flicker exactly like those reflections on the ceiling, and I lose count of them at seven."}, {"md": "So I get up, kick open the door to the changing room, then the one outside, and yank the damper all the way open, burning my palm. My father-in-law is heavy, wet and slippery as a burbot. I drag him under the arms, and we both fall in the snow. The snow hisses under him."}, {"md": "He throws up in the snowdrift. Then he sits up, shakes his head and asks, \"What's got into you, Andryukha?\" I sit next to him in nothing but a towel, watching the snow hiss under him, and I can't answer. The one in the one-room place by the station was lying there alone, and there was nobody to drag him out."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Shut the damper early, so the heat stays in"}, "B": {"label": "Drag my father-in-law out into the snow and throw the door wide open"}, "C": {"label": "My head's heavy, so I've overdone the steam. Step out alone for some air"}, "D": {"label": "\"I remember how people fall asleep in here.\" Throw the door open and haul him outside"}, "E": {"label": "\"I've fallen asleep in air like this. Not in this life.\" Kick the door open, yank the damper out and drag him into the snow"}}}, "В7-05": {"title": "Passing", "date": "9 January 2016, 13:10", "photo_caption": "The Novosibirsk highway. Before Yurga", "text": [{"md": "We got the Lada Granta on credit the spring before last, silver, straight from the factory, and I still wipe the dashboard with my sleeve. Kolka was born in October, nine pounds even, and he hollers in a bass voice, like his grandpa. In January, over the holidays, we're driving him to Novosibirsk to show him to the family."}, {"md": "We're going to Nika's mother, who's only seen her grandson on Skype and cried every time that he's \"the spitting image of a Samokhin.\" Honestly, that flatters me.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "We're going to Olya's godmother, Aunt Galya, who baptizes, reconciles and matchmakes the whole family, and without her blessing nobody in that family so much as gets a cat.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "Kolka is asleep in his car seat in the back, my wife next to him. Varya is five; she sits in her booster seat watching *Smeshariki*, the cartoon, on the tablet with the sound off, because the sound will wake Kolka. We've been on the Novosibirsk highway for two hours, and for the last twenty minutes there's been a semi in front of us."}, {"md": "The semi is long, with a trailer, and snow dust comes up off its tarp at us, white and thick. The wipers can't keep up; there's nothing to see ahead but the tarp with the ad on it."}, {"md": "The tarp has mayonnaise painted on it, a jar as tall as a man.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 10}}, {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-05.A", "life": true}]}}}, {"md": "The tarp has dumplings painted on it, a whole mountain of them, with sour cream. I remember for sure that last time there was mayonnaise here, a jar as tall as a man.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 10}}, {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-05.A", "life": true}]}, "drift": true}, {"md": "Sixty kilometers an hour. A straight stretch all the way to Yurga, a dashed center line, no oncoming traffic for a long time."}, {"md": "\"Dad, are we gonna pass it?\" Varya asks without looking up from the tablet."}, {"md": "\"Samokhin,\" Nika says from the back, \"you've got two kids and a doctor in this car. Don't speed.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "\"Andryush,\" Olya says from the back, \"just don't speed. Aunt Galya can wait. She's been waiting forty years.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "The back of my neck goes cold, but so do my feet; the Granta's heater only blows on the windshield, so it's hard to tell which is which.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "Somebody behind my ear is whispering, quietly, under the drone of the engine. I can't make out the words.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "Kolka doesn't remember this trip, of course. All Varya remembers is that the Novosibirsk relatives had a dog that ate tangerines peel and all.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "I take my foot off the gas and settle in behind the semi, about fifty meters back. Sixty it is. Varya sighs the way only five-year-olds sigh and buries herself in the tablet again."}, {"md": "A couple of kilometers later a KAMAZ truck bursts out of the white cloud ahead, in the oncoming lane. It's passing somebody of its own, right down the middle of the road, and it roars past us so close that the Granta rocks in the wind. I watch it in the mirror and start counting. The semi's speed, the length of its trailer, how many seconds I would have been short. It comes to about three."}, {"md": "In Yurga we stop at a roadside café. Varya eats dumplings with sour cream, my wife feeds Kolka, and I sit over my tea and can't seem to finish it."}], "C": [{"md": "I put on the turn signal and pull off at the gas station by the turn-off. Varya goes to the restroom with her mom, Kolka wakes up and demands to be fed. I drink coffee from the machine, bitter as diesel, and watch the semi head off down the straight in its cloud."}, {"md": "Twenty minutes later we drive on. Fifteen kilometers on, blue lights are flashing on the shoulder. A KAMAZ is lying on its side in the ditch, and in the oncoming lane stands what used to be somebody's Lada Nine. A traffic cop waves his baton at us: keep moving, don't stop. I drive past slowly."}, {"md": "My wife doesn't say anything all the way to Novosibirsk. Varya asks why the truck is lying on its side, and I tell her it got tired. Somebody else went to pass today instead of me."}], "D": [{"md": "I remember two headlights at the height of my head and the steering wheel in my chest as clearly as Dad's garage. So I slow down to forty and hug the right edge, right up against the snow ridge. The semi pulls away ahead."}, {"md": "A minute later a KAMAZ bursts out of the white cloud in the oncoming lane and roars past us, so close I can see a little icon on the dashboard in the driver's cab. The Granta rocks. Varya looks up from the tablet and asks, \"Dad, what's wrong?\""}, {"md": "\"Nothing,\" I say. \"Cramp in my leg.\""}, {"md": "At the gas station in Yurga I get out to wipe the headlights. My Granta is cherry red. I picked the silver one myself, the spring before last, at the factory, and argued with my wife, who liked the cherry one. My wife rolls down her window: \"Why are you petting it? You'll freeze.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 10}}, "drift": true}], "E": [{"md": "I remember fog on the R-255, which I've never had in this life, and a gray wall across both lanes, a wet tarp with the letters \"…TRANS,\" and a drop on an iron beam. I remember a crunch inside me, long, like footsteps on a snow crust. My hands on the wheel remember being forty-four and alone in the car, and I'm twenty-six, and in the back Kolka is snuffling in his car seat."}, {"md": "I take my foot off the gas and settle in behind the semi, about fifty meters back. Sixty it is. Varya sighs the way only five-year-olds sigh. A couple of kilometers later a KAMAZ bursts out of the white cloud in the oncoming lane and roars past, so close the Granta rocks."}, {"md": "\"Why are you so white?\" my wife asks from the back."}, {"md": "\"Thinking,\" I say. In Yurga I buy the kids dumplings and can't eat a single one myself."}], "F": [{"md": "I remember those two headlights at the height of my head down to the second. So I sit on the semi's tail and count. When the KAMAZ roars past in the oncoming lane and the Granta rocks, I pull out from behind the semi right away and floor it. Kolka is asleep, Varya is watching the tablet, my wife is holding the grab handle over the door."}, {"md": "The pass is long; the trailer goes on and on like a fence. The oncoming lane is empty, I know that in advance, and I still don't breathe until the end of the trailer. Then we're ahead, alone on the white straight, and my wife says quietly, so as not to wake Kolka, \"You're insane.\" I say it's faster to Grandma's this way. We get to Novosibirsk an hour early, and all evening I can't get my hands warm."}, {"md": "The family in Novosibirsk lives on Krasny Prospekt, in a building with an archway; I've been there a dozen times. Today there's a two-story spruce growing by the entrance, all covered in snow, that was never here before, and my wife says her great-grandfather planted it after the war. I stand under it with Kolka in my arms, brushing off the snow that falls from the branches.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}, "drift": true}], "G": [{"md": "Now I remember two of them. The first KAMAZ roars past, the Granta rocks, and I don't move, I sit on the semi's tail and count to forty. At thirty-one the second one comes out of the white, just as rust-orange, with the same little icon on the dashboard, as if the first had come back for me. My wife says from the back, \"Lord.\""}, {"md": "At forty I pull out from behind the semi and floor it. The oncoming lane is empty. I don't trust it until the end of the trailer, and I don't trust it for another ten kilometers after. The world I remembered isn't here anymore, and I only recognize this one by my deaths."}, {"md": "We get to Novosibirsk an hour early, and all evening I can't get my hands warm."}, {"md": "The family in Novosibirsk lives on Krasny Prospekt, in a building with an archway; I've been there a dozen times. Today there's a two-story spruce growing by the entrance, all covered in snow, that was never here before, and my wife says her great-grandfather planted it after the war. I stand under it with Kolka in my arms, brushing off the snow that falls from the branches.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}, "drift": true}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Pass the semi while the road's straight"}, "B": {"label": "Stay behind the semi till Yurga"}, "C": {"label": "Let it go, pull off at the gas station and wait"}, "D": {"label": "\"I've seen that oncoming KAMAZ.\" Slow down and hug the right edge"}, "E": {"label": "\"I've passed a semi blind before. Not in this life.\" Stay on its tail and don't stick my nose out till Yurga"}, "F": {"label": "\"I know when the KAMAZ goes by.\" Pass the semi right behind it, while the other lane is empty"}, "G": {"label": "\"I remember the second KAMAZ too.\" Let both go by, and only then pass"}}}, "В7-06": {"title": "The Garland", "date": "31 December 2019, 03:40", "photo_caption": "Lesnaya Polyana. The kids' room, 30 December", "clock_label": "before the fire gets into the hallway", "text": [{"md": "This year Varya demanded we put the tree up in her room. She's eight, she's a grown-up now. On the evening of the thirtieth the two of us decorate it, a plastic tree from the top shelf of the closet, and Varya bosses me around about where to hang the ornaments. I bought the lights at Fix Price, the one-price discount chain, for ninety-nine rubles: Chinese, a hundred bulbs. They blink in eight modes, and Varya picks the dumbest one, \"wave.\""}, {"md": "Before bed I go around the apartment myself: vent windows, range hood, stove. My wife laughs that I'm like an old man. I don't explain. Since that banya I've still got the fumes stuck in my throat.", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-04.A", "life": true}, "body": true}, {"md": "Since the fall Varya has been propping a chair against her door at night so Kolka can't come in after her with his toy cars. Kolka is four; he comes in after everybody, always. At first my wife and I argued about the chair, and then we gave up."}, {"md": "I wake up at 3:40 to a smell. Burning plastic, sweet and acrid, like at the plant last week when a panel shorted out. My wife is asleep, Kolka snuffling against her side. The hallway is dark, except under the door of Varya's room there's a strip of orange light, too bright for Christmas lights, and gray smoke is creeping out from under it."}, {"md": "I yank the handle. The door gives a centimeter and stops against the chair."}, {"md": "\"Varya!\" I yell. \"Varya, open up!\""}, {"md": "Behind the door somebody coughs, thin, a child's cough, and then stops coughing. Something is crackling, maybe the tree, maybe the curtains. Behind me my wife screams my name and snatches Kolka up. Out on the landing, I know, there's a red cabinet with a fire extinguisher; I walk past it to the elevator every day."}, {"md": "There's an icy cold at the back of my neck, worse than at the apartment block, worse than on the highway. As if somebody's holding a hand there and won't let go.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "Since the banya my mother-in-law lives with us half the year, sleeps on a cot in the living room and takes sleeping pills at night. She hears nothing.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В704"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УМЕР"}}, "fate": true}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "On the third kick the door tears off its hinges along with the chair, and I fall into the smoke. The tree is burning top to bottom like a torch, fire is climbing the curtain to the ceiling. Varya is sitting on the floor under the window, hugging her knees. I rip the blanket off her bed, throw it over her head and carry her out, and while I'm carrying her my sleeves are on fire."}, {"md": "My wife is already calling 112 and pounding on the neighbors' doors. The fire crew arrives in nine minutes and puts out the room in five. They take Varya to the Regional Hospital with mild smoke poisoning, and me with her, with second-degree burns on both arms. We see in the New Year in a hospital room, hooked up to IVs, with tangerines a nurse brought us."}, {"md": "\"Dad,\" Varya says hoarsely, \"I only put the chair there so Kolka wouldn't come in.\""}, {"md": "My arms are in bandages until February. The scars on my forearms will stay as white patches that look like a map."}], "C": [{"md": "…and I'm standing in Varya's room at eight in the evening on the thirtieth, with the string of lights in my hands. Varya is bouncing on the bed, yelling, \"Dad, come on, plug them in! Wave!\" I remember 3:40, the strip of orange under the door, and how behind it the coughing stopped."}, {"md": "I wind the lights back up, put them in the Fix Price box and say they're broken. Varya bawls, calls me a cheapskate, and hangs her candy wrappers on the tree instead of lights. That night I don't sleep. I sit on the hallway floor by her door, with the chair on the other side, until morning. At 3:40 nothing happens. Only the fridge clicks in the kitchen."}, {"md": "In the world where I didn't get to her through the smoke, somebody carried Varya out. A firefighter, a neighbor, somebody. Again I don't know who, and again I wasn't there."}, {"md": "In the morning Varya sits down at the table to sign a card for her grandma and picks up the pen in her left hand. Her letters slant the wrong way.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В706"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЗНАНИЕ"}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "\"You write with your right hand,\" I say.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В706"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЗНАНИЕ"}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "\"Dad, what's wrong with you?\" Varya looks at me like I'm the little one. From the kitchen my wife says Varya has been left-handed since kindergarten and we spent a year fighting her teacher so she wouldn't be retrained. I don't remember that. I remember holding her right hand in mine in September and writing out \"Mama\" with it.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В706"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЗНАНИЕ"}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "The Varya who was carried out of the smoke without me wrote with her right hand.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В706"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЗНАНИЕ"}}, "drift": true}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Break the door down"}, "B": {"label": "Run out to the landing for the fire extinguisher"}, "C": {"label": "\"I know when the lights catch fire.\" Go back to eight in the evening and don't plug them in at all"}, "D": {"label": "First to the bathroom: shove a blanket under the shower and pull it over my head"}, "E": {"label": "Don't lose a second: a jacket off the hook over my head, and to the door"}}}, "В7-06б": {"title": "The Threshold", "date": "31 December 2019, 03:41", "photo_caption": "The hallway. A strip of light under the door", "clock_label": "before the fire gets into the hallway", "text": [{"md": "The blanket from the bathroom is heavy and ice-cold, water runs off it down my collar and into my shorts. I stand under it by the door like under a wet tent and breathe through the cover. It smells of laundry powder and burning plastic.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ОДЕЯЛО"}}, {"md": "On my head is my wife's winter jacket with the hood, the first thing I grabbed off the hook. It smells of her perfume, and the fur on the hood tickles my nose.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ОДЕЯЛО"}}}, {"md": "The strip under the door has turned yellow, and the smoke is pouring out from under it thick, along the floor, over my bare feet, warm as steam from a banya. I touch the door with my palm, and it's hot, and I can't grab the handle; my hand jerks away by itself. Behind the door Varya is silent, and a minute ago she was coughing."}, {"md": "The kitchen window opens onto a shared balcony, a long one that runs past two apartments, and our part is separated from Varya's window only by a grille with a padlock I hung myself. The key to the padlock is in the front hall, on a nail."}, {"md": "The chill at the back of my neck doesn't let go, it just creeps behind my ear, as if somebody had turned their head to see where I'll go.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I hit the door with my shoulder under the wet blanket, and on the third try it splits by the handle. The chair behind it slides away, and instead of stepping into the room I drop to the floor under the blanket. The room breathes fire out at me all at once, like a stove. The fire goes over me, across the blanket, and the blanket hisses, and I crawl under it toward the window, where Varya was coughing.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ОДЕЯЛО"}}, {"md": "She's sitting on the floor under the windowsill, hugging her knees. I pull the blanket over her and me together, and we crawl back, the two of us under one tent, the cover smoking. In the hallway my wife grabs Varya, and I lie on the floor and look at my hands. The skin on them has blistered, but they're my hands, and there are two of them.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ОДЕЯЛО"}}, {"md": "I hit the door with my shoulder, running at it the whole length of the hallway, and on the second blow the hinges fly off, chair and all. I manage to duck, I don't know why; my knees fold before I can think. The fire rolls out over my head and licks the hallway ceiling, the hood of the jacket flares up, and I tear it off, jacket and all. The tree is burning like a torch in the room, and Varya is sitting on the floor under the window.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ОДЕЯЛО"}}}, {"md": "I grab her off the floor, press her face against me and carry her out, and while I'm carrying her my sleeves are on fire.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_ОДЕЯЛО"}}}, {"md": "My wife is already calling 112 and pounding on the neighbors' doors. The fire crew arrives in nine minutes. They take Varya to the Regional Hospital with smoke poisoning, and me with her."}, {"md": "\"Dad,\" Varya says hoarsely in the ambulance, \"I only put the chair there so Kolka wouldn't come in.\""}], "B": [{"md": "I rip the key off the nail and run to the kitchen window. On the balcony it's thirty below, and I'm barefoot, in my shorts, with a jacket on my head. The padlock on the grille is frozen, and the key only turns on the third try, after I breathe on it. Varya's window glows yellow from inside and trembles."}, {"md": "I smash the glass with my elbow wrapped in the jacket. The window is old, double-paned; I punch the second pane with my fist. The fire stretches toward the window for air, and I dive under it, into the smoke, to the windowsill, where Varya is sitting on the floor. She isn't coughing anymore. I drag her out through the frame onto the balcony, into the snow, and she starts coughing again, loud, choking, and I kneel over her in the snow and count every cough as if somebody were going to take them away from me."}, {"md": "The fire crew arrives in nine minutes. They take Varya to the Regional Hospital with smoke poisoning; she breathed in more than she should have. My hands are cut up from the glass, eight stitches. We see in the New Year in a hospital room, hooked up to IVs, with tangerines a nurse brought us."}, {"md": "\"Dad,\" Varya says hoarsely, \"did you climb in the window? Like Spider-Man?\""}], "C": [{"md": "I remember the room breathing fire into my face and me breathing that fire in. So I kick with my heel, at the door right by the handle, and on the fourth kick, when the wood splits, I drop to the floor before the door opens. Face in the carpet, hands over the back of my head."}, {"md": "The door bursts open by itself from the heat. The fire passes over me, along the hallway ceiling, roaring like a train overhead, and goes on toward my wife and Kolka, but my wife is already out on the landing. I crawl inside, under the smoke, across the hot floor, to the window, to Varya, and drag her back by the collar of her pajamas on my belly, army-crawling, pushing off with my elbows."}, {"md": "The fire crew arrives in nine minutes. They take Varya to the Regional Hospital with smoke poisoning, and me with her, with burns on my back and the back of my head. We see in the New Year in a hospital room."}, {"md": "\"Dad,\" Varya says hoarsely, \"why were you crawling like a lizard?\""}, {"md": "That night in the hospital Varya asks for water and then asks, \"Dad, did they get Timofey out?\" Timofey, she says, is our cat, ginger, sleeps on the radiator. We've never had a cat. In the morning my wife brings oranges and says Timofey is with the neighbor downstairs, alive, only his whiskers got singed. I nod, though my wife has hated cats all her life; that much I remember for sure.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 10}}, "drift": true}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Break the door down, now"}, "B": {"label": "The key off the nail, through the kitchen onto the balcony, open the grille and smash Varya's window from outside"}, "C": {"label": "\"I remember how the room breathed out.\" Kick the door by the handle and drop straight to the floor, face in the carpet, let the fire go over me"}}}, "В7-07": {"title": "I'll Do It Myself", "date": "November 2022, 19:20", "photo_caption": "The kids' room. The outlet by the desk", "text": [{"md": "In October two men from our shift were called up with draft notices. Rustam left for Kazakhstan and sends photos of the steppe from there, no captions. Gena Shubin is fifty-five, and they won't let him retire because there's nobody to do the work, so now he smokes right in the operator room and nobody says a word to him. In the evenings people talk quietly in their kitchens, and the TV talks loudly."}, {"md": "On Sunday Sonka comes over after a night shift and falls asleep on our couch in her jacket, right in front of Varya. She's twenty-four, in her second year working in the ER on Vesennyaya Street. When she wakes up, she finds me in the hallway with a hammer drill and watches me drill the wall for a new outlet. \"You're the only brother I've got,\" she says. \"Stop doing everything yourself. Call somebody for once.\" I say it's faster this way."}, {"md": "After the fire we redid Varya's room from scratch: wallpaper with cats, a new door, no chair against it anymore. The wiring back then was done by a friend of my father-in-law's, in a hurry, for a bottle and a thank-you. Now the outlet by Varya's desk wobbles in the wall and sparks when Varya plugs her phone in to charge. On the wallpaper around it there's a soot mark the size of a little finger.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В706"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДВЕРЬ"}}}, {"md": "The outlet by Varya's desk wobbles in the wall and sparks when Varya plugs her phone in to charge. That's the outlet she was stretching the Christmas lights to, that evening at eight. For three years I've been steering around it like that footpath along the apartment block, and now there's a soot mark around it on the wallpaper, the size of a little finger.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В706"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЗНАНИЕ"}}}, {"md": "Varya is eleven, she's left-handed, and everybody around says it's always been that way. Sometimes I think even her laugh is different. She used to cover her mouth with her hand, and now she roars with laughter like Kolka, and my wife is surprised that I'm surprised.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В706"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЗНАНИЕ"}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "\"Call an electrician,\" my wife says from the kitchen."}, {"md": "\"I'll do it myself,\" I say. \"It's a five-minute job.\""}, {"md": "I've got a screwdriver in my hand, a voltage tester with a little bulb in the handle. Our breaker box is out on the landing, behind an iron door somebody has scratched HOUSING OFFICE = GOATS on."}, {"md": "Vitka, Varya's godfather, has been an electrician with the city power company for fourteen years now. Every time I pick up a screwdriver in front of him, he says the same thing: \"Don't go in there yourself. Call me.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "Vitka and I talk once a year these days, at New Year's, and he's never the one who calls first.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "Varya is sitting on the bed with her feet tucked under her, watching me with interest, like I'm a magician."}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "\"The breaker, you idiot!\" Vitka yells into the phone so loud Varya can hear. \"Don't touch anything, I'm coming.\""}, {"md": "He gets there in forty minutes, with a tool bag and a bobble hat his daughter knitted him. He flips the breaker on the landing, shines a flashlight into the wall, and curses me, the developer, and every Chinese outlet manufacturer by name. In ten minutes the outlet is sitting in the wall like it grew there."}, {"md": "Afterward we drink tea in the kitchen. Vitka stirs his sugar for a long time and says, without looking at me:"}, {"md": "\"You're supposed to be smart, Andryukha. But all your life somebody's been pulling you out. First some old man out of the river, then me.\""}], "C": [{"md": "Before screwing in a light bulb, Dad always went out on the landing and unscrewed the fuses, even when Mom laughed at him. I go out on the landing, open the iron door and flip the breaker. The lights in the apartment go out, Kolka yells \"Hey!\" from the living room, and my wife laughs. I change the outlet by the light of my phone. Before I touch the wire, I test it with the back of my hand, the way Dad taught me, so that if anything happens my hand won't clench."}, {"md": "Then I call Dad. He coughs into the phone for a long time, a miner's cough, and finally says, \"Good man. The breaker comes first.\" He doesn't say anything else, but I can hear he's pleased."}, {"md": "That was one of the last times he praised me. Two years later he'd slip at a bus stop, and I wouldn't be there.", "kind": "old"}], "D": [{"md": "The electrician from the housing office comes a week later, smelling of last night and without any tools, and borrows my screwdriver. All week the hole in the wall is taped over with an X of electrical tape, and Kolka pushes a toy soldier into it, and I fish it out with tweezers."}, {"md": "The outlet in the living room is on the same circuit, though, so the TV doesn't work, and for a week we play Monopoly in the evenings. Varya beats everybody and demands to be paid in real money. My wife laughs, and I feel like a man who can't change an outlet in his own home, and I don't like that feeling."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Myself, quick"}, "B": {"label": "Call Vitka, Varya's godfather"}, "C": {"label": "The breaker box first. Dad always did it that way"}, "D": {"label": "Call the electrician from the housing office and live with a hole in the wall for a week"}}}, "В7-08": {"title": "The Pond", "date": "22 March 2026, 14:10", "photo_caption": "Yagunovsky. The pond at the end of the street", "clock_label": "while Kolka can still hold on to the edge", "text": [{"md": "Dad has been gone since last summer. In December of '24 he slipped at the bus stop by Krasnaya Gorka, on flat ground, broke his hip, spent six months in bed at home, and in June pneumonia took him. I wasn't with him at the bus stop or that night. His death had no minute I could go back to, and there was nothing for me to die for. Now Mom calls me every evening at nine and asks if I've eaten."}, {"md": "At the end of March we're at the dacha in Yagunovsky, shoveling the last snow off the roof. The snow is heavy and wet, the shovel sticks in it."}, {"md": "The dacha belongs to my mother-in-law now. My father-in-law has been gone since that banya, and I throw the snow where he would have told me to. My mother-in-law watches from the porch and says nothing.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В704"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УМЕР"}}, "fate": true}, {"md": "My father-in-law sits on the porch telling me where to throw it. He won't let me into the banya alone anymore, and when I'm there he's the only one who touches the damper.", "if": {"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В704"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕСТЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В704"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПОМНИЛ"}}]}}, {"md": "At the end of the street there's a pond, round, an old flooded pit that all winter is the boys' skating rink. Now the ice on it is gray and spongy, and there's already water along the edges. Kolka is ten, and you can't drag him away from that pond. An hour ago I told him not to go on the ice. He said, \"I know, I know.\""}, {"md": "Now I hear the boys yelling over the fence. Kolka runs out to the middle after a puck, and the ice under him sags with a soft wet sound. Kolka is gone. Then his head comes up over the water, no hat, he grabs the edge, and the edge breaks off under his hands."}, {"md": "It's thirty meters to the hole."}, {"md": "By the fence lies a long board from an old greenhouse. Kolka is holding on to the edge with one hand and slapping the water with the other, and every time he slaps, another hand's width of the edge breaks off."}, {"md": "I know ice. At fourteen Vitka and I went through it in Uncle Tolya's Lada Nine and crawled to the bank on our bellies, in clothes frozen stiff as boards. My body remembers it better than my head does.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т07"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЛЁД"}}}, {"md": "Somebody whispers behind my ear, through the boys' yelling, perfectly clearly: \"Don't crawl. You won't make it.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I run across the ice, and the ice cracks under my feet, but I know what to do with it. Don't stop, don't jump, and just before the hole drop flat and slide on your belly. I go down at the edge, grab Kolka by the hood and pull. He's heavy with water, like a sack of cement, and he clings to me so hard he scratches my neck."}, {"md": "A minute later we're both lying on the bank, soaked through. Kolka is shaking, and the first thing he says is, \"Dad, the puck's still out there.\" They fire up the banya right there in the middle of the day, and I check the damper three times."}], "C": [{"md": "Under the middle of the pond there's a spring, and the ice there is as thin as newspaper. Now I know that with my whole body. So I don't run across the pond; I run along the bank to the reeds, where the ice is thicker and stitched through with stalks, and from there, the last three meters, I slide on my belly to the hole with the board."}, {"md": "I pull Kolka out by the hand on the first try. He doesn't even have time to swallow much. The boys on the far bank look at me as if I knew in advance, and they're right about that."}, {"md": "That evening Kolka sits in a hot bath and shows me a scar on his knee, old and white. \"From my bike, when I was seven,\" he says. \"You put the green stuff on it yourself.\" I put the green antiseptic on his right knee, by the garages. His scar is on the left, and he strokes it with his finger.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДРЕЙФ"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 10}}, "drift": true}], "D": [{"md": "I remember the Tom at the end of March, a hole in the ice, a backpack on the edge, and the current taking me by the legs, calmly, like it owned the place. In this life I've never drowned in the Tom as a grown man. Whose memory that is, I'm not going to sort out. I remember the main thing: ice gets eaten from below by water that moves. And in this pond the water only moves in the middle, where the spring comes up in summer."}, {"md": "So I run in an arc along the bank, where the ice is gray but thick, and only by the hole itself do I drop on my belly and slide toward it with the shovel, handle first. Kolka grabs the handle on the second try, and I yank him out onto the ice like a fish."}, {"md": "On the bank I wrap him in my jacket and rub his ears, and he fights me off. Only in the banya, once he's warmed up and started arguing again, does he ask why I ran the long way around. \"Because I've already run straight,\" I say, and of course he doesn't understand a thing."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Run across the ice to the hole"}, "B": {"label": "Lie down, crawl, push the board to him"}, "C": {"label": "\"I remember where the ice is thin.\" Run around along the bank and come in from the reeds"}, "D": {"label": "\"I've drowned under ice like this before. Not in this life.\" Don't run straight, run in an arc along the bank, where there's no current under the ice"}}}, "В7-09": {"title": "Twelve Sensors", "date": "September 2033", "photo_caption": "The front hall. Varya is leaving", "text": [{"md": "I have twelve smoke detectors in the apartment. I counted. Two in every room, one in the hallway, one in the kitchen over the stove, one in the bathroom, though there's nothing in there to burn. There's also a carbon monoxide detector, a leak sensor under the washing machine, and a breaker box I can switch off from my phone at the plant. They all blink green, and at night, when I can't sleep, I lie there looking at the green dot on the ceiling."}, {"md": "Since I was sixteen I've known that people tape over sensors so they can make quota. Nobody's taping over mine.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т08"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДАТЧИКИ"}}}, {"md": "\"Call me when you get there,\" I say to everybody, even Gena when he walks home from his shift, three blocks. Gena says, \"Andryukha, you're sick,\" but he calls."}, {"md": "Varya is twenty-two. She's finished university and is leaving for Novosibirsk, for Akademgorodok, the science town, for a lab that took three people from her whole year. She's driving her own car, down that same highway, through Yurga. Her suitcase has been standing in the front hall since last night, with her backpack on top."}, {"md": "Last night at the farewell dinner my wife told Varya's boyfriend how she and I met. She told it cheerfully, in detail: the skating rink by the Drama Theater, where I supposedly knocked her off her feet. I don't remember anything like that. I didn't correct her, because everybody at the table was nodding, even Kolka, who wasn't born yet.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В706"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЗНАНИЕ"}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "Kolka is seventeen; he writes something for neural networks and laughs out loud at my sensors. \"Dad, your apartment's like a spaceship, it just doesn't fly.\" My wife doesn't laugh. Two weeks ago in the kitchen she said to me quietly, so the kids couldn't hear, \"You hold on to everybody like a stroller on the ice. With both hands. She's twenty-two, Andrei.\""}, {"md": "I never told her about the stroller on the ice."}, {"md": "Varya is standing in the front hall in her sneakers, waiting for what I'll say. On my phone I've got an app open that can put a tracker on her phone, and then I'll be able to see her dot on the map the whole way, right up to Akademgorodok, and after."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I close the app and put the phone in my pocket. I hug Varya, and at first she stands there stiff, waiting for a catch, and then she hugs me back. I don't say \"Call me when you get there.\" I want to say it so badly my jaw aches."}, {"md": "She leaves at eight. I sit down on the stool in the front hall, my wife sits next to me and takes my hand. We sit like that until the phone rings. It's Varya, from Yurga: \"Dad, I'm in Yurga, they've got dumplings. Don't worry.\" I'm not worried, I say, and I'm lying."}], "B": [{"md": "\"Either the tracker or you don't go,\" I say."}, {"md": "Varya goes pale, then red, and yells that she's twenty-two, that she's not a stroller, and that I'm sick, like Gena would say. In the end she installs the tracker herself, out of spite, right in front of me, and slams the door so hard the hallway detector goes off."}, {"md": "That night I lie there watching her dot crawl along the highway through Yurga. My wife sleeps on the couch in the living room. In the morning she says, \"You're not saving her, you're locking her up,\" and leaves for work without breakfast. Varya doesn't call me for two months."}], "C": [{"md": "That night, after Varya leaves, I sit down with my wife in the kitchen and tell her everything. About the corner of the apartment block, about the stroller rolling toward the road, and about how I was standing there alive a minute before. About what came after. I talk and wait for her to call an ambulance."}, {"md": "Nika listens in silence, without interrupting, a cup in her hands. When I finish, she puts the cup down on the table.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "\"I believe you,\" she says. \"I counted that day, on the bank. Two hundred and fifty-two. Normal people don't live that long without air, Samokhin.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "\"You know what's funny,\" she says later, at the sink. \"Sometimes I dream that I smoke. Thin ones, on the steps of some institute, with squirrels jumping around in the birches. And you're sitting there in a cap full of wires, and I'm peeling them off one at a time. I don't even know how to smoke.\"", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}, {"op": "lived", "id": "В2-05"}]}}, {"md": "Olya listens in silence and looks out the window at the pines for a long time. Then she says:", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "\"So that's why you were sitting on the floor in the front hall that day, by the stroller, in your jacket. I thought you were just tired.\" She takes my hand and studies it as if she's looking for marks. \"How many times?\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "I don't answer, and she doesn't ask again.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "Toward morning she takes down one of the twelve detectors herself, the one in the hallway that beeps for no reason, and puts it in the toolbox. I don't object."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Let her go"}, "B": {"label": "Put a tracker on her phone, let her be mad"}, "C": {"label": "Tell my wife the truth: the icicle, the Christmas lights, the pond"}, "D": {"label": "Drive ahead of her in my own car, a minute in front. And die wherever she might die a minute later", "locked": "for this I'd have to hear that I come back"}}}, "В7-09п": {"title": "Habit", "date": "March 2035", "photo_caption": "The kitchen. A map on the wall", "text": [{"md": "Over two years I drive every one of their roads ahead of them. Varya's highway to Akademgorodok when she comes home for the holidays. Kolka's route to college, through the crosswalk by the Lapland mall. My wife's way to the dacha over the old bridge. I'm always a minute ahead, and on every road I die where I need to, wake up, and get the call in in time. On the kitchen wall hangs a map of the region, covered in my little crosses. My wife thinks I'm marking the potholes.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_РАЗВЕДЧИК"}}, {"md": "Since that morning on the highway I've driven ahead of my family twice more. Once ahead of Kolka, when he started driving, and once ahead of my wife, when she went to see her sister in Belovo. Both times everybody got there, and both times my wife asked why I'd followed.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_РАЗВЕДЧИК"}}}, {"md": "In April Kolka is flying to Moscow for a programming olympiad, his first time on a plane. He bounces around the kitchen, knocks over a stool and says there'll be people from Yandex there."}, {"md": "Varya calls on Sundays, like before. Only now she calls me \"Daddy\" instead of \"Dad\" and laughs differently, short, through her nose. I ask my wife how long Varya's been talking like that. My wife looks at me like I'm sick: \"Always, Andrei.\" Kolka writes with his left hand. I try to remember teaching him to hold a spoon and can't remember which hand.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_РАЗВЕДЧИК"}, "drift": true}, {"md": "My wife sits across from me with her tea, watching me watch Kolka."}, {"md": "\"You're going to go ahead of him again,\" she says. It isn't a question. \"Why do you always go first, Andrei? I'm not jealous. I'm scared.\""}, {"md": "You can't drive ahead of a plane. But you can buy a ticket for a flight an hour earlier and walk his route in Moscow first. And wake up, if it comes to that."}], "after": {"B": [{"md": "In the evening, when everyone's asleep, I take the map off the wall and fold it in four, then in four again, until it won't fold anymore. I put it in the toolbox, under the drill. The crosses on it are where I lay dead, and I keep my hand on it for a long time before I close the box."}, {"md": "Kolka flies to Moscow in April. I don't see him off at the airport, because I'm afraid I won't be able to stand it and I'll buy a second ticket. He calls from Sheremetyevo: \"Dad, landed, it's huge here.\" I sit by the phone for another hour after he hangs up."}], "C": [{"md": "I tell her everything: the highway, the moose by Taiga, the Niva in Yurga, the minute I always keep between us. The map on the wall and what the crosses mean. I talk and wait for her to cry or laugh."}, {"md": "She's quiet for a long time. Then she gets up, goes to the map and touches one cross, by the old bridge, on her own road to the dacha."}, {"md": "\"Was that for me?\" she asks."}, {"md": "\"For you.\""}, {"md": "She takes the map down herself, carefully, pin by pin, and carries it into the bedroom. In the morning the map is in her closet, under her underwear. \"Let it stay with me,\" she says. \"And you don't drive anymore.\" I don't."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Die one more time. For Kolka. I'll find a way", "locked": "for this I'd have to get used to dying for them"}, "B": {"label": "Enough. Let them live on their own, the way I lived once. Take the map off the wall"}, "C": {"label": "Tell her why I always go first", "locked": "you can only tell this to someone who still believes you"}}}, "В7-09р": {"title": "Scouting", "date": "September 2033, morning", "photo_caption": "The Novosibirsk highway. Ahead of Varya", "text": [{"md": "I tell Varya I'll drive with her as far as Novosibirsk, I've got business there. I don't have any business there. She's in her red Lada, I'm in mine, in front, and I keep exactly one minute between us. I measure it by the roadside posts: a little over a kilometer. My phone is in my pocket on speaker; she can hear it."}, {"md": "I know how this works. If I die on this road, I'll wake up a minute before my death and remember what killed me. And a minute later she'll be in that spot. So every death of mine is a trouble of hers that I'll see first and have time to name over the phone. Nobody has ever done this, except maybe Granddad, and not for a daughter."}, {"md": "The highway is wet, the semis run one after another like a freight train. Ahead is the long straight before Yurga, the same one where I once drove behind a semi with Varya and Kolka in the back seat. After that, the turn-off for Taiga, the town, where moose-crossing signs stand along the shoulders, and in Yurga a gas station where everybody stops for coffee."}, {"md": "Varya will drive behind me exactly the way I drive. If I want to know what's waiting for her here, it has to happen to me first."}], "after": {"D": [{"md": "On the straight before Yurga I call her: \"Varya, drop to seventy and don't pass anybody, stay behind me.\" She grumbles but slows down. A minute later a small dark car with no headlights comes over the rise in the oncoming lane, and I let it go by, tucked in tight behind a semi. Varya says afterward that she saw something dark rush past her, and that her hands are still shaking."}, {"md": "We reach Akademgorodok by lunchtime. I sit in the car outside her dorm and look at my hands. They're not shaking. That scares me more than anything."}], "E": [{"md": "The whole way I talk to her on the phone, like a dispatcher talking a train through. Don't pass here, even if it's empty. Slow to sixty here, there are moose. In Yurga don't turn in at the gas station; I poured you coffee in a thermos, it's on your back seat. Varya does what I say and laughs."}, {"md": "\"Dad, it's like you've already been there,\" she says."}, {"md": "\"I have,\" I say. \"A minute ago.\""}, {"md": "She gets to Akademgorodok without a scratch and texts me: \"Made it. You're scary.\" I drive back alone, slowly. That night I lie looking at the green dot of the detector on the ceiling. I died three times in one morning, and I want more."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "On the straight before Yurga, pull out to pass a semi, the way she would if she were in a hurry"}, "B": {"label": "At the Taiga turn-off, don't brake; drive the way people drive when they're in a hurry"}, "C": {"label": "Stop at the gas station in Yurga. Drink coffee where she'll drink hers"}, "D": {"label": "\"I've already died here.\" Call her and say one thing: slow down and stay behind me"}, "E": {"label": "\"I've died on this road three times.\" Talk her through it all the way to Akademgorodok: where not to pass, where to brake, where not to stop"}, "F": {"label": "Turn around and go home. Whatever is out there, it's her road"}}}, "В7-10": {"title": "Fatigue", "date": "October 2038", "photo_caption": "The stairs. Between the fourth and fifth floors", "text": [{"md": "Our elevator's out again, third week running; there's a note on the door: \"Under repair. We apologize for the inconvenience.\" They've been apologizing for about twenty years."}, {"md": "I come home from my shift and find my wife on the stairs, between the fourth and fifth floors. She's sitting on the windowsill, a Maria-Ra bag at her feet, and she smiles at me as if I'd caught her doing something indecent.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КОНТРОЛЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}]}}}, {"md": "\"I'm resting,\" she says. \"What are you staring at? It's autumn.\"", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КОНТРОЛЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}]}}}, {"md": "She's been like this a long time. She falls asleep at nine in front of the TV, in the middle of a movie. The bruises on her arms are from bumping into the closet door. In August at the dacha she didn't make it to the pond; she turned back halfway and said it was too hot.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КОНТРОЛЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}]}}}, {"md": "My wife left me three years ago, without a scene, with two bags, and rents a one-room apartment in Rudnichny, in the same five-story block where we lived when Varya was little. \"I'm tired of living inside your sensors,\" she said then. On Saturdays she comes by to pick up her mail and water the violets she didn't want to take. Today is Saturday, and I find her on the stairs, between the fourth and fifth floors. She's sitting on the windowsill, a bag at her feet.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КОНТРОЛЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}]}}, {"md": "\"I'm resting,\" she says. \"What are you staring at? It's none of your business anymore.\"", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КОНТРОЛЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}]}}, {"md": "Kolka says she falls asleep at his place at nine, in the middle of a movie. The bruises on her arms are from bumping into the closet door.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КОНТРОЛЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 30}}]}}, {"md": "\"I'm a doctor myself, Samokhin,\" Nika says when I reach for her bag. \"Don't start. It's age and October; half my clinic is like this.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "\"It's my blood pressure,\" Olya says when I reach for her bag. \"Mom had the same thing at my age. I'll drink some viburnum tea.\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ОЛЯ"}}}, {"md": "In the stairwell light her face is gray, yellowish, like an old newspaper on top of a closet. Her lips are pale. I've been looking at that face for thirty years, and I don't remember when it got like this."}, {"md": "The chill at the back of my neck is silent. It always started talking when death was standing next to me, by the icicle, at the door of the kids' room. Now it's silent, and I'm glad.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 20}}}, {"md": "At night I lie there and wonder whether to drive ahead of her, the way I drove ahead of Varya. Then I realize there's nowhere to drive. This trouble has no turn-off and no gas station, no minute in which I can die instead of her and wake up knowing.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "РАЗВЕДКА"}}}, {"md": "All my life I was afraid of icicles, cars and outlets. The thing that comes quietly and leaves you not one minute to go back to, I wasn't afraid of at all.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I take the bag, take her by the elbow and walk her back down to the car. She grumbles all the way to the clinic, and in the hallway she goes quiet. A blood test that same day: hemoglobin at eighty, about half what it should be. A week later, more tests; a week after that the doctor says a word, looking at the monitor. Then he adds \"early\" and \"lucky,\" and those two words I remember for the rest of my life.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЖИВА"}}}, {"md": "They operate in December. By spring she's cursing the elevator again and climbing to the ninth floor on foot, by herself, with one stop. The chill at the back of my neck was silent that whole autumn. I just saw her on the stairs between the fourth and fifth floors, with a bag, holding the railing, and that was enough.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЖИВА"}}}, {"md": "She never did move back in with me. But in December I'm the one who brings her home from the hospital, and on Saturdays now she stays for tea.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЖИВА"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КОНТРОЛЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}]}}, {"md": "I take the bag, take her by the elbow and walk her to the car. She grumbles all the way to the clinic. In the hallway she looks at the line stretching to the window, at the old women with their numbered slips, and says, \"Andrei, honestly, it's nothing. I'll sleep it off.\" I say she has to. She says she doesn't, and walks back to the car. I follow her. I don't know the words that would have made her stay. All my life the only thing I knew how to do was get there in time.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УМЕРЛА"}}}, {"md": "She has the blood test a year later, when it's already too late. She dies in November of '47, in the hospital, toward morning, holding my finger.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УМЕРЛА"}}}, {"md": "I noticed her. What I never learned was to hear what \"it's nothing\" meant when she said it.", "kind": "old", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УМЕРЛА"}}}], "B": [{"md": "At the Gubernskie pharmacy I buy vitamins, a big jar with a smiling woman on the label. She takes them until spring and sleeps a lot. In spring she's better, in summer worse. The doctors find the tumor in '41, when it's too late to find it, and start treating it, honestly, for a long time, with remissions."}, {"md": "She dies in November of '47, in the hospital, toward morning, holding my finger. That month a new screen goes up at the bus stop by the cancer center, showing a smiling gray-haired man in a good coat and the words \"Orpheus. Second Chance — for those who have something to lose.\" I have something to lose, and I lose it, slowly, over nine years, and there isn't one of those minutes I can go back to."}, {"md": "What would have saved her wasn't my gut feeling, it was one doctor's appointment in October. I still count those nine years back, to that staircase.", "kind": "old"}], "C": [{"md": "At four-twelve I sit alone in the kitchen with the light off and ask. Out loud, like an idiot. The whisper behind my ear came all my life whenever death was waiting for me, and it always knew where. Now it's silent. This isn't the kind of trouble that has a minute. You can't wait it out in a stairwell or go around it along the bank."}, {"md": "The gift never let me go back further than one evening. And this is years, and where they began even the whisper doesn't know. The doctors find the tumor in '41, when it's too late. She dies in November of '47, in the hospital, toward morning, holding my finger."}, {"md": "That month there's a screen at the bus stop by the cancer center: a gray-haired man in a good coat and the words \"Orpheus. Second Chance — for those who have something to lose.\" One step back, one minute, for the price of a three-room apartment downtown. I'd need to go back nine years, and steps like that aren't sold, not even to them."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Take her to a doctor. Right now"}, "B": {"label": "Buy vitamins; she'll sleep it off"}, "C": {"label": "Ask the voices"}}}, "В7-11": {"title": "Family Tree", "date": "May 2059", "photo_caption": "\"My Family Tree.\" A printout", "text": [{"md": "I live in Mom's two-room apartment in Rudnichny now, the one I grew up in. Mom's been gone three years. She went at ninety, in her sleep, and that death didn't have a minute to go back to either. I left her china cabinet where it stood, with the crystal and the documents behind the crystal."}, {"md": "My wife grumbles that everything in this apartment creaks, me included, but her violets were the first thing she moved in.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЖИВА"}}}, {"md": "My wife has been gone twelve years. Her cup hangs on a hook over the sink, and I don't drink from it, but I don't put it away either.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УМЕРЛА"}}}, {"md": "This year the Tom has been getting shallower since April. By Rudnichny, stones I didn't know about for seventy years have come up out of the water, and on TV they talk about an anticyclone and spread their hands."}, {"md": "For the May holidays Varya sends Liza to me from Novosibirsk, \"for the fresh air.\" Liza is fourteen and tall, like her mother. At school she's been given a project, \"My Family Tree.\" After the Wager, after those two years when the generals with their Second Chance decided dying wasn't frightening, everything was declassified in '58, and half the class is digging for great-grandfathers in the archives now. The other half argues about how many bridges there are over the Tom in Kemerovo, one or three. The teacher doesn't step in; it's like that everywhere now."}, {"md": "Liza writes with her left hand, like her mother. In our family, Varya says, all the girls are left-handed. I don't argue.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В706"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЗНАНИЕ"}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "\"Granddad, look who I found,\" says Liza, and puts a printout down on the oilcloth. \"Your granddad. My great-great-grandfather.\""}, {"md": "\"SAMOKHIN, Tikhon Savelyevich, born 1930, senior research fellow. Head of research topic TUNING FORK, facility Level 640, Glubokaya mine. Killed 28.01.1986 at 04:12 in a sudden gas outburst.\""}, {"md": "Below it, in small print, a scan from another file is pinned, from '98: \"Blood samples of the carrier (Samokhin A. N., born 1989), 2 units, transferred to the Orpheus Foundation under contract.\" The carrier is me. The two test tubes the doctor from Tomsk took from me in the hospital when I was seven. Orpheus I remember from the ads in '47; they hung at every bus stop then: a gray-haired man in a good coat and the words \"Second Chance.\""}, {"md": "I never saw Granddad Tisha. He died \"at the mine,\" the way all the granddads in Kemerovo died. I read the time of death twice. At four-twelve Dad woke up every night of his life and went out on the balcony to smoke. And at dawn after graduation my Nokia suddenly showed 4:12, though a second before it had said exactly four."}, {"md": "The watch I pulled out of Black Backwater when I was seven is lying in the china cabinet behind the crystal. For sixty-three years it's stood at four-twelve.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧАСЫ"}}}, {"md": "\"Under the mine. There was a door down there,\" Dad told me in the garage, after the second bottle, and didn't say another word.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДВЕРЬ"}}}, {"md": "Under the text there's a photograph. A miners' choir in a House of Culture, men in identical jackets, and in front of them a man in glasses with his hand raised. His face is like Dad's, only older. I saw a face like that in the shed when I was eleven, in a photo by a river: \"T. S. and V. P., the Tom, 1981.\""}, {"md": "\"What's he holding?\" asks Liza."}, {"md": "\"A tuning fork. It gives the choir its note.\""}, {"md": "The last page of the printout is a scan from a working log, handwritten, with an ink blot. \"Experiment 41. 04:12. The choir is clearly audible. There are more of them than of us. They are counting.\" And under it, in the archivist's typed letters: \"In his working notes, T. S. Samokhin referred to the recorded phenomenon as 'the choir.'\""}, {"md": "I recognize the handwriting before I finish reading. The same loops on the d's, the same slant. In the shed, by flashlight, I read the column \"what I heard\" in his notebook and copied it into my own school notebook.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ТЕТРАДИ"}}}, {"md": "Dad burned his notebooks in the potbelly stove, one at a time, without looking. Now I know what was burning.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СОЖЖЕНО"}}}, {"md": "And his notebooks, when I was eleven, I took to the junk dealer on Sovetsky for a Sega cartridge.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т05"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ПРОДАЛ"}}}, {"md": "For sixty-odd years the chill behind my ear had no name. It turns out Granddad had one for it."}, {"md": "When they pulled me out of the backwater, Grandma said about that old man in the striped shirt, \"Tisha…?\" By then Tisha had been lying in the cemetery under Krasnaya Gorka for ten years. Somebody pulled me out. In 2011 somebody picked Varya up where I'd stopped being."}, {"md": "Somebody carried Varya out of the smoke.", "if": {"op": "or", "a": [{"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-06.A", "life": true}, {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-06.B", "life": true}]}}, {"md": "Somebody with a boat hook was running across the ice toward Kolka; I saw him from below, through the ice.", "if": {"op": "seen", "id": "✝В7-08.A", "life": true}}, {"md": "All my life somebody caught my people when I wasn't there. Maybe it runs in the Samokhin family."}, {"md": "In the other room five-year-old Andryusha, Kolka's son, is sitting on the floor building a dam out of blocks. He's so afraid of water that he won't sit in the bath, only the shower, and only with the door open."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "In July the Tom by Rudnichny is waist-deep where as a kid I couldn't touch bottom. Andryusha and I go in every morning, before it gets hot. The first week he hangs on my neck and squeals, the second he lies on his back on the water while I hold my palm under his shoulder blades and lie that I'm not holding him."}, {"md": "I talk to him the way nobody ever talked to me. Water is just water; it won't do anything to you if you're not afraid of it. In August he dog-paddles five meters to me with his eyes open and comes out on the bank as proud as a cosmonaut. Every week the river pulls back another hand's width."}], "B": [{"md": "Liza and I take the bus to Krasnaya Gorka. Past the museum, past the old waste dumps, stands the Glubokaya headframe, rusty, behind a fence with a sign: \"Site declassified. No entry.\" Liza photographs the sign for her project, and I stand looking at my feet. Six hundred and forty meters below them, somewhere down there, Granddad wrote up his forty-first experiment."}, {"md": "The ground under my soles is humming, steady, on one note. Liza doesn't hear a thing.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}}, {"md": "Liza takes my hand like a little girl, though she's fourteen. \"Granddad, your hands are freezing,\" she says, and it's twenty-five degrees out."}], "C": [{"md": "I fold the printout in four and put it in the china cabinet, behind the crystal. There, against the back panel, lies an envelope with a wax seal marked \"For Kolya.\" Mom put it there in 2000, and in fifty-nine years nobody has opened it. Dad has been gone for thirty-four years."}, {"md": "I put the printout next to the envelope, close the glass door and stand next to it for another minute, the way Mom used to."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Teach Andryusha to swim. This summer, in the Tom, while it's still there"}, "B": {"label": "Take Liza to Krasnaya Gorka, to Granddad's mine"}, "C": {"label": "Put the printout away in the china cabinet. Let the dead lie"}}}, "В7-12": {"title": "First Light", "date": "27 July 2061, 22:40", "photo_caption": "Fishing rods in the front hall", "text": [{"md": "Andryusha doesn't remember the Tom with water in it. He was five when it went, and for him the river is a road you can take from our building to the bridge without crossing a single street. Under the bridge he tips his head back and counts the dark bands on the piers, one above the other, like rings on a stump. I tell him that's the water signing its name, how high it came each year, and he asks where I signed mine."}, {"md": "There are so many fishhooks on the bottom that he collects them in a candy tin, the dark ones separate from the shiny ones, and asks about every one whether it's mine. Bloggers fly drones over the backwater, the drones fall into the black water, and nobody gets them out."}, {"md": "In the evenings a comet hangs in the sky. \"It'll come back on the twenty-eighth of July, sixty-one,\" Galina Ivanovna said at graduation, and we cracked up: seventy-two was never."}, {"md": "Andryusha is seven, named after me, and he spends the summer with me. Kolka brings him in June with a backpack and a tablet and says, \"Dad, no experiments.\" Andryusha doesn't know about experiments. He knows that all the fish from the Tom have gone into the backwater, because they've got nowhere else to go, and the boys from the yard pull perch out of it at first light, until the police chase them off."}, {"md": "He can dog-paddle now and is very proud of it. Every evening he shows me on the living room floor how you paddle, and bangs his elbows on the couch.", "if": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_НАУЧИЛ"}}, {"md": "He's still afraid of water. He wants to catch fish, but he doesn't want to go near the water. \"I'll do it from the bank, Granddad, from far away.\"", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "truthy", "n": "F_НАУЧИЛ"}}}, {"md": "\"Granddad, let's go tomorrow at first light,\" he says for the tenth time this evening. \"Come on, Granddad. I dug worms.\""}, {"md": "I'm drawn there myself. Every evening my feet turn toward the river by themselves, the way at eighteen they carried me past the bus stop after a night shift, down to the Tom. In the past year the whisper behind my ear has turned into a steady hum, like the hum at night in the unit by the synthesis columns. Granddad, it turns out, called it a choir, and I've gotten used to it."}, {"md": "In the kitchen my wife is darning the old storm jacket I got down from the top of the closet yesterday. Dad gave it to me once, saying, \"Must be Granddad's,\" and I've never worn it since. My wife doesn't ask anything. \"A child needs to be asleep at four in the morning,\" she says, without looking up from her needle.", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЖИВА"}}, {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КОНТРОЛЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}]}}]}}, {"md": "My wife lives separately, at the other end of Rudnichny, but on the evening of the twenty-seventh she comes over on her own, with a thermos. She takes the old storm jacket I got down from the top of the closet yesterday out of my hands without a word and sews up the hole in the elbow. \"I'm coming with you,\" she says. \"Don't argue.\"", "if": {"op": "and", "a": [{"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЖИВА"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КОНТРОЛЬ"}}, {"op": "cmp", "c": "<", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "СВЯЗЬ_ПАРТНЁР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 40}}]}}, {"md": "Yesterday I got Granddad's old storm jacket down from the top of the closet. Dad gave it to me once, saying, \"Must be Granddad's,\" and I've never worn it since. I sew up the hole in the elbow myself, with big stitches, the best I can. My wife would have done it better.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_ЖЕНА"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "УМЕРЛА"}}}, {"md": "Varya calls from Novosibirsk and calls me \"Daddy.\" All her life she said \"Dad.\" Andryusha laughs at that, and I don't.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В706"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЗНАНИЕ"}}, "drift": true}, {"md": "There are sixteen smoke detectors in this apartment; I brought twelve of them from the old one. At night I lock the front door with two locks and the chain. When Kolka brought Andryusha, he looked at the chain, then at me, and said his \"Dad, no experiments\" in a voice as if he were asking for something else.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_В709"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "КОНТРОЛЬ"}}}, {"md": "The fishing rods stand in the front hall. Andryusha's rubber boots stand next to my shoes, toes pointing at the door."}, {"md": "\"Granddad,\" says Andryusha from under the blanket, \"if I fall in the water, will you catch me?\""}, {"md": "\"I'll catch you,\" I say."}, {"md": "Three people from our graduating class lived to see the comet. As far as I know, I was the only one who went to look at it.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "At three I wake Andryusha, and he jumps up as if he hadn't been asleep at all. He dresses himself, pulls his boots on the wrong feet, switches them. He carries the jar of worms out in front of him with both hands, the way I once pushed a stroller. On the stairs he whispers, do fish bite in the dark? I say we're about to find out."}, {"md": "The yard smells of dust and dry grass. The comet hangs over the roofs. Andryusha holds my hand tight and doesn't let go all the way to the riverbed."}], "B": [{"md": "At three I get up, dress in the dark and stand over Andryusha for a long time. He's asleep with his arms flung out, his fishing rod lying on the blanket. I take the rod, tuck him in and write on a slip of paper: \"Gone fishing. Go back to sleep. Granddad.\" I put the note on his boots."}, {"md": "I close the door quietly, two turns of the key. The yard smells of dust and dry grass, and the comet hangs over the roofs."}], "C": [{"md": "At three my wife is already sitting in the kitchen, dressed, with a thermos. \"Don't wake Andryusha,\" she says, and I don't. I write him a note: \"Gone fishing. Go back to sleep,\" and put it on his boots."}, {"md": "On the stairs she goes first, holding the railing. In the yard she takes my arm, and we walk like that all the way to the riverbed, slowly, the way old people walk, and the comet hangs over us."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Take Andryusha out at first light"}, "B": {"label": "Go alone, leave Andryusha asleep"}, "C": {"label": "Take my wife"}, "D": {"label": "Go nowhere. Lock the door and sit by Andryusha's bed"}, "E": {"label": "Leave this house. Alone, in the other direction from the river"}}}, "Т-00": {"title": "Prologue. The Dry Riverbed", "date": "28 July 2061, 03:42", "photo_caption": "The bed of the Tom. 2061", "text": [{"md": "The Tom has been dry for the third summer running. Where in ninety-six I was scared to swim out past the buoys, people now walk. Stones, a rusty old Lada lying wheels-up, dust as white as the flour in the humanitarian-aid sacks. From Kemerovo all the way to Tomsk the riverbed is dry, and people walk their dogs down the shipping channel."}, {"md": "The backwater is still where it always was: by the left bank, under the willow thickets, behind the spit with the old pilings. Round, forty meters across, full to the brim and black. Scientists from Tomsk spent three summers running sonar over it, and their report says it has no bottom. They could have saved the grant money and asked me. It has a bottom. I've lain on it."}, {"md": "A comet hangs over the riverbed. Its tail lies exactly along the river; you could set a compass by it. In eleventh grade our astronomy teacher promised us this comet. \"You'll be seventy-two. Live that long, and you'll see it.\" I lived that long, Galina Ivanovna. Here I am, sitting on a rock, looking."}, {"md": "How many times I've died, I honestly don't remember. You'd have to ask them; they remember everything. Before dawn you can hardly hear them, just a hiss, like Grandma's old Rekord TV after the last station went off the air."}, {"md": "The snag sticks up in the same place, white and smooth, its branches spread like the fingers of a hand. Sixty-five years ago I couldn't hold on to it."}, {"md": "It's eighteen minutes to four, and there's time before dawn to tell it all in order, starting with that July when I was seven and had never died yet."}]}, "Т-01": {"title": "Black Backwater", "date": "28 July 1996", "photo_caption": "Vitka, Nika and Bublik. Summer of '96", "text": [{"md": "In the summer of ninety-six our yard smells of tar, poplar fluff and coal, because here everything smells of coal. Even Mom's hair smells of coal when she gets home from her shift at the coal-washing plant. I'm seven. I own half a bike (a Kama with one pedal missing; the other half is Vitka's), a Turbo bubblegum wrapper with a red Lamborghini on it, and the whole of August."}, {"md": "Nobody's allowed to go to Black Backwater. The old woman on the ground floor says a boy from Shakhtyorskaya Street drowned there in nineteen-eighty-something and they never found him, because there are old pilings there, and a vodyanoy, the old man who lives under the water and grabs kids by the ankles. Vitka says there's no such thing as a vodyanoy, but the pilings are real, and they suck you under. So that's exactly where we go."}, {"md": "There are three of us. Vitka is a year older, ginger and freckled all over, the loudest kid in the yard, and he lies that he swims like a dolphin. He's seen dolphins on TV. Nika is his cousin from Novosibirsk; her parents sent her to us for the whole summer \"for the fresh air.\" Mom says there's nothing here to breathe but dust and chemicals, but Nika likes it. She has a panama hat with cherries on it, a face as serious as the vice principal's, and a ginger puppy called Bublik who doesn't know the word \"no\" yet. And Nika counts everything in the world: the steps in our stairwell, even how long Vitka can stay under the water in the bathtub. Vitka lasts forty seconds and then tells everybody it was a hundred."}, {"md": "The water in the backwater is black, like the tea Dad drinks after his shift. Wade in up to your knees and your legs are gone, just your swim shorts sticking up. At the far edge, by the old pilings, a white snag climbs out of the water with its branches spread out, exactly like a hand. The sun is smacking me right in the eyes. I squint and see something under the water by the snag, shining and winking. I nudge Vitka. He looks and says, \"Huh? There's nothing there.\" So it's only winking at me."}, {"md": "\"Bet you can't make it to the snag.\" Vitka is pulling his T-shirt off over his head and gets stuck in it. \"Last one there's a girl.\""}, {"md": "\"I am a girl,\" says Nika. \"And I'm not stupid enough to swim out there.\""}, {"md": "Vitka comes out of his T-shirt red in the face and, so he can beat at least somebody, chucks a stick into the water. Bublik yelps and flies off the rock after it. At first it's funny. He snorts and paddles with all four legs and even grabs the stick in his teeth. Then the water turns him sideways. The stick floats off by itself, and Bublik gets pulled toward the snag, not fast, just smooth and steady, like a sled when somebody's pulling it by the rope. Only his ears are left above the water."}, {"md": "Nika screams so loud that crows fly up off the far bank."}, {"md": "The old woman downstairs was right about that backwater. In almost every world where I went into the water that day, they never found me.", "kind": "old"}, {"md": "Vitka just stands there with his T-shirt in his fist and doesn't say anything, for the first time all summer. Nika has stopped screaming. She's looking at me, and the ears by the snag keep getting smaller."}], "choices": {"A": {"label": "\"I'm not chicken!\" Race Vitka to the snag"}, "B": {"label": "Jump in after Bublik"}, "C": {"label": "Dive for the shiny thing by the snag"}, "D": {"label": "Run for the grown-ups"}}}, "Т-02": {"title": "Four Minutes", "date": "28 July 1996", "photo_caption": "(frame overexposed)", "text": [{"md": "Halfway there I pass Vitka, for the first time in my life, and I even have time to feel glad about it. By the pilings the water turns heavy, like a wet blanket, and somebody takes me by the legs, calm as anything, like they own the place, and pulls me down. I kick, and it holds on and takes its time. The last thing I see above me is Vitka's heels. The heels are kicking back toward the shore.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СПОР"}}}, {"md": "Bublik squeals and slaps at the water, claws right across my arms. I grab him by the scruff and shove him toward the shore with both hands, as hard as I can. He scrambles out onto a rock and shakes himself off all over Nika. And the current turns me around and lays me down under the snag, gently, the way Mom puts me to bed.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БУБЛИК"}}}, {"md": "The shiny thing under the water turns out to be closer than it looked. I reach out, grab something cold and heavy on a metal bracelet, and squeeze my fist shut. And then the snag catches me by the shorts, and I can't get back up. I could push off with both hands, but then I'd have to open my fist, and for some reason I don't want to.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧАСЫ"}}}, {"md": "I make it about twenty steps along the bank, and then Nika screams in a voice that isn't hers. Vitka the Dolphin has gone in after the puppy, and he's thrashing by the snag, and his head keeps going under. I don't remember how I got into the water. I remember pushing Vitka up from underneath by his wet back, and him grabbing the snag and climbing up right over me, over my head, like I'm a ladder.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ"}}}, {"md": "The snag rips my palm open from the thumb all the way to the wrist. I feel it even through the cold. The last air comes out of my mouth in big bubbles, and the bubbles run up toward the sun, and I stay down. Then the cold goes away."}, {"md": "Under the water it's dark and warm, and I'm not alone. I can't see anyone, but there are lots of them. It's like the bus in the morning, when you close your eyes and still know there are people all around you and everybody's shoving. Somebody's breathing right in my ear, even though there's nothing to breathe down here."}, {"md": "From above, through the water, somebody is counting in a thin girl's voice: sixty, sixty-one… She loses her place, sniffs, and keeps counting."}, {"md": "Later the doctors talked to Mom about hypothermia and the diving reflex. Clever words. I don't remember any reflexes down there. I remember it was crowded.", "kind": "old"}, {"md": "I'm not scared, honest. It's like hide-and-seek, when whoever's it is already counting and you still haven't decided where to run."}], "choices": {"A": {"label": "Swim toward the voice"}, "B": {"label": "Listen to what they're whispering"}, "C": {"label": "Look down"}, "D": {"label": "Call for Mom"}}}, "Т-02б": {"title": "Two Hundred and Fifty-Two", "date": "28 July 1996", "photo_caption": "City Hospital No. 2, children's ward. July 1996", "text": [{"md": "The voice in the dark is a kid's voice, a voice from somebody's yard.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧЕЙ"}}}, {"md": "\"Whose kid are you?\"", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧЕЙ"}}}, {"md": "\"I'm a Samokhin,\" I say, even though I don't have a mouth down here.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧЕЙ"}}}, {"md": "\"So go back to your own,\" says the voice, and shoves me in the chest. It hurts, like a ball in the stomach.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧЕЙ"}}}, {"md": "I listen. The whisper comes apart into voices, and they all talk in my voice, only one is thinner, one is lower, and one wheezes like Dad after a shift. They're counting along with the girl up above: one hundred ninety, one hundred ninety-one. They really, really need to not lose count.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ХОР"}}}, {"md": "I look down and see myself. I'm lying on the sand, as white as that snag, and an old man in a wet striped sailor's shirt is kneeling over me and pushing on my chest with both hands. His face is like Dad's, only old and unshaven. He wipes his eyes with his palm, and there's an old scar on the palm.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЛИЦО"}}}, {"md": "I call for Mom, and the dark smells of coal, of Mom's hair after a shift. Somebody puts a big rough palm on my forehead. It isn't Mom's, but I'm not scared anyway.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "МАМА"}}}, {"md": "Then something hits me in the chest like a horse's hoof, and I cough. Half the river pours out of my mouth. Sand in my teeth, sun, the sky too bright. Somebody turns me on my side and holds me by the shoulder with a heavy wet hand, and the water keeps coming and coming out of me. Then the hand on my shoulder is gone."}, {"md": "By the time I can see, whoever pumped the water out of me is gone too. Somebody has covered my legs with a wet canvas storm jacket, and bare footprints lead off into the willows, long ones, a grown-up's. The sand in them is still dark with water, and the bushes are swaying, though there's no wind."}, {"md": "Nika is squatting next to me, lips blue, panama hat in her fist, looking at me like I'm somebody else."}, {"md": "\"Two hundred and fifty-two,\" she says. \"I counted. Two hundred and fifty-two seconds.\"", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "one_life"}}}, {"md": "Vitka once stayed under the water in the bathtub for forty seconds, and the whole yard said he held the record. I lie on the sand and think: so now the record's mine.", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "one_life"}}}, {"md": "\"Forty,\" she says. \"I counted. Forty seconds.\"", "if": {"op": "one_life"}}, {"md": "Vitka once stayed under the water in the bathtub for forty seconds, and the whole yard said he held the record. I lie on the sand and think: so now we're even.", "if": {"op": "one_life"}}, {"md": "I open my fist. On top of the fresh cut lies a man's watch on a metal bracelet, wet, with a little star on the dial. The hands are stopped at four-twelve. Vitka leans in to look, and I don't know why, but I close my fist again.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧАСЫ"}}}, {"md": "Vitka is standing knee-deep in the water and shaking all over, from the top of his head to his heels. He isn't crying. Vitka never cries. Then he says, \"You stupid or what?\" and sits down right in the water.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ"}}}, {"md": "Vitka stands off to one side and doesn't look at me at all, like if he doesn't look, none of it happened.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "СПОР"}}}, {"md": "Wet Bublik, skinny as a rat, climbs into my face and licks the sand off my cheeks. Nika doesn't pull him away.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БУБЛИК"}}}, {"md": "Half an hour later the ambulance comes: an old UAZ van with a red cross and one headlight missing, the kind everybody calls a \"loaf,\" because that's what it looks like. Mom flies into it still in her work overalls, slaps my face, and then hugs me so hard I can't breathe again. At the hospital they sew up my palm, four stitches. In the hallway the doctor says \"hypothermia\" and \"lucky\" to Mom. Until evening the police go around Rudnichny, our end of town, asking about the old man in the striped shirt. Nobody knows anyone like that."}, {"md": "The next day another doctor comes into the ward, not one of ours. He's in a gray suit, no white coat, with a briefcase, and he says he's from Tomsk, \"sharing experience.\" He talks quietly and keeps rubbing his eyes. He takes my blood, two test tubes, even though they already took some in the morning and Nurse Zina grumbled that my veins are thin as threads. His fingers shake, but he gets the vein on the first try, not like Zina. Then he leans in really close, so Zina can't hear, and asks:"}, {"md": "\"What did you hear down there?\""}, {"md": "I tell him they were counting. He sits down on my bed right away, even though you're not allowed to sit on the beds; Zina chases people off for it. He sits there all white. I think maybe they're going to sew up his palm too.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ХОР"}}}, {"md": "I don't know what to say, so I shrug. He nods like I answered him anyway, ruffles my hair and leaves. He puts the test tubes in his briefcase.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ХОР"}}}, {"md": "That \"doctor\" was forty. Looked a good sixty. In ninety-six everybody did.", "kind": "old"}, {"md": "At night I lie holding my stitched-up palm on top of the blanket so it won't get wet. I sleep badly, and I keep wondering how that man from Tomsk knew you could hear anything down there under the water."}, {"md": "Childhood comes next. You can't truly die in it: the first fork where death becomes a memory and a rewind comes after graduation. The Choir is already listening.", "kind": "sys", "if": {"op": "not", "a": {"op": "lived", "id": "Т-02б"}}}]}, "Т-03": {"title": "Vodyanoy", "date": "September 1996", "photo_caption": "Class 1A, School No. 41. 1 September 1996", "text": [{"md": "On the first of September I go to first grade with gladioli and a pink scar right across my palm, from the thumb to the wrist. On the first day of school every kid carries flowers for the teacher. Mom traded the neighbor a jar of blackcurrant jam for the gladioli, and I got the scar for free, along with four stitches they took out back in August."}, {"md": "By the second day they're already calling me Vodyanoy. People on Rudnichny gossip more about how the Samokhin kid drowned and didn't drown than about Santa Barbara, and even Auntie Lyuba from the school cafeteria knows. She gives me an extra jam bun, like I'm sick."}, {"md": "On Saturday Grandma comes in from her wooden house under Krasnaya Gorka. She brings a jar of pickled mushrooms and takes the storm jacket I was carried home in off the balcony. Mom washed it but couldn't bring herself to throw it out. Grandma holds the jacket on her lap for a long time and asks me to tell her about the old man who pulled me out. I barely saw him myself, so I tell it the way Nika did: he's old, in a striped shirt, and his face looks like Dad's."}, {"md": "\"Tisha…?\" Grandma says very quietly, and she isn't saying it to me."}, {"md": "Tisha was what they called my grandfather. He died before I was born, \"at the mine.\" In Kemerovo that's what they say about all the grandfathers. Mom takes Grandma into the kitchen, and they rattle cups in there for a long time, though nobody pours any tea."}, {"md": "At supper Dad tells us about ninety-one, when he overslept his shift with a hangover, and that same day the cage, the lift that takes the miners down, broke loose and fell."}, {"md": "\"We Samokhins are lucky,\" he says, and knocks on the table with his knuckles."}, {"md": "\"Dad, whose kid are you?\" I ask, and I don't know why.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧЕЙ"}}}, {"md": "Dad drops his mug. Tea runs all over the oilcloth, Mom swears and runs for a rag, and Dad just sits there and looks at me while she wipes it up. Nobody explains anything to me.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧЕЙ"}}}, {"md": "The cage in ninety-one really did fall. Two men died. Every year after that Dad drank to them on Pokrov, the October church holiday, and never once to himself.", "kind": "old"}, {"md": "At school there's Shmelyov from 1B, and Shmelyov really likes the word Vodyanoy. Every break he gurgles after me and shows with his hands how I drowned."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I punch Shmelyov in the nose right by the coat room, and we roll around on the floor until the PE teacher pulls us apart. Shmelyov's nose is bleeding, and I've split my eyebrow on the corner of a bench. They stitch the eyebrow at the same City Hospital No. 2 where they did my palm, and when the doctor sees Mom he says, \"You again?\""}, {"md": "After that Shmelyov calls me by my last name. The scar over my eyebrow will stay for the rest of my life."}], "B": [{"md": "\"Yeah, Vodyanoy. So?\" I say to Shmelyov in front of the whole class, and I gurgle right back at him, loud, with bubbles, like a drowned man in a cartoon. The class cracks up. Shmelyov doesn't know what to do, because this time they're not laughing at me."}, {"md": "A week later even the girls call me Vodyanoy, and I like it. The nickname will stick for a long time."}], "C": [{"md": "I write in marker on pages from my exercise book: LOOKING FOR THE GRANDPA IN THE STRIPED SHIRT WHO SAVED ME. Mom sighs, but she helps me stick them up on lampposts all over Rudnichny."}, {"md": "Nobody finds the grandpa. But in October the *Kuzbass* newspaper prints half a column about a boy looking for the man who saved him. Mom cuts it out with scissors and hides it in the china cabinet."}], "D": [{"md": "I don't talk about water anymore. I only go in the bathroom with the door open, and when Mom runs a bath, I go to my room. At first Mom gets angry, then she takes me to the psychologist at the clinic, then she gives up."}, {"md": "Shmelyov soon gets bored of gurgling. What's the fun of teasing somebody who doesn't answer back."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Punch Shmelyov, and whatever happens, happens"}, "B": {"label": "\"Yeah, Vodyanoy. So?\" Make the nickname mine"}, "C": {"label": "Find the old man in the striped shirt who pulled me out"}, "D": {"label": "Never talk about water again. Ever"}}}, "Т-04": {"title": "The Rail War", "date": "May 1998", "photo_caption": "Frying pans. Our front hall, 1998", "text": [{"md": "The mine hasn't paid wages for seven months. In February Sonka was born, red and loud like a fire engine, and in April, instead of money, they gave Dad forty cast-iron frying pans. They stand in the front hall in a tower, and everybody trips over it except Sonka, who can't walk yet."}, {"md": "On TV they keep saying the word \"barter.\" At first I thought it was the name of some minister."}, {"md": "In May the miners go and sit down on the railroad tracks. Near Anzhero-Sudzhensk the trains are stuck, nothing is moving on the Trans-Siberian, and outside Kemerovo they're burning fires on our branch line too. Dad comes home at night smelling of smoke and says to Mom:"}, {"md": "\"Till they pay up, we don't get off the tracks.\""}, {"md": "\"And what are we going to eat?\" Mom asks. \"Frying pans?\""}, {"md": "On Friday at supper he tells me for the first time, \"Tomorrow you're coming with me. Let them see we've got kids.\" Mom says nothing and slices the bread thinner than usual."}, {"md": "But back on Wednesday Vitka made me promise. At the old substation behind the garages there's copper cable lying around that doesn't belong to anybody, and the scrap yard on Shakhtyorov Street pays real cash for copper. \"I can't haul it without you,\" he said. \"You promised.\" Vitka's mom has been out of work for three months, and now he's hungry all the time."}, {"md": "On Friday Nina Petrovna herself comes to our apartment, in her teacher's coat, and talks with Mom in the kitchen for a long time. I'm the only one in class who solved the problem about the two trains, and on Saturday there's a math olympiad in town for the younger grades. In the front hall, next to the frying pans, she bends down to me. \"A chance like this comes once, Samokhin.\""}, {"md": "Mom doesn't ask for anything. She just says she can't haul forty frying pans to the market by herself, and looks at me."}, {"md": "On Saturday morning all four of them are waiting for me."}, {"md": "Three months later came the '98 default, when the ruble fell through the floor, and the frying pans turned out to be the hardest currency in the house. Mom used to joke that they were the only wages inflation didn't eat.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I'll remember that night by the fire on the embankment for the rest of my life. The men bang their helmets on the rails, steady as a clock, and somebody is roasting pork fat on a piece of wire. Dad sits me down next to him and lets me hold his helmet, heavy and warm inside."}, {"md": "Toward morning he talks to me like a grown-up. \"Remember this, Andryukha. Asking gets you nothing. You stand, and you don't budge.\" A week later they pay half of what they owe, in real money. The frying pans stay in the front hall."}], "B": [{"md": "Mom and I stand at the Central Market from eight in the morning till dark. By lunchtime I've figured out that the old ladies buy from whoever they feel sorry for, so I start lying that these are our last ones and my dad's down the mine. The part about the mine is true, by the way."}, {"md": "Mom goes red but doesn't say anything. By evening we've sold nine frying pans, and Mom laughs for the first time in a month."}], "C": [{"md": "Vitka and I spend two days sawing through the cable with a hacksaw. On the second day some older guys from Rudnichny show up at the substation and tell us we're idiots: the real copper isn't lying in the grass, it's hanging up there on the pole, thick as your arm. Lyokha, the oldest, climbs the pole with wire cutters and tells me to hold the end of the wire down below while he cuts it."}, {"md": "I take hold of the wire, and the back of my neck goes cold, the way it did that time under the water. I don't know what it is. I just let go and step back, even though Lyokha is swearing at me from up top."}, {"md": "He cuts the wire. There's a bang so loud my ears go deaf for a second, and a white flash. Lyokha falls off the pole backwards and lies in the grass with the wire cutters smoking next to him. The wire I was holding a second ago is thrashing on the ground, spitting sparks. The ambulance takes Lyokha away with his arms black to the elbows. He'll live, but he won't be back at school until winter."}, {"md": "We still turn our own cable in at the scrap yard, for a hundred and forty rubles. That's more than Mom got paid for April. The older guys see us there, slap Vitka on the shoulder, and remember us both."}, {"md": "I give Mom seventy rubles and lie that I found it. She looks at me for a long time and doesn't ask anything."}], "D": [{"md": "At the olympiad in the Pioneer Palace I solve every problem except the last one, the one about the snail climbing the pole, and I come in third. Nina Petrovna buys me a chocolate-covered ice cream bar and says I've got \"a head on my shoulders.\""}, {"md": "Mom hangs the certificate in the kitchen, next to the calendar. Dad looks at it and doesn't say anything, but later I see him showing it to Uncle Tolya."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "To the tracks, with Dad"}, "B": {"label": "To the market with Mom, to sell frying pans"}, "C": {"label": "With Vitka, for the cable"}, "D": {"label": "To the olympiad with Nina Petrovna"}}}, "Т-05": {"title": "The Box", "date": "August 2000", "photo_caption": "T. S. and V. P., the Tom, 1981", "text": [{"md": "All through August the TV shows the submarine *Kursk*, all day long. Mom watches the news and cries; Dad turns off the TV and goes out on the balcony to smoke. They think there's nothing in the world scarier than down there in the dark. I've been there. It's crowded, but it isn't scary. Only you can't talk about it, I've figured that out already, so I sit there and keep my mouth shut."}, {"md": "To keep me from getting under her feet, Mom sends me to clear out the shed. In there are skis without bindings, Dad's fishing rods, jars of nails, and a box from a pair of Yugoslav boots, wrapped round and round with blue electrical tape. In the box there are graph-paper notebooks full of formulas, one photograph, and an envelope with a wax seal. On the envelope it says \"For Kolya.\" Kolya is Dad."}, {"md": "The photo is of two men by a river. One is in glasses and a hat and looks like Dad, only older. The other is young and skinny, with a briefcase. On the back, in faded ink: \"T. S. and V. P., the Tom, 1981.\""}, {"md": "Mom looks into the shed, sees the envelope, and takes it right away. \"That's your father's. Don't touch it.\" The envelope goes into the china cabinet, behind the crystal glasses, where the documents and the rainy-day money are kept."}, {"md": "At night I can't sleep, and I hear a knocking, steady like the metronome in music class. I can't tell where it's coming from: the radiator, maybe, though they don't heat them in summer, or my head.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т02"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ХОР"}}}, {"md": "Mom wore the boots from that box for eight winters. Yugoslavia still knew how to make boots back then.", "kind": "old"}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I read the notebooks in the shed by flashlight, three evenings in a row. Out of all the formulas, the only thing I understand is one sentence penciled in a margin: \"The observer does not see his own death. Tested.\""}, {"md": "The last notebook has no formulas. There's a table in it, ruled with a ruler: number, date, time, place, how. And a last column, a narrow one: \"what I heard.\" There are forty rows, and almost all of them have the same time, 4:12."}, {"md": "I copy the table onto the last page of my school notebook. I tape the box shut again, and nobody notices a thing."}], "B": [{"md": "\"That's your grandpa's grad student,\" Mom says, after looking at the photo, and takes it away right off. \"Don't ask your father about Grandpa. Grandpa loved his work more than he loved them, your dad and your grandma. Your father never forgave him for that.\""}, {"md": "The photo goes into the china cabinet with the envelope. Mom closes the glass door and stands next to it for another minute."}], "C": [{"md": "For the box, the junk dealer on Sovetsky gives me a *Mortal Kombat 3* cartridge and fifty rubles on top. He says yes suspiciously fast, but I'm eleven, and I don't know how to be suspicious yet."}, {"md": "I spend the rest of the summer in front of the Sega, learning Sub-Zero's fatality. Mom doesn't ask about the box until September. I tell her I threw it out with the trash."}], "D": [{"md": "Dad takes the box without a word and carries it out to the garage. I follow him and watch him throw the notebooks into the potbelly stove one at a time, without looking. On the floor there's one scorched page left, with half of some formula on it."}, {"md": "\"Your grandpa was smart,\" Dad says. \"Too smart.\""}, {"md": "He shuts the stove door, and we walk home together. He puts his hand on my shoulder, the first time all summer."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Read the notebooks while nobody's looking"}, "B": {"label": "Ask Mom who the second man in the photo is"}, "C": {"label": "Take the box to the junk dealer on Sovetsky Prospekt: he trades Sega cartridges"}, "D": {"label": "Give the box to Dad"}}}, "Т-06": {"title": "The Buoy Keeper", "date": "July 2002", "photo_caption": "Vitka, Nika and me. Summer 2002", "text": [{"md": "In July Nika comes again, for the first time since ninety-six. At first I don't recognize her. She has braces, there are Strugatsky novels in her backpack, and she calls Vitka \"Viktor,\" which makes Vitka so mad his ears turn red. Vitka is fourteen. He smokes Yava cigarettes, shows off, and spits through his teeth like his stepdad, Uncle Tolya the cab driver."}, {"md": "Bublik is mine now. Back in ninety-six Nika went home without him; she said he'd \"made his choice.\" He's grown into a big floppy-eared ginger dog, and when he sees Nika he howls like a lunatic and rolls on his back. Nika pretends she doesn't care and scratches his belly for ten minutes.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БУБЛИК"}}}, {"md": "On the third day the three of us go to Black Backwater. Nobody suggested it; our feet just went that way. The backwater is just as black, and the snag still sticks up out of the water. I'm thirteen, and I'm trying as hard as I can to look like I don't care."}, {"md": "For six years I haven't gone into water deeper than my knees. When I'm in the bath, the door stays open. Vitka knows and keeps his mouth shut, and for that I forgive him a lot.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т03"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВОДОБОЯЗНЬ"}}}, {"md": "\"So, Vodyanoy,\" says Vitka. \"Rematch?\""}, {"md": "Nika doesn't say anything. She's looking at the snag, and I'm looking at her and thinking she probably still remembers how high she counted."}, {"md": "Upriver, around the bend, the old buoy keeper's hut sticks up from the bank. Nobody has set out buoys on the Tom for years, but the buoy keeper lives there anyway. People say he's about a hundred and sees everything that happens on the water."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "Nika and I swim to the snag side by side, her a little ahead. It's so cold my jaw aches. About five meters from the snag the back of my neck goes cold, and a second later my leg cramps, the whole leg, from heel to hip, like somebody's tied it in a knot. I go under with my eyes open and see white branches in the black water."}, {"md": "My hand finds a branch by itself. I climb up onto the snag, coughing, and hold on with both hands, and my leg sticks out stiff as a plank. Nika is treading water next to me, white in the face, and I hear her whispering, \"One. Two. Three.\" She's counting without noticing."}, {"md": "\"It's gone,\" I say at eleven. She stops counting. Five minutes later the leg lets go, and the water turns back into plain water."}, {"md": "We race back. Nika wins by half a length and laughs with all her braces showing, and on the shore Vitka yells that we're both nuts."}, {"md": "For six years I haven't gone into water deeper than my knees. That evening, for the first time in six years, I take a bath with the door shut.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т03"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ВОДОБОЯЗНЬ"}}}], "B": [{"md": "We sit on a warm rock away from Vitka, and I tell her about the dark under the water. About the voices, about how crowded it was, about the counting. I talk and wait for her to laugh."}, {"md": "Nika doesn't laugh."}, {"md": "\"I believe you,\" she says. \"I'm the one who was counting.\""}, {"md": "I don't tell anybody else about it after that. Neither does she."}], "C": [{"md": "Vitka and I crack up over her braces and the way she says \"Viktor.\" Nika doesn't say anything. Then she packs up her backpack and walks off alone, without looking back."}, {"md": "Three days later she goes home to Novosibirsk early. Vitka says, \"Who cares.\" And I feel lousy all evening and can't figure out why."}], "D": [{"md": "The buoy keeper is sitting by his hut, mending a net. He looks about a hundred, and his hands are blue with tattoos. He looks me over for a long time and says:"}, {"md": "\"Every July a fellow from the city comes out here, in a suit. Fifteen years now. Stands by the backwater till first light. Doesn't fish, just looks. Lost a boy here, people say, back in eighty-eight. Never found him.\""}, {"md": "He spits in the water and doesn't say another word to me. All the way back I think about that boy from Shakhtyorskaya Street the old woman downstairs told us about."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Swim across the backwater. With Nika"}, "B": {"label": "Take Nika aside and tell her about the dark under the water"}, "C": {"label": "Laugh at \"the Novosibirsk girl\" along with Vitka"}, "D": {"label": "Walk upriver to the buoy keeper"}}}, "Т-07": {"title": "Ice", "date": "December 2003", "photo_caption": "Uncle Tolya's Lada Nine. Winter 2003", "text": [{"md": "In December Vitka steals Uncle Tolya's Lada Nine. Not for keeps, just for the night. Uncle Tolya is on a bender, the keys are hanging on a nail in the front hall, and across the river there's a disco at the club, and the girls from School No. 110 will be there. You can't take the bridge, the traffic cops are there. So it's across the ice, straight over."}, {"md": "\"The ice is solid,\" says Vitka. \"Some guys drove over in a Niva yesterday. I saw it myself.\""}, {"md": "It's thirty-one below outside, and yesterday it was nine below. The Nine starts on the third try and breathes steam like a cow. Ruki Vverh! is blasting out of the tape deck, the cheesy dance pop everybody says they hate. Vitka sits behind the wheel in Uncle Tolya's fur hat with the earflaps, trying to find first gear."}, {"md": "I'm standing by the passenger door. The door is open, and Vitka's waiting."}, {"md": "The back of my neck goes cold, though there's no wind. For a second, like after a camera flash, I see the green glow of the dashboard lights, only for some reason they're underwater, and Vitka's red hair is floating up.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": ">=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "ДАР"}, "r": {"op": "num", "v": 10}}}, {"md": "\"You coming or what?\" Vitka yells. \"The slow dances are about to start and we're out here freezing our snot off.\""}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "I yank the keys out of the ignition and stuff them in my pocket. Vitka yells and comes at me, then kicks a tire and walks off without his hat, in thirty-one below."}, {"md": "For a week he doesn't even say hi to me. On the eighth day he comes over to make up, with a bottle of Baltika beer, one for the two of us."}], "B": [{"md": "About twenty meters from the far bank the Nine goes through the ice nose first. Water pours in over our feet, Vitka is swearing, and suddenly I say to him, very calmly, \"Out the window. Now.\" I don't know how I know."}, {"md": "We climb out through the windows, crawl across the ice on our bellies, and run home in our wet clothes, which are frozen stiff as boards by the time we get to our building. They pull the Nine out with a tractor three days later. Uncle Tolya never finds out Vitka was the one driving."}], "C": [{"md": "I run to the pay phone by the store and call Dad. He hitches a ride, gets there in fifteen minutes, and pulls Vitka out of the car by the collar."}, {"md": "Afterward Uncle Tolya whips Vitka with his belt, and the police put Vitka on their list of juvenile delinquents. Vitka only says one thing to me: \"You're a real rat, Andryukha.\""}], "D": [{"md": "I tell Vitka what I just saw: the green dashboard lights underwater, and his hair. I say it quietly, and I guess the way I say it makes Vitka go pale and switch off the engine."}, {"md": "We sit in the cooling car for about ten minutes without talking. Then he gets out and says, \"Screw you, Vodyanoy,\" and we walk home. He hangs the keys back on their nail."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Yank the keys out of the ignition"}, "B": {"label": "Get in and go"}, "C": {"label": "Run to the pay phone and call Dad"}, "D": {"label": "Tell Vitka what I just saw"}}}, "Т-08": {"title": "Lucky", "date": "April 2005", "photo_caption": "The Glubokaya pithead. Day three", "text": [{"md": "In April there's a methane explosion at the Glubokaya mine. I find out in algebra. A guy in the back row's Nokia beeps, he reads the text, then looks at me, and his face goes so strange that I get up and walk out of class without asking anybody. Twelve men are still underground, and Dad is one of them."}, {"md": "For three days and nights all of Rudnichny stands outside the pithead building. Women in puffy coats, a handwritten list pinned to the board, the prosecutor, a TV crew from Novosibirsk. One of them, the one with the microphone, goes after Mom: \"What are you feeling right now?\" I say a word to him that on any other day would get me slapped on the mouth by Mom. Today she doesn't hear. She stands there holding Sonka by the hood so she won't run off. The first evening I stand next to her and count the names on the list every hour, and there are still twelve. Where I was for the other two days, ask me even now, and I won't be able to answer right away."}, {"md": "Bublik howls in the yard three nights running, and none of the neighbors yell at him.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БУБЛИК"}}}, {"md": "On the first day they bring up six, then nobody for a long time. On the fourth morning I'm at the pithead again when the cage brings up three more. The first one isn't Dad; you can tell by the walk before you can see the face. Dad comes out second. He's gray as plaster, and he bums a cigarette off somebody, though his whole life he's only ever smoked his own. Mom doesn't run to him. She squats down right in the mud and covers her face with her hands. And my knee starts jumping by itself, and I can't make it stop."}, {"md": "That night I get up for a drink of water. Dad is sitting in the kitchen in the dark, in his undershirt, a cigarette in his fingers, unlit. The clock over the stove says four-twelve."}, {"md": "Exactly what my watch says, the one from the backwater. For some reason I put my hand behind my back.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ЧАСЫ"}}}, {"md": "\"Down there I could feel I was dead, Andryukha,\" he says quietly, without turning around. \"Three times I felt it. And then I'm standing by the crosscut, alive, breathing.\""}, {"md": "Bublik growls at him from under the table, the first and last time in his life.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т01"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "БУБЛИК"}}}, {"md": "Then he gets up and goes out on the balcony to smoke. He never talks about it again, not then, not later."}, {"md": "Of the twelve, nine came up alive. Every Pokrov after that Dad remembered the other three, along with the two from ninety-one.", "kind": "old"}, {"md": "Later on, people often asked me where I was those three days. I always kept it short."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "The leader of the mine rescue squad, gray-haired Palych (everybody calls him that, from his patronymic), has a voice like it's coming out of a barrel. First he chases me off, and then he lets me haul tanks and water. For three days I sleep in snatches on a bench in their locker room."}, {"md": "On the third night Palych teaches me which way to run if there's smoke in the tunnel. \"Into the draft, got it? Always into the draft.\" I keep repeating it to myself until I fall asleep."}], "B": [{"md": "I make soup out of whatever's in the fridge, put Sonka to bed, and lie to her that Dad's gone fishing. Mom sits in the kitchen for three nights in her coat and never takes it off."}, {"md": "I don't cry once in front of them. Later I was very proud of that, though there was nothing to be proud of."}], "C": [{"md": "Every night I go to the backwater and sit on the bank. I don't go in, I just talk to the water out loud, like an idiot: \"Take me instead.\""}, {"md": "The water doesn't answer. On the fourth morning I run from the river to the pithead, and the cage has already brought up three."}], "D": [{"md": "In the smoking room by the pithead I hear a foreman say under his breath that the methane sensors in the section were taped over so they could make their quota. I find the reporter from Novosibirsk and tell her everything."}, {"md": "Afterward I'm in bed for three days with a fever, because I stood in the wind for three days and nights. The story goes out on TV, and the mine director remembers the name Samokhin."}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "\"Hauling air tanks for the mine rescuers.\" Snuck in with them the first night, and they didn't throw me out"}, "B": {"label": "\"At home.\" Mom, Sonka, soup, and not one tear in front of them"}, "C": {"label": "\"At the river.\" Went to the backwater every night to talk to the water"}, "D": {"label": "\"Finding out why it blew.\" And told a reporter from Novosibirsk everything"}}}, "Т-09": {"title": "The Send-Off", "date": "June 2006", "photo_caption": "The send-off. Vitka in the middle", "text": [{"md": "Vitka is eighteen, and the draft office has remembered him. His notice says the twentieth: two years of service, start to finish. Vitka walks around the yard with his head shaved and acts like it's all a big joke."}, {"md": "That same week Nika comes. It's her last summer before senior year, and her parents let her visit us \"one last time, then it's college applications.\" The braces are gone. She's cut her hair short, and now the old ladies in our yard don't recognize her right away."}, {"md": "And Welder, who runs the scrap yard on Shakhtyorov Street and half of Rudnichny on the side, tells Vitka by the kiosk that the army thing can be \"sorted.\" Not for free, of course. \"You'll pay me back later.\""}, {"md": "These days Dad comes home from his shift and goes straight out to the garage. He sits there till late. Mom says, \"Go sit with him. He won't ask you himself.\""}, {"md": "On Friday Vitka catches me outside our building, already shaved, his head blue from the cold water. \"Andryukh,\" he says. \"Maybe screw the army? Welder said he'd sort it.\" He looks at me like it's my call and not his."}, {"md": "In the evening Nika phones, on our home line. On Saturday she's going out to her aunt's dacha; from the porch there you can watch the sun come up over the Tom, and it's her last sunrise here. \"Come with me,\" she says. \"Or don't.\" And she hangs up without waiting for an answer."}, {"md": "The send-off party is on Saturday. And on Saturday Dad will be sitting in the garage alone, and he won't call me himself. Mom's right about that."}], "after": {"A": [{"md": "The whole yard throws the send-off: an accordion, vodka, Vitka's mom wailing out loud. At two in the morning Sanyok, who lives in the next stairwell over, pulls up in his dad's Lada Nine and says let's go for a drive out to Pritomsky while Vitka's still not a soldier. Vitka climbs in front, I open the back door, and the back of my neck goes so cold that I shut it again."}, {"md": "\"Chickenshit,\" says Sanyok, and the three of them drive off."}, {"md": "Half an hour later the Nine is lying in the ditch near Pritomsky with its wheels in the air. All three climb out by themselves, through the windows, covered in blood and glass. Vitka is holding his arm and laughing like a lunatic. The back seat, where I would have been sitting, is crushed flat to the floor."}, {"md": "Toward morning Vitka, drunk, shaved, with his arm bandaged, hugs me and says into my ear, \"Listen. Don't leave my mom on her own.\""}, {"md": "In the morning the bus from the draft office takes him away. I wave until it disappears around the corner."}], "B": [{"md": "Welder sorts out the army in a week: at the draft office they suddenly discover that Vitka has flat feet. We don't have the money, so Welder says, \"You'll work it off.\""}, {"md": "We shake his hand, one after the other. His palm is soft and warm, and for some reason that's the scariest part."}], "C": [{"md": "Nika and I take the commuter train out to her aunt's dacha and watch the sun come up. We talk till morning about everything except the send-off."}, {"md": "In the morning I see her off at the station. Vitka's send-off happened without me, and from the army he doesn't write to me for a long time. The first letter only comes a year later."}], "D": [{"md": "Dad and I sit in the garage until late at night. He drinks vodka, I drink Kolokolchik soda. After the second bottle Dad suddenly says, looking at the wall:"}, {"md": "\"Your grandpa didn't work at the mine. He worked under it. There was a door down there.\""}, {"md": "I wait for him to go on. He's quiet for a long time, and then he says, \"Go to bed.\""}]}, "choices": {"A": {"label": "Give Vitka a proper send-off"}, "B": {"label": "Help Vitka \"sort out\" the army through Welder"}, "C": {"label": "Go to the dacha with Nika. The send-off can manage without me"}, "D": {"label": "Go out to the garage, to Dad"}}}, "Т-10": {"title": "Dawn", "date": "24 June 2007, 04:12", "photo_caption": "Graduation. The embankment, 4 a.m.", "text": [{"md": "Graduation night ends on the river embankment, same as everybody's. Girls in dresses from the Assorti store, guys in jackets they'll grow into, Sovetskoye champagne straight from the bottle, smuggled past our homeroom teacher up somebody's sleeve. Our homeroom teacher pretends not to see and keeps digging in her purse for a handkerchief."}, {"md": "Galina Ivanovna, our astronomy teacher, walks among us in a shawl, handing out parting words. To me she says the same thing she told the whole class in our last lesson:"}, {"md": "\"Halley's Comet comes back on the twenty-eighth of July, twenty sixty-one. You'll be seventy-two. Live that long, and you'll see it.\""}, {"md": "Everybody cracks up. Seventy-two is never."}, {"md": "Vitka's here too, though he graduated a year ahead of us. He came \"to see us off,\" in Welder's leather jacket, and he's handing out Parliaments to everybody. Now the two of us owe Welder together, and it hangs over us like the smog over the Kirovsky district.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "Vitka is doing his service near Chita. A month ago he sent a half-page letter, and half of it was blacked out by the military censor, or maybe by Vitka himself.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "!=", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ"}}}, {"md": "The sky over the Tom goes pink. And then everybody's Nokias beep at once, some alarms, some \"congrats\" texts. My 3310 says 4:00. I blink, and it says 4:12. I blink again, and it still says 4:12."}, {"md": "A text from Nika, in Novosibirsk: \"Awake? Me too. Looking at the river. Ours is different.\" I don't answer for ten minutes, because I don't know how.", "if": {"op": "cmp", "c": "==", "l": {"op": "var", "n": "F_Т09"}, "r": {"op": "lit", "v": "НИКА"}}}, {"md": "On the far bank somebody's standing with a fishing rod, or maybe it's a bush. While I'm trying to make it out, mist comes up off the river, and the far bank disappears."}, {"md": "Eighteen is the right age to decide who you're going to be, so I decided, differently in every world. In one of them I probably got it right. If only I knew which.", "kind": "old"}], "choices": {"A": {"label": "\"Like Dad.\" Mining college and the Glubokaya", "hint": "the mine and the rescue teams · can you get everyone out"}, "B": {"label": "Apply to the physics department in Tomsk", "hint": "science and a lab · can you die on purpose for the truth"}, "C": {"label": "Sign on as a laborer with a geological survey party in Gornaya Shoria", "hint": "taiga, the North, rock · a gift with no instructions"}, "D": {"label": "Welder said, \"Need money? I've got work\"", "hint": "the street, debts, betting your death for cash"}, "E": {"label": "Anywhere, as long as it's away from here", "hint": "the sea, other countries · a life where you never learn about your grandfather"}, "F": {"label": "The voices in my head got louder than people", "hint": "a hospital, a hermitage, the road · hearing everyone you didn't pull out"}, "G": {"label": "Stay. A job, a wedding, a dacha. Just live", "hint": "family, kids, smoke detectors · how many times you'll die for your own"}}}}, "deaths": {"✝В1-02.A": {"title": "Black Water", "date": "14 November 2009, 04:13", "lesson": "The face shoots, then collapses", "epitaph": "Age 20. The Glubokaya, the third longwall, the end stall. Kept cutting.", "mourner": "In the morning Tanya looked at the empty slot numbered two hundred forty-six for a long time and wouldn't hand that tag to anyone, not even the foreman.", "text": [{"md": "I lean on the jackhammer, and the seam breathes out in my face. The crack opens all at once, from floor to roof, and coal comes out of it. It pours like water from a burst pipe, black, loose and heavy, hits my knees, my chest, and knocks me backward."}, {"md": "I'm carried backward down the face, turned over, and I can't tell up from down anymore. Coal gets into my mouth, my nose, my ears, fine and cold. Just like the water in the backwater, only this presses in from all sides, tighter with every shove. I try to breathe once, and it crunches in my mouth."}, {"md": "My lamp is shining somewhere under my arm, in the black, a yellow spot. I think, \"Oh, come on. Again.\" Then I smell Mom's hair."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the face again with the jackhammer in my hands. The seam is crackling, and Mikhalych is listening to it with his head cocked."}, {"md": "Something crunches between my teeth. I run my tongue over my gums, and there's nothing there; they're clean. My hands grip the jackhammer so hard my fingers are white."}, {"md": "The crack above the jackhammer is thin as a hair. In a minute it will open from floor to roof, and I'll be flying backward down the face with a mouthful of coal. How I know this I don't understand. But the taste of that minute is still in my mouth."}, {"md": "The seam above the stall clicks, short, like a sunflower seed on a frying pan. It clicked like that then too, and I stood right here, in the stall, right by the face, counting the clicks."}]}, "✝В1-02.B": {"title": "Black Water", "date": "14 November 2009, 04:13", "lesson": "The gas comes first", "epitaph": "Age 20. The Glubokaya, the third longwall. Called the old man over to take a look.", "mourner": "They found Mikhalych next to me, shoulder to shoulder. For forty days afterward Dad visited both of us at the cemetery, first one, then the other, and never once mixed up the order.", "text": [{"md": "\"Mikhalych, take a look.\""}, {"md": "He comes over and shines his lamp on the crack. We stand side by side, two helmets, two spots of light on the coal. Mikhalych says, \"Uh—\" and doesn't get to say anything else."}, {"md": "Gas comes out of the crack, ahead of the coal. You can't see it or hear it; my ears just suddenly pop, like in the cage. The air goes thick and empty, like in a jar with everything pumped out of it. I open my mouth, and there's nothing in it to breathe. I grab for the self-rescuer on my belt, and my fingers can't find the clasp. Mikhalych sits down on the floor, slowly, as if he's tired."}, {"md": "The coal comes later. I can't see it anymore, I only hear it rustling over my head, steady, like rain on a garage roof. Under my cheek there's cold grit, and suddenly it's crowded and calm, like that time under the snag, where it was crowded with voices. The voices are here, close by, and they seem glad to see me."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the face again with the jackhammer in my hands. Mikhalych is listening to the seam with his head cocked."}, {"md": "I'm breathing through my mouth, loudly, like after a run, and I can't get enough. There's air. There's even plenty of it. I slap my belt, find the clasp of the self-rescuer and keep a finger on it."}, {"md": "I'm not going to call Mikhalych over. He's standing three steps away, alive, with his shovel, and he doesn't know that a moment ago he was sitting on the floor next to me."}, {"md": "I remember it got hard to breathe before the seam moved. The coal was still standing like a wall, and the props were already swimming in front of my eyes, and Mikhalych sat down on the floor without a word, the way you sit down for a smoke."}]}, "✝В1-02.C": {"title": "Black Water", "date": "14 November 2009, 04:13", "lesson": "The roof catches those who run", "epitaph": "Age 20. The Glubokaya, the third longwall, by the conveyor drive. Ran.", "mourner": "They dug Seryoga out alive by the drive. A month later he quit the mine to drive a cab and never went underground again, not even on a tour with his son.", "text": [{"md": "I drop the jackhammer and run. The face is low, I have to run bent over, my helmet knocks against the props, tok, tok, like a stick along a fence. Behind me something sighs, and then starts rustling, louder and louder, and the rustling catches up with me."}, {"md": "It hits me behind the knees and knocks my legs out, like a wave on the Tom when the Zarya hydrofoil goes by. I'm thrown onto the conveyor, face down on the belt, and the belt is moving, and coal piles onto my back, layer after layer. I still try to crawl. My hands scrape at the rubber; my legs aren't mine anymore, something heavy and cold is holding them."}, {"md": "Seryoga's lamp shines right in my eyes; he's shouting something, and I can't make out the words. Then the coal reaches the back of my head. It gets dark and quiet, and it smells of Mom's hair."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the face again with the jackhammer in my hands. Seryoga is singing off-key by the drive."}, {"md": "My back hurts as if the seam were still lying on it. I turn around and look back along the face, the way I ran. Twenty meters to the drive. I didn't make it. I made twelve; I remember by the props."}, {"md": "Mikhalych is listening to the seam and doesn't know anything. But in my ears it's still rustling."}, {"md": "I started running when the seam breathed out. Before that it was shooting for a whole minute, click, click, and I stood that minute out next to Mikhalych, listening. Seryoga by the drive, by the way, they dug out alive; I remember that too, from somewhere."}]}, "✝В1-02.D": {"title": "Black Water", "date": "14 November 2009, 04:13", "epitaph": "Age 20. The Glubokaya, the third longwall, behind the conveyor drive. Lay down where someone else had survived.", "mourner": "Afterward Seryoga told everyone that Samokhin lay down next to him and covered him with his body. That isn't true, but there was nobody left to argue with him.", "text": [{"md": "I drop the jackhammer and run to the drive while the seam is still only crackling. Seryoga is standing by the frame, finishing his verse. I knock him to the floor, under the frame, and lie down beside him."}, {"md": "\"What the hell are you doing?!\""}, {"md": "Behind us something sighs, and the coal comes down the face in a rustling wave. The iron frame of the drive stands over Seryoga, and the coal flows around it on both sides, the way water flows around a bridge pier. Under the frame there's a hollow left, and he breathes in it. There's room for one, and I'm lying half outside, up to my waist."}, {"md": "The coal settles on my legs, then on my back, layer after layer, cold and heavy. Seryoga holds my hand under the frame and shouts. I hear him until the coal reaches the back of my head."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the face again with the jackhammer in my hands. Seryoga is singing off-key by the drive."}, {"md": "They really did dig him out alive, under the frame; I remembered that right. I remember his hand holding mine, warm, in a fingerless glove, squeezing every time a new layer settled on me. One man fits under that frame, and that man is Seryoga."}, {"md": "Seryoga surviving wasn't a clue to anything; I made that up myself. I just wanted to lie down where somebody didn't die. I bang the jackhammer against a prop in anger, and Mikhalych turns around at the sound."}]}, "✝В1-03.A": {"title": "Methane", "date": "3 March 2010, 04:52", "lesson": "Gas catches the man running", "epitaph": "Age 21. The Glubokaya, the main drift, meter five hundred twenty. Ran for the shaft.", "mourner": "Seryoga made it. Every third of March after that he drank alone, in his garage, and at the eighth shot he'd call him his brother.", "text": [{"md": "I run over the sleepers with the self-rescuer in my mouth. Up top I can run eight hundred meters in three minutes; I ran it in PE at college. Down here every sleeper is higher than the last. The mouthpiece rubs my gums, my mouth is all rubber and sourness, and breathing through it is like breathing through a pillow."}, {"md": "At four hundred meters I start counting sleepers so I won't count breaths. At five hundred I find I'm on my knees and don't remember getting down on them. I get up. I take another twenty steps and lie down, carefully, on my side, the way you lie down on a couch."}, {"md": "My lamp shines on the roof, on a rusty lagging bar with \"520\" written on it in chalk. I really want to sleep, and I give myself five minutes. For some reason Mom is sitting next to me, saying her hair smells of coal again, however much she washes it."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the face again. The gas detector is beeping, and Seryoga is swearing forty meters behind me."}, {"md": "My legs are buzzing, the way they did after that run. I look at them, at my boots, at the wet floor under them. Not a single sleeper yet."}, {"md": "I remember the \"520\" on the lagging as if I'd read it out loud. Eight hundred meters, it turns out, is a lot for me. Up top I didn't know that."}]}, "✝В1-03.B": {"title": "Methane", "date": "3 March 2010, 05:30", "epitaph": "Age 21. The Glubokaya, the old crosscut by the stopping. Waited by the book.", "mourner": "They found him two days later. Mom identified him by the scar on his palm, and after that she stopped dyeing her hair: let it smell of whatever it smells of.", "text": [{"md": "The crosscut is dark and quiet and smells of old rock, like a root cellar. I sit down with my back against the cold stopping and wait. The self-rescuer hisses, my mouth full of rubber. By the book you're supposed to wait calmly, not move, save oxygen, and that's what I do. I sit with my hands on my knees, like at a school assembly."}, {"md": "The air in the crosscut stands still, like in a three-liter jar. Gas seeps in from the drift above, quiet as a cat into a kitchen. I don't notice until my arms go limp and the self-rescuer feels heavy and pointless, like a winter hat in May."}, {"md": "I take it off. Right away I feel good, warm and calm, like after a banya. Behind my back is the stopping, and in it a door, and behind the door a fresh wind is blowing. I don't know about the door. It's the last thing I don't know."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the face again, and the gas detector is beeping."}, {"md": "My shoulder blades remember the rough concrete. Cold. I sat pressed against it with my back for that whole hour and never once turned around."}, {"md": "What was there, behind my back? I don't know. I never looked. The ventilation plan hanging in the assignment room I never studied properly; there was never time."}]}, "✝В1-03.C": {"title": "Methane", "date": "3 March 2010, 04:58", "epitaph": "Age 21. The Glubokaya, longwall 5, by the conveyor drive. Went back for his partner.", "mourner": "They got Seryoga out alive. Six years later he named his son Andrei and couldn't get through his toast at the christening.", "text": [{"md": "I head back toward his lamp. Seryoga is sitting by the drive, ripping the self-rescuer off his face and yelling that there's nothing in it to breathe. I grab his hands, and he fights me off like a drunk in a sobering-up cell and catches my mouthpiece with his elbow. My self-rescuer flies out of my mouth."}, {"md": "It takes half a minute to find it on the floor, by feel. I do find it. Only I've already breathed without it, twice, deep, like before a dive."}, {"md": "My head goes light. Seryoga is still yelling, and I sit down next to him and think I ought to calm him down, say something boring, like Mikhalych. I open my mouth and can't remember a single word."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the face again. Seryoga's lamp is darting around the walls forty meters behind me."}, {"md": "My teeth remember his elbow. I touch my lips, and there's no blood; the mouthpiece is in place."}, {"md": "He wasn't suffocating in there. He only thought he was. And I sat next to him and couldn't find anything to say."}]}, "✝В1-04.A": {"title": "The Second Explosion", "date": "15 May 2010, 03:50", "lesson": "It will blow a second time", "epitaph": "Age 21. The Glubokaya, the drift to longwall five, meter one hundred forty. Ran in after the men.", "mourner": "Dad came out through the ventilation shaft after seven in the morning. At the wake he sat with a shot glass until morning and never once raised it.", "text": [{"md": "I jump into the cage with the second squad, and nobody stops me. Down at the shaft bottom it smells of burning and warm iron. The guys in masks go into the drift to longwall five, and I follow them without a mask, with just a self-rescuer, my collar pulled up over my nose. It's hot in the drift, like on the top bench of a banya, and hotter with every step."}, {"md": "About a hundred and forty meters in we find two men. One is sitting against the wall holding his head; the other is lying down. I take the one lying down under the arms. He's light; I drag him and think: there, it's working. I'll get him out."}, {"md": "Behind us, from the direction of the face, comes light. It's white and everywhere at once. I don't hear any sound. I'm lifted and thrown against the supports, and the air I breathe in burns inside me, all the way down to my stomach. My arms are still holding the man I was dragging, and then I stop feeling my arms, and him."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing by the cage again, and smoke and warm iron drift out of it."}, {"md": "Under my arms there's still somebody else's weight. I lower my arms, and they won't lower; they just hang there, bent. My face is burning from the inside, from the throat."}, {"md": "Down below, in three hours, it will blow a second time. I know it as surely as I know my own name. The guys from the second squad are checking their masks and joking about Palych's mother-in-law."}]}, "✝В1-08.A": {"title": "A Year Later", "date": "9 March 2016, 11:44", "lesson": "A bag over the sensor", "epitaph": "Age 27. The Glubokaya, longwall seven, the top corner by the sensor. Went to measure the gas.", "mourner": "The commission found the bag on the sensor afterward. Bondarenko served three years, and for forty years Tanya kept his number on the rack, tag next to tag, and never gave it to anyone.", "text": [{"md": "I go up to the top corner, to the junction. The instrument on my belt suddenly starts beeping without a break, on one note. I look at the screen, it says four point two, and I have time to be surprised, because the sensor on the prop is silent. Then I see the bag. Gray with dust, on blue tape, three turns, like a bandage. My three turns."}, {"md": "Down at the bottom of the face, by the shearer, a cutting pick strikes pyrite, like a match against a matchbox."}, {"md": "The fire comes up the face in an even wall, faster than I can turn around. It goes into my mouth and nose at the same time, and my eyelashes crackle. I'm torn off the floor and slammed back against a prop, under the sensor. The last thing I see is the bag. It melts and drips onto my sleeve."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing by the shearer again, and Seryoga is showing me Mishka on his phone, in the bobble hat."}, {"md": "I look up toward the top corner, where I was going to go. You can't see the sensor from here. But I know what's on it, and whose three turns of tape those are."}, {"md": "It hung there for a whole year, and I stopped noticing it after a week. A drop of hot plastic still burns my arm through the sleeve, though the sleeve is whole."}, {"md": "The back of my neck pulls me there, past this minute, to February, to that shift at this same junction. Bondarenko says \"just till the end of the shift,\" and the bag rustles in my fingers."}]}, "✝В1-08.B": {"title": "A Year Later", "date": "9 March 2016, 11:44", "lesson": "Gas built up under the bag", "epitaph": "Age 27. The Glubokaya, longwall seven, by the shearer. Was helping his partner with a cable.", "mourner": "They dug Seryoga out a day later, without his left arm. He taught Mishka to ride a bike with his right arm alone, and every year on the ninth of March Nika left the house for the whole day and came back without explaining anything.", "text": [{"md": "Seryoga and I are pulling the cable; he's cursing the shearer, and the shearer is cursing the seam. A cutting pick scrapes something hard and strikes a spark, small, like from a lighter, and the whole face flares up, from the shearer to the top corner."}, {"md": "I don't hear the explosion. I'm just laid down on the conveyor, and above, where the roof was, there's suddenly nothing. The support sections fold one after another, like dominoes, with an iron clang, and my turn comes."}, {"md": "The roof doesn't settle on my back all at once but gradually, as if somebody heavy were getting comfortable. Seryoga is somewhere close, under the same roof, calling me by my last name. I want to answer. There's no air left for an answer, and it smells of Mom's hair."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing by the shearer again. Seryoga takes off his glove and shows me Mishka on his phone."}, {"md": "He's alive, both his arms are there, the left one holding the phone, the right one poking at the screen. I look at his left arm longer than I should, and he asks what's up with me."}, {"md": "The spark came from the pick. It'll come from the pick if the shearer doesn't stop. And it'll only stop if the sensor sees the gas. I know why it doesn't."}, {"md": "Plastic rustles in my fingers, a Maria-Ra bag that isn't here. February '15, the junction, Bondarenko. Why I'm remembering it now I don't understand, but the back of my neck pulls me there like a sore tooth."}]}, "✝В1-09.A": {"title": "Going Back for Vitka", "date": "18 March 2021, 06:41", "lesson": "The beam settles a second time", "epitaph": "Age 32. The Glubokaya, the conveyor drift, by the crosscut. Was pulling a friend out from under a beam.", "mourner": "They were found side by side, Vitka's hand on his shoulder. Until the end of her life Vitka's mother set out two shot glasses on Radonitsa, the spring day for the dead, and said they had more fun that way.", "text": [{"md": "I take hold of the beam, brace my boots against the floor and pull up. The beam won't move. I pull harder, the veins in my neck swell so much the mouthpiece slips out of my mouth, and I catch it with my teeth. The beam gives a finger's width, and the whole section above it sighs and settles back down, lower than before. Vitka screams into the rubber."}, {"md": "I pull again and again. The self-rescuer is breathing harder and harder, I'm hot, my mouth tastes sweetish, like caramel. My arms don't stop obeying all at once; first my fingers, then my wrists. I sit down next to Vitka on the floor, just to rest, and he turns his head toward me."}, {"md": "His lamp shines at the roof, mine on his face. He says something, and I can't hear what. I feel good and sleepy, like on the bottom bench in a banya. Vitka puts his hand on my shoulder, heavy, and I fall asleep under his hand."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing over Vitka by the crosscut again, and he spits out his mouthpiece and says, \"Samokha. That you?\""}, {"md": "The sweetness in my mouth is still there. I spit and spit, and it won't go away, caramel, carbon monoxide."}, {"md": "A beam you pull without support settles lower. My shoulders know that now. The hydraulic prop is lying a meter away from me, and a minute ago I didn't even notice it."}]}, "✝В1-11.A": {"title": "The Door", "date": "September 2044", "lesson": "There's an air pocket inside", "epitaph": "Age 55. The old incline under Rudnichny, the flooded corridor of Level 640, meter twenty-six. Dove in.", "mourner": "Gosha and Rustam hauled him out by the rope forty minutes later. Tanya took his helmet from the morgue and set the lamp on her windowsill, number facing the street.", "text": [{"md": "The water is ice-cold, and the cold squeezes my chest at once like a hoop. I swim along the wall, holding a cable on hooks with one hand. The flashlight reaches half a meter and stops in the murk, and something white floats in the murk, in flakes. At fifteen meters the cable ends, torn off, and my hand grabs nothing."}, {"md": "I swim on without the cable, by a memory of the plan that I don't have. The corridor turns, I hit my shoulder on the corner and lose which way is up. The rope at my waist snags on something and won't let me back. I yank it and yank it, and the air goes out of me in bubbles, ticklish, through my nose."}, {"md": "I don't take the next breath; my body takes it for me. Water comes into my throat, cold and heavy, tasting of rust. Everything like when I was seven. Black water, crowded, and right by my ear somebody starts counting. Only the girl who counted isn't here."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the edge of the corridor again, knee-deep in black water, and Gosha is asking whether to put in the pump."}, {"md": "The water is inside me. I cough and cough and bring nothing up; my lungs are clear. Rustam pounds me on the back and asks if I've swallowed some dust."}, {"md": "The cable on its hooks ends at fifteen meters. At twenty my helmet knocked against something under the ceiling, hollow, with emptiness inside it, and I swam on, down, after the cable. The top of my head still remembers that sound."}]}, "✝В1.2-01.A": {"title": "The Belt", "date": "14 November 2023, 10:09", "lesson": "The belt dives under the drum", "epitaph": "Age 34. The Glubokaya, the conveyor drift, the transfer point at crosscut number three. Rode the belt to close a door.", "mourner": "The crew reached the crosscut forty minutes later; Rustam closed the door. Every year on the fourteenth of November Vitka came into the assignment room and put a glass on my old desk without a word.", "text": [{"md": "I lie flat on the coal, and the belt carries me into the dark. The arches flash so low overhead that I press my helmet into the coal. Before the crosscut the belt tilts up toward the transfer point, and I get up on all fours to jump sideways."}, {"md": "My knees slide apart on the fine coal. I push off, but there's nothing to push off from; the coal moves with me. A rubber boot catches on the edge of the belt, and I'm swung around feet first. I see the drum whole, in the light of my lamp, wet, black, turning evenly and not fast."}, {"md": "My boot goes under it first, with a crunch, like a dry board broken over a knee. I grab the frame with my hands, and my fingernails tear off one after another. At first there's no pain, just a pull downward, steady and smooth, like a winch. Then the pain comes all at once, and I scream. My lamp hits the roof, and the roof spins."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the face by the shearer again, the tablet beeping, and Vitka saying through his mouthpiece, \"Boss. Well?\""}, {"md": "My right leg is burning up to the hip, though it's whole. I shift from foot to foot and can't stop."}, {"md": "There's nothing to hold on to on the coal; my palms know that now. And along the frame before the transfer point, at shoulder height, ran a red emergency-stop cord. I flew right past it and didn't even turn my head."}]}, "✝В1.2-02.A": {"title": "The Crust", "date": "9 September 2033, 17:34", "lesson": "Where there's steam, it's hollow", "epitaph": "Age 44. The Glubokaya spoil heap, the middle of the slope. Climbed up after Vitka's son.", "mourner": "They got Yegorka down an hour later along the bulldozer's path, with burned feet. After the funeral Vitka came to the spoil heap with a shovel and threw cold earth on the smoking slope until night, until they led him away.", "text": [{"md": "I run up, straight at him, to where Yegorka is standing. The crust crunches under my boots like snow crust in March. Heat comes up through my soles, and after ten steps I can't feel my toes."}, {"md": "At the twentieth step the crust under me rings differently, dull, and I have time to hear it. My right leg goes down to the knee, all at once, without a crack, as if I'd stepped into a snowdrift. Below it's empty and bright, orange. My pant leg flares up, my boot runs."}, {"md": "I yank my leg, and the crust around it breaks in a circle, like ice around a hole. I fall in chest first. The heat comes into my mouth, and I stop breathing before I understand I'm burning. The last thing I hear is Vitka shouting \"Yegor!\" down below."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing by the fence with the \"DANGER! BURNING WASTE DUMP\" sign again, and Vitka is yelling, \"Yegor! Get down, I said!\""}, {"md": "My right leg is baking from the inside, from knee to heel. I look at my boot and wait for it to run."}, {"md": "On that slope, where steam comes out of the cracks, the crust is hollow, and it's bright underneath. Where nothing grows, it rings dull. I heard that a second before I went through."}]}, "✝В1.2-02.B": {"title": "The Crust", "date": "9 September 2033, 17:41", "lesson": "Wind lays smoke on the slope", "epitaph": "Age 44. The Glubokaya spoil heap, the downwind slope. Went up after Vitka's son with a crowbar.", "mourner": "They found him with the crowbar in his hands, ten meters from the boy. Yegorka later spent thirty years in the gas rescue service, and the first thing he told every new guy was to check where the wind was coming from.", "text": [{"md": "I go around the slope from the road side, where it's gentler, and climb, tapping every step with the crowbar. The crust rings and holds. I go fast and right, like in a drill, and I'm glad I didn't run."}, {"md": "The wind is blowing off the river. It lays the smoke from the upper cracks right on this slope, on me, and at first I hardly see it, just the smell, rotten, sweetish. Then my throat starts to burn as if I'd swallowed a box of match heads. My eyes water, and I rub them with my mitten."}, {"md": "Ten meters from Yegorka my knees give. I sit down on the crust, warm as a stove, and think I'll sit for a minute. The crowbar slips out of my fingers and rolls down, clanging. Yegorka shouts something to me from above. My head goes heavy and calm, and I lie down with my cheek on the warm stone."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing by the fence with the \"DANGER! BURNING WASTE DUMP\" sign again, and Vitka is yelling, \"Yegor! Get down, I said!\""}, {"md": "My throat is raw, like after a pack of Primas on an empty stomach. I cough and spit, and my spit is gray."}, {"md": "I tapped the crust right. I tapped the crust right, and it was the wind that killed me. It blew off the river and laid all the smoke on the slope I was climbing. I should have gone into the wind, from the river side, where the smoke is carried off the slope."}]}, "✝В2-02.A": {"title": "The Freight Elevator", "date": "9 December 2008, 16:52", "lesson": "Nitrogen sinks to the floor", "epitaph": "Age 19. TSU main building, the freight elevator between the second and third floors. Waited for the dispatcher.", "mourner": "Until she retired, Zoya Mikhailovna took students up the stairs, even after the elevator was fixed.", "text": [{"md": "I press the call button. The speaker crackles, and a woman's voice says, \"The mechanic's on his way, wait, don't panic.\" I'm not panicking; I stand and wait, and the mist rises to my knees. I want to sit down, and I do; it's more comfortable. The last thing I notice is that I'm not scared at all."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing in the elevator between the second and third floors again. The bulb is flickering. The flask's lid is hissing."}, {"md": "I remember sitting on the floor, leaning against the wall, not scared, even cozy, like on a bus in winter by the heater. That's what's making me shake now. I died and didn't even notice."}, {"md": "The mist is creeping across the floor again, low, right at my sneakers. I don't feel like sitting down anymore."}, {"md": "The mist lay on the floor, and I sat right down in it, like in a bath. The cold is heavy; it runs down and collects there, and up under the ceiling, by the grille, it's warm and smells of dust. I lift my face and feel it on my cheek already, right now."}]}, "✝В2-02.B": {"title": "The Freight Elevator", "date": "9 December 2008, 16:44", "lesson": "The elevator moves by itself", "epitaph": "Age 19. TSU main building, the freight elevator shaft. Climbed into the gap.", "mourner": "Mom came for his things and took everything from room 412, even an empty stewed-meat can.", "text": [{"md": "I pry the doors apart with my hands; they give with a creak. Above is a half-meter gap, and beyond it the third-floor landing and somebody's shoes. I pull myself up, get through to the waist, and then the elevator, which it turns out nobody cut the power to, remembers it needs to go up."}, {"md": "It all happens fast. It only hurts for one second."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing in the elevator between the second and third floors again. The bulb is flickering. The flask's lid is hissing."}, {"md": "I grab my stomach, because I know for sure it isn't there. My stomach is there, my sweater whole. The shoes beyond the gap were brown, by the way; I remember that from somewhere."}, {"md": "The mist creeps across the floor."}, {"md": "The elevator moved while I was hanging in the gap, by itself; somebody called it from upstairs. I remember the panel inside, a row of buttons with worn-off numbers and one red one, set apart, like in the elevators we rode as kids, pressing every button except that one."}]}, "✝В2-02.C": {"title": "The Freight Elevator", "date": "9 December 2008, 16:46", "lesson": "The flask's mist is nitrogen", "epitaph": "Age 19. TSU main building, the freight elevator. Opened a flask of nitrogen.", "mourner": "Den from Seversk quit the physics department a month later and went home. He said it was because of exams.", "text": [{"md": "I unscrew the lid so the flask will stop hissing. The hissing does die down, but a white cloud tumbles out of the neck, dense as cotton wool, and falls onto my hands. At first my hands feel nothing, and then I feel nothing either, because the cloud fills the whole cabin."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing in the elevator between the second and third floors again. The bulb is flickering. The flask's lid is hissing."}, {"md": "I look at my hands, pink and warm. A minute ago they were white and not mine."}, {"md": "I just died, right here in this elevator. I stand repeating it to myself and can't stop."}, {"md": "The cloud from the neck fell to the floor and started rising, from my sneakers to my knees, from my knees to my face, like water in a bath when you forget to turn off the tap. The last thing I saw was the grille under the ceiling, dry, dusty, and the cloud hadn't reached it yet."}]}, "✝В2-02.D": {"title": "The Freight Elevator", "date": "9 December 2008, 16:43", "epitaph": "Age 19. TSU main building, the freight elevator. Plugged the flask with his jacket.", "mourner": "For forty years Zoya Mikhailovna hung a tag that said \"Do not seal!\" on every Dewar flask, and students drew skulls on it.", "text": [{"md": "I pull off my jacket, twist it into a rope and jam it into the neck over the lid, tight, like a cork in a bottle. The hissing stops. The mist at my feet stops rising, and I even laugh out loud, alone in an iron box."}, {"md": "The flask is silent for about forty seconds. Then something inside it hums, low, like pipes in winter, and its fat side goes ice-cold under my palm. The nitrogen inside keeps boiling and won't stop; it has nowhere to go. The jacket blows out along with the lid, with a bang that makes my ears pop, and a white cloud hits me in the face."}, {"md": "I fall on my back. The cloud settles on top of me, heavy and soft, and there's nothing to breathe under it. The last thing I see is my jacket sleeve on the ceiling; it's caught on the grille and hangs there."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing in the elevator between the second and third floors again. The bulb is flickering. The flask's lid is hissing, and my jacket is on me."}, {"md": "I remember the flask humming under my palm before it fired. The cold was coming from the neck, that's true. But you can't plug it. The nitrogen in it is boiling all the time, the vapor is looking for a way out, and if there's no way out, it makes one itself, lid and all."}, {"md": "I remember everything right; I just drew a stupid conclusion, like at a colloquium when you know the formula and plug in the wrong thing. Just in case, I zip my jacket up to my throat."}]}, "✝В2-03.A": {"title": "Lagerny Garden", "date": "14 January 2009, 03:40", "epitaph": "Age 19. Lagerny Garden, the bench by the monument. Sat down for a rest.", "mourner": "A street sweeper found him in the morning. For a long time Lena kept telling everyone she'd asked him to stay.", "text": [{"md": "The first two kilometers I walk briskly and even sing Splean's \"No Way Out\" to myself. By the third I can't feel my toes. In Lagerny Garden the bench by the monument is all frost and for some reason looks warm. I sit down for a minute to catch my breath. The stars over the Tom are big, and I don't feel like getting up anymore."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing outside Lyokha's entrance again. Forty-two below. Streetlights in rust-colored fog."}, {"md": "I'm hot, and my feet feel like somebody else's, though I haven't taken a step yet. The worst of it is that the bench was warm."}]}, "✝В2-03.B": {"title": "Lagerny Garden", "date": "14 January 2009, 03:20", "lesson": "Sneakers slide on the bluff", "epitaph": "Age 19. Tomsk, Lagerny Garden, the steep bank above the Tom. Walked to the dorm in sneakers at forty-two below.", "mourner": "In the spring Tagir took my Adidas to the Tomsk-2 yard himself and gave them to the foreman. He said, \"Let somebody wear them out, since he didn't get the chance.\"", "text": [{"md": "I get to the park fast, because I'm angry and strong, and at first the frost seems funny to me. By the third kilometer the soles of my Adidas are like the plastic of a ruler. They don't bend, and with every step I feel the ice under my heel all in one piece."}, {"md": "The path runs along the very edge, above the Tom. Down below, about twenty meters, is the white riverbed, with steam rising off it like a pot. At the turn by the bench my right foot slides forward without a sound. I grab a bush, the bush snaps like glass, and I slide down on my back, faster and faster, until the slope runs out."}, {"md": "At the bottom I lie on the ice by the bank and can't get up: my right leg is folded under me the wrong way. First it hurts, then it's cold, then it's so warm I want to take off my jacket. The steam from the river spreads white over me, and I feel good in it."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing outside Lyokha's entrance again, and Lena is picking at the frost on the railing with her mitten."}, {"md": "My toes are burning inside my Adidas, as if I'd already frozen them to the bone. I look down and see white sneakers with three stripes, bought with freight-car money."}, {"md": "My mistake wasn't today. My mistake was that first fall, at the Tomsk-2 yard, when I threw out my old boots because in new sneakers my shoulders look wider. In Lyokha's front hall stand his dad's felt boots with galoshes."}]}, "✝В2-03.C": {"title": "Lagerny Garden", "date": "14 January 2009, 03:50", "epitaph": "Age 19. Lagerny Garden. Didn't understand he was being asked to stay, and walked.", "mourner": "Lena transferred to Novosibirsk and never celebrated Old New Year again.", "text": [{"md": "I say something to Lena about the cold and how it's time to go. She says, \"Well, go on, then.\" What that means, I don't understand, and I walk. On along the same path through Lagerny Garden, until I can't feel my toes, all the way to the bench by the monument."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing outside Lyokha's entrance again. Lena is picking at the frost on the railing with her mitten."}, {"md": "She was asking me to stay. It hits me now as clearly as if she'd said it out loud. And that me didn't understand and walked off to freeze."}]}, "✝В2-08.A": {"title": "Twelve Minutes", "date": "14 April 2016, 04:23", "epitaph": "Age 27. The Glubokaya mine, level minus six hundred forty. His heart didn't restart.", "mourner": "Lunyov came up top himself and went to see my mother himself. They say she didn't hit him.", "text": [{"md": "I stay. The voices get louder, then quieter, and outside there's no more beeping. There's a steady tone, like the dial tone when somebody's hung up on the other end. Lunyov is pressing on my chest with both hands, counting out loud. I recognize that counting, only this time it doesn't reach two hundred and fifty-two."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm lying in the warm, crowded dark again, and outside the monitor is beeping wrong. Minute nine."}, {"md": "I remember the steady tone. I remember Lunyov stopping counting. The cold now is the same, and the voices are calling the same way. Hearing it all once was enough for me."}]}, "✝В2-08.C": {"title": "Twelve Minutes", "date": "14 April 2016, 04:21", "epitaph": "Age 27. Level minus six hundred forty. Went after a child's voice and couldn't find the way back.", "mourner": "For a long time Nika couldn't look at water. Not even from a train window.", "text": [{"md": "I go toward the child's voice, and the dark turns into water, black as the backwater. The boy is very close, above me, in the light; I can see his shorts and his white heels. I swim toward him, and he doesn't get any closer. The Choir, it turns out, is so big you can get lost in it."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm lying in the warm, crowded dark again, and outside the monitor is beeping wrong. Minute nine."}, {"md": "I swam after the boy and didn't catch him, though he stayed in one place the whole time. It must be too early for me to reach him."}]}, "✝В2.1-04.A": {"title": "The Water Heater", "date": "14 November 2024, 03:40", "lesson": "Carbon monoxide has no smell", "epitaph": "Age 35. Sosnovka village, a rented one-room apartment by the station. Went to bed with a headache.", "mourner": "Mom never believed it was the water heater. Until she died she turned off the gas every night and went back twice to check the valve.", "text": [{"md": "I lie down without undressing, on top of the bedspread. The pillow smells of somebody else's detergent. My head doesn't ache in one spot anymore, it aches all over, and the nausea comes in waves, in time with the water heater behind the wall."}, {"md": "An hour later I wake up because I've thrown up on the pillow. I need to get up and open the window; I know that. Only my hand lies on the blanket and won't lift, as if it's gone to sleep, and I look at it for a long time. The blue window of the water heater glows in the dark kitchen, its reflections trembling on the ceiling. I start counting them and lose count at seven."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm sitting on the edge of the couch in my bathrobe again, my head aching, the water heater humming behind the wall."}, {"md": "I remember the hand on the blanket, heavy and not mine, and losing count at seven. I remember the sour smell of the pillow, though the pillow now is clean and smells of detergent."}, {"md": "I'm unsteady, but my legs do what I tell them. I'm not lying down on this couch tonight."}]}, "✝В2.1-06.A": {"title": "The R-255", "date": "17 October 2033, 03:41", "lesson": "A semi in the fog", "epitaph": "Age 44. The R-255 Siberia highway, kilometer one hundred forty-two, fog. Floored it before they noticed.", "mourner": "Every October the driver of the first semi set a plastic wreath at kilometer one hundred forty-two. He never even saw me; I passed him a minute before.", "text": [{"md": "I pass the semi in the oncoming lane, blind, into the milk, and push it to a hundred and thirty. The red lights disappear from the mirror. For three minutes I drive alone through the white, and the white glows in the headlights like a screen."}, {"md": "Then right in front of me a gray wall grows out of the fog. A tarp across both lanes, wet, with the letters \"…TRANS.\" I slam the brakes, and the wheel tears out of my hands. The blow comes in the chest. The belt cuts into my collarbone, and something inside me crunches, many times in a row, like footsteps on snow crust. I can't breathe in. In front of my face, wet canvas and an iron beam, with a drop hanging from the beam. The last thing I hear is the box from Kemerovo flying off the seat and banging against the glove compartment."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm driving through the fog again behind the semi's red lights, about fifty meters back, the needle at ninety."}, {"md": "I remember the gray wall and the drop on the beam. I remember the crunching inside, long, like footsteps on snow crust. The wheel is wet under my palms."}, {"md": "I take my foot off the gas, and the needle slowly creeps down."}]}, "✝В2.2-03.A": {"title": "March Ice", "date": "24 March 2029, 15:20", "lesson": "Ice won't hold a running man", "epitaph": "Age 40. The Tom by Rudnichny, a hole in the ice. Ran straight for his student.", "mourner": "Sixth Grade A graduated without a homeroom teacher. On their last day of school they put my chair by the blackboard, empty.", "text": [{"md": "I run straight across the ice, and about ten meters out the ice goes out from under my feet. The water is so cold that at first it feels hot. My jacket drags me down; I grab the edge, and the edge crumbles like a cookie. The current takes me by the legs, calmly, like it owns me, and I go under the ice almost without a sound. The last thing I see is gray light above me, cloudy, as if through frosted glass, and a dark spot at the edge: Timur's backpack."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing on the embankment again, and the ice under Timur is sagging with a quiet, wet sound. The backpack at the edge of the hole, twenty meters away."}, {"md": "I'm forty years old, and I've drowned in the Tom again, thirty-three years after the first time. The water is the same and pulls the same way. I'm not running straight across ever again, I don't care what happens."}]}, "✝В2.2-03г.A": {"title": "The Bridge", "date": "14 June 2045, 20:10", "lesson": "Never reach over railings from above", "epitaph": "Age 56. Kuznetsky Bridge, the third span. Pulled his student back over the railings and went over them himself.", "mourner": "Yegor Shmelyov became a rescuer. He tells every new recruit about the teacher who threw him back over the railings onto the bridge, and never once finishes the story.", "text": [{"md": "I come up from behind, quietly, and say, \"Come on, Yegor. Climb back.\" He turns toward my voice, and his heel slips off the ledge. I grab his hood with both hands and yank him toward me with all my strength."}, {"md": "He flies back over the railings and lands on the asphalt. The railing jams into my stomach, right at my belt buckle, and I roll over the handrail like over a chin-up bar in gym class, slowly and clumsily."}, {"md": "The icebreaker rushes up at me from below, a gray wet wedge. I have time to see the little river shells stuck to it and the green waterline. The blow hits me in the chest, and I don't hear the sound anymore."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing on the bridge again, ten steps from Yegor, my hand still held out."}, {"md": "The hood crackles in my fist, though my fist is empty. The railing presses into my stomach, right at the buckle, and I step back from it."}, {"md": "You can't pull a person back over railings from above if you're standing higher than the railings yourself: your weight tips you and you go over. You hold on lying down, when the bridge is holding you."}]}, "✝В2.2-06.A": {"title": "The Wager", "date": "14 March 2055, 02:40", "epitaph": "Age 66. Novosibirsk, Krasny Avenue, outside the Orpheus clinic. Went through the crowd.", "mourner": "Dasha found out from the news, in hospital pajamas, on the third floor, by the glass.", "text": [{"md": "I go through the crowd, and the crowd suddenly surges toward the line, all at once, like coupled railcars when the locomotive jerks. I'm crushed chest-first against a metal barrier. My ribs crack, first one, then several at once. I can't breathe in; I can only stare at the white body armor right in front of my face. A streetlight is reflected in it. My sixty-six-year-old heart gives out before my ribs do."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing in the crowd outside the clinic again. The guards stand in a line; someone throws a snowball at the glass."}, {"md": "I touch my ribs through my coat and count them, checking they're all whole. I'm sixty-six, and I've gone and pushed in where you push in at sixteen."}]}, "✝В3-02.A": {"title": "The Golets", "date": "16 July 2008, 15:58", "lesson": "Lightning strikes the cedar", "epitaph": "Age 19. Kara-Tag golets, the saddle, under the old cedar. Took shelter from the rain.", "mourner": "Miron Todyshev found me by nightfall, brought me down across his saddle and never once looked back. Zoya Arkadyevna wrote to Mom herself. Afterward Miron cut down the cedar on the saddle, alone, in two days, with a hand saw.", "text": [{"md": "It's dry under the cedar and smells of resin. I sit down on the needles with my back to the trunk, hug my pack and watch the rain pour off the branches in front of me like a wall, and beyond the wall there's nothing to see, only white. It even makes me laugh how cozy I am here, like in the hut behind the garages."}, {"md": "I don't hear the strike. I see all the needles under my palms flare blue, every single needle, and then someone enormous grabs me by the back, by the spine, and squeezes, the way you squeeze a sparrow in your fist. It comes from below, from the roots, through the ground, through me. My legs jerk on their own; the pack flies off."}, {"md": "I'm lying on my side and can't breathe in. My heart beats once in the wrong place, high up, by my throat, and doesn't beat again. In front of my eyes are needles, wet, with steam rising off them. It smells scorched. It smells of coal dust, like Mom's hair when she comes home from a shift and leans over me."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing on the saddle in the downpour again, and the hammer above my shoulder is humming like a transformer."}, {"md": "My back is still cramping, all along my spine, as if someone had drawn a red-hot wire down it. I remember that it struck the cedar, but it killed me through the roots. Its roots run across the rock in every direction, under the lichen, a long way. I was sitting by the trunk, right in the middle."}, {"md": "The soles of my feet are tingling, like a leg that's gone to sleep. The lichen under them is dry, and under it are roots, thick as an arm, stretching from the cedar across the whole clearing."}]}, "✝В3-02.B": {"title": "The Golets", "date": "16 July 2008, 15:56", "lesson": "The hammer sings before it strikes", "epitaph": "Age 19. Kara-Tag golets, the boulder field below the saddle, between two blocks. Ran downhill.", "mourner": "Miron Todyshev searched for me until morning with a flashlight and found me by my pack. The next year Zoya Arkadyevna left the field party. She kept the hammer and never once tapped with it until she retired.", "text": [{"md": "I run down the boulder field, from block to block, the way I used to run across the garage roofs in Rudnichny. The rocks are wet; the rusty lichen on them is slippery as soap. Three hundred meters to the forest. The hammer above my shoulder hums louder and thinner, and it seems to me that it's singing. I run, and it sings in my ear."}, {"md": "Then the song breaks off. White comes from every side at once, and I'm lifted and thrown, the way you throw a sack into a truck bed. I don't remember the blow, only flying. The blocks pass beneath me sideways."}, {"md": "I'm lying between two rocks, head downhill, and water is running over me. My legs won't obey. My head is lying on the rock somehow wrong, too far to one side, and I can't lift it. The hammer lies nearby, on the next block, and its pick is still glowing a little, blue. Then it goes out. It smells of wet rock and coal dust, Mom's hair."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing on the saddle in the downpour again, and the hammer above my shoulder is humming like a transformer."}, {"md": "I remember how it sang. Thinner, thinner, and then it broke off, and the strike came at once. I was running down an open slope, and the only thing higher than me on the whole boulder field was that pick above my shoulder."}, {"md": "The shoulder the hammer hangs on aches like a tooth in cold water. The hammer is on it now too, and the pick above my shoulder sticks up higher than my head."}]}, "✝В3-02.C": {"title": "The Golets", "date": "16 July 2008, 16:03", "lesson": "Water scours out the hollow", "epitaph": "Age 19. Kara-Tag golets, the hollow by the stream below the saddle. Lay down low.", "mourner": "They found me three days later, down at the tree line, in a heap of gravel. Since then Miron Todyshev doesn't lead parties up Kara-Tag. Zoya Arkadyevna wrote in her report: \"Accident on route,\" and underlined it twice.", "text": [{"md": "I lie down in the hollow, face down, beside the stream, and press myself against the rocks. Here I'm lower than everything around. Let it hit the cedar, the boulder, wherever it likes. The stream runs right by my face, thin, cold; I can hear it gurgling."}, {"md": "Then it stops gurgling and starts to roar. The water rises in a minute, first to my elbows, then to my shoulders, brown, icy, and something knocks in it, louder and louder, as if someone were rolling empty barrels down the chute from above. I get up on my knees."}, {"md": "The first block hits me in the side, and I fall face first into the water. I'm dragged downhill with the gravel, flipped over, slammed against rocks with my shoulder, my back, my head. Water in my mouth, in my nose, icy, with sand. I don't know which way is up. The scar on my palm snags on something and tears off with the skin. The last thing I feel is a smell. Even here, in icy water, it smells of coal dust and Mom."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing on the saddle in the downpour again, and the hammer above my shoulder is humming like a transformer."}, {"md": "There's sand in my mouth. I spit, and there's none; my mouth is clean, only my tongue remembers. I remember how the stream stopped gurgling and started to roar. The rocks in the hollow are smooth, water-worn, and higher up on the sides lie sharp ones, covered in lichen."}, {"md": "And water again. I ran from it into the mountains, and it found me here, twelve years later, in a chute two steps wide."}]}, "✝В3-02.D": {"title": "The Golets", "date": "16 July 2008, 16:01", "epitaph": "Age 19. Kara-Tag golets, the flat boulder beyond the stream on the saddle. Climbed up high.", "mourner": "Miron Todyshev took me down off the boulder himself and called no one. Later the Shors named that boulder Andrei's Stone, and they don't let tourists climb it.", "text": [{"md": "The boulder is wet, but I climb up, clinging to the cracks, and stand on its flat top. From here I can see the whole saddle: the cedar, the boulder field, the hollow where brown water is already running and knocking rocks along. It won't reach me. I'm higher than everything. I think I'm laughing out loud."}, {"md": "The hammer above my shoulder is already shrieking. The hair on my head stands up; I feel it rising under my hood, and my nose smells of a socket."}, {"md": "White comes straight into me, from above, into the crown of my head. I don't have time to notice pain, only loudness, so loud that sound becomes light and light becomes sound. I'm not on the boulder, and then I am, lying on my side on wet rock, and smoke is rising from my boots. The hand I was holding the strap with is black. My heart has stopped. It smells of scorched canvas and coal dust from Mom's hair."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing on the saddle in the downpour again, and the hammer above my shoulder is humming like a transformer."}, {"md": "I remember how the hammer shrieked, and how it smelled of a socket. I remember the whole saddle below me and nothing higher than me."}, {"md": "The water in the hollow was carrying rocks; that much I remembered right. But what I made of it was stupid. Climbing up high in a thunderstorm means turning yourself into the cedar. Below the water is no good, above everything is no good either. You need a place in the middle."}]}, "✝В3-05.A": {"title": "Overflow Ice", "date": "7 February 2012, 14:41", "epitaph": "Age 22. Yakutia, a nameless creek, two thousand two hundred steps from the rig. Ran for camp.", "mourner": "Petrovich found me by my tracks three hours later. He carried the core boxes to camp himself and handed them in against the inventory. Zoya Arkadyevna flew in from Yakutsk for the funeral in Kemerovo and stood by the door the whole time, never going up to Mom.", "text": [{"md": "I run. The boot knocks against the ice, wooden, ringing, and with every step it gets heavier, as if someone were pouring lead into it. My lungs burn. The scarf on my face is a crust of ice; I rip it off, and the frost grabs my cheeks so that they stop being mine at once."}, {"md": "At step six hundred I fall. My leg won't bend, the knee is in a crust of ice like a cast, and I get up off one knee like an old man. At step one thousand I fall again and sit on the ice for a long time, because I can't get up on the first try, or the second. Then I get warm. First in my leg, then everywhere. I know that's wrong, they told me, but I feel good, and I take off my mitten because it's hot."}, {"md": "The last thing I see is the creek ahead, white, flat as a road, and the sun over the hill, small and red. The larches around me stand in hoarfrost and are silent. It smells of coal dust, of Mom's hair when she comes home from a shift."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing on the creek again, knee-deep in the overflow, and steam is rising off my wet boot."}, {"md": "I'm hot. I remember how it got hot, how I took off my mitten. I remember which step I fell on the second time: one thousand, halfway. It was two thousand steps to camp."}, {"md": "My foot in the boot is wet and doesn't hurt anymore. The felt on it crunches when I shift my weight, still softly, for now."}]}, "✝В3-06.A": {"title": "The Tusk", "date": "18 July 2015, 17:20", "epitaph": "Age 26. Northern Yakutia, the tusk hunters' camp under the yedoma, by the diesel barrel. Went to explain we weren't an inspection.", "mourner": "Sardaana brought me back to base in our boat alone, eight hours against the current, and wouldn't let Zoya Arkadyevna close my eyes until she'd finished the measurements. Later, in Yakutsk, the mammoth skeleton was named after me. She signed the plaque herself.", "text": [{"md": "I walk toward the barrel, toward Gena, talking as I go, loudly, cheerfully, the way you talk to drunks outside your building: guys, it's all fine, we're not an inspection, we dig permafrost, here's the tape, here's the notebook. I reach into my storm jacket pocket for the notebook, to show them."}, {"md": "Slavik sees me reach into my pocket. He gets up with the shotgun, fast, drops his mug. Gena yells something at him, \"what are you doing\" or \"stop,\" I can't make it out. The shot is very loud, louder than the storm on the golets, and I sit down in the mud, because suddenly my legs won't hold me."}, {"md": "At first there's no pain; it's just hot under my ribs, on the right, as if someone had put a baked potato there. Then I see that the mud under me is turning red. Sardaana screams in Yakut, terribly, and runs to me, and drops to her knees beside me, and presses her tape measure to my side, all of it, in a wad, like a bandage. Above us hangs the mammoth's leg in reddish wool; it's thirty thousand years old, and it doesn't care. It smells of frozen rot, of the river and of coal dust from Mom's hair."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing by the tunnel again, and Slavik by the barrel says for the third time, \"Inspection, Uncle Gen.\""}, {"md": "Under my ribs on the right it's hot. I touch the spot with my hand, and the storm jacket there is dry. I remember how he stood up when I reached into my pocket. I remember Sardaana's tape in my side, with little flowers, red."}, {"md": "Words don't work here when there's a gun in someone's hands and vodka in his mug, and mine are the worst of all if I'm reaching into a pocket while I say them."}]}, "✝В3-08.A": {"title": "The Cockpit", "date": "12 September 2023, 10:42", "lesson": "It's the boot, not the pedal", "epitaph": "Age 34. A nameless ridge, the cockpit of an Mi-8, the doorway into the cabin. Pulled his friend by the shoulders.", "mourner": "Kostya pulled Vitka out through the window, a minute after I stopped pulling. Vitka walked with a cane until he died, and every year on the twelfth of September he called Sardaana and stayed silent on the line until she said, \"Me too.\"", "text": [{"md": "I grab him under the arms and pull. He doesn't scream, just breathes out through his teeth. Something below holds. I brace my foot against the bulkhead and pull again, harder, and something in my back tears, hot, and below it holds, the way an anchor holds."}, {"md": "Behind me, from the cabin, it suddenly gets bright. I look back. Under the cabin ceiling there's fire, yellow, along the wiring, along the canvas of the jump seats, and it's coming toward us, into the cockpit door, because the window above us draws like a stovepipe. The smoke turns black and thick. I'm breathing it. Vitka coughs in my arms and says, \"Andryukh, get out. Get out, you idiot.\""}, {"md": "I don't get out. I pull, and below it holds. Then I'm lying on top of him, with fire above us, and it's so hot the hair on my head crackles. The last thing I see is his boot under the pedal, tarpaulin, wide in the shaft, and the foot sitting loose inside it. It smells of burnt rubber and of coal dust from Mom's hair, like when I was little and she leaned over me before sleep."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm on my knees in the cockpit again, and Vitka is hanging in his harness saying, \"Give me a yank, huh? Only a hard one.\""}, {"md": "My hair is crackling. I touch my head, and it's all there. I remember how the fire came from the cabin toward us, into the chimney of the window, and how I lay on top of Vitka. I remember his boot."}, {"md": "I was pulling him by the shoulders, and the boot stayed put, tarpaulin, with a shaft up to the knee. His foot moved freely inside it; I could feel that through his trouser leg when I yanked."}]}, "✝В7-03.A": {"title": "The Icicle", "date": "4 February 2011, 11:22", "lesson": "Icicles hang over the wall", "epitaph": "Age 21. Rudnichny, the footpath along the apartment block. Took a shortcut to the pharmacy.", "mourner": "Until spring my wife wouldn't go near the window that looks out on that footpath. Varya grew up and all her life walked around buildings through the middle of the yard, without knowing why.", "text": [{"md": "The footpath along the building is polished like a mirror; the stroller rolls along it by itself, you only have to hold on. I walk right under the wall, where it's less slippery, and look at Varya's nose in her snowsuit bag."}, {"md": "There's a short crack overhead. The blow lands on my neck and shoulder at once, so I don't even hear it; I'm just lying with my cheek on the ice. My arms are somewhere apart from me, I can't feel them. Next to me pieces of ice glitter, clear, with a birch leaf frozen inside one."}, {"md": "I've let go of the stroller. It's rolling downhill, toward the road, slowly, bouncing over the ruts, and I watch it with one eye, because the other one is in the snow. I can't shout; my mouth is warm and salty. The stroller rolls, and I lie there watching until I can't see anymore."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the corner of the building again, holding the stroller handle, and Varya smacks her lips in her sleep."}, {"md": "I remember lying with my cheek on the ice and not feeling my arms. Now I'm squeezing the stroller handle so hard the plastic creaks, and my arms feel it, both of them."}, {"md": "I remember the stroller rolling toward the road, bouncing, and I couldn't shout. At the edge of the roof over the footpath hangs an icicle as tall as I am. A minute ago I didn't even look at it."}, {"md": "My neck aches on the right side, in one spot, and I rub it with my mitten. Along the wall old ice splinters are frozen into the packed snow, a whole strip of them along the entire building. In the middle of the yard the snow is knee-deep and untouched, and there isn't a single splinter in it."}]}, "✝В7-03.B": {"title": "The Icicle", "date": "4 February 2011, 11:23", "lesson": "The grader on the shoulder", "epitaph": "Age 21. Rudnichny, the shoulder by the bus stop. Walked along the road.", "mourner": "My wife never learned to drive. She walked Varya to kindergarten and then to school by the hand, on the far side of the street, right up until graduation.", "text": [{"md": "The snow ridge on the shoulder is waist-high, so I have to walk on the very edge of the asphalt, in the packed rut. Behind me a horn gives a short beep. I turn around. On the curve a gray Toyota is sliding sideways, slowly, like in a dream, straight at us."}, {"md": "I shove the stroller into the snowbank with both hands, as hard as I can, and it sinks in up to the wheels. I don't have time to jump clear myself. The bumper hits me behind the knees, my legs fold the wrong way, I'm thrown onto the hood and then onto the asphalt, the back of my head first."}, {"md": "The car stops a meter from me. Somebody inside is shouting. I can't turn my head, and I look up at the sky, white as the ceiling in the maternity ward. Right next to me, in the snowbank, Varya has woken up and is screaming, loud and deep, and I lie there and can't answer her."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the corner of the building again, holding the stroller handle, and Varya smacks her lips in her sleep."}, {"md": "I remember my legs folding the wrong way. I shift from foot to foot, stamp a felt boot on the ice, and both legs are there, both bending the right way."}, {"md": "I remember the gray Toyota coming at us sideways, and Varya screaming in the snowbank while I lay on my back and couldn't turn my head. Behind the garages the grader is grinding along. Soon the cars will come after it."}, {"md": "The Toyota came around the curve by the bus stop right behind the grader, on the freshly packed snow. The grader grinds on, and my knees twitch by themselves, the way they did when they folded."}]}, "✝В7-03.C": {"title": "The Icicle", "date": "4 February 2011, 11:24", "lesson": "A manhole under the snow", "epitaph": "Age 21. Rudnichny, the garages over the heating main. Somebody had sold the manhole cover for scrap.", "mourner": "For three years my wife wrote to the city council about open manholes, and every time they wrote back. Until she started school, Varya was afraid to go into any stairwell that smelled of steam.", "text": [{"md": "Through the garages it's three minutes shorter. A path is trampled between the lockups, steam stands over the heating main, and the snow there is gray and soft. I push the stroller along the trampled part, step onto the smooth white patch, and the smooth patch drops out from under my feet."}, {"md": "I let go of the handle right away. At least I get that right. I fall down into the warm steam, hit my temple on a pipe, and my neck cracks, loud, like a dry branch under a felt boot."}, {"md": "After that I lie on the pipes, in the warmth, and feel nothing below my chin. Up above, in the round hole, there's gray sky and the edge of a wheel. Varya is crying. I listen to her for as long as I can hear."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the corner of the building again, holding the stroller handle, and Varya smacks her lips in her sleep."}, {"md": "I remember the crack in my neck. I turn my head right, left, and my neck turns obediently, and my scarf itches the way it always does."}, {"md": "I remember the round hole with gray sky in it, the edge of the wheel, and Varya crying up there while I lay in the warm steam and couldn't even blink back at her. Steam is rising over the garages. Now I know there's no cover on the manhole there, only a crust of snow."}, {"md": "The smooth white patch by the heating main, the one nobody had stepped on: my heel remembers it giving way like the crust on a snowdrift. Right next to it ran somebody else's path, trampled and crooked, and I went around it because my way was shorter."}]}, "✝В7-03.D": {"title": "The Icicle", "date": "4 February 2011, 11:21", "epitaph": "Age 21. Rudnichny, the footpath along the apartment block. Ran to beat the icicle.", "mourner": "A boy of about twelve with a hockey stick caught the stroller by the handle. Every first of September after that my wife sent him a chocolate bar through his mother, until he went into the army.", "text": [{"md": "I know how much time I have, and I run. On the mirror ice the stroller rolls by itself, faster than me, pulling the handle out of my hands. Under the wall, in the middle of the footpath, my felt boot slides out from under me, and I sit down hard on the ice, right on my tailbone, still holding the handle."}, {"md": "I'm sitting exactly where I lay last time. There's a short crack overhead. The icicle falls when it's supposed to, not a second earlier. It doesn't know I was running."}, {"md": "The blow lands on my shoulder and head. I let go of the handle, and the stroller rolls on, downhill. I lie on my side and watch it bounce over the ruts until I can't see anymore."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the corner of the building again, holding the stroller handle, and Varya smacks her lips in her sleep."}, {"md": "I remember my boot sliding out. My tailbone aches, though I haven't sat on it today. The icicle fell to the minute, just as I remembered, only I ended up under it again, because nobody runs on ice like that."}, {"md": "I remembered the time right, and I was wrong to think I'd make it. You don't go under that wall at all, not walking, not running. I look up, and the icicle is hanging there, long, as tall as I am, and the sun breaks inside it."}]}, "✝В7-04.A": {"title": "The Banya", "date": "9 March 2013, 21:50", "lesson": "A shut damper means monoxide", "epitaph": "Age 24. Yagunovsky, my father-in-law's banya. Shut the damper early, so the heat would stay in.", "mourner": "They buried my father-in-law and me on the same day. My mother-in-law sold the dacha that summer, banya and all, and for a long time afterward Varya asked why everybody else had a grandpa.", "text": [{"md": "I get up and push the damper shut all the way. The heat right away turns even and soft, better than before. My father-in-law lies down on the lower bench and says he'll just lie there a minute. I sit down next to him."}, {"md": "My head gets heavy, like after two shifts back to back, and my temples pound, but it feels so good I don't want to move. I think I'll get up in a moment and go out. Then it turns out I've been lying on the floor for a long time, cheek on the wet boards, and the boards are warm. I throw up right on them, and I don't care."}, {"md": "My father-in-law is snoring. Then the snoring stops in the middle. I want to tell him he's stopped snoring, and I say it somewhere inside my head, but my mouth doesn't open."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm sitting on the bench in the steam room again, and my father-in-law is waving at the flue: \"Shut the damper, Andryukha.\""}, {"md": "I remember the warm wet boards under my cheek. I run my palm over my face, and it's dry and hot, no boards."}, {"md": "I remember the snoring stopping in the middle. That stopping makes me sicker than the heat does. Little blue flames run over the coals."}]}, "✝В7-05.A": {"title": "Passing", "date": "9 January 2016, 13:12", "lesson": "A KAMAZ behind the semi", "epitaph": "Age 26. The Novosibirsk highway, the straight before Yurga. Pulled out to pass a semi.", "mourner": "My wife got Kolka out through the back door in his car seat, and Varya out of her booster. For ten years after that Varya wouldn't sit in a front seat, not even in a taxi.", "text": [{"md": "I put on the turn signal and pull into the oncoming lane. The semi beside me is as long as a fence, and the snow dust off it hits the windshield so hard the wipers just smear the white around. I floor it. The Granta roars and picks up speed slowly, too slowly."}, {"md": "Out of the white ahead come two headlights, high up, at the height of my head. I manage to jerk the wheel right, toward the semi, and the KAMAZ doesn't hit us head-on, it hits my door."}, {"md": "Glass pours into my lap like grain. The steering wheel is pressed into my chest, and I can't take another breath, however hard I try. In the back Varya is screaming, and Kolka is crying in his bass voice, the way he always does when he's woken up. I listen to them and try to take one more breath."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm driving behind the semi on the straight before Yurga again, and the wipers are chasing snow dust across the glass."}, {"md": "I remember two headlights at the height of my head and the wheel in my chest. I slide the seat back one notch, and it gets easier to breathe, though the wheel has nothing to do with it."}, {"md": "I remember Varya screaming and Kolka crying, and I couldn't breathe in to tell them everything was all right. Varya asks, \"Dad, are we gonna pass it?\" There's been no oncoming traffic for a long time."}]}, "✝В7-05.F": {"title": "Passing", "date": "9 January 2016, 13:13", "lesson": "There are two KAMAZes", "epitaph": "Age 26. The Novosibirsk highway, the straight before Yurga. Pulled out to pass right behind the KAMAZ, because he remembered it.", "mourner": "At the trial the driver of the second KAMAZ kept repeating that he'd been following his partner nose to tail and only saw the Granta when it came out of the snow. My wife said afterward that she saw his headlights first and didn't have time to scream.", "text": [{"md": "The first KAMAZ roars past, the Granta rocks, and I pull out from behind the semi just as I planned, right behind it. The oncoming lane is empty, I know it is. I floor it and don't even look ahead; I look at the trailer, at how much of it is left."}, {"md": "Halfway along the trailer two more headlights come out of the white, high up, at the height of my head. A rust-orange KAMAZ, just like the first. In the life I remembered, it wasn't there."}, {"md": "I don't have time to jerk the wheel. The last thing I see is the little icon on its dashboard, the same as the first one's."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm driving behind the semi on the straight before Yurga again, and the wipers are chasing snow dust across the glass."}, {"md": "I remember two pairs of headlights, one after the other. The first I knew by heart. Nobody knew the second."}, {"md": "I remember the icon on the dashboard, two identical icons. Varya asks, \"Dad, are we gonna pass it?\" I look at the tarp with the dumplings on it and understand there's nothing left to remember here."}]}, "✝В7-06.A": {"title": "The Garland", "date": "31 December 2019, 03:44", "lesson": "The room breathes out fire", "epitaph": "Age 30. Lesnaya Polyana, ninth floor, the doorway of the kids' room. Breaking down the door.", "mourner": "A firefighter carried Varya out. My wife never did learn his name: he turned down the award certificate and left for his next call. Every year on the thirtieth of December Varya puts a candle in a jar in the window.", "text": [{"md": "I hit the door with my shoulder. Our door is pressed sawdust, but the frame is solid, and the chair on the other side holds. On the fourth blow the door splits by the handle. I shove my arm through the hole, feel for the back of the chair, and push it away."}, {"md": "The door opens, and the room breathes out at me. The fire comes out into the hallway all at once, like out of a stove when you open its door, right into my face. My hair crackles. I breathe in, because there's no way not to, and the air burns me from the inside, all the way down to my stomach."}, {"md": "I fall to the hallway floor and crawl toward where Varya was coughing behind the smoke. The skin on my hands is blistering. The carpet under my palms is hot and sticky. I crawl for as long as I still have hands."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the door of Varya's room again, and the handle won't give. A strip of orange light burns under the door."}, {"md": "I remember the room breathing fire into my face and me breathing that fire in. I run my palm over my face. My eyebrows are there, my skin is smooth, only wet with sweat."}, {"md": "I remember the hot, sticky carpet under my palms. Behind the door Varya is coughing, thin, a child's cough."}]}, "✝В7-06.B": {"title": "The Garland", "date": "31 December 2019, 03:47", "lesson": "No time for the extinguisher", "epitaph": "Age 30. Lesnaya Polyana, the stairs between the eighth and ninth floors. Ran for the fire extinguisher.", "mourner": "A neighbor from the tenth floor carried Varya out, in his shorts and felt boots. Every New Year my wife sent him a card, and he never once answered and looked away in the elevator.", "text": [{"md": "I burst out onto the landing, leaving our front door wide open. The red cabinet on the wall opens easily. Inside it's empty, just a rolled-up fire hose with no nozzle and VANYA IS A LOSER written in marker. There's no extinguisher."}, {"md": "I run down to the eighth floor, then the seventh; that cabinet is empty too. Smoke is crawling down after me, out of our open door into the stairwell, and the stairwell sucks it upward like a chimney. The light on the landing goes off on its timer. I turn back."}, {"md": "Between the eighth and ninth floors the smoke is already thick, greasy and hot. It gets into my throat, I cough and can't stop coughing. I can't see the steps. I sit down on a step, then lie down with my cheek on it. It's eight steps to our door. I count them and can't finish counting."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the door of Varya's room again, and the handle won't give. A strip of orange light burns under the door."}, {"md": "I remember the empty red cabinet and VANYA IS A LOSER in marker. I remember eight steps in the dark that I never finished counting."}, {"md": "My throat is raw, though there's no smoke yet, only the smell. Behind me my wife is screaming my name."}]}, "✝В7-07.A": {"title": "I'll Do It Myself", "date": "November 2022, 19:23", "epitaph": "Age 33. Lesnaya Polyana, the kids' room, the outlet by Varya's desk. Did it himself, quick.", "mourner": "Ever since, my wife flips the breaker on the landing every time she leaves home, even in summer when she goes to the dacha. Varya trained as an electrical engineer and never explained to anyone why.", "text": [{"md": "I undo two screws, and the outlet falls out of the wall into my palm, hanging on its wires. I don't check the live wire with the tester; no time. With my left hand I hold on to the radiator under the window so I won't fall over from my crouch, and with my right I take hold of the wires to pull them out longer."}, {"md": "My right arm cramps at once, all the way to the shoulder. My fingers clench on the wire by themselves, and I can't open them, though I try very hard. The current goes through me into the radiator. Something in my chest jerks like a fish on a line, once, twice, and my heart misses a beat and never gets back in step."}, {"md": "I'm lying on the floor by Varya's desk, my cheek on her slipper with bunny ears. The screwdriver rolls under the bed. Varya is screaming on the bed, thin and long, and I look at the slipper."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm crouching by Varya's desk again, the screwdriver in my hand, and the outlet is dangling from the wall."}, {"md": "I remember my fingers clenching on the wire by themselves and not letting go. I put the screwdriver down on the floor, farther away, and wipe my palm on my pants, though it's dry."}, {"md": "I remember the bunny-eared slipper right next to my face, and Varya screaming. Right now she's sitting on the bed with her feet tucked under her, watching me like a magician."}]}, "✝В7-08.A": {"title": "The Pond", "date": "22 March 2026, 14:11", "lesson": "The ice is thin midpond", "epitaph": "Age 37. Yagunovsky, the dacha pond. Ran straight to his son.", "mourner": "Somebody from a neighboring dacha pulled Kolka out with a boat hook, and Kolka never remembered his face. All his life he said the only thing he remembered was his dad running to him across the ice.", "text": [{"md": "I run straight, across the middle. About ten meters from the hole the ice under me doesn't crack, it just ends, and I go under at once, head and all, in my jacket and felt boots."}, {"md": "The cold squeezes my chest so I can't breathe out. My jacket fills with water and pulls me down. Under the ice it's light, gray-green, like inside a bottle. I push up and hit the top of my head on the ice. There's no hole above me; it's somewhere off to the side, and I can't tell where anymore."}, {"md": "I scrape at the ice from below with my nails and pound on it with my fist. Up above, a dark shape is running across the ice, somebody with a boat hook, and I pound for him, pound, until the air runs out and I breathe in water."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing by the fence again, shovel in my hands, and Kolka's head comes up over the water and disappears."}, {"md": "I remember the ice above my head from the inside, gray, with bubbles in it. I remember pounding on it with my fist. I open my fist, dry and warm, and my fingers won't do what I tell them."}, {"md": "I remember the ice didn't end at the hole, it ended earlier, in the middle. Where a cold spring comes up in the pond in summer."}]}, "✝В7-08.B": {"title": "The Pond", "date": "22 March 2026, 14:12", "lesson": "Crawling takes too long", "epitaph": "Age 37. Yagunovsky, the dacha pond. Crawled to his son with a board, carefully.", "mourner": "The neighbors from the dacha across the road pulled Kolka out with a boat hook and got him breathing on the bank. Divers from Kemerovo only got me out by evening. All his life after that Kolka was in a hurry, even when there was nowhere to hurry to.", "text": [{"md": "I grab the board, drop on my belly by the bank and crawl, pushing it ahead of me. The ice crunches but holds, because my whole body is lying on it. It's thirty meters to crawl. I count them in elbows, and at twelve Kolka's hand lets go of the edge."}, {"md": "He goes under quietly, without a scream. I drop the board and crawl faster, then get up on my knees, then on my feet. The ice by the hole sags under me like a mattress, and I go through right where he just was."}, {"md": "Under the water it's gray and murky. I feel around with my hands, find a hood, pull up, and we both hit the ice. The hole is somewhere off to the side. Kolka stops struggling before I do, and I hold on to him by the hood until I run out of air."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing by the fence again, shovel in my hands, and Kolka is holding on to the edge with one hand."}, {"md": "I remember counting elbows to twelve and his hand letting go of the edge. I look at his hand, and it's holding on, and another hand's width of the edge breaks off under it."}, {"md": "I remember the hood in my fist under the water. The board is lying by the fence, long and reliable, and it's thirty meters to the hole."}]}, "✝В7-09р.A": {"title": "Scouting", "date": "September 2033, 09:40", "lesson": "Before Yurga, a car without lights", "epitaph": "Age 44. The Novosibirsk highway, the straight before Yurga. Pulled out to pass a semi. Was driving ahead of his daughter.", "mourner": "A minute later Varya drove past his car and recognized it by the license plate. She never went to Akademgorodok. She stayed in Kemerovo, went to work in the Azot lab, and every year on the twenty-first of September she drove out to that straight with a thermos and stood by the shoulder until she was frozen.", "text": [{"md": "I pull into the oncoming lane behind the semi without looking, the way Varya would if she were in a hurry. Far back in the mirror her red Lada bobs along. The semi beside me is long and wet, spray from it hits the windshield, and the wipers can't keep up."}, {"md": "The oncoming car comes over the rise with no headlights. Small, dark, almost invisible in the morning drizzle. I see it when it's a semi's length away, and I only have time to think that a minute from now Varya would see it just like this."}, {"md": "It hits my left headlight. The car spins and is thrown under the semi's rear wheel. The wheel goes over the hood, then over me. It smells of wet asphalt and gasoline."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm driving behind the semi on the straight before Yurga again, and the wipers are chasing spray across the glass."}, {"md": "I remember the small dark car with no headlights, coming over the rise in the oncoming lane. I remember the semi's wheel going over me, and having time to think about Varya."}, {"md": "So that's what's waiting for her here, a minute from now. I slow down and stay behind the semi. My hands on the wheel are wet. For the first time I died on purpose, and I woke up pleased."}]}, "✝В7-09р.B": {"title": "Scouting", "date": "September 2033, 10:25", "lesson": "A moose at the Taiga turn-off", "epitaph": "Age 44. The Novosibirsk highway, the turn-off for Taiga. A moose. His daughter was driving a minute behind him.", "mourner": "A minute later Varya braked by a moose lying across the road and her father's crumpled car. For twenty years after that she drove to Kemerovo the long way, through Anzhero-Sudzhensk.", "text": [{"md": "At the Taiga turn-off I don't brake. Ninety, the way people drive in the morning when they're late. The moose-crossing sign flashes past as a white blur."}, {"md": "The moose comes out of the birch trees not from the side but right in front of me, all of it at once, tall as a garage door. My high beams in the drizzle stop at its legs, long gray legs, and its head is somewhere higher, above the roof. I stand on the brake, and the car still goes in under its belly."}, {"md": "It comes down on the roof. The roof folds down onto me, heavy and soft, and the cold fur smells of swamp. The last thing I see is its hoof twitching in front of my face, slowly, like a pendulum."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm coming up on the Taiga turn-off again, and the moose sign shows white in the drizzle."}, {"md": "I remember the gray legs in front of the hood and the hoof in front of my face. I remember the fur smelling of swamp, and the roof folding."}, {"md": "I slow to sixty. It comes out of the birches in the same place, huge, and stands on the shoulder. I go around it in the oncoming lane, slowly, and call Varya: \"Slow down to sixty. Moose.\" Varya will be here in a minute, doing ninety."}]}, "✝В7-09р.C": {"title": "Scouting", "date": "September 2033, 11:10", "lesson": "In Yurga, a Niva backs up", "epitaph": "Age 44. Yurga, the highway gas station. Was standing at the pump with a coffee, waiting for his daughter.", "mourner": "Varya pulled into the gas station a minute later and saw her father's car at the pump. Later my wife found a map on his phone with marks all along the highway, and until she was an old woman she wondered what he'd been looking for.", "text": [{"md": "In Yurga I pull into the gas station and buy coffee in a paper cup, the way Varya would. I stand with it by the pump, outside, because it's stuffy in the car. The coffee is hot, and I blow on it."}, {"md": "Behind me a Niva revs. The driver's hung over; he can't see me behind the pump and backs up hard to swing around toward the exit. The tow hitch hits me behind the knees, I fall backward, and the Niva rolls over me with a rear wheel, and then, panicking, forward again."}, {"md": "The coffee runs across the asphalt, steaming. I lie on my back and see the gas station canopy over me, white lamps still on from the night. Somebody is shouting from the cashier's window."}], "awake": [{"md": "…and I'm standing at the pump in Yurga again with a paper cup of coffee, and it's still too hot."}, {"md": "I remember the tow hitch behind my knees and the wheel going over me twice, back and forth. I remember the white lamps overhead."}, {"md": "The Niva is sitting by the exit; the driver is just starting the engine. I step behind the pump, up against the cashier's wall, and watch him back up to where I was just standing and drive off without noticing. A minute from now Varya will be standing here."}]}}, "config": {"_comment": "Английские тексты конфига (только текстовые поля; логика — в scenes/_config.json). Сливается поверх русского в graph_en.js.", "title": "Black Backwater", "vars": {"ТЕЛ": {"name": "Body", "tiers": [[0, "frail"], [20, "ordinary"], [40, "sturdy"], [60, "strong"], [80, "iron"]], "desc": "Health, strength, stamina.", "affects": "surviving cold, a fight, a cave-in, a long run; some deaths are decided by the body"}, "РАЗ": {"name": "Mind", "tiers": [[0, "scatterbrained"], [20, "ordinary"], [40, "sharp"], [60, "keen"], [80, "brilliant"]], "desc": "Memory, reckoning, learning.", "affects": "hunches and exact decisions, school and instruments, talking as an equal with clever people"}, "ЭМП": {"name": "Heart", "tiers": [[0, "deaf to people"], [20, "ordinary"], [40, "perceptive"], [60, "attuned"], [80, "sees right through you"]], "desc": "How well I feel other people. Not health, not romance: noticing.", "affects": "heart-to-heart talks and making up, closeness with Nika and family, who I manage to notice in time"}, "ВОЛ": {"name": "Will", "tiers": [[0, "timid"], [20, "ordinary"], [40, "stubborn"], [60, "firm"], [80, "unbending"]], "desc": "Stubbornness and endurance.", "affects": "saying no, not breaking, holding on to someone, seeing it through"}, "ДАР": {"name": "Resonance", "tiers": [[0, "silence"], [20, "a chill"], [40, "voices"], [60, "a choir"], [80, "choirmaster"]], "desc": "My link to the ones I was in other worlds.", "affects": "the chill before a death, the voices of the Choir, how far back I can rewind"}, "ДРЕЙФ": {"name": "Drift", "tiers": [[0, "the world holds still"], [5, "small things don't add up"], [40, "the world is sliding"], [60, "a stranger's world"], [80, "the splice"]], "desc": "How far the world has slid from the one I was born in. It grows when I bend fate to suit myself.", "affects": "small things around me stop adding up, people and endings change; with little drift the bright endings stay open"}, "СВЯЗЬ_НИКА": {"name": "Nika"}, "СВЯЗЬ_ВИТЬКА": {"name": "Vitka"}, "СВЯЗЬ_МАМА": {"name": "Mom"}, "СВЯЗЬ_ОТЕЦ": {"name": "Dad"}, "СВЯЗЬ_ЛУНЁВ": {"name": "Lunyov"}, "СВЯЗЬ_ПАРТНЁР": {"name": "The one beside me"}}, "shock_first": ["At first I don't understand where I am. And when I do, it only gets worse.", "I just died. For real, all the way to the end, and I remember all of it, every second, the way you remember yesterday. And here I am, alive, and everything around me is exactly the way it was a minute ago, before it happened.", "The death already feels like a memory, as if it happened long ago and to somebody else, though only a second has gone by. But no dream hurts like this afterward. My body remembers everything down to the last breath, and my hands are shaking.", "I don't understand what's happening to me. Maybe I've gone crazy. Maybe it was a premonition. Only a premonition doesn't leave the taste of that minute in your mouth.", "One thing I know for sure: I'm not going back there."], "shock_again": ["It happened again. I remember my death as clearly as an old photograph, and I'm here again, alive, a minute before it.", "It's no easier for not being the first time. But at least now I know I have this minute."], "shock_later": ["I'm here again, alive, a minute before my death, and my hands are shaking. I don't understand what's wrong with me, and some part of me already knows."], "meta_entries": [{"id": "сосед-Т07", "title": "✝ — the me next door", "date": "December 2003", "text": "Ice by the bank, the green glow of a dashboard underwater. This me got into the Lada Nine and never got out. For one second I saw through his eyes."}], "photo_eras": {"bw": "old black-and-white photo, 1990s", "color": "glossy color print from a photo shop, 2000s", "digital": "digital point-and-shoot, 2010s", "phone": "smartphone, 2020s–2040s", "future": "a picture of the future that's too clean, 2050–2061"}}, "memos": {"_comment": "Английские подписи флагов для «Я» → «Что со мной было» (scenes/_memos.json). Только переведённые жизни; остальное — по-русски до перевода.", "F_Т01": {"СПОР": "At seven I raced Vitka to the snag. I didn't make it.", "БУБЛИК": "At seven I jumped into the backwater after Bublik. Bublik made it out.", "ЧАСЫ": "At seven I dove for the glint by the snag and came up with a watch in my fist. It's stopped at 4:12.", "ДОЛГ": "At seven I ran for the grown-ups, and Vitka stayed in the water. I owe him."}, "F_Т02": {"ЧЕЙ": "Under the water somebody asked me, \"Whose kid are you?\"", "ХОР": "Under the water somebody was counting along with the girl. Lots of voices.", "ЛИЦО": "Under the water I looked down and saw myself on the sand, with an old man over me.", "МАМА": "Under the water I called for Mom, and a stranger's palm lay on my forehead."}, "F_Т03": {"ШРАМ": "Punched Shmelyov for calling me Vodyanoy, and whatever happened, happened.", "ВОДЯНОЙ": "Made the nickname Vodyanoy my own.", "ЗАМЕТКА": "Put up notices, and the Kuzbass paper wrote about it: looking for the old man in the striped shirt.", "ВОДОБОЯЗНЬ": "Swore never to talk about water again."}, "F_Т04": {"РЕЛЬСЫ": "Sat on the railroad tracks with Dad in ninety-eight.", "РЫНОК": "Sold frying pans at the market with Mom.", "ЦВЕТМЕТ": "Cut cable for scrap copper with Vitka.", "ОЛИМПИАДА": "Went to the math olympiad with Nina Petrovna."}, "F_Т05": {"ТЕТРАДИ": "Read Granddad's notebooks. I remember a sentence in the margin.", "ФОТО": "I know who the second man in Granddad's photo is.", "ПРОДАЛ": "Sold Granddad's box to the junk dealer for Sega cartridges.", "СОЖЖЕНО": "Gave Granddad's box to Dad. Dad burned it."}, "F_Т06": {"ПЕРЕПЛЫЛ": "Swam across Black Backwater with Nika.", "ТАЙНА_НИКИ": "Told Nika about the dark under the water. Nobody else.", "ПАЦАНЫ": "Laughed at \"the Novosibirsk girl\" with the guys.", "БАКЕНЩИК": "The buoy keeper told me about a man who stands by the backwater every July."}, "F_Т07": {"КЛЮЧИ": "Yanked the keys out of Vitka's Lada Nine on the ice.", "ЛЁД": "Drove across the frozen Tom. My body remembers what to do.", "СДАЛ": "Called Dad from a pay phone so Vitka wouldn't drive on the ice.", "ВИДЕНИЕ": "Saw Vitka go under the ice. In this world he didn't."}, "F_Т08": {"ПАЛЫЧ": "Hauled air tanks for the mine rescuers for three nights while Dad was underground.", "ДОМ": "Held it together at home for three nights, for Mom and Sonka.", "ТОРГ": "Went to the backwater for three nights and bargained with the water for Dad.", "ДАТЧИКИ": "Found out why the Glubokaya blew and told a reporter."}, "F_Т09": {"АРМИЯ": "Gave Vitka a proper send-off to the army.", "ДОЛГ_СВАРНОМУ": "Helped Vitka dodge the draft through Welder. Now I owe Welder.", "НИКА": "Went to the dacha with Nika instead of Vitka's send-off.", "ДВЕРЬ": "In the garage Dad said, \"Under the mine. There was a door down there.\""}, "F_ЗНАЕТ": "I've already died once. I remember it like a memory.", "F_ЗНАЕТ_ДЕДА": "I know who my grandfather was.", "F_НАУЧИЛ": "Taught Andryusha to swim.", "F_ЖЕНА": {"ЖИВА": "Noticed my wife was tired, and got there in time.", "УМЕРЛА": "My wife has been gone since '47."}, "F_ПАРТНЁР": {"НИКА": "Nika is beside me.", "ОЛЯ": "Olga is beside me.", "ТАНЯ": "Tanya is beside me. She doesn't hand me a lamp anymore; she hides it.", "НЕТ": "Nobody beside me.", "ПЛОЙ": "Ploy is beside me. Under the water she holds my hand.", "ЛЕРА": "Lera is beside me. Her scar is on her arm, and I remember which one.", "САРДААНА": "Sardaana is beside me. She measures mammoths with her mother's flowery tape measure."}, "F_РАЗВЕДЧИК": "I died three times on Varya's highway in a single morning and talked her down it minute by minute.", "F_ОДЕЯЛО": "Went into the burning kids' room under a wet blanket.", "F_В701": {"НИКА": "Answered Nika with a long letter.", "ОЛЯ": "Went to the movies with Olya from the next stairwell.", "СМЕНА": "Took extra shifts at Azot."}, "F_В703": {"ВАРЯ": "Got Varya to the pharmacy alive. In another world I didn't."}, "F_В704": {"ТЕСТЬ": "Dragged my father-in-law out of the banya into the snow.", "ПОМНИЛ": "Dragged my father-in-law out of the banya because I remembered how people fall asleep in there. The next morning he had a different father.", "УМЕР": "Walked out of the banya alone. My father-in-law stayed on the bench, and there was no going back for him."}, "F_В705": {"ВИШНЯ": "Let the oncoming KAMAZ by. The silver Granta turned cherry red.", "ПЕРВЫЙ": "Passed the semi right behind the KAMAZ, because I remembered it.", "ВТОРОЙ": "Passed the semi only after the second KAMAZ, the one that wasn't there the first time."}, "F_В706": {"ДВЕРЬ": "Pulled Varya out of the burning kids' room.", "ЗНАНИЕ": "Went back to eight in the evening and didn't plug in the lights. Varya has written with her left hand ever since."}, "F_В708": {"СПАС": "Pulled Kolka out from under the ice.", "ПОМНИЛ": "Ran around Kolka along the bank, because I remembered where the ice was thin. Now Kolka's scar is on the other knee."}, "F_В709": {"ОТПУСТИЛ": "Let Varya go to Novosibirsk.", "КОНТРОЛЬ": "Put a tracker on Varya.", "ПРАВДА": "Told my wife about the icicle, the lights and the pond.", "РАЗВЕДКА": "I died on Varya's road so I'd know what was waiting for her."}}, "missing": ["В3-09", "В3-10", "В3-11", "В3-12", "В3-13", "В4-01", "В4-02", "В4-03", "В4-04", "В4-05", "В4-06", "В4-07", "В4-08", "В4-09", "В4-10", "В4-11", "В4-12", "В4-13", "В5-01", "В5-02", "В5-03", "В5-04", "В5-05", "В5-06", "В5-07", "В5-08", "В5-09", "В5-10", "В5-11", "В5-12", "В5-13", "В6-01", "В6-02", "В6-03", "В6-04", "В6-05", "В6-06", "В6-07", "В6-08", "В6-09", "В6-10", "В6-11", "В6-12", "В6-13", "П1-01", "П1-02", "П1-03", "П2-01", "П3-01", "П4-01", "П4-02", "Ф-01", "Ф-02", "Ф-03", "Ф-04", "Ф-05", "Ф-06", "Ф-07", "Ф-08", "Ф-09", "Ф-10", "Ф-11", "Ф-12", "Ф-13", "Я-01", "Я-02", "Я-03", "⊣К-01", "⊣К-02", "⊣К-03", "⊣К-04", "⊣К-06", "⊣К-07", "⊣К-08", "⊣К-10", "⊣К-11", "⊣К-12", "⊣К-13", "⊣К-14", "⊣К-15", "✝В3-09.A", "✝В4-02.A", "✝В4-02.B", "✝В4-02.C", "✝В4-02.D", "✝В4-05.A", "✝В4-06.A", "✝В4-09.A", "✝В4-12.A", "✝В5-02.A", "✝В5-02.B", "✝В5-02.C", "✝В5-03.A", "✝В5-04.A", "✝В5-04.B", "✝В5-05.A", "✝В5-08.A", "✝В5-08.B", "✝В5-11.A", "✝В6-02.A", "✝В6-02.B", "✝В6-02.C", "✝В6-04.A", "✝В6-06.A", "✝В6-08.A", "✝В6-10.A", "✝В6-10.B", "✝В6-12.A", "✝Я-01.B", "✝Я-03.B"], "stats": {"done": 134, "total": 244}, "stale": [], "broken": []};

(function () {
  // слой английских текстов поверх русского графа (tools/build_en.py); непереведённое остаётся русским
  var G = window.GRAPH, L = window.GRAPH_EN_LAYER;
  if (!G || !L) return;
  function put(dst, o) {
    for (var k in o) {
      if (k === "choices") {
        (dst.choices || []).forEach(function (c) { var t = o.choices[c.id]; if (t) for (var j in t) c[j] = t[j]; });
      } else dst[k] = o[k];
    }
  }
  ["scenes", "deaths"].forEach(function (part) {
    for (var id in L[part]) if (G[part][id]) { put(G[part][id], L[part][id]); G[part][id].en = true; }
  });
  var C = G.config, E = L.config || {};
  ["title", "shock_first", "shock_again", "shock_later", "photo_eras"].forEach(function (k) { if (E[k]) C[k] = E[k]; });
  for (var v in (E.vars || {})) if (C.vars[v]) for (var f in E.vars[v]) C.vars[v][f] = E.vars[v][f];
  (E.meta_entries || []).forEach(function (m) { (C.meta_entries || []).forEach(function (x) { if (x.id === m.id) for (var f in m) if (f !== "id") x[f] = m[f]; }); });
  if (E.stihii_names) C.stihii_names = E.stihii_names;
  if (L.memos) for (var m in L.memos) if (G.memos && m.charAt(0) !== "_") G.memos[m] = L.memos[m];
  // путеводитель: названия и подписи шагов — из переведённых сцен; ручные подсказки — из _guides.json
  var GU = L.guides || {};
  for (var fid in (G.guides || {})) {
    var gd = G.guides[fid], sc = G.scenes[fid];
    if (sc && sc.en) gd.title = sc.title;
    if (GU[fid]) { if (GU[fid].hint) gd.hint = GU[fid].hint; if (GU[fid].forks) gd.forks = GU[fid].forks; }
    (gd.steps || []).forEach(function (s) {
      var x = G.scenes[s.scene];
      if (!x || !x.en) return;
      s.title = x.title;
      if (s.choice == null) s.label = "Next";
      else (x.choices || []).forEach(function (c) { if (c.id === s.choice) s.label = c.label; });
    });
  }
  // непереведённые сцены: русский текст с пометкой (откат, чтобы игра не ломалась)
  L.missing.forEach(function (id) { var s = G.scenes[id] || G.deaths[id]; if (s && s.text && id.charAt(0) !== "✝") s.text = [{ md: "This scene hasn't been translated yet, so it's shown in Russian.", kind: "sys" }].concat(s.text); });
  G.en_missing = L.missing; G.en_stats = L.stats; G.lang = "en";
})();
